// --- Preface (recto) ---
#metadata((kind: "front", title: "Preface"))
#heading(level: 1, outlined: false, numbering: none)[#text(size: 22pt, weight: "semibold")[Preface]]

#v(1em)

This book is a daily companion drawn from the great compendium of Christian hagiography, the _Acta Sanctorum_, gathered and edited from 1607 forward by the Bollandist Society of Antwerp. The Bollandists set themselves the task of collecting, sifting, and printing every credible _vita_ of every saint of the Christian East and West, day by day through the calendar year. The work continued for three hundred years; sixty-eight folio volumes appeared between 1643 and 1940, reaching as far through the calendar as the early days of November before publication effectively halted.

Three hundred and fifteen entries follow here, one for each day from the first of January to the tenth of November — the calendar reach of the published _Acta_. Each entry is grounded in the _Acta_'s own treatment of the day's saint: where the Bollandists wrote, the entry draws from what they wrote; where they declined to write, the entry yields the day to a saint they did treat substantively. Twelve great Christological and Marian feasts of the modern Roman calendar — the Presentation, the Annunciation, the Visitation, the Transfiguration, the Assumption, the Nativity of Mary, the Holy Cross, the Guardian Angels, the Rosary, All Saints, All Souls, the Dedication of the Lateran — are not represented as feasts here, because the Bollandists did not write substantive lives for them. Each yields, on its day, to a particular saint whose life the _Acta_ preserve. The Roman calendar holds the feasts; this book holds the saints.

The voice across the entries is meant for slow reading. Each is about three hundred and forty words, the length of a short morning's attention, in three or four paragraphs that move from a scene through the substance of the life into a quiet turn at the end. The aim is the _Lives of the Saints_ tradition that runs from Butler in the eighteenth century to Daniel-Rops in the twentieth — narrative, literary, and unhurried, neither apologetic nor hagiographic. A pull-quote closes each entry. Most quotes are from the Bollandist source itself. The few exceptions are quietly marked.

The fuller machinery of this project — the Bollandist source files in Latin, the audit of every pick, the record of the correction passes that brought the calendar into its present shape — lives at _actasanctorum.org_. Readers who want to follow a saint back to the _Acta_ will find a path there. The book in your hand is the surface of a much longer work.

