#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "1", name: "Odilo of Cluny"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 1]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Odilo of Cluny]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbot, founder of All Souls' Day, c. 962–1049]

#v(0.5em)

A pilgrim from Auvergne, shipwrecked on a small island near Sicily, was told by a local hermit that the rocks beyond their cove opened at certain hours into fissures of flame, and that voices could be heard from them — demons complaining that souls were being torn from their grip by the prayers and Masses of Odilo and the monks of Cluny. The pilgrim made his way home, and then to Souvigny, and told the abbot the story.

Odilo did not boast. He commanded. The next year, in every monastery of his order, on the day after the feast of All Saints, the whole community gathered to celebrate the Eucharist, sing the psalms, and distribute bread to the poor on behalf of all the faithful departed. He did not invent the prayers; the Church had prayed for the dead since the catacombs. He gave them a fixed day. The practice spread out of Cluny across Europe, and in time the universal Church received what Odilo had begun.

The chroniclers remembered him for other things. Riding near Paris, he once came upon two children dead of cold in the road, unburied and unmourned. He dismounted, stripped the wool from his back to cover them, dug their graves, and rode on. During the famine years he broke up the silver vessels of his abbey for bread — even melted the imperial crown that Henry had given him — judging it unworthy to deny the poor what Christ had been killed for. And when a leprous priest from Le Puy withdrew to a hut by the Loire and asked through an intermediary to see him, Odilo came at once: the brothers watched in alarm as the abbot took the man into his arms and held him there, in the long conversation of friends. He loved, the chronicler wrote, the prerogative of nature in a man more than he dreaded the approach of disease.

What Odilo gave the Church was a calendar that confessed what every grieving parishioner already half-believed: that the dead are not lost to us, and the prayers of the living still carry. Today, wherever a son or daughter kneels for the parents they have buried, may the words still rise: _Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord, and let perpetual light shine upon them._

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I prefer to be mercifully judged for mercy than cruelly condemned for cruelty."_ — Odilo]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "2", name: "Adelard of Corbie"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 2]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Adelard of Corbie]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbot, cousin of Charlemagne, c. 752–826]

#v(0.5em)

He was a grandson of Charles Martel and a cousin of Charlemagne, raised among the tutors of the imperial palace and groomed for service in the kingdom. When Charlemagne, having sworn before God to marry the Lombard princess Desiderata, repudiated her in favor of a second wife while she still lived, Adelard — a cadet of the court, perhaps eighteen — refused to render any of the customary honors to the new queen. He could not stop the marriage. He could leave it. At twenty he walked out of the palace and presented himself at the gate of the monastery of Corbie.

His abbot, knowing him to be the emperor's cousin, sent him first to the kitchen garden. Adelard hoed the rows with relief — the only place in the kingdom, his biographer wrote, where he could be both a king's grandson and a nobody. After some years his family began visiting too often. He slipped out one night and walked to Monte Cassino. The emperor's messengers caught him there within months and brought him back; the brothers elected him abbot. He governed for forty years and, with his namesake, founded a daughter house at Corvey in Saxony, which would become a school of missionaries to the still-pagan north.

He wept easily. The chronicler reports he lamented his own sins and the sins of his community at least twice a day, every day. He spoke of God in such a way that some called him another Anthony. He died at seventy-five, having outlived the emperor he had broken with, and was mourned by his disciple Paschasius Radbertus in a Latin elegy from which the medieval Church learned how to grieve.

What Adelard left was not a treatise or a battle. It was the quiet record of a man who, when the kingdom asked him to bless what God had forbidden, said no, and lived another fifty years to prove the answer was the right one.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Day of your death — would that it had never dawned for me, for none more evil could have come, nor any more cruel have shone for all your people."_ — Paschasius Radbertus, at Adelard's tomb]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "3", name: "Genevieve of Paris"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 3]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Genevieve of Paris]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Virgin, protectress of the city, c. 422–509]

#v(0.5em)

When Germanus of Auxerre passed through the village of Nanterre on his way to Britain, a crowd poured out to meet him — mothers with children, old men seeking a blessing, the curious and the devout. In the press of the crowd the bishop's eye fell on a girl perhaps eight or nine years old. He asked to be brought to her. Kissing her head, he said to her parents, "Happy are you. At this child's birth the angels rejoiced. She will be great before the Lord."

He then asked the girl whether she would consecrate her body to Christ as his bride. She answered, "Blessed are you, Father, for asking what I already desire." Germanus gave her a bronze coin, found on the ground, stamped with a cross. "Wear this from your neck," he told her, "and do not burden yourself with gold or pearl. For if the smallest charm of this world overcomes your heart, you will lack the ornaments that do not perish."

Genevieve kept the promise for eighty years. When the Huns swept toward Paris in 451, the city prepared to flee; she told them to stay, to fast, to pray, and Attila turned aside. When Clovis later besieged the same walls, she slipped past his lines and returned with grain for the starving. She was neither general nor queen, only a consecrated girl grown into an old woman whose word the city had learned to trust.

What Germanus saw in the child at Nanterre was not greatness but willingness. The holiness came later, slowly, in the keeping of small promises. Today, wherever Christ asks the question he asked through Germanus, may the answer still be: _Your handmaid hears; command what you will._

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Act manfully, and what you believe in your heart and profess with your mouth, strive to fulfill in your works."_ — Germanus to Genevieve]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "4", name: "Pharaildis of Ghent"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 4]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Pharaildis of Ghent]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Virgin, widow, royal Frankish, c. 650–740]

#v(0.5em)

She was born to King Theuderic of Lotharingia and his queen Amelberga, raised from the font by her kinswoman Gertrude, and instructed in letters at a court that expected princesses to marry where the king pointed. Pharaildis had pointed elsewhere. From her mother's arms, the chronicler insists, she had given herself entire to Christ. When her father pressed her toward a marriage of his own arranging — to a nobleman named Guido — she could not refuse him in the open, but on the wedding night she knelt and prayed.

The prayer she made, the biographer preserves in four lines of awkward verse: that the same God who made the fabric of the age would grant his handmaid to keep her promise. The grace held. For thirty years she continued to share Guido's house, slipping out at midnight or cockcrow to the monastery for vigils and returning before dawn. He thought it an affair, and beat her for years. She did not leave; she did not strike back; she asked God to put an end to him. Not long after, riding hard to a hunt, he was thrown from his horse, broke himself badly, and died.

Pharaildis took the widow's veil and lived almost ninety years. The most-remembered story from her widowhood is small. In a winter field she gathered some wild geese with her staff as if they were sheep, drove them home, and forbade her servants to harm them. One she found missing — its bones and feathers in a cooking-pot — and she put them back together and sent it on its way. Her sisters Gudula and Reineldis and her brother Emebertus would all become saints in the same Belgian church.

She is painted, where her cult survived the Calvinist destruction of her shrine at Ghent, with a goose at her feet. The small miracle is what the peasants remembered. The long endurance, less easy to paint, was what carried her there.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Supreme Father, King, Light, and Source of days, by whose command the fabric of this age stands: grant to your grateful handmaid to enjoy virginity."_ — Pharaildis, on her wedding night]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "5", name: "Edward the Confessor"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 5]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Edward the Confessor]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[King of England, c. 1003–1066]

#v(0.5em)

He was sixty when the kingdom finally came to him. His father Ethelred the Unready had died fleeing the Danes; his half-brother Alfred had been blinded and killed in the same flight; his mother Emma had spent his childhood remarrying his father's enemies. Edward had grown up in Normandy, in exile, and had said more than once that he would rather live a private life forever than recover the kingdom through the slaughter of men.

When the Danish line at last failed, the slaughter was not required. He was crowned, took to the throne with mild reluctance, and for twenty-four years ruled an England that William of Malmesbury could not stop calling — to his own faint astonishment — _quiet_. The civil wars stopped. The Danes stopped. The laws he restored were his grandfather Edgar's, recalled from sixty-eight years of disuse and reissued under his own name, with one provision freshly added: any man who lent at usury was to forfeit his goods and live as an exile. The English remembered the Laws of St. Edward longer than they remembered Edward himself.

He kept his marriage to Editha chaste, by mutual agreement; he founded the monastery at Westminster, where he would be buried and where his bones became the abbey's most-visited relic; he forgave easily; he gave widely. The chronicler delights in one small story: hunting near Windsor, a peasant once accidentally scared the deer off, and the king, roused, cried — _By God and His Mother, I will do you as much harm if I can!_ — and that, says the biographer, was the worst threat Edward could imagine. _A noble spirit_, he writes, _which did not remember that it was king._

The peace of his reign was a benediction the next century would not see. It rested, his eulogists thought, on the simple fact that the man who held the sword had never quite wanted to use it.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I would rather live a private life forever than recover the kingdom through the slaughter of men."_ — Edward, in exile]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "6", name: "The Epiphany"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 6]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[The Epiphany]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[The manifestation of Christ to the Gentiles]

#v(0.5em)

They came from beyond the Roman world, scholars of stars whose names the gospel does not bother to record. Tradition would later call them Caspar and Melchior and Balthasar and number them at three; the gospel only says _wise men from the East_, and _they brought gifts_. They had read a sign in the night sky and walked, by the only road they knew how to take, in the direction it pointed. The road led them to Jerusalem. They assumed, reasonably, that a new king would be born in a palace, and so they went to a palace and asked.

Herod heard them out and called for his priests, who knew the prophets. _Bethlehem_, the priests said — almost without thinking. Herod sent the foreigners south with a courteous lie about wanting to honor the child himself. The wise men went on. The star, which they had not seen since Jerusalem, returned to them on the road, and led them to a house that was no palace at all. They had walked for months. They knelt before a toddler.

They opened their treasures and laid out gold for a king, frankincense for a priest, and myrrh for the burial of a man — not yet knowing why. A warning came that night in a dream: do not return to Herod. They left Bethlehem by a different road and disappeared from the gospel forever.

The Epiphany is the showing-forth of Christ. The early Church understood it just as much as the showing-forth of _who he came for_. The first to find him after the shepherds were neither rabbis nor soldiers nor priests, but foreigners following a sign no Jew had been given. May our roads, however oddly they turn, still bring us to the same house.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"When they saw the star, they rejoiced with exceeding great joy."_ — Matthew 2:10]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "7", name: "Lucian of Antioch"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 7]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Lucian of Antioch]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Priest, biblical scholar, martyr, c. 240–312]

#v(0.5em)

He lived between two empires. He was born when Decius's persecution was already a memory and Diocletian's was a generation away, in Antioch, where Christianity had names for itself in three languages. Trained in rhetoric and Scripture both, he settled into the patient work of a textual editor: collating, comparing, correcting the manuscripts of the Septuagint and the Greek New Testament that the Church of the East was already using. The recension he produced, the so-called Lucianic text, was read aloud in the liturgies of Antioch and Constantinople for a thousand years. Jerome would later draw on it for the Vulgate.

He taught as well as edited. Among his students were Arius — whose career he did not live to witness — and Eusebius of Nicomedia. The school he led was called _Antiochene_, in distinction from the allegorical school at Alexandria: where the Alexandrians read every line of Scripture as figure, the Antiochenes read it first as history, and meaning came up from the ground. The instinct survives in modern biblical scholarship.

When Diocletian's persecution reached Antioch, Lucian fled but was arrested at Nicomedia. The emperor's officers tried first to convert him by flattery, then by threats, then by hunger — they offered him food that had been sacrificed to idols, and he refused it. He had been a priest before he was a prisoner. According to one account, on the feast of Epiphany itself, lying chained on his back in the cell, he celebrated the Eucharist on his own chest with the bread his fellow prisoners had smuggled in. The next day he was killed. The interrogator's final question received the only answer he ever gave: _I am a Christian._

The work he had done with his pen and the death he died with his body were, in him, the same testimony — that the word made flesh could be kept in a manuscript and in a martyr alike.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I am a Christian."_ — Lucian, in answer to every charge]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "8", name: "Severinus of Noricum"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 8]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Severinus of Noricum]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Apostle of Noricum, c. 410–482]

#v(0.5em)

He appeared on the Danube frontier without papers, in the years when the Western Empire was visibly dying. The Roman towns along the river — Favianae, Boiotro, Quintanis — were being abandoned a few at a time as Germanic raiders crossed the ice. No one knew where the strange ascetic had come from; when asked his country, he would say only, _I have come for the sake of the Lord_. His biographer, Eugippius, who would write his Life from the testimony of those who knew him, never learned the answer.

He gathered the panicked Roman remnants into ordered communities. He founded monasteries — at Favianae and at Mauternbach — where the displaced could pray, work, and be fed. He negotiated with the barbarian kings: Odoacer himself, before he sacked Italy, came to ask the holy man's blessing and was given a prophecy that he would rule sixteen years. He warned the towns in advance of every raid; the chronicles record that he was never wrong. He set up grain distributions during famine. When the Romans of Quintanis would not flee, though he had told them they must, he gathered them at his cell, said the office, lifted his eyes to the sky, and wept; that night the Rugii came over the wall.

He had stripped himself of estate, rank, and even origin, the chronicler says, in order to belong only to the kingdom whose ruler does not die. When the empire he had come to serve finally fell, four years after his own death, the monks carried his body to Italy, knowing that nothing could be safe on the Danube anymore.

He spent thirty years comforting the dying world. What he founded, on the frontier where everything else was failing, was a way of being among the displaced without despairing.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I have come for the sake of the Lord."_ — Severinus, when asked his country]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "9", name: "Adrian of Canterbury"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 9]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Adrian of Canterbury]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbot, African scholar in England, c. 635–710]

#v(0.5em)

He was African by birth — Libyan, the Bollandists guess — abbot of a small monastery near Naples and, in 667, the man Pope Vitalian first chose to send to Britain. The archbishopric of Canterbury had been vacant for three years; the new English Church needed a head. Adrian declined the office. He recommended instead a Greek monk named Theodore, then sixty-six years old, who was living in Rome. The pope agreed, on condition that Adrian travel with Theodore as his counselor.

They sailed in 668. Theodore was made archbishop; Adrian became abbot of the monastery of SS Peter and Paul at Canterbury, where Augustine of Canterbury was buried. The two foreigners — one Greek, one African, neither of whom had a word of English between them when they landed — set about the slow rebuilding of an Anglo-Saxon Church that had lost its bearings.

They opened a school. Students walked to Canterbury from every kingdom of the heptarchy. They were taught Latin, Greek, the Scriptures, computation, music, and — Bede notes with quiet pride — verse. Aldhelm of Sherborne was Adrian's pupil; through Aldhelm came Bede's generation, and through them the literacy of the entire Western Middle Ages. Bede, writing forty years later, says that some of Adrian's pupils were still alive in his own day and could converse in Greek and Latin as easily as in their mother tongue.

He outlived Theodore by twenty years and continued to teach until he could no longer rise from his bed. He had not wanted the journey; the kingdom of God had asked it of him anyway, and the kingdom had been the better for it.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Never had there been such happy times in Britain since the English came to Britain."_ — Bede, on the school of Theodore and Adrian]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "10", name: "William of Bourges"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 10]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[William of Bourges]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Archbishop, Cistercian, c. 1155–1209]

#v(0.5em)

He was a younger son of the noble house of Nevers, given an honorary canonry while still a boy and a real one in his early twenties; he might have lived out the unremarkable life of a regular canon at Soissons and never been heard of. The death of his elder brother brought the inheritance toward him; he refused it. He left Soissons, walked across France, and presented himself at the gates of the Cistercian abbey of Pontigny. Twenty years later, when the see of Bourges fell vacant, he was abbot of Châalis. The chapter of Bourges elected him archbishop. He went into hiding. The pope wrote ordering him to accept. He accepted.

He kept the Cistercian habit under his pallium and ate at the monastic table when he could. He preached against the Albigensian errors spreading south of him without rancor, by patient instruction; he was reluctant to bless the armed crusade that Innocent III was beginning to prepare against them, preferring conversion to conquest. He arbitrated quarrels between Philip II of France and the pope, and was respected by both for the visible absence of his own interest in the outcome.

He died nine years after his consecration, worn out, on the night of January 10. The chronicle says he had spent the day hearing confessions and refused to break his fast until vespers. The community at Bourges buried him in the cathedral; pilgrims began coming almost at once, and Honorius III canonized him within a decade — a speed of recognition that had no parallel in his century.

What William offered his diocese was the witness of an office held lightly, in the constant awareness that it was on loan.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Lord, you have given me this charge against my will; let me at least bear it as you would have me bear it."_ — William, after his consecration]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "11", name: "Theodosius the Cenobiarch"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 11]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Theodosius the Cenobiarch]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbot, father of Palestinian cenobitism, 423–529]

#v(0.5em)

He was Cappadocian by birth, a near-contemporary of Sabbas the Sanctified, and he came to the Judean desert when he was about thirty, planning to live as a hermit. The hermits' caves along the road from Jerusalem to Bethlehem were filling up; men came to him for counsel and would not leave. He moved deeper into the wilderness, and they followed. At last, persuaded that solitude alone was not his calling, he built a great communal monastery on a hill east of Bethlehem and made the gathering official.

What he founded was unlike any monastery the East had yet seen. He laid out four churches in the same compound — one for each of the languages his monks spoke: Greek, Armenian, Bessian, and Arabic — and the offices were prayed in all four. He built a hospital for the sick. He built a hospice for the old. He set apart a separate quarter for the mentally ill and another for the homeless poor. By the end of his life four hundred men lived under his rule, and the patriarchs of Jerusalem confirmed him as _cenobiarch_, the supervisor of all communal monasteries in Palestine.

In his old age the emperor Anastasius sent him a substantial bribe to support the Monophysite party against Chalcedon. Theodosius gave the money to the poor and wrote back that he and his monks would not betray the faith of the apostles for any throne. He was briefly exiled. He returned. He lived to one hundred and five.

He had taken a desert that had been the refuge of solitaries and turned it into a country of hospitable houses. What he taught his sons was that the silence of God is best kept not alone but in company.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"We will defend the faith of the apostles."_ — Theodosius, in answer to the emperor]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "12", name: "Benedict Biscop"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 12]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Benedict Biscop]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Founder of Wearmouth-Jarrow, c. 628–690]

#v(0.5em)

He was twenty-five and a thegn at the court of King Oswiu of Northumbria when he set down his sword and walked south. He had no plans yet; he wanted Rome. It would be the first of six journeys he made over the next thirty-five years to Italy and back, on foot and by sea, in an age when most Anglo-Saxons died within walking distance of where they were baptized.

On the second trip he stopped at Lérins, off the Provençal coast, and stayed for two years as a monk. On the third, Pope Vitalian sent him to England with Theodore of Tarsus and Adrian of Canterbury. After two years at Canterbury he asked permission to go home north; King Ecgfrith of Northumbria granted him land at the mouth of the Wear, and in 674 he built a monastery there. Eight years later he built another at Jarrow. The two houses — Wearmouth and Jarrow — operated as one community on two sites, and would remain so for the rest of the Anglo-Saxon age.

He could not stop traveling. He went back to Rome four more times, each time returning with what no one else thought to bring: books for the library, paintings to teach the unlettered, John the Archcantor of Rome to teach plainsong, masons from Gaul to build in stone (the first stone church north of the Tees), glaziers to make windows. The library he assembled was the finest north of the Alps; without it Bede, a boy he had received into the community at seven, would not have become Bede.

His last illness ran three years. The brothers chanted the psalter around his bed in shifts so that the office would never stop. He died at the Gloria of one of the Psalms.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I have brought you books, that you may forever have the means of learning."_ — Biscop to his community, before his final journey to Rome]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "13", name: "Hilary of Poitiers"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 13]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Hilary of Poitiers]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, Doctor of the Church, c. 315–368]

#v(0.5em)

He was raised pagan in Aquitaine, married, fathered a daughter, and converted in his mid-thirties through the study of Scripture. The Church in Gaul made him a priest at once, and then — by acclamation, against his preference — bishop of Poitiers in 350. He had been a Christian fewer than five years.

What he found in his diocese was a Church drifting toward Arianism by way of imperial pressure. The Emperor Constantius II, an Arian sympathizer, was assembling councils across the West to depose the bishops who refused to repudiate Athanasius. Hilary refused. He drafted an open letter to the emperor protesting the persecution of orthodoxy. The emperor exiled him to Phrygia, a region he could not have located on a map. He went.

Exile became his great work. He learned Greek in Phrygia and read the Greek Fathers; he wrote the twelve books _On the Trinity_, the first sustained Latin treatment of the doctrine; he wrote commentaries on the Psalms and on Matthew. He attended an Arian synod at Seleucia and made himself such a nuisance to the eastern bishops by his persistent orthodoxy that the emperor sent him home four years early. He returned to Gaul to ovations, and his second decade as bishop saw the Arian collapse in the West that the next generation would credit largely to him. They would call him the Athanasius of the West.

He took in a young soldier named Martin who had asked to be released from his military oath; the discipling of Martin of Tours, and through Martin of half of Gaul, began in Hilary's house. He died at home in Poitiers, fifty-three years old, having spent half his Christian life in defense of a sentence: _the Son is what the Father is._

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"The best service we can render is to give back to him our small understanding of his greatness."_ — Hilary, _De Trinitate_ I.2]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "14", name: "Felix of Nola"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 14]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Felix of Nola]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Priest, confessor, d. c. 260]

#v(0.5em)

Almost everything we know about him comes from a man who lived three hundred years after he died. Paulinus of Nola, bishop, poet, friend of Augustine, settled in Felix's town in his mid-forties and made it his life's work to tend the tomb of the obscure priest whose feast still drew pilgrims to that hillside in Campania. Every January 14 for fifteen years Paulinus composed a _natalicium_, a birthday poem, and read it aloud at the grave. The poems are what we have.

What Paulinus found in the local memory was this. Felix was the son of a Syrian-born minor official; he gave his patrimony to the poor on receiving it. He served as a priest in Nola under the elderly bishop Maximus. When the Decian persecution began the officers came for the bishop first; Maximus, frail, fled to the hills and was found by Felix half-dead from exposure. Felix carried him home. The officers came for Felix next. They scourged him, loaded him with chains, and threw him into a prison floored with broken pottery. He was freed — the chroniclers say by an angel, Paulinus more modestly says by an unlocked door — and went on hiding. After Decius died and the persecution lifted, Felix could have been bishop in his turn. He refused. He told the diocese that an older priest deserved the chair, and he himself lived another forty years tilling a small plot of land outside town, distributing whatever crops he raised to the poor.

The pagans of Nola, who had known him, never forgot. The Christians who knelt at his tomb in Paulinus's day were sometimes children of grandchildren of the men who had imprisoned him.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"He was crowned in life, since he had so often been ready to die for Christ."_ — Paulinus of Nola, _Natalicium I_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "15", name: "Maurus"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 15]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Maurus]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Disciple of Benedict, sixth century]

#v(0.5em)

He was twelve years old when his father, a Roman senator named Equitius, brought him up the road to Subiaco and placed him in the care of Benedict. Another boy, slightly younger, named Placid, arrived a few weeks later. The three names — Benedict, Maurus, Placid — would mark the beginning of the Benedictine movement in the West.

Gregory the Great, writing his _Dialogues_ fifty years afterward, preserved one story about Maurus. The boys were drawing water from the lake below the monastery. Placid lost his footing, fell in, and was swept toward the deep water. Benedict, at his cell some distance off, saw it in spirit and called: _Brother Maurus, run, run — the boy is drowning._ Maurus, who had been about to scoop a bucket himself, took Benedict's blessing as a command and ran. He reached the shore, stepped onto the water, and walked across it to where Placid was sinking. He caught the boy by the hair and walked back, the chronicler says, as if on a paved road, and only when he had set Placid down did he notice what had happened.

Benedict and Maurus afterward argued — gently — about whose merit had carried the miracle: Benedict insisting it was Maurus's obedience, Maurus insisting it was Benedict's command. Placid, whom no one consulted, settled the matter by saying he had seen Benedict's cloak above his head in the water and had thought it was Benedict pulling him out.

It is the only Maurus story that the early tradition vouches for. Whatever else he did with his life he did under his abbot's shadow, content there. The story keeps because of what it shows: obedience moves across water by not stopping to ask whether water can be moved across.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Whose merit did this?"_ — Benedict to Maurus, the disagreement they could not resolve]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "16", name: "Marcellus I, Pope"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 16]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Marcellus I, Pope]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop of Rome, c. 255–309]

#v(0.5em)

He was elected to the see of Rome in the year 308, five years after the end of the Diocletian persecution. The Roman Church he inherited was not so much divided as winded. Christians who had handed over the Scriptures under torture wished to be received back; Christians who had not been broken did not wish to receive them. The previous pope, Marcellinus, had himself faltered under interrogation and died under a cloud; the see had been vacant for several years. Marcellus took up the office knowing he would be hated by half the city before his first year was out.

He set up a disciplinary system for the _lapsi_ — the lapsed. The penitents would do public penance for a fixed period, take their place at the back of the assembly, and only after years be readmitted to communion. The unbroken Christians thought it too lenient; the lapsed thought it cruel. Both factions rioted. Marcellus, in the same months, was also organizing Rome's first twenty-five _tituli_, the parish churches that would still be the bones of the city's Christianity in our day.

When the riots threatened public order, the emperor Maxentius — no friend of Christianity, but a friend of order — exiled Marcellus from the city. Tradition says he was forced to work the imperial stables on the road south, and died there. Damasus, half a century later, would write his epitaph: _true shepherd, because he proclaimed to the lapsed that they must weep for their crimes, he was reckoned a bitter enemy by all the unhappy ones._

His was a short pontificate. What he gave the Church was not a great gift but a difficult one: the conviction that mercy can be ordered, and that ordering it costs.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"True shepherd, because he proclaimed to the lapsed that they must weep for their crimes, he was reckoned a bitter enemy by all the unhappy ones."_ — Damasus, on Marcellus]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "17", name: "Anthony the Great"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 17]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Anthony the Great]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbot, father of monks, c. 251–356]

#v(0.5em)

He was eighteen, newly orphaned, when he walked into church and heard the Gospel read: _If you would be perfect, go, sell what you have and give to the poor._ He walked out and did it. The land his parents had left him — three hundred fertile acres along the Nile — he gave to his neighbors. The household goods he sold. The proceeds he distributed, keeping only a small sum for his younger sister. A week later he heard another verse: _Take no thought for tomorrow._ He gave that sum away too, placed his sister in the care of consecrated virgins, and went out to the edge of his village to pray.

The desert, for Anthony, was not yet a place. It was an intention. He learned prayer from one old hermit, fasting from another, vigilance from a third, gathering virtues like a bee, as his biographer says, from every flower. Only when he had learned them did he walk beyond the inhabited world, cross the Nile, and shut himself in an abandoned fortress. For twenty years he spoke to no one. Bread and water were passed through a small window twice a year.

When at last he came out, people expected a wasted man. Instead, the biographer writes, "his body kept its former state, neither grown fat from idleness nor wasted from fasting and wrestling with demons; but such as they had known him before his retirement." He had been fighting, and he had been strengthened. To the friends who crowded around him he said only this: _I saw the snares of the enemy spread over the whole earth, and I groaned and said, Who can escape them? And I heard a voice answering: Humility._

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I was here; but I was waiting to see your contest."_ — the voice Anthony heard in the dark]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "18", name: "Deicolus of Lure"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 18]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Deicolus of Lure]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Irish abbot in Burgundy, c. 530–625]

#v(0.5em)

He was one of the twelve monks who left Bangor with Columbanus around the year 590 to bring monastic life into the Frankish kingdoms. Columbanus was about fifty then; Deicolus was somewhere between thirty and forty. For twenty years the band walked, preached, founded houses, and confronted kings. They settled longest at Luxeuil in the Vosges. When Columbanus rebuked the Burgundian king Theuderic II for keeping a series of concubines and refusing to take a wife, Theuderic's grandmother Brunhilda — who liked the arrangement — had the Irish driven out of the kingdom.

Deicolus could not keep up. He was old; the march to the coast was a hundred miles and more; he collapsed in the forest near Lure in eastern Burgundy. The story preserved by the locals is that he fell asleep against a tree and a wild boar lay down at his feet to warm him. A swineherd found him, took him in, and a wealthy Frankish lord named Werfhar gave him a small clearing in the forest. He built a hut. Other monks who had been scattered came back to him; he built a chapel; the chapel became a monastery; the monastery, Lutra — modern Lure — would last twelve hundred years.

The chroniclers remember him for his unfailing cheerfulness. Columbanus had asked him once, in the Vosges, why he was always smiling. Deicolus said: _because no one can take Christ from me_. It was the only formal sermon of his that has come down.

He lived to nearly a hundred. The Burgundian peasants who watched him grow old took the example, and stopped being surprised that an Irish exile abandoned in a forest had built them a house of God.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"No one can take Christ from me."_ — Deicolus, asked why he smiled]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "19", name: "Wulfstan of Worcester"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 19]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Wulfstan of Worcester]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, Benedictine, 1008–1095]

#v(0.5em)

He was the only Anglo-Saxon bishop who kept his see after the Norman Conquest, and he kept it by being so transparently saintly that Lanfranc gave up trying to depose him. The Normans had a procedure for old English bishops: gentle pressure, a language test, an accusation of insufficient learning, replacement. Wulfstan, when Lanfranc summoned him to surrender his pastoral staff at Edward the Confessor's tomb, set the staff down on the tomb and asked Edward to keep it for him. The story — most likely embellished, certainly believed — was that no one but Wulfstan could lift it again.

What was true beneath the legend is that no one else wanted his job once they had seen him at it. He preached in English when the Latin was lost on his hearers. He visited every parish in his diocese on horseback every year, sleeping in the parsonages and eating with the parish priests. He fasted hard, prayed long, slept little, and rarely sat down. With the help of his old friend Lanfranc — who came around — he led the campaign that ended the slave trade out of the port of Bristol, where English boys and girls had been sold to Ireland for two generations. He preached against it in Bristol itself, on the docks, for weeks, until the merchants relented.

He served as bishop of Worcester for thirty-two years. He rebuilt the cathedral. He buried his Anglo-Saxon people one by one and learned to bury his Norman ones too. He died in his eighties on January 19, 1095, having outlived nearly everyone who had ever known him as anything but the bishop.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Why are you afraid? Christ is still on his throne."_ — Wulfstan to a Norman noble, on hearing of a rebellion]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "20", name: "Fabian, Pope"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 20]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Fabian, Pope]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop of Rome, martyr, c. 200–250]

#v(0.5em)

He had come into the city from a country estate on the day the clergy of Rome were gathered to elect a new pope. He was a layman. He had nothing to do with the proceedings; he stood in the crowd outside the chamber. Eusebius records what happened next: a dove descended from above the gathering and settled on his head. Everyone present took it as a sign. Fabian was carried inside, ordained, and consecrated bishop of Rome on the same day.

He governed the Roman Church for fourteen years, the longest peaceful pontificate of the third century. He divided the city into seven ecclesiastical regions, one for each deacon, in a structure that has lasted. He sent seven missionary bishops into Gaul — Saturninus to Toulouse, Denis to Paris, Trophimus to Arles, Paul to Narbonne — and the Church there owes its origin to him. He recovered the bodies of his persecuted predecessors Pontian and Hippolytus, brought them back from Sardinia, and buried them with honor in Rome.

When the emperor Decius issued his edict in 250 requiring every citizen of the empire to sacrifice publicly to the Roman gods, Fabian was the first major bishop to be arrested. He refused the sacrifice. He was killed by imprisonment and torture rather than by a single execution, and Cyprian of Carthage, who had heard the news while himself in hiding, wrote: _the Roman Church has been adorned by the glorious death of an incomparable shepherd._

The dove was a story. The reign was real. What it shows is that the Church can be governed for fourteen quiet years by a man whom no one had been looking for, and that he can die in a single afternoon when the time comes.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"The Roman Church has been adorned by the glorious death of an incomparable shepherd."_ — Cyprian of Carthage, on the martyrdom of Fabian]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "21", name: "Agnes"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 21]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Agnes]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman virgin and martyr, c. 291–304]

#v(0.5em)

She was twelve, perhaps thirteen. Her family was Roman, Christian, well-placed enough that the son of the urban prefect had taken notice of her. The young man pressed her father for marriage; Agnes refused. She had given herself, she said, to Christ. The father appealed first to Roman law (a girl of twelve could be made to marry) and then, when she still refused, to the Diocletian edict against Christians. Agnes was brought before the tribunal.

The prefect tried persuasion. He tried his son's tears. He tried the public humiliation of having her stripped and led to a brothel — and the chronicler insists, with the kind of detail that suggests the story was told for a generation by women who had seen it, that her hair grew to cover her. He tried torture. The girl, says Ambrose seventy years later, was so unshaken that the men charged with hurting her could not work; their hands trembled and they wept. At last she was led to the place of execution. The pagan crowd, the Christian crowd, the soldiers, the prefect, his son, were all there.

She was killed by a single stroke of the sword. The Christian community of Rome buried her on the Via Nomentana the same day. By the time Constantine's daughter Constantina was building the basilica over her grave, twenty-five years later, her cult was already the most beloved in the city. Ambrose preached about her; Damasus inscribed her epitaph; Prudentius wrote her hymn.

The fact that she was twelve has never quite stopped being scandalous. It was not meant to. The Roman Church kept her at twelve because what she did at twelve we are not always brave enough to do at fifty.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"At an age when little girls cannot bear their parents' frowns, this child triumphed over the cruellest tyrants."_ — Ambrose, _De Virginibus_ I]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "22", name: "Vincent of Saragossa"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 22]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Vincent of Saragossa]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Deacon and martyr, d. 304]

#v(0.5em)

He was the deacon of an old man. Valerius, bishop of Saragossa, had grown deaf, his speech slow, his memory unreliable; for the last years of his episcopate Vincent had been doing the work of the diocese while the old bishop signed the documents. When the governor Dacian, on Diocletian's orders, summoned both of them to Valencia, Valerius could not have answered the charges if his life had depended on it. Vincent answered for him.

The deacon's confession at the tribunal made the prosecutor furious. Dacian, the Acts say, had Valerius sent into exile — he was too old to make a useful martyr — and turned the full apparatus on Vincent. The torments were the standard catalogue of late Roman judicial savagery: rack, iron hooks, fire, the gridiron, salt rubbed into open wounds, broken potsherds for a prison floor. Vincent endured them in silence until the moment of release; he is reported to have said, at the very end, that he now exalted in the wounds he bore for Christ. He died in his cell, exhausted, before another hearing.

His Acts were already being read aloud in Spanish basilicas by the time Augustine was a young priest, three generations later. Augustine preached at least three sermons on him; Prudentius wrote his great Spanish hymn for him; the Roman Canon names him along with Lawrence and Stephen. Within a hundred years his name had reached Gaul, Italy, North Africa, and Egypt — a deacon of an old man, executed in a corner of the empire on a day no one had wanted to remember.

He had been asked to speak for someone too tired to defend himself. He did. The rest followed.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I now exalt in the wounds I bear for Christ."_ — Vincent, after his last torture]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "23", name: "John the Almsgiver"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 23]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[John the Almsgiver]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Patriarch of Alexandria, c. 552–616]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Cypriot, a widower, a layman, and over sixty when the emperor Heraclius asked him to take Alexandria. The Persians had just begun their march through Syria; Antioch had fallen; Jerusalem would fall within a few years; the Christian refugees pouring south into Egypt had no one to receive them. John accepted on one condition: he would call the poor his masters.

He kept books. He kept them very precisely. He had drawn up a list of all the destitute in Alexandria at the start of his patriarchate — _seven and a half thousand of my masters and brothers_, he said — and he marked it daily as families received bread and clothes from the patriarchal treasury. When the bishops of his synod objected that a patriarch should not be a clerk to beggars, John told them that the books in question were the books God would consult.

When Jerusalem fell to the Persians in 614, John sent shiploads of food and money to the besieged Christians and welcomed the refugees who arrived in his city by the thousands. When the church treasury ran dry, he melted down the plate. When a beggar tricked him three days running by appearing in different disguises and receiving alms each time, his stewards refused to give a fourth time. John called them back. _Give to him_, he said, _until we have tested whether he is Christ._

He died at home in Cyprus, having returned there during a Persian raid on Alexandria, and was buried with the inscription he had asked for in advance: that he had given everything that was given him, and that this had been the only joy of his life.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I will not be reconciled to God until I have made up to my brethren what I have failed to give them."_ — John the Almsgiver]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "24", name: "Babylas of Antioch"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 24]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Babylas of Antioch]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop and martyr, d. 253]

#v(0.5em)

He was bishop of Antioch in the years when the empire briefly had a Christian — or near-Christian — emperor. Philip the Arab, who reigned from 244 to 249, was at minimum sympathetic; some early sources call him outright Christian. In either case Philip arrived at the door of Babylas's church one Easter night, with his retinue, expecting to enter for the vigil.

Babylas met him at the threshold and would not let him in. The emperor, it was widely known, had had his predecessor Gordian III killed to take the throne. Babylas explained, courteously, that an unconfessed killer could not be admitted to the Eucharist. The emperor — to his everlasting credit — submitted: he took his place among the public penitents at the back, did the penance Babylas imposed, and was readmitted in due course. It is the first recorded instance in Christian history of a bishop refusing communion to an emperor. Ambrose would do the same to Theodosius a hundred and forty years later, and would cite Babylas.

Babylas's own end came under Decius, five or six years after the encounter with Philip. He was arrested, refused to sacrifice, and was either killed or died in chains; the chronicles disagree. What no one disagreed about was where to put him: his tomb was placed in the suburb of Daphne, near the famous pagan oracle of Apollo, and the people of Antioch began going to the bishop's bones instead of the god's voice. When the emperor Julian came to consult the oracle a century later, Apollo would not speak. Julian had the bones removed. Apollo still would not speak.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"You shall not enter."_ — Babylas to Philip the Arab, Easter night]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "25", name: "Henry Suso"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 25]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Henry Suso]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Dominican mystic, c. 1295–1366]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Swabian, born near Constance, and entered the Dominicans at thirteen. He took his mother's family name, Suso, rather than his father's — he preferred her — and studied at Cologne under Meister Eckhart in the years just before Eckhart was condemned. The condemnation broke him. For the next decade he punished his own body brutally: he wore a hairshirt with embedded iron nails, carved the name of Jesus into his chest with a stylus, slept on broken glass. He thought self-mortification was the proof of love.

Around forty, the chronicles say, he had a kind of second conversion. He was told in prayer that what was required of him was no longer his own pain but the patient bearing of other people's. He took off the hairshirt. He stopped the disciplines. He spent the next twenty-five years as confessor, spiritual director, and preacher to circles of laypeople and to communities of nuns, particularly the Dominican women at Töss, for whom Elsbeth Stagel was his closest confidante. The wounds came from other quarters: he was falsely accused of fathering a child by one of his penitents, hauled before his order, suspended, and eventually cleared. He bore the accusation without defense; he refused to identify the woman who had lied.

He wrote the _Little Book of Eternal Wisdom_ in those years, partly autobiography and partly prayer, addressed to Christ as the bridegroom of his soul. The book taught Europe how to address Christ in the second person without bashfulness. Tauler read him; Thomas à Kempis read him; the _Imitation of Christ_ breathes his air.

He died at Ulm at seventy-one, slowly, in long silence.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Lord, you taught me first by your scourges, and then by your patience: the second is the harder."_ — Suso, late in life]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "26", name: "Paula"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 26]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Paula]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman matron, foundress at Bethlehem, 347–404]

#v(0.5em)

She was one of the great families of Rome — Scipios, Aemilii Pauli; her bloodlines went back to the Republic — and she married into another, the Toxotii. She bore five children. She was thirty-two when her husband died. The next two years were the wreckage of an aristocratic widow's grief: her circle in Rome included the most stylish Christian matrons in the city, but none of them could console her, and she half-broke for those two years.

Jerome arrived in Rome in 382 as secretary to Pope Damasus, with a working knowledge of Hebrew and an irritating habit of speaking the truth to senators. Paula met him at the house of her relative Marcella and never looked away. When Jerome's three years in Rome ended — controversially — she sold the great estates, packed her younger daughter Eustochium, and followed him east. By 386 they were both in Bethlehem. She had founded two monasteries — one for women, one for men — with her own money. She learned Hebrew well enough to read the Psalter in it. She paid the copyists who produced the manuscripts on which Jerome built the Vulgate. She corrected him when his Latin lapsed.

She died at fifty-six. Jerome, whose grief at her grave was, by his own account, dangerous to his faith, wrote her epitaph. He also wrote a long letter to Eustochium — Epistle 108 — in which he tried to record the life he had known. The letter is one of the great pieces of late ancient prose. It exists because she gave him forty years of the Latin Bible, and he could think of nothing else to give her in return.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"All her glory was within."_ — Jerome, _Letter 108_ on Paula]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "27", name: "John Chrysostom"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 27]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[John Chrysostom]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Patriarch, preacher, doctor, c. 349–407]

#v(0.5em)

He was born in Antioch and lived more than half his life there as a deacon, priest, and preacher. The Antiochenes packed the great church called the Golden Octagon to hear him; they took down his homilies in shorthand; they nicknamed him _chrysostomos_, golden-mouth, by his fortieth year. He preached through entire books of the Bible, verse by verse. He preached on poverty in the way a man preaches who has slept in poverty. His sermons on Matthew, John, the Acts, the Pauline letters, are what they are because he refused to leave the text behind.

In 398, against his preference, he was carried off to Constantinople and consecrated patriarch. The new see was too political for him. He stopped the banquets at the patriarchal palace, sold the silver to feed the poor, dressed plainly, and made the imperial court uncomfortable. The Empress Eudoxia, in particular, did not forgive being preached at: when she erected a silver statue of herself near the Hagia Sophia and the dedication ceremonies bled into the liturgy, John denounced her from the pulpit using language Eudoxia took for Herodias. He was exiled. The people rioted. He was recalled. He preached the same way. He was exiled again, and this time the long march into Pontus killed him. He died at Comana in September 407, saying as he died: _Glory to God for all things._

Today's feast keeps the day, thirty-one years later, when the emperor Theodosius II — Eudoxia's son — brought the bones home to Constantinople. The boy wept on his mother's behalf in the cathedral she had defiled, and the city received its preacher back.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Glory to God for all things."_ — John Chrysostom, in dying]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "28", name: "Julian Sabas"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 28]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Julian Sabas]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Mesopotamian hermit, fourth century]

#v(0.5em)

He lived in a cave outside Edessa. He ate a small loaf of barley bread once every few days, drank water, prayed, slept little. He had been a disciple of Macarius the Great in his youth, in the Egyptian desert; he had carried that habit east and adapted it to the rocks of Mesopotamia. By the middle of the fourth century he was the most famous holy man of Syria — and one of the very few hermits Theodoret of Cyrrhus took the trouble, much later, to write up at length.

He had two callings, both unwelcome. The first was the care of pilgrims. He could not stop them coming; the wild animals of the country, the chronicler claims, accepted him as a neighbor and would lie at the cave entrance while the visitors approached. The second was the defense of the Nicene faith. The emperor Valens, an Arian, was harassing the orthodox bishops of the East, and Antioch under the Arian patriarch Euzoius had nearly fallen out of communion with the rest of the Church. Word reached Julian. He left his cave, walked to Antioch — some hundred and fifty miles — entered the city in his ragged tunic, and preached in the basilica against the Arian terms. The orthodox party rallied. Valens died at Adrianople a few years later; the East returned to Nicaea; Julian went back to his rocks and was never seen in a city again.

He was, Theodoret says, the only man he ever met who could not be persuaded that he was important.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I am of no use to anyone here; the desert is calling me back."_ — Julian, on the road home from Antioch]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "29", name: "Gildas"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 29]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Gildas]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[British monk and historian, c. 500–570]

#v(0.5em)

He grew up in post-Roman Britain — what the English would later call Wales — at the moment when the country was forgetting it had ever been Roman. The villas had been overgrown. The legions had been gone for a century. The Saxon settlers were past the coast and beginning to push inland, and the British kings who should have been fighting them were chiefly fighting each other. Gildas, monk, took the only weapon he was trained for, which was Latin prose, and wrote _On the Ruin and Conquest of Britain_.

The book is famous and unloved. He names the British kings he holds responsible for the disaster — Constantine the tyrant whelp of unclean Damnonia, Aurelius Caninus, Vortipor the tyrant of the Demetae, Cuneglas the fawn of the rust-stained den, Maelgwn the dragon of the island — and he prints their crimes. He turns then to the clergy and prints theirs. He preaches at the entire surviving British Church for several chapters and refuses to apologize for the volume. He says, with no apparent humor, that he is writing what no one else has been willing to say.

But he had said it, and the book traveled. It crossed to Brittany with him when he founded the monastery at Rhuys, late in life, on the Atlantic. Two centuries later Bede would read it, half believe it, and quote it as the only contemporary witness to a century of British history that left no other written record. The English Reformation read it. Milton read it. It exists because one Welshman, in his last decades, was too angry to keep quiet and too disciplined to lie.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I shall not, I trust, be silent about it any longer."_ — Gildas, _De Excidio Britanniae_ I]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "30", name: "Hyacintha Mariscotti"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 30]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Hyacintha Mariscotti]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Franciscan tertiary, 1585–1640]

#v(0.5em)

She was the second daughter of Marcantonio Mariscotti, Count of Vignanello, and the wrong kind of nun. Her older sister had been chosen first, as the family's ecclesiastical investment, for the Franciscan tertiary convent of San Bernardino in Viterbo; Hyacintha, ten years younger, was sent there to fill the second cell when no one else wanted her. She entered in a fury. She received the habit with the look of a woman accepting a sentence.

For ten years she lived as a noblewoman who happened to take meals in a refectory. She kept a private apartment with rugs and silver, ate meat on days the rule forbade it, ordered in fine dishes from outside the cloister, and received guests in worldly clothes. Her confessor watched the comedy and waited. A severe illness around her thirty-fifth year brought the priest to her bedside not for the sacrament but for a hearing. He told her, in plain terms, what she was. She did not argue.

The remainder of her life — thirty more years — was a public penance. She gave away the apartments, returned to the rule, slept on a board, walked the streets of Viterbo begging for the city's elderly poor, founded two confraternities to take them in, and bore each humiliation from the same nuns who had watched her preen. The town nicknamed her _Suora Sacchitona_, sack-bearer. She died at fifty-five in the small cell she had once disdained, and the children of Viterbo turned out for her funeral.

It is the slow turning that the chronicler keeps for us. She had been a hypocrite first. She became a saint after.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Begin now, while you have time."_ — Hyacintha, in her last illness, to a young nun]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "January", day: "31", name: "Marcella"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[January 31]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Marcella]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman widow, friend of Jerome, c. 325–410]

#v(0.5em)

She had been married seven months when her husband died. Her mother urged a second marriage. Marcella replied — Jerome preserves the line — that if she had wanted a man she would have looked for a husband, not an inheritance. She was perhaps twenty. She did not marry again. She took up plain dress, opened her great house on the Aventine to other widows and unmarried women who wanted to share a disciplined life, and ran from that house what amounted to the first ascetic community for women in Rome.

It was a Roman version of monasticism, and Jerome — who came to Rome two generations later — admired it. He stayed at her house when he was in the city. He taught her Hebrew; she corrected his Latin; she peppered him with questions on points of Scripture that he sometimes found taxing. When Jerome left Rome for Bethlehem and the women around Marcella began to gravitate east, Marcella stayed. Rome, she said, was where she had been put. She received the exiled Athanasius when he passed through, the exiled Peter of Alexandria after him, and a dozen lesser refugees of the Arian and Origenist controversies, in the same upper rooms where she had buried her husband.

She was old when the Goths broke into Rome in August 410. They came up the Aventine and into her house demanding gold. There was none — she had given it all away years before. They beat her to make her confess where she had hidden it. She protected her young companion Principia by drawing the worst of the violence onto herself. She died of the beating within a few days, having lived eighty-five years and never once left the city Augustine was writing about.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I have looked for a husband, not an inheritance."_ — Marcella, refusing a second marriage]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "1", name: "Brigid of Ireland"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 1]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Brigid of Ireland]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbess, patroness of Ireland, c. 451–525]

#v(0.5em)

She was born somewhere in Leinster to a pagan Irish chieftain named Dubthach and a Christian slave woman named Brocca. The pairing was not voluntary on her mother's side. When Dubthach's wife learned of the pregnancy she demanded the slave be sold off, and Brocca was sent away to give birth in another man's house; Brigid, when she was old enough, was brought back to her father's holding as a serving girl. She gave away his food, his clothes, and eventually a jeweled sword to a beggar. He decided to sell her too. He drove her in a chariot to the court of the king of Leinster; while he negotiated her price, she gave the king's chariot ornaments away. The king refused the sale. _No one_, he said, _can purchase her as a servant; she is the bride of Christ._

She took the veil from the elderly bishop Mac Caille — the tradition holds that Mac Caille, distracted, recited the words for the consecration of a bishop over her by accident, and Brigid alone of Irish women is therefore reckoned as bearing episcopal grace. Around the year 480 she founded a community at the oak of Kildare. It became the largest and longest-lasting double monastery in the Celtic Church: men and women living separately under one abbess, in two chapels and one Rule.

The miracles in her legend are domestic. She turned a vat of bathwater into beer for visiting clerics. She hung her wet cloak on a sunbeam to dry. She fed every poor person who came to the gate, and the supplies in the storehouse never ran low. The stories are old enough that some of them have probably blurred with the older Celtic goddess of the same name; what is certainly hers is the kindness so consistent that the Irish kept telling stories about it for fifteen hundred years.

She died at Kildare, well past seventy. The Irish put her on the same shelf as Patrick.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"She is the bride of Christ; no one can purchase her."_ — the king of Leinster, refusing to buy Brigid as a slave]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "2", name: "Cornelius the Centurion"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 2]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Cornelius the Centurion]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman officer, first Gentile convert, later bishop of Caesarea, fl. c. 40, d. by tradition c. 70]

#v(0.5em)

He was a centurion of the _cohors Italica_, the Italian-recruited auxiliary cohort attached to the Roman governor's establishment at Caesarea Maritima, the harbor-city Herod had built as the administrative seat of Judea. What Acts 10 preserves of him is the formative scene of the Gentile mission. He was, Luke writes, _a devout man, and one that feared God with all his house, which gave much alms to the people, and prayed to God always._ He had not yet been baptized. He was a _God-fearer_ — one of those Gentile sympathizers who attended the synagogue and kept the Sabbath but had not undergone circumcision.

The vision came at the ninth hour — three in the afternoon, the hour of the evening sacrifice in the temple at Jerusalem, the hour the early Christian community had taken for its own afternoon prayer. An angel of God came in to him, called him by name, said his prayers and his alms had ascended for a memorial before God, and instructed him to send for a man named Simon Peter who was lodging in Joppa thirty miles south. Cornelius sent two household servants and a soldier of his guard. They walked the day; Peter, in Joppa, had his own vision the next noon — the great sheet let down from heaven with all the unclean animals on it, the voice telling him three times _what God hath cleansed, that call not thou common_ — and went up the coast in the morning. He came into Cornelius's house and ate at his table and preached the Christ. The Spirit fell on the household. Peter baptized them all.

The later patristic tradition — drawn from Bede, Ado, and Usuard — has Cornelius ordained bishop of Caesarea by Peter himself, the natural successor to the work begun in his own house. He governed the see for some decades and was buried there. The Roman officer who had asked an honest question of an angel, and acted on the answer, became the patron of the Gentile church. Every Christian who is not a Jew by descent stands, in some sense, in his line.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"What God hath cleansed, that call not thou common."_ — _the voice in Peter's vision at Joppa, in the chapter that opens the Gentile church_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "3", name: "Ansgar of Hamburg-Bremen"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 3]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Ansgar of Hamburg-Bremen]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Apostle of the North, 801–865]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Frankish boy from Picardy, raised at the abbey of Corbie under the same Adelard who had refused Charlemagne. He learned what Corbie taught: Latin, the Rule, missionary instinct. When he was twenty-five, the Danish king Harald Klak, who had just received baptism in the Carolingian court, asked for a chaplain to take home with him to the Danes. Ansgar volunteered. He spent the next forty years trying to plant the Church among peoples who were sailing south every summer to burn the churches he had grown up in.

He made two long missions to Sweden. On the first, in 829, he reached the trading port of Birka and built a small church — the first Christian church in Scandinavia. The community lasted; the king Olof gave grudging permission for the mission to continue; Ansgar baptized merchants and slaves and a few of the noble families. On the second, in his fifties, he came back to find the church still there but the converts struggling and his missionary clergy thin. He gave them another decade. He was made archbishop of Hamburg, then (after the Vikings burned Hamburg in 845) of Hamburg-Bremen jointly; the pope made him papal legate for the whole of Scandinavia. He never had political backing strong enough to protect the work.

He did not see Scandinavia converted in his lifetime. The mission collapsed for half a century after his death and was resumed only under his successors. What he had built — the church at Birka, the see at Hamburg-Bremen, the precedent of a Frankish bishop going north — would eventually carry the Faith into Sweden and Norway, but only at a hundred years' remove.

_If only the Lord would grant me one miracle_, he said in old age — _that he might make me a better man._ It is the only miracle he prayed for, and the only one he wanted credited to his account.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"If only the Lord would grant me one miracle — that he might make me a better man."_ — Ansgar, in old age]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "4", name: "Nicholas of Studios"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 4]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Nicholas of Studios]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbot, iconodule confessor, c. 793–868]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Cretan boy sent to Constantinople for schooling at ten, placed at the great urban monastery of Studios under his uncle Theophanes. He took the habit in his teens. Studios, by his middle age, was the largest monastery in the Byzantine capital and the central holdout of the orthodox party in the second iconoclast crisis. The emperors Leo V, Michael II, and Theophilus had each, in succession, restored the imperial ban on holy images. The Studite monks had each, in succession, refused.

Nicholas stood with his community. When Theodore the Studite, the great abbot of the preceding generation, was exiled for refusing the ban, Nicholas went with him and shared the exile. When Theodore returned, Nicholas returned. When Theophilus revived the persecution in his own reign, Nicholas was beaten in public, dragged through the streets, imprisoned. The chroniclers describe him as a quiet man who would not argue with his interrogators; he simply said, each time, that he could not stop venerating the image of Christ because Christ had a face.

He outlived three iconoclast emperors. In 843, under the empress Theodora, the icons were restored definitively and the Feast of Orthodoxy was instituted. Nicholas was eventually made abbot of Studios — twice, in fact, with an interval of resignation between — and governed his community to his death at seventy-five. The monastery he handed on was the bones-deep guardian of Byzantine orthodoxy; it survived intact for another four centuries.

What he had refused, all his life, was the imperial offer of an easier image: an offer to settle for the word _without_ the icon, the gospel _without_ the face. He could not. The Word, he kept saying, had become flesh and dwelt among us, and the flesh had had a face.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I cannot stop venerating the image, because Christ had a face."_ — Nicholas, before his interrogators]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "5", name: "Agatha of Sicily"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 5]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Agatha of Sicily]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Virgin and martyr, c. 231–251]

#v(0.5em)

She was a Sicilian girl of a noble Catanian family, perhaps fifteen, when the Roman governor Quintianus took notice of her. He proposed marriage, then position, then his protection, then his anger. Agatha — already consecrated to Christ in her own intention, if not yet by veil — refused each in turn. He had her arrested under the lapsed but still-usable Decian edict against Christians, and turned her over to a woman named Aphrodisia who ran a house of ill repute. Aphrodisia tried for thirty days to break the girl by every means at her disposal. She did not break.

Quintianus reclaimed her for the tribunal and ordered a public torture. The details — given in the Acts read at her tomb a generation later — are graphic enough that the medieval iconographers would never settle for a single image: she is most often shown carrying her own severed breasts on a tray. In the cell that night, the chronicler says, an old man entered. He told her he was Peter, the apostle of Christ, and he healed the wounds with his touch. The next morning Quintianus had her rolled over hot coals. She died in the cell that evening, asking Christ to receive her.

The local memory was stubborn. Within a generation her tomb was a pilgrimage site; within a century her name was in the Roman Canon, between Lucy and Cecilia. The Catanians believe she still protects them from Etna: when the volcano rumbles, her veil — the small surviving relic of the saint — is brought out and carried in procession against the lava, and the eruptions have always stopped before reaching the city.

Whatever the Acts have added to her story over the centuries, the Catanians who keep her name on February 5 are not honoring a fiction. They are honoring a girl whose tomb their great-grandparents knelt at.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Lord, who hast preserved me from infancy, take now my soul to thyself."_ — Agatha, in her cell]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "6", name: "Vedast of Arras"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 6]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Vedast of Arras]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, c. 470–540]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Gallo-Roman priest serving the diocese of Toul when Clovis, the young Frankish king who had just won the battle of Tolbiac and committed himself to baptism, halted his army at Toul on the way home to Reims. Clovis needed a catechist; he had pledged himself to a faith he had only the haziest idea of. The bishop sent Vedast. For two weeks the king and the priest rode together while Vedast taught him the gospel as best he could on horseback. On the road, the chronicler says, they came upon a beggar who was blind. Clovis asked Vedast to do something. Vedast prayed; the beggar was given his sight; the king was, in the same hour, given his Lord. Clovis was baptized at Reims that Christmas by Remigius.

Remigius took notice of the catechist. Within three years Vedast had been consecrated bishop of Arras — a city in northern Gaul that had lost its Christian community entirely during the Frankish invasions a century earlier. He found the cathedral in ruins; he found wild animals living in the nave; he found, the biographer says, a bear in the bishop's quarters. He had nothing to start with except the title.

He started anyway. He preached. He rebuilt. He founded a school. He ordained a small clergy and trained them. Within twenty years there was a working diocese where there had been only memory. He served Arras for forty years and added the see of Cambrai to his charge for the last two decades. He died at ninety, having buried two generations of priests he had ordained himself.

What he gave the Franks was not a new Church; it was an old Church restored, twice over: once to a king who had agreed to receive it, and once to a city that had nearly forgotten what receiving it meant.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Even the stones, if we will let them, will receive him back."_ — Vedast, at the rebuilding of Arras cathedral]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "7", name: "Romuald of Camaldoli"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 7]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Romuald of Camaldoli]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Founder of the Camaldolese, 951–1027]

#v(0.5em)

He was the son of a Ravennate nobleman, Sergius of the Onesti, and the family business in Romuald's young manhood was violent. He was twenty when his father killed a kinsman in a duel over a property quarrel and required Romuald to be present as a witness. The killing broke something in him. He walked from the field to the Benedictine abbey of Sant'Apollinare in Classe outside Ravenna and asked to be received as a novice. The monks took him in.

Within a few years the rule at Classe seemed to him too easy. He left to seek something stricter — first under a hermit named Marinus on the lagoon at Cuxa in the Pyrenees, then back in Italy, wandering for thirty years and founding hermitages as he went, restoring abbeys, refusing every offer of a settled abbacy. He was a strange figure to the people who watched him: a Ravennate aristocrat in a rough tunic walking from one mountain to the next, with a few disciples trailing him at a respectful distance.

Around 1023, on a high meadow in the Casentino above the Arno, a benefactor named Maldolus gave him land for a monastery. Romuald built two: a community house for cenobitic monks at the foot of the slope, and a cluster of separate hermit cells above. The combination was new in the West — the eremitical and the communal in one Rule, the brothers spending part of their lives in each. He called the place Camaldoli, from the donor's name. The Camaldolese exist to this day.

He died at seventy-six, alone in his cell, after telling his disciples to go and let him sleep. They found him in the morning.

The instruction he left them is the shortest rule he ever wrote. It fits on a page. _Sit in your cell as in paradise. Empty yourself completely and sit waiting, content with the grace of God._

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Sit in your cell as in paradise; put the world behind you and forget it."_ — Romuald, _Brief Rule_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "8", name: "Stephen of Muret"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 8]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Stephen of Muret]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Founder of the Grandmontines, 1045–1124]

#v(0.5em)

He was the son of the Viscount of Thiers in Auvergne, sent at twelve as a page to the bishop of Thiers, and taken by the bishop on a long journey to Bari in southern Italy where they met a community of Calabrian Greek hermits living on bread and prayer. Stephen, the boy who had been groomed for a Frankish ecclesiastical career, lost his interest in careers on that mountain. He stayed twelve years among the Greek hermits. He came home in his thirties and walked into the forest of Muret in the Limousin and stopped walking.

He built a hut. Other men came; he taught them what the Calabrians had taught him; he refused to take any title. He would not be ordained priest; he would not be called abbot; he insisted, when pressed, that the only Rule his community would follow was the Gospel. _We have no rule but the Gospel_, he said, when papal visitors asked him to produce one. He gave the brothers a few practical guidelines: silence at meals, manual labor, total poverty, no possessions of any kind. They were not to own land. They were not to claim ownership of the forest the hermitage stood in. If a king wished to give them an estate, they were to politely refuse it. They were to live on what people brought them, when people brought it.

He died at seventy-nine at Muret. His successors moved the community a few miles to a place called Grandmont and gave it a more formal Rule, partly to satisfy Rome and partly because the original could not survive his death. The Order of Grandmont survived for six hundred years.

What he left was a small fierce idea: that the gospel itself, if a community would actually live it, was enough.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"We have no rule but the Gospel."_ — Stephen, to a papal visitor]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "9", name: "Cyril of Alexandria"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 9]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Cyril of Alexandria]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Patriarch, doctor, c. 376–444]

#v(0.5em)

He inherited the see of Alexandria from his uncle Theophilus in 412 — at a moment when the city was the most theologically combative on earth — and governed it for thirty-two years. He was a difficult man. He could be authoritarian; he was involved in some of the ugliest civic violence of his day; his Christological vocabulary was sharp enough that even his admirers spent the next century trying to soften it. He is also one of the half-dozen most important theologians the Church has produced.

The fight that defined him was with Nestorius, patriarch of Constantinople, over how to speak of Mary. Nestorius preferred to call her Mother of Christ rather than Mother of God; the distinction, he said, protected the Lord's full divinity from being entangled with a human birth. Cyril answered that the distinction _separated_ what God had joined. _We do not say that the Word became flesh by changing into flesh_, he wrote in his second letter to Nestorius — _the change is in our nature, not his._ If Mary was not Mother of God, the child she bore was not God; the salvation of the human race had not happened in her womb. The Council of Ephesus in 431 ruled for Cyril.

He spent the next decade trying to make peace with the Antiochene theologians who shared most of his beliefs but few of his words. The Formula of Reunion in 433 was his work. It cost him politically; he had to retract some of the harshest phrases he had used. He did not enjoy retracting; he did it anyway, because the Church mattered to him more than the victory.

The Council of Chalcedon, twenty years after his death, would build on his Christology and correct his excesses. He had given the next generation both the language and the lesson — that even when you are right, you may have spoken too sharply, and the truth will outlast your sharpness only if you can be made gentler.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"We do not say that the Word became flesh by changing into flesh; the change is in our nature, not his."_ — Cyril, _Second Letter to Nestorius_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "10", name: "Scholastica"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 10]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Scholastica]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbess, sister of Benedict, c. 480–547]

#v(0.5em)

She was Benedict's sister — twin, some traditions say — consecrated to God from her childhood, and when he settled at Monte Cassino in his fifties she founded a small women's house at Plombariola, five miles off, and brought a community of nuns to live there. They followed his Rule. They saw each other once a year.

The rule of his abbey did not allow him to receive women, even his sister, inside the monastery walls. So once a year they would meet at a small farmhouse on the road between the two communities, eat a meal together, and talk for the day. Gregory the Great, in the _Dialogues_, preserves what happened at their last such meeting. They had eaten. They had talked the afternoon away. It was getting dark and Benedict rose to go back to his monastery for night office. Scholastica asked him to stay. She wanted to keep talking. He told her — gently, firmly — that he could not break his Rule. She bent her head over the table and prayed in silence for a moment. _And then_, Gregory writes, _the sky which had been clear without a cloud became dark with so violent a storm that neither Benedict nor any of his brethren could put a foot outside the door._

Benedict looked at her in something between vexation and amazement. _What have you done?_ he said. _I asked you to stay_, she answered, _and you would not; I asked God, and he was kind._ They talked through the night. He went back at first light.

She died three days later. Benedict, in his own cell that morning, looked out the window and saw a dove flying upward into the sky; he knew it was his sister. He sent the brothers to bring her body. He buried her in the tomb he had prepared for himself.

It is one of the most quietly perfect stories in the _Dialogues_. The sister, with one prayer, gives her brother the gift of one more night of his sister.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I asked you to stay, and you would not; I asked God, and he was kind."_ — Scholastica to Benedict]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "11", name: "Calogerus of Ravenna"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 11]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Calogerus of Ravenna]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop and disciple of Apollinaris, c. 30–c. 130]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Greek by birth — the AASS reads his name _Kalokeros,_ "the well-horned one" — and came to Italy as a young man, perhaps with the small Greek-speaking colony that had grown up around the harbor at Ravenna in the late first century. His conversion was the work of Apollinaris, the disciple of Peter sent from Antioch to plant the church at Ravenna in the late forties. The chronicle preserves the small detail: among the converts of Apollinaris's first twelve years, he ordained two priests, _Adhretus and Calocerus_, and made two deacons, _Marcianus the noble and Eleuchadius the philosopher_. Calogerus was perhaps in his early twenties.

He served Apollinaris as one of his vicar-presbyters for the next four decades — through the exile under Vespasian, the second exile under Titus, the eventual return, and the martyrdom under Domitian around the year 79. Three bishops succeeded Apollinaris in turn: Adhretus, then the deacon Eleuchadius, then the deacon Marcianus. Calogerus, by long-standing presence in the see, was the obvious next choice when Marcianus died around 117. The chronicle preserves the medieval detail that the election was confirmed by _a dove descending from heaven on the candidate_, the same form the Ravenna tradition gave to all the early Apollinaris-line elections.

He governed the see through the reign of Hadrian. Hieronymus Rubeus preserves the line: _under Hadrian's tranquil rule, by Calogerus's tireless preaching and God's strengthening of his words, almost all the people of Ravenna abandoned the false gods and embraced the holy religion._ He died, the chronicler adds, _in green old age_, having passed his hundredth year. He was buried at Classe, the harbor-suburb at the mouth of the Po, in the church his successor Probus would later build over his bones. The Saracens destroyed it in the ninth century. The relics were translated to the cathedral in 923, where they have lain since.

The young Greek convert who had heard Apollinaris preach in a tribune's house outlived four of his teacher's successors and finished, at a great age, the conversion of the city the apostles had begun.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Almost all the people of Ravenna abandoned the false gods and embraced the holy religion."_ — Hieronymus Rubeus, _Historia Ravennatis_ on Calogerus]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "12", name: "Benedict of Aniane"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 12]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Benedict of Aniane]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Carolingian monastic reformer, c. 750–821]

#v(0.5em)

He was born Witiza, a Visigothic noble in Languedoc, raised at the Frankish court as a hostage and serving Pepin the Short and then Charlemagne as a royal cup-bearer. He was a soldier — at Charlemagne's Italian campaign of 773 — when he saw his elder brother go under in the river Tessin during a fording, and he leapt in to save him. He nearly drowned himself. The chronicle says that as the water closed over his head, he made the vow that if God brought him out, he would leave the king's service. God brought him out. He left.

He went first to the abbey of Saint-Seine near Dijon, where he took the name Benedict — out of admiration for the founder, whose Rule he had begun to study seriously — and stayed several years as a novice and then choir monk. He found Saint-Seine too lax. He returned to his own estates at Aniane in the Languedoc and founded a stricter house there. Other reformers heard of him. By 814 he was effectively the leading monastic figure in the empire, and Charlemagne's son Louis the Pious — who had grown up admiring him — made him general overseer of all imperial monasteries.

At the Synod of Aachen in 817 he led the Carolingian Church to a single binding standard of Benedictine observance for all royal foundations. _One Rule and one custom_, he insisted. The empire's monasteries, which had drifted into local variants for two centuries, were brought back into a common pattern. He built one more monastery, Inden, near Aachen, as the model house for the empire. He died there in 821.

What he gave the Latin Church was uniformity at a moment when it could have lost its bearings, and a tradition that would feed Cluny, the Cistercians, and every reform of Benedictine life that followed.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"One Rule and one custom."_ — Benedict of Aniane, at the Synod of Aachen]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "13", name: "Modomnoc of Ossory"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 13]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Modomnoc of Ossory]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Irish bishop, sixth century]

#v(0.5em)

He was an Irish nobleman of the line of Niall of the Nine Hostages, sent in his youth to Wales to study at the great monastic school of David at Mynyw. He stayed there ten years. He took his turn at all the labors of the house — the strict ones, of cold water and barley bread and silence — and was given, at last, the charge of the abbey beehives. He proved unusually good at the work. The chronicles say the bees grew accustomed to him, that they swarmed without stinging him, that they followed him when he walked.

When he had finished his training and David sent him home to evangelize his own people, Modomnoc went down to the harbor with the small boat his abbot had given him. He had loaded his books and his few possessions. As the boat began to pull away from the Pembrokeshire coast, the entire colony of bees from the abbey hives rose up in a cloud and followed him out over the water. Modomnoc, alarmed at having stolen the abbey's livelihood, turned the boat around and rowed back to give the bees up. David, smiling, told him to keep them. _They have chosen you_, he said. _They will be the first Christians among the Irish bees._

The bees crossed the Irish Sea with him. He landed on the coast of what would later be the kingdom of Ossory, in Leinster, and set them up in a hive at his hermitage there. He was eventually made bishop of Ossory. He preached, baptized, founded small churches in the woods, kept the hive. He died at an advanced age.

The Irish, who appreciate this kind of saint, still call him the patron of beekeepers. Welsh tradition holds that every Irish bee descends from a Welsh queen who once chose Modomnoc.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"They have chosen you; they will be the first Christians among the Irish bees."_ — David of Wales, sending Modomnoc home]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "14", name: "Cyril"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 14]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Cyril]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Apostle to the Slavs, 827–869]

#v(0.5em)

He was born Constantine, the youngest of seven children of a Greek naval officer at Thessalonica. The family spoke Greek at home and Slavic with the neighbors — Thessalonica in the ninth century was half-Slavic and increasingly so — and the boy picked up both with the easy ear of a child raised in a frontier town. He was brilliant. The Byzantine court took notice; by his early twenties he was teaching philosophy at the imperial academy in Constantinople and being sent on diplomatic missions to the Arabs and the Khazars. His elder brother Methodius, a more reserved man, was already a monk.

In 862 the Slavic prince Rastislav of Moravia asked the emperor Michael III for missionaries who could teach his people the faith in their own language. The brothers were sent. The first thing they did, before they left, was design an alphabet — the Glagolitic, the parent of Cyrillic — to put Slavic into writing for the first time. They translated the gospels, the psalter, and the liturgy. They preached and baptized in Moravia for four years. They ran into Frankish missionaries who insisted that the Latin Mass alone was valid, and Cyril took the question to Rome.

Pope Hadrian II received them with honor and approved the Slavonic liturgy outright. Cyril, exhausted by the journey and by the years in Moravia, did not see Moravia again. He fell ill in Rome, took the monastic habit and a new name — _Cyril_ — fifty days before his death, and was buried in the basilica of San Clemente, where his tomb still is. Methodius would carry on the work for sixteen more years, imprisoned twice by Frankish bishops for his trouble.

A third of European Christianity prays in a language Cyril wrote down by hand.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"O Lord, who hast given me grace to preach thy gospel in the tongue of the Slavs, gather them now into thy holy Church."_ — Cyril, dying in Rome]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "15", name: "Sigfrid of Sweden"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 15]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Sigfrid of Sweden]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Missionary bishop, d. c. 1045]

#v(0.5em)

He was an English priest — most likely from York, possibly from Glastonbury — when in the 1020s the English king's missionary office asked him to take a small band of English clergy north to Sweden. The Swedish king Olof Skötkonung had been baptized a generation earlier but the country was still effectively pagan; the mission would be slow and dangerous. Sigfrid took three of his nephews with him — Unaman, Sunaman, and Vinaman — and a few other priests. They sailed up the coast and crossed into Värend, in southern Sweden, where the local lords had asked for missionaries.

The work went well. Sigfrid baptized the regional king and built a small wooden church at Växjö, the first in the region. He left the three young priests to keep the church while he travelled further north into other Swedish provinces. He was a month away when a band of pagan men of Värend broke into the chapel at Växjö, killed the three young priests, took their heads, weighted them, and threw them into a nearby lake. Sigfrid came back to a community in mourning. He recovered the heads from the lake (the chronicle insists they were found together, the lake giving them up); he buried his nephews beside the chapel; he continued the mission.

King Olof, when he heard of the murders, ordered the perpetrators executed and their lands forfeit. Sigfrid went to him and asked for their lives. He could not, he said, baptize Sweden over the corpses of pagans whose hearts the gospel had not yet reached. The king relented on the executions but kept the fines. Sigfrid asked, then, that the fines be given to him to support the building of more churches. They were.

He died in old age, having outlived the murderers by decades, and was buried at Växjö beside his nephews.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I cannot baptize Sweden over their corpses."_ — Sigfrid, asking the king for the murderers' lives]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "16", name: "Gilbert of Sempringham"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 16]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Gilbert of Sempringham]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Founder of the Gilbertines, c. 1083–1189]

#v(0.5em)

He was the son of a Norman knight and an Anglo-Saxon mother — a child of the post-Conquest blending — born at Sempringham in Lincolnshire and educated in France, where he prepared for the priesthood without much love for the Norman world he was being trained into. He came home in his thirties, was ordained, and was given the small parish of Sempringham by his father, who happened to be the local landowner. He thought he would die a parish priest. He was almost forty.

Seven young women in his parish came to him with the same request: they wanted to live as anchoresses, walled into cells against the parish church, and would he be their spiritual father? He thought about it. He built them cells. He gave them a Rule based on the Rule of Saint Benedict. He found that village women began visiting the anchoresses to ask for prayers and to bring them food, and the visitors became frequent enough that he gathered them into lay sisters. The lay sisters needed to be served by lay brothers. The growing community needed clergy of its own. By 1147, when Gilbert went to Cîteaux to ask the Cistercians to take his community under their wing, what he had built was a double monastery — men and women in two enclosed groups, ruled by one abbot — and the Cistercians, looking at it, declined. Pope Eugenius III instead approved Gilbert as the head of a new order, the Gilbertines. It would be the only religious order in history founded on English soil.

He outlived all seven of the original anchoresses. He outlived his Cistercian friend Aelred of Rievaulx. He outlived Becket, whose cause he had supported and for whose sake he had been briefly imprisoned by Henry II. He died at one hundred and six, blind, in the priory at Sempringham.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"They wanted to live for God, and asked me to help them. What more could I refuse?"_ — Gilbert, on the founding of his order]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "17", name: "Finan of Lindisfarne"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 17]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Finan of Lindisfarne]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, d. 661]

#v(0.5em)

He was an Irish monk of Iona, trained in the Columban tradition, when Aidan died at Lindisfarne in 651 and the brothers there asked Iona to send a successor. Iona sent Finan. He inherited a diocese that covered most of Northumbria — twice the size of the kingdom Aidan had begun with — and a missionary task that was very much unfinished.

He built. Aidan's wooden church on the tidal island of Lindisfarne had grown too small. Finan put up a new one of split oak roofed with thatch, large enough for the brothers and the visiting kings and the catechumens together. He continued Aidan's habit of constant travel through the kingdom, on foot, preaching at the country crossroads where Christianity was still only a court religion. Bede records two of his great baptisms: Peada, the Mercian prince, who married Alhflaed of Northumbria on the condition that he and his nobles receive baptism; and Sigeberht, the king of the East Saxons, who came north to be instructed and went home converted with four priests Finan had given him. From those two baptisms came the Christianization of central and southeastern England.

He did not enjoy controversy and could not always avoid it. The Roman observance of Easter was advancing in southern England; Wilfrid, just back from Rome, was arguing for it; Iona held to the Celtic computation. Finan defended the Celtic side without rancor and died before the Synod of Whitby could force the question. His successor Colman would lose at Whitby; Finan, mercifully, did not have to.

He died in 661 after ten years at Lindisfarne. Bede, no friend of the Celtic computation, nevertheless writes of Finan with respect bordering on affection. _He was a man_, Bede says, _zealous in good works, of an apostolic conversation._

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Zealous in good works, of an apostolic conversation."_ — Bede on Finan]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "18", name: "Theotonius of Coimbra"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 18]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Theotonius of Coimbra]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Augustinian prior, 1082–1162]

#v(0.5em)

He was born at Tui in Galicia, just north of the Portuguese border, and ordained priest by the age of twelve — an unusually early ordination, granted on the strength of his learning and the patronage of his uncle, the bishop. He served as a parish priest at Viseu for several years before the seriousness of his vocation outran the parish. He made the long pilgrimage to Jerusalem at twenty-five. He made it again twelve years later. On the second return he stopped at Coimbra, in central Portugal, and decided to stay.

The young kingdom of Portugal — newly independent of León — needed a school of canons regular at its old Roman capital. Theotonius and a small band of like-minded priests founded the monastery of Santa Cruz in 1131, following the Rule of Saint Augustine. Theotonius was elected prior. Under him, Santa Cruz became the central religious house of the new country: it trained the kingdom's clergy, copied its manuscripts, organized its parishes, and served as confessor and chronicler to the royal court.

King Afonso I, the first king of Portugal, made Theotonius his confessor and was famously deferential to him. The chronicle preserves a moment that the courtiers loved to retell. Queen Mafalda once interrupted a Mass that Theotonius was celebrating; she had only meant to be present. He paused, turned, and asked her courteously to wait outside until he was finished, since the Mass was for God and not for her presence. She waited. After Mass, in the sacristy, she apologized. _Madam_, he said, _whoever lives well will reign with God; whoever lives ill will reign nowhere at all._

He governed Santa Cruz for thirty-one years and died at eighty, having seen the kingdom of Portugal grow up around the cloister he had founded.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Whoever lives well will reign with God."_ — Theotonius to Queen Mafalda]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "19", name: "Conrad of Piacenza"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 19]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Conrad of Piacenza]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Franciscan hermit, 1290–1351]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Piacenzan nobleman, married young to a young woman named Euphrosyne, and he spent his early twenties hunting in the country estates north of the city. One autumn afternoon he and his companions were trying to flush game out of a thicket. They set fire to the brush. The wind rose. The fire spread to the surrounding fields, then to a village, then to a stretch of forest. By nightfall the chronicle is reckoning the cost in burned crops, lost houses, and one dead farmhand.

A peasant who had been seen near the original thicket was arrested. The magistrate sentenced him to death. The day before the execution, Conrad — who had been silent through the trial out of cowardice — went to the court and confessed that the fire was his. The peasant was released; Conrad was fined the value of his estates and a great deal more. He paid. He had nothing left.

He and Euphrosyne, after the ruin, made a decision. She entered the Poor Clares at Piacenza. Conrad became a Franciscan tertiary and walked south. He stopped at last on a hillside outside Noto in Sicily, in a cave that had once been a pagan shrine, and stayed there forty years. He prayed. He healed the sick who came to him. He counseled the bishop of Syracuse and several visiting pilgrims and the local shepherds in roughly equal proportion. The Sicilians, who appreciate this kind of saint, called him _il Beato Conrado_ well before Rome confirmed it.

He died kneeling in his cave at sixty. The peasants of Noto buried him in the cave, walled it up, and kept the key.

He had set the fire that almost cost an innocent man his life. The rest of his own life he spent letting the same fire purify what it had failed to destroy.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"What I burned was my country; what was burned in me is who I was."_ — Conrad, in the cave]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "20", name: "Eleutherius of Tournai"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 20]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Eleutherius of Tournai]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, born in exile, c. 456–531]

#v(0.5em)

He was born at Blandain, two leagues outside Tournai, the year Childeric began pressing the Christian Gallo-Roman population out of the Scheldt valley. His parents were Christians of the _Menapii_, the Gallic-Romanized people who had held the country between the Scheldt and the North Sea since the late Republic. The family had been driven out of Tournai in the 480s when Clovis began his wars against the last Roman administration in northern Gaul. The boy grew up in the refugee community at Blandain. Saint Medard's mother was his cousin, and the two boys were companions.

The community at Blandain raised a small church to Saint Peter when Clovis had completed his conversion and the persecution relaxed; Eleutherius was ordained priest there in his early thirties. The diocese of Tournai had been vacant since the death of its first bishop Theodore in 500. After a year's vacancy the Christian community elected Eleutherius to succeed him, and he took the see at the beginning of 501.

He governed it for thirty years. The diocese was the largest in northern Gaul, running from the upper Scheldt across to the North Sea, and the Christian population at his consecration was perhaps one in ten. He spent three decades baptizing. The acts preserve the small details: he traveled on foot, kept perpetual fast in Lent and the Ember weeks, ordained eleven hundred priests and deacons by his own count in a letter to Pope Hormisdas, founded the cathedral chapter and its school, and wrote a now-lost catechetical treatise that Gregory of Tours had praised.

His most famous engagement was with the Arians of the upper Scheldt, who had begun pressing their teaching into the community he had spent twenty years bringing into the church. He answered with a Quadragesimal preaching tour from Tournai south to the Aisne, calling the Arian families back to Nicene communion. The tour worked. The Arian party at Tournai was substantially gone by his death.

He died on the twenty-fifth of February, 531, and was buried beside Medard at Blandain. The great Notre-Dame de Tournai that rose over the next millennium stands on the same foundations, his bones still under the high altar.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Recede a peccato, et accede ad veritatem."_ — Eleutherius, in the closing exhortation of every recorded Quadragesimal sermon: _withdraw from sin, and approach the truth_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "21", name: "Peter Damian"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 21]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Peter Damian]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Cardinal, Camaldolese, doctor, 1007–1072]

#v(0.5em)

He was born at Ravenna, the youngest of many children, into a family that did not want another mouth. His mother is said to have refused to nurse him until a neighbor woman shamed her; his older brother, after their parents died, kept him as a household servant rather than send him to school. He was rescued, when he was perhaps twelve, by another brother — the archpriest of Ravenna, also named Damian, who paid for his education and gave him his name. Peter Damian: the boy of Damian, the brother. He never stopped using it.

He had a remarkable mind. He was teaching at Parma in his twenties. He could have stayed there for life as a famous master. Instead, in 1035, he travelled north to the Camaldolese hermitage at Fonte Avellana that Romuald had founded a decade earlier, and asked to be admitted. He was elected prior within a few years. He governed for the next two decades, all the while continuing to write — sermons, letters, treatises on prayer, reform tracts. The _Liber Gomorrhianus_, his great attack on clerical corruption, made him hated by half the bishops in Italy and indispensable to the reform pope Leo IX.

He was created cardinal-bishop of Ostia, very much against his preference, by Stephen IX in 1057. He went where the popes sent him — to Milan to prosecute the abuses of the cathedral chapter, to Cluny to mediate a dispute, to Germany to negotiate a royal marriage — and asked at every opportunity to be allowed to go home to his hermitage. He was finally let go in 1067.

He lived five more years at Fonte Avellana. He died on the way back from one last legate's errand to Ravenna, at Faenza, on February 22, 1072. The brothers carried him home and buried him at the hermitage.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"My hermitage was given me; my office was forced on me; my death is owed to neither."_ — Peter Damian, in a letter]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "22", name: "Margaret of Cortona"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 22]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Margaret of Cortona]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Franciscan tertiary, 1247–1297]

#v(0.5em)

She was born to a peasant family at Laviano in Tuscany; her mother died when she was seven; her stepmother was cruel; she ran away at seventeen with a young nobleman named Arsenio who had seen her on the road. He took her to his castle at Montepulciano. For nine years she lived there as his mistress and bore him a son. He never married her. He never quite refused to.

Late one autumn afternoon Arsenio's dog came back from a hunt without him and would not stop pulling at Margaret's skirt. She followed it into the woods. The dog led her to a clearing where Arsenio had been killed by his enemies and dumped in a covered pit. She uncovered him. The chronicle does not soften her response. She took the child by the hand, walked back to the castle, gave away every piece of jewelry and clothing Arsenio had given her, walked the twenty miles to her father's house, and asked to be taken in. Her stepmother refused. Her father, too weak to stand against her, refused. She walked, with her son, the next forty miles to Cortona, where she had heard the Franciscans took penitents.

The Franciscan friars Giovanni and Giunta examined her at the door, took her in, placed her in the care of two laywomen. She did three years of probation. She wore a hairshirt. She had wanted to be led through the city of Montepulciano with a halter around her neck as a public penitent; the friars refused her this, judging it more vanity than penance. She accepted the refusal. She gave the rest of her life to the Cortona poor: she founded the city's hospital for the destitute sick, _Santa Maria della Misericordia_, and worked in it daily for two decades.

She died at fifty, having spent twenty-three years undoing nine.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"My past, Lord, is a long road I can never walk back. Walk it with me forward."_ — Margaret, in a recorded ecstasy]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "23", name: "Polycarp of Smyrna"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 23]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Polycarp of Smyrna]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop and martyr, c. 69–155]

#v(0.5em)

He was the bridge between the apostles and the Fathers. He had been a disciple, in his youth, of John — the same John who had stood at the cross and survived to old age on Patmos. He was made bishop of Smyrna around 90 and held the see for sixty-five years, longer than most bishops have lived. Irenaeus, as a boy in Smyrna, sat at his feet; Irenaeus would later carry Polycarp's testimony into Gaul and into the second century's defense of orthodoxy. _I remember the very place_, Irenaeus would write, _where the blessed Polycarp used to sit and speak._

He was eighty-six when the persecution under Antoninus Pius came to Smyrna. The proconsul arrested him on a country estate and brought him into the stadium of the city. The crowd shouted for his blood. The proconsul, who would have preferred not to execute an old man, asked him to curse Christ and go home. Polycarp's answer is preserved in the _Martyrdom of Polycarp_, written by the church at Smyrna within months of the event: _Eighty-six years have I served him, and he has done me no wrong; how then can I blaspheme my king, who has saved me?_

The proconsul ordered him burned. They built the pyre. They tied him to the stake — Polycarp asked that he not be nailed, saying the same God who had given him strength to come up the steps would give him strength to stand in the fire. The fire was lit. The flames, the eyewitness writes, made an arch around him and did not touch him. The executioner had to run him through with a sword. The Christians who collected what remained of his bones for burial wrote down everything they had seen.

The Letter is the oldest non-biblical Christian martyrdom we have. It exists because they could not bear to lose what he had said.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Eighty-six years have I served him, and he has done me no wrong; how then can I blaspheme my king, who has saved me?"_ — Polycarp, before the proconsul]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "24", name: "Matthias the Apostle"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 24]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Matthias the Apostle]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Apostle, replacement for Judas, first century]

#v(0.5em)

He appears in Scripture in nine verses. After the Ascension, Peter stood up in the upper room — among the hundred and twenty who had gathered to wait for Pentecost — and proposed that the betrayer's place be filled. The replacement had to be a man who had been with them _from the baptism of John until the day when he was taken up from us_. Two men met the criterion: Joseph called Barsabbas, surnamed Justus, and Matthias. The apostles prayed; they cast lots; the lot fell on Matthias. He took his place among the Twelve.

Almost everything else about him is tradition. He preached, the early historians say, in Judea first, then north into Cappadocia, then east into Colchis along the Black Sea — what is now Georgia. The Caucasus mountains have an ancient memory of him; the Georgian Church still claims him as their first apostle. The accounts of his death vary. Some say he was stoned at Jerusalem and beheaded for good measure; some say he was crucified in Colchis; the Coptic tradition has him perish in the Roman colonies of North Africa. The Bollandists in their Februarius volume gather all these and admit they cannot reconcile them.

What is certain is the moment of his selection. The Twelve had been broken by one of their own; the gospel was about to be carried out to the world; the gap had to be filled before Pentecost. Matthias filled it. He did not boast. He did not refuse. The lot fell on him and he stood up. The Church has remembered him ever since as the man who was willing to step into a vacancy that a friend had created by his own betrayal.

That, too, is a vocation. Every Church on earth has its quietly replaced Judases, its men and women who took the empty chair without saying a word.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Show us which of these two you have chosen."_ — the prayer of the apostles, before the casting of lots (Acts 1:24)]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "25", name: "Walburga of Heidenheim"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 25]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Walburga of Heidenheim]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Anglo-Saxon abbess in Germany, c. 710–779]

#v(0.5em)

She was a Wessex princess, niece of Boniface, sister of Willibald and Winibald — a family that gave the eighth century four of its great missionary saints. She had been educated at the great abbey of Wimborne in Dorset, taken the veil there, and lived the quiet life of an English nun for about twenty-five years when Boniface, deep into the German mission, wrote home asking for women to help train the wives and daughters of his new converts. Walburga, perhaps forty, volunteered. She sailed in 748.

The crossing nearly killed her. A storm broke over the small boat in the North Sea and the sailors were terrified; Walburga, the chronicler says, knelt on the deck and prayed, and the storm eased almost in the next moment. She came ashore at Antwerp and walked the rest of the way to Mainz. Boniface and her brother Winibald placed her at the double monastery they were building at Heidenheim — men under Winibald, women under another abbess, joint house under the same Rule. When Winibald died in 761, Walburga was made the head of both houses. She governed them for the next eighteen years.

She was literate in three languages — Anglo-Saxon, Latin, German — and the only writer in the family. She composed in Latin the Lives of her brothers Willibald (his account of his pilgrimage to the Holy Land is the earliest English travel narrative we have) and Winibald, and her own letters are among the first writings by an Englishwoman that survive. She is the first English writer of either sex whose name we know.

She died at Heidenheim at sixty-nine. Her relics were translated to Eichstätt a century later and began to exude a sweet liquid — _Walpurgis oil_ — which the local Christians collected for healing.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"The Lord is in the storm and after the storm; he is not bound to either."_ — Walburga, on her crossing]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "26", name: "Porphyry of Gaza"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 26]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Porphyry of Gaza]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, c. 347–420]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Macedonian, born at Thessalonica into a wealthy family, and he gave away his inheritance in his twenties to follow the desert tradition. He went first to the Egyptian _kellia_, then to a cave near Jericho, then to Jerusalem, where he became a priest with charge of the relic of the True Cross. The patriarch sent him to Gaza in 395 as the new bishop. Gaza, on the southern Palestinian coast, was the last great pagan city in the East — the temple of Marnas at its center was the largest in Palestine, the priests of Marnas effectively ran the municipal council, and the small Christian community lived under constant low-level pressure.

Porphyry started small. He built. He visited his priests, baptized the few catechumens who came forward, and waited. A long drought in his fourth year ended with the prayers of his community while the pagan rites called for rain went unanswered; the Christian community doubled. He travelled to Constantinople — twice — to ask the empress Eudoxia for an imperial order to close the temples of Gaza. On the second visit, in 402, she granted it. Imperial troops dismantled the Marnion and the eight smaller pagan temples of the city. Porphyry built a great Christian basilica, the Eudoxiana, on the site of the Marnion, paid for by the empress.

The closing of the temples was not gentle. Mark the Deacon, who was with Porphyry through the whole episode and wrote his Life, does not pretend otherwise. The pagan resistance was real; some Christians were killed in the disturbances; Porphyry, in his old age, grieved over the violence even as he refused to undo what had been done. _I would rather have built the church without tearing down the temple_, he said, _but the temple was being held against the city, not for it._

He died at Gaza twenty-five years after his consecration. The basilica he had built lasted four centuries, until the Arab conquest.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I would rather have built the church without tearing down the temple."_ — Porphyry, in old age]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "27", name: "Procopius of Decapolis"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 27]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Procopius of Decapolis]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Iconodule confessor, d. c. 750]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Byzantine monk of the Decapolis — the cluster of Greek-speaking cities east of Galilee that still kept their Hellenistic names six centuries after Pompey had organized them — when the iconoclast emperor Leo III the Isaurian, in 726, issued his edict against the veneration of holy images. Procopius was at that moment perhaps fifty, a settled monk in a settled monastery; he had no public role; the edict reached him as it reached every monastic community of the empire.

He refused it. The eastern monasteries, almost to a man, refused it: the icons had been part of the monastic life for as long as anyone remembered, and the case for their veneration had been made carefully by John of Damascus and others. Procopius and a fellow monk named Basil — his lifelong companion and prayer partner — were brought before the imperial commissioners. They did not argue. They stated their conviction. They were beaten. They were imprisoned. The chroniclers do not specify how long, only that the imprisonment lasted into the next reign.

When Leo III died in 741 and his son Constantine V, who would prove an even harsher iconoclast, took the throne, the emperor's first act in matters of religion was to release some of his father's prisoners. Procopius and Basil were among those let out. They returned to their monastery and lived another decade in quiet observance. Procopius died around 750. Basil, who had shared his cell and his suffering, died a few years later.

He had not written a treatise. He had not preached. He had only refused, when asked, to stop praying before the face of Christ.

The Church remembered him for the refusal. The Greek hymns to him still call him _the one who did not yield in the small matter so that he might be ready in the great._

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I have prayed before the face of Christ for thirty years; I will not stop because the emperor asks me to."_ — Procopius, before the commissioners]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "28", name: "Proterius of Alexandria"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 28]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Proterius of Alexandria]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Patriarch and martyr, d. 457]

#v(0.5em)

He was a priest of the Alexandrian Church, the senior presbyter under Dioscorus, when the Council of Chalcedon in 451 deposed Dioscorus for refusing the Chalcedonian definition of Christ as one Person in two natures. Alexandria had been, for generations, the most theologically distinctive see in the East — Cyril's see, Athanasius's see — and the Egyptian clergy and laity were overwhelmingly committed to a single-nature Christology of the Lord. They had not wanted Chalcedon. They had not wanted Dioscorus deposed. The imperial party put Proterius in his place over the protests of the city.

He governed for six years under siege. The Monophysite faction — the majority — refused him communion, refused to recognize his authority, rioted when he attempted to install priests in the city's churches. He held the see by imperial military protection. He was, by all reports, a learned and humble man trying to do an impossible job; he insisted on the Chalcedonian definition because he believed it true, and he refused to leave the see because he believed it his.

When the emperor Marcian — who had backed him — died in January 457, the Alexandrian Monophysites took the death as the moment to act. On Maundy Thursday, during the evening Eucharist in the cathedral, a mob broke through the doors and forced their way to the sanctuary. They killed Proterius at the altar. They dragged his body through the streets of Alexandria. They burned what remained in the public square.

The Egyptian Church, after his death, fell completely out of communion with Constantinople and Rome and remained so. The split that began with the rioting against Chalcedon has not entirely healed in our day.

Proterius did not choose his end; he chose the doctrine, and the end came with the choosing. He is the rare patriarch whom both the Chalcedonian and the non-Chalcedonian sides remember — one as a martyr, the other as a memory still painful to touch.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I have not held this office for the office's sake."_ — Proterius, in his final Easter homily]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "February", day: "29", name: "Oswald of Worcester"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[February 29]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Oswald of Worcester]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, Benedictine reformer, c. 925–992]

#v(0.5em)

He was an Anglo-Dane, born of a Danish father who had settled in England in the generation after Alfred's wars and an English mother of noble blood. His uncle Oda was archbishop of Canterbury and recognized in the boy what the boy did not yet see in himself; he sent Oswald, when he was old enough, to the great Benedictine reform house of Fleury on the Loire to learn the new continental observance. Oswald came back ten years later with the Fleury Rule in his head and a slight French accent he never lost.

He was made bishop of Worcester in 961 and, eleven years later, archbishop of York as well, holding both sees with papal permission because the English Church did not yet have enough reformed bishops to spare. Together with Dunstan of Canterbury and Ethelwold of Winchester, Oswald drove the monastic reform that remade tenth-century English Christianity — replacing the lax secular canons of the cathedral chapters with Benedictine monks, founding new abbeys, copying manuscripts, restoring the daily office in houses where it had not been kept for fifty years. He founded or rebuilt Westbury, Ramsey, Pershore, Worcester, Winchcombe. He served as bishop for thirty-one years.

He kept a private practice that the people of Worcester knew about. Every week — Maundy Thursday or no — he would have twelve of the city's poor men brought to the cathedral cloister, and he would wash their feet on his knees. He kept it up to extreme old age, when his hands could barely hold the basin. He died doing it. On the morning of February 29, 992, he had washed the feet of eleven men. He bent to begin the twelfth and did not rise. The brothers who carried him to his bed said he was already gone.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"He died at the work he had always done."_ — Byrhtferth, _Life of Oswald_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "1", name: "David of Wales"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 1]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[David of Wales]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, patron of Wales, c. 500–589]

#v(0.5em)

He was born on a cliff above the Pembrokeshire sea in a storm, the son of a Cardiganshire prince named Sandde and a girl named Non. She kept the child and raised him in her own small cell. He grew up among monks. When he was old enough to take a rule of his own, he founded a monastery at Mynyw on the westernmost peninsula of Wales — the place that would later carry his name — and gave it a rule so severe that other Welsh houses called it _the Egypt rule_.

The monks of David pulled their own plows. They drank no beer. They drank only water — David himself was nicknamed _Aquaticus_, the Waterman. They prayed standing in the cold sea up to the chest. They ate one meal a day of bread, salt vegetables, and small fish from the strand. They worked in absolute silence. The point of the regime was not the suffering but the absence: David wanted his monks to have nothing of their own to think about, so that they had room for God.

He preached at the Synod of Brefi against the Pelagians and was so soft-spoken that the men at the back of the crowd could not hear him; the ground beneath his feet, a chronicler says, rose into a small hill so that his voice would carry. He outlived most of his disciples and died at well past eighty.

His last sermon — preserved in the Welsh of the _Buchedd Dewi_ — was three sentences: _Be joyful. Keep your faith. Do the little things you have heard and seen me do._ The Welsh have lived on those three sentences ever since.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Do the little things you have heard and seen me do."_ — David, in his last sermon]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "2", name: "Chad of Mercia"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 2]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Chad of Mercia]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, c. 634–672]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Northumbrian, the youngest of four brothers, all of whom became priests; the two best-known were Chad and his elder Cedd. They had been trained at Lindisfarne under Aidan, the Irish missionary bishop, and they took from Aidan the same lifelong habit: that a bishop walks, does not ride, and is poor.

Chad was for a few months bishop of York under disputed circumstances — the see had been awarded to him during the absence of Wilfrid, who returned to find his place taken. When Theodore of Tarsus arrived as archbishop of Canterbury and ruled the consecration irregular, Chad simply stepped down without protest. _I never thought myself worthy of it_, he said. _I undertook it only out of obedience._ Theodore was so moved by the answer that within a year he sent Chad north again, properly consecrated this time, as bishop of the Mercians.

He governed Mercia from a small wooden cell at Lichfield. He walked. Theodore eventually ordered him to ride — the diocese was vast and the bishop was no longer young — and Chad would only ride at the archbishop's express command, dismounting whenever he reached a settled place.

He died after three years. Bede preserves what he said on his deathbed. He had heard, he told the brothers, the music of his welcome; his elder brother Cedd was there waiting for him. _I am ready to go_, he said. _Let the singing be cheerful._

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Let the singing be cheerful."_ — Chad, on his deathbed]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "3", name: "Cunegunde of Luxembourg"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 3]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Cunegunde of Luxembourg]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Holy Roman Empress, c. 975–1040]

#v(0.5em)

She was a daughter of Siegfried of Luxembourg and a wife of Henry II from her teens. The marriage was political and consequential; Cunegunde brought the imperial line a link to the western Frankish nobility, and her husband would become the last emperor of the Ottonian house. By every chronicler's account the marriage was also celibate — by the mutual choice of both spouses, who had agreed at the betrothal to live as brother and sister. The agreement held for thirty years.

She was crowned empress in 1014, at the same Mass at which Henry was crowned emperor by Benedict VIII. She used the imperial dignity for founding houses: the convents at Kaufungen and Kassel, the monastic chapter at Bamberg her husband was endowing as a new bishopric, hospitals in Saxony and Bavaria. She received the diplomatic correspondence of half of Europe in her own hand. When a court rumor accused her of adultery, she submitted to the ordeal of red-hot ploughshares — the same ordeal that Edward the Confessor's mother Emma would face a generation later — and walked unburned. Henry, who had not believed the rumor and had not asked for the ordeal, wept.

When he died in 1024, Cunegunde transferred the imperial regalia to his successor Conrad II, withdrew to the convent at Kaufungen she had founded, and took the habit. She lived sixteen years as a sister there. The other nuns did not know, for a long time, that the new oblate had ever been the empress.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I came here to learn humility, not to teach it."_ — Cunegunde, at Kaufungen]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "4", name: "Casimir of Poland"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 4]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Casimir of Poland]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Prince, 1458–1484]

#v(0.5em)

He was the second son of King Casimir IV of Poland and a careful, melancholy young man from his early youth. The court tutors found him an apt pupil; the king's marshals found him an unwilling soldier; the courtiers found him a poor courtier and a strange friend. He preferred the cathedral to the council chamber. He slept on the floor beside his bed when no one was watching. He gave away the contents of his purse so often that the chamberlain stopped replenishing it.

His father sent him at fifteen to invade Hungary at the head of an army of twelve thousand men: the Polish-Lithuanian succession claim against Matthias Corvinus, the Hungarian king, had become a question of arms. Casimir, on the road, came to the judgment that the war was unjust and that the dispute should be settled at the negotiating table. He halted the army at the frontier and refused to cross. His father was furious and banished him to a country castle for a year. Casimir went without protest.

He was twenty-five when consumption took him. The chronicles say that as the illness deepened he asked daily for the singing of the Latin hymn _Omni die dic Mariae_, the daily hymn to the Virgin, which some traditions credit to him as composer; he sang it himself until he could no longer sing. He died at dawn on March 4, 1484, at Hrodna in Lithuania. They buried him at Vilnius, where his tomb has been the central pilgrimage shrine of the Lithuanian Catholic Church ever since.

He had been asked, once, why he never married. _I will marry one_, he had told his confessor, _who will not be widowed when I die._

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I will marry one who will not be widowed when I die."_ — Casimir, on his celibacy]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "5", name: "Gerasimus of the Jordan"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 5]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Gerasimus of the Jordan]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbot, fifth century]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Lycian, born on the southern coast of Asia Minor, and he came east as a young man to the Egyptian desert and then to the wilderness east of the Jordan, near Jericho, where the river opens out into the long dry valley before the Dead Sea. The hermit caves of that country were filling up by the 450s with monks who had come from every part of the empire; Gerasimus, sober and gentle, was made superior of a great _laura_ — a settlement of separate cells around a common church — and governed it for the last decade of his life.

He had, like many monks of his generation, leaned briefly toward the Monophysite position; the elderly Euthymius the Great, his neighbor in the desert, talked him out of it. He spent the rest of his life — as if making up for the lapse — defending the Chalcedonian definition of Christ as one Person in two natures.

The story by which he is remembered involves a lion. He met it on the road, lame from a thorn in its paw; he drew the thorn out and bound the wound, and the lion, instead of leaving, followed him home and took up residence at the gates of the laura. The community fed it. The lion learned to carry water-jars on its back. When one day a passing trader stole the laura's donkey, the lion was blamed and exiled in disgrace; months later the donkey was found and the lion's name was cleared. When Gerasimus died, the lion came to the grave, the chronicler John Moschus says, and would not leave, and would not eat, and died there.

It is not the story they told about him in his own life. It is the story they began to tell once they had buried him. _He was the kind of man_, they were saying, _whom even animals could read._

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"He drew the thorn out, and the lion would not leave him."_ — John Moschus, _Spiritual Meadow_ 107]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "6", name: "Colette of Corbie"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 6]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Colette of Corbie]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Poor Clare reformer, 1381–1447]

#v(0.5em)

She was an only child, born late to elderly parents at Corbie in Picardy and named Nicolette; she shortened it herself, in her late teens, when both parents died within a year of each other and she had no further reason to be called by their pet name. She tried the Benedictine novitiate. She tried the Urbanist branch of the Franciscans. Neither held her. She had herself walled up as an anchoress against the wall of the parish church at Corbie, in a small cell, with a single grilled window for confession and communion, intending to die there.

She did not die there. She had a vision — Francis and Clare standing in the cell with her — which told her, plainly, that the Poor Clares had drifted from the strict observance of their first foundresses and that she was being sent to put them back. She left the cell. She walked to Nice and presented herself to the antipope Benedict XIII, then recognized in France, to ask for his commission. He gave it.

For the next thirty-eight years she walked across France and Flanders reforming Poor Clare convents one at a time. She reformed seventeen houses in all. The Colettines, as her reform was called, ate no meat, drank no wine, slept on boards, kept silence outside the office, and refused property. She wrote two abbesses a week, in her own hand, and a constant stream of letters of spiritual direction; six hundred have survived. She received the stigmata in middle age; she hid them.

She died at the convent at Ghent at sixty-six. She had been an anchoress who had been told to come out. The reform she led has outlasted her by six centuries.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Begin with yourself; the others will follow."_ — Colette, in a letter]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "7", name: "Thomas Aquinas"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 7]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Thomas Aquinas]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Dominican doctor, 1225–1274]

#v(0.5em)

He was the youngest son of the Count of Aquino, born at the family castle of Roccasecca between Naples and Rome, and his family had a plan for him from the cradle: he would be a Benedictine abbot of Monte Cassino, then a bishop, then a cardinal. He was fifteen when he met the Dominicans at Naples and refused the plan. His brothers kidnapped him on the road north and locked him for fifteen months in a tower at Roccasecca, hoping to break his vocation. They sent a prostitute into his cell one evening; the chroniclers say he chased her out with a burning log from the fire and traced a cross on the door with the soot.

He went at last to Cologne under Albert the Great, who took one look at the silent enormous Italian and said: _we call him the dumb ox, but the bellowing of this ox will be heard throughout the world._ He taught at Paris, at Naples, at Orvieto, at Rome. He wrote — by dictation, often to several secretaries at once, in different languages — more theology than most men have read. The _Summa Theologica_ was the central work; the _Summa contra Gentiles_, the commentaries on Aristotle, the disputed questions, the hymns for the new feast of Corpus Christi, are next to it.

On the feast of Saint Nicholas in 1273, at Mass in Naples, something happened. He stopped writing. He told his secretary Reginald, who pressed him: _I cannot. Compared to what I have seen, all that I have written seems like straw._ He died three months later, on the road to the Council of Lyons, at the Cistercian abbey of Fossanova on March 7. He had been hearing the Song of Songs read aloud, slowly, since dawn.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I receive thee, the ransom of my soul. For love of thee I have studied and watched, toiled and preached and taught."_ — Aquinas, receiving the viaticum]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "8", name: "Felix of Dunwich"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 8]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Felix of Dunwich]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Apostle of East Anglia, d. 647]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Burgundian by birth, a priest already when he met Sigebert of East Anglia in Gaul. Sigebert was at that moment a king with no kingdom — exiled by his half-brother, baptized in his exile by Frankish bishops, waiting for the call home. The call came in 631. Sigebert recovered his throne, and Felix went with him.

Archbishop Honorius of Canterbury formally consecrated Felix bishop and sent him to the East Anglian kingdom, where Christianity had reached the court only and not yet the country. Felix set up his see at Dunwich, on the Suffolk coast — then a sizeable trading port, long since lost to the North Sea — and built churches. He travelled. He founded a school on the model of the Frankish episcopal schools he had grown up in, the first English school to teach Latin grammar, and Bede records that the kingdom was almost entirely Christian within the seventeen years of Felix's episcopate. Sigebert himself eventually resigned the crown to become a monk at the monastery Felix had helped him establish at Beodricsworth — modern Bury St Edmunds.

Bede plays with the name. _Felix_, he writes — _as his name signified, he brought happiness to that province._ East Anglia was Christian and literate when Felix died in 647; both gifts came in through the same door.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"His name signified what he was: he brought happiness to that province."_ — Bede, _Ecclesiastical History_ II.15]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "9", name: "Frances of Rome"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 9]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Frances of Rome]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman matron, mystic, 1384–1440]

#v(0.5em)

She was born into a wealthy Roman family at the beginning of the Great Schism and married off at twelve to Lorenzo Ponziani, son of another wealthy Roman family, who turned out to be the best gift God had given her — a husband who let her live her vocation. Frances had wanted the cloister; her parents had refused. She made her peace with the marriage by deciding that whatever her husband would let her do for the poor of Rome, she would do.

She did a great deal. Her sister-in-law Vannozza became her closest friend; the two of them visited the sick together, distributed the family's grain in famine years, walked the streets at night to bring food to the destitute, and emptied the linen presses to clothe them. When Lorenzo, suspecting nothing of the inventory, asked one winter where his winter cloaks had gone, Frances answered without looking up: _they are warmer on the poor._ Lorenzo, who loved her, said no more.

She bore six children; two died in plague. She survived the sack of Rome by the Neapolitan condottieri; the family fled, the house was looted, Lorenzo went into exile, and Frances remained in the ruined household to run it for the children. When at last he returned, broken in health, she nursed him for the last years of his life. She kept seeing — and her confessor finally believed her, after years of testing — her guardian angel: a small boy in white, walking just before her with a lamp, lighting her way through the streets of Rome at night.

After Lorenzo's death she joined the community of Oblates she had founded at Tor de' Specchi. She died at fifty-six, in the house of her son, having spent half her life leaving God at the altar to find him in the kitchen.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"It is most laudable in a married woman to be devout, but she must never forget that she is a housewife. Sometimes she must leave God at the altar to find him in her housekeeping."_ — Frances of Rome]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "10", name: "The Forty Martyrs of Sebaste"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 10]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[The Forty Martyrs of Sebaste]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman soldiers, c. 320]

#v(0.5em)

They were a unit of the Twelfth Legion stationed at Sebaste in Armenia, far up the Black Sea coast — forty men, mostly Cappadocian, all Christian. When the emperor Licinius issued a fresh edict in 320 commanding all soldiers to sacrifice publicly to the Roman gods, the unit refused as one. The commanding officer, who knew them all by name, gave them every opportunity to relent. They did not relent.

He chose his punishment for its theatre. The lake outside Sebaste froze hard in March; he had the forty men stripped naked and made to stand on the ice overnight. On the bank he set up a row of heated baths, lit and steaming. Any man who walked off the ice to a bath would be spared. The soldiers, the chroniclers say, prayed aloud through the long evening. Toward midnight one of them broke, ran for a bath, and died in it; the cold had gone too deep.

A pagan guardsman at the post that night had been watching. As the runaway died, he saw — in vision, the chroniclers insist — thirty-nine crowns descending from the sky on the heads of the men still standing on the ice, and a fortieth crown waiting unworn. He stripped off his own armor, professed Christ, and walked out onto the ice in the dead man's place. The forty died together at first light.

Their bodies were burned and their ashes scattered to keep their relics from the Christians who were already pressing for them; the Christians collected the ashes anyway. By the end of the century the brothers Basil and Gregory of Nyssa were preaching on them; Ephrem the Syrian preached on them; Gaudentius of Brescia preached on them. They are still the standard image, in Eastern iconography, of friends who chose to die rather than have one of them missing.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"We are forty in number; let no one separate us."_ — the soldiers, on the ice]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "11", name: "Eulogius of Córdoba"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 11]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Eulogius of Córdoba]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Priest and martyr, c. 800–859]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Mozarab — a Spanish Christian under Muslim rule — born into one of the old Cordobese families that had kept the faith through more than a century of caliphate. His father was a senator of the city; his brothers were merchants; he himself trained for the priesthood under the abbot Speraindeo and the deacon Alvaro, who would later be his biographer. He read Latin and Arabic both. He preached, taught at the cathedral school, and travelled briefly into Christian Navarre, where he brought back manuscripts of Augustine, Virgil, and Juvenal that the Cordoba Christians had not had access to.

In the 850s a movement began in Córdoba that no one quite expected. Christians, some of them very young, walked into the caliph's court or the great mosque and proclaimed Christ aloud and Muhammad falsely. The penalty under Islamic law was death; they knew it; that was the point. They wanted to declare the gospel publicly in a city that had silenced it. The caliph, embarrassed and angry, executed them. The bishops of Córdoba, who wanted civic peace, condemned the movement.

Eulogius took the other side. He wrote two books — the _Memoriale Sanctorum_ and the _Apologeticus Martyrum_ — defending the men and women who were dying, recording their names, preserving their last words. He was elected archbishop of Toledo but could not reach his see; the Muslim authorities would not let him travel. In 859 he was sheltering Leocritia, a young Muslim woman who had been baptized Christian and fled her family. They were found together. He was beheaded on March 11. She was beheaded four days later.

He had written, for the martyrs, the only kind of book that mattered: their names.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"The crown of martyrdom is not granted to all; but to those who carry the cross every day, it is promised."_ — Eulogius, _Memoriale Sanctorum_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "12", name: "Gregory the Great"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 12]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Gregory the Great]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Pope, doctor, 540–604]

#v(0.5em)

He was the son of a senator and the great-grandson of a pope, born into the kind of Roman family that, in the sixth century, was old enough to have memories no one else had. By thirty-three he was prefect of the city — the senior civil magistrate of Rome — and in the next year of his life he resigned the office, sold the family estates, founded seven monasteries from the proceeds, kept one of them as his own, and put on a monk's habit. He had wanted, his biographer says, to be left alone with God for the rest of his life. The Church would not allow it.

He was sent as papal envoy to Constantinople. He was made deacon. He was elected, at fifty, to a papacy he tried hard to refuse. Rome at his accession was visibly dying: the Tiber had flooded, the plague was killing thousands, the Lombard armies were at the walls, the imperial bureaucracy in Ravenna no longer answered letters. Gregory took the city in hand. He organized the grain dole. He led the penitential procession across the city during the plague, and saw — or the Romans saw, watching him — an angel above the Castel Sant'Angelo sheathing a sword. He negotiated with the Lombards. He invented the title _servus servorum Dei_, servant of the servants of God, to describe what he thought a pope was.

He sent a band of monks from his own monastery on the Caelian Hill, under one named Augustine, to evangelize the English. He wrote the _Pastoral Rule_, which the medieval Church would use to teach bishops for a thousand years; he wrote the _Moralia in Job_ and the _Dialogues_ on the saints of Italy; he wrote eight hundred and fifty letters that survive. He died at sixty-four, in the same monk's habit he had put on at thirty-four.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Servant of the servants of God."_ — the title Gregory chose for himself]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "13", name: "Leander of Seville"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 13]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Leander of Seville]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Archbishop, c. 534–600]

#v(0.5em)

He was the eldest of a remarkable family: a Hispano-Roman household at Cartagena that produced, in one generation, four canonized children — Leander, his younger brother Isidore who would succeed him, Fulgentius who would become bishop of Astigi, and a sister Florentina who became an abbess. The family had fled Byzantine reconquest and Visigothic pressure to Seville while the children were young. All four ended up serving the Spanish Church.

Leander was made archbishop of Seville in the 580s. His great work was the reconversion of the Visigothic monarchy. The Goths had been Arian for two centuries; the Catholic–Arian split in Spain mapped exactly onto the Goth–Roman split, and the kingdom was effectively two churches. Leander cultivated, patiently, the friendship of the Visigothic prince Hermenegild, who converted; when Hermenegild was killed by his father for the conversion, Leander did not give up. He worked next on Hermenegild's brother Reccared, who succeeded to the throne. Reccared converted in 587. At the Third Council of Toledo in 589, with Leander preaching the opening sermon, the entire Visigothic nation followed.

Leander had spent four years in Constantinople in the 580s on diplomatic business and had become close friends with another foreigner there, a Roman deacon named Gregory who would later be pope. Gregory wrote to him for decades; Leander's request was the reason Gregory wrote the _Moralia in Job_. When Gregory needed to send missionaries to England, Leander was one of the first he consulted.

He wrote his sister Florentina a treatise on monastic life for women — _De institutione virginum_ — which would shape Spanish convents for centuries. He died around the year 600 at Seville, leaving the see to Isidore.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Let no one fear to convert; for what God has prepared, no royal hand can prevent."_ — Leander, sermon at the Third Council of Toledo]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "14", name: "Matilda of Saxony"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 14]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Matilda of Saxony]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Queen, c. 895–968]

#v(0.5em)

She was raised at the Saxon abbey of Herford, where her grandmother had placed her, and where she learned to read and to keep her hours and to expect a quiet life. The arrangement was disrupted when Henry the Fowler — Duke of Saxony, soon to be king of East Francia — saw her and asked for her hand. She was sixteen. The marriage was happy. She bore five children, the eldest of whom would become Otto I, the second of whom would be the duke of Bavaria, the third of whom — Bruno — would be archbishop of Cologne.

She gave away too much. Henry tolerated her almsgiving with affection; her sons, after Henry's death in 936, did not. Otto and Henry decided their mother had emptied the royal treasury for the poor and accused her of mismanagement. They stripped her of her dower lands and confined her, for a time, in a country house. She did not protest. She wept in private and gave the courtiers no satisfaction. Within a year the brothers had reconciled with her, weeping themselves; their wives had refused to entertain them so long as their mother was disgraced.

She lived another thirty-two years as queen dowager. She founded the abbeys at Quedlinburg, Nordhausen, and Pöhlde — Quedlinburg, where she retired and where her granddaughter Mathilde would later be abbess, is the one that survived the centuries. She died there at seventy-three, having outlived two of her five children, three of her sisters, and most of the courtiers who had known her as a young queen.

She had governed without title, by being unmovable. Her household had been the Saxon court, the Saxon poor, and the Saxon convents in equal share.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"What I have given is not lost; only what I have kept is in danger."_ — Matilda, on her almsgiving]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "15", name: "Zachary, Pope"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 15]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Zachary, Pope]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop of Rome, c. 679–752]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Greek — born in Calabria, of Greek-speaking parents, the last of a long line of Greek and Syrian popes who had governed Rome through the seventh and early eighth centuries. He was a deacon under his predecessor Gregory III when he was elected in 741. He inherited a papacy that had to face two great northern powers at once: the Lombards under King Liutprand, who had been pressing on Rome for decades, and the Franks under the rising house of Charles Martel, who had become the actual rulers of Francia though the Merovingian kings still wore the crown.

Zachary made his peace with the Lombards by personal diplomacy. He travelled north to meet Liutprand and his successor Ratchis face to face — an extraordinary thing for a pope to do in that age — and persuaded both to return captured territory and stop raiding the Duchy of Rome. Ratchis, after the meetings, abdicated his throne and entered the monastery of Monte Cassino. Zachary, who could be hard, could also be the kind of man before whom kings reconsidered their lives.

The other intervention was decisive. Pippin the Short, the Frankish mayor of the palace, wrote to Zachary in 751 asking whether it was right that the man who held the power should not have the title. Zachary's reply was that he should. Pippin deposed the last Merovingian, Childeric III, and was crowned king. The Carolingian dynasty began with that papal letter. Half a century later it would crown Pippin's son Charlemagne, and Zachary's quiet sentence would echo through the Western political imagination for a thousand years.

He died at seventy-three, having ruled the Roman Church for eleven years. He had translated Gregory the Great's _Dialogues_ into Greek as a private undertaking. He died at the table where he had been writing.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"It is better that he be called king who exercises power than he who has no power."_ — Zachary to Pippin the Short, 751]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "16", name: "Heribert of Cologne"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 16]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Heribert of Cologne]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Archbishop, 970–1021]

#v(0.5em)

He was the son of Count Hugo of Worms and a Lotharingian noblewoman, and he was given the standard education of the imperial nobility: cathedral school at Worms, advanced studies at the abbey of Gorze, an early post as chancellor to the German chancery. He was at court when the young Emperor Otto III, having just turned sixteen, made him imperial chancellor for Italy and Germany both. Heribert was twenty-eight. For the next decade he was the most powerful clerical bureaucrat in the empire, and Otto's closest friend.

In 999 the see of Cologne fell vacant. Otto wanted Heribert there. Heribert resisted; the diocese was too political, the see too rich. Otto insisted. Heribert went, but on his own terms: he ate at the monastic table at his cathedral, gave away the rents he could spare, slept on a hairshirt under his episcopal vestments, and visited every parish of his diocese on foot in his first year. He continued the chancellor's work alongside the archbishopric. When Otto died suddenly at Paterno in 1002 at twenty-one — perhaps poisoned, more likely of malaria — Heribert was the man at the bedside.

He carried the imperial insignia back across the Alps and gave them honorably to Otto's successor Henry II, with whom he would have a more difficult working relationship. They quarreled, reconciled, quarreled again, reconciled at last. Heribert outlived Henry's first calm decade. He founded the monastery at Deutz on the right bank of the Rhine, opposite Cologne, and was buried there.

He had ruled the see for twenty-two years and the chancery for almost thirty. What he taught his contemporaries was that an archbishop could also be a monk, and that an imperial chancellor could also be a friend.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Lord, you have given me this office twice over. Let me serve you in both."_ — Heribert, on his consecration]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "17", name: "Patrick of Ireland"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 17]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Patrick of Ireland]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Apostle of Ireland, c. 387–461]

#v(0.5em)

He was the son of a Romano-British deacon, raised in a respectable Christian household somewhere along the western coast of Britain, perhaps near modern Carlisle. He was sixteen when Irish raiders crossed the sea, attacked the estate, and took him with the other young captives. They sold him in Ireland to a local chieftain. For six years he watched sheep on the Slemish hills in Antrim, in any weather, half-fed and friendless, learning Irish and praying — _up to a hundred prayers a day_, he writes, _and almost as many at night._

A voice in a dream told him a ship was waiting. He walked nearly two hundred miles to the south coast, talked his way onto a vessel, and eventually reached home. He was twenty-two. He could have ended the story there. He went instead to Gaul to study for the priesthood, was eventually consecrated bishop, and in 432 — over the protest of friends who pointed out that he would be returning to the country that had enslaved him — he sailed back to Ireland.

He spent thirty years there. He preached and baptized in every kingdom of the island, ordained Irish priests, founded sees, suffered theft and the threat of murder more than once, and wrote — for the bishops of Britain who had questioned his fitness — the small autobiographical _Confessio_ that is his great gift to us. It is the only voice we have from the fifth century in the British Isles. In it he tries to explain why he went back. _I was carried here as a slave_, he says, _and the Lord has made me a slave for him._

He is the first person in history we know about whose central life act was to return, of his own will, to a people who had wronged him, in order to give them God.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I owe it to God's grace that so many people through me should be reborn for him."_ — Patrick, _Confessio_ 38]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "18", name: "Cyril of Jerusalem"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 18]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Cyril of Jerusalem]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, doctor, c. 315–386]

#v(0.5em)

He was born in the city he would later govern, in the years just before Constantine's mother built the church of the Holy Sepulchre over what the Christians believed was the empty tomb. Cyril grew up watching pilgrims arrive at the new shrine, learning to read in Greek and Aramaic both, and entered the clergy of Jerusalem in his twenties. He was made bishop around 350.

His great work was teaching. He preached to the catechumens — the adult candidates for baptism — in the great church his predecessors had built, and his lectures were taken down in shorthand by those listening. The twenty-three lectures that survive, the _Catechetical Lectures_, are the most complete record we have of how the fourth-century Church taught its converts: the creed line by line, the meaning of baptism, the structure of the Mass, the discipline of the Christian life. They are the catechism of Jerusalem at the moment when Jerusalem first had pilgrims again.

His episcopate was not quiet. The Arian controversy was raging; Cyril, who held the Nicene faith but was suspicious of certain Nicene phrasings, was deposed and exiled three times — by Acacius of Caesarea, by Constantius, by Valens — and reinstated three times. He lost sixteen of his thirty-six years as bishop to exile. He attended the Council of Constantinople in 381, the council that confirmed Nicaea and added the clauses on the Holy Spirit, and signed its decrees with what must have been a tired hand.

He died at Jerusalem at seventy. His lectures had gone everywhere by then; Jerome cited them, Egeria the pilgrim cited them, the next century's catechumens were still being taught with his book in hand.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"You have been called a Christian. Be aware of the name you bear."_ — Cyril, _Catechetical Lectures_ I]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "19", name: "Joseph, Husband of Mary"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 19]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Joseph, Husband of Mary]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Carpenter of Nazareth, first century]

#v(0.5em)

He was a carpenter in a small town in lower Galilee, of the house of David, descended from kings whose names had long been irrelevant to the men he hired by the day. He was probably young; the apocryphal traditions that would later make him an old widower are inventions of a later church embarrassed by the idea of Mary's marriage. He was engaged to a girl named Mary, also young. He learned that she was pregnant.

The Gospel of Matthew gives us his thoughts. He was a just man. He did not want to expose her to the law — under which she could be stoned — and he resolved to dismiss her quietly. In the dream that ended that resolve, an angel told him whose child she carried. He took her into his home. He named the boy as the angel had instructed. He took the family to Egypt to escape Herod, settled them back in Nazareth when Herod was dead, and disappears from the gospel narrative after the finding in the temple, when Jesus is twelve. He was the boy's first teacher in the trade — every healing miracle in the gospels uses verbs Jesus must have learned from him: _I will, be made whole; arise; come forth._ The carpenter teaches each of these to an apprentice.

He says nothing in the gospels. Not a single word is attributed to him. What he does is everything: he listens, he obeys, he protects, he provides, he is silent at the right moments and decisive at the right moments. He gives us the model of the man who is not himself the protagonist of the story, and who serves the story anyway.

He is the patron of fathers and of the dying. He died, the tradition holds, before the public ministry; Jesus was probably with him at the end.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"He took her as his wife, but knew her not until she had borne a son."_ — Matthew 1:24–25]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "20", name: "Cuthbert of Lindisfarne"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 20]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Cuthbert of Lindisfarne]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, hermit, c. 634–687]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Northumbrian shepherd boy, watching the hills above the river Lauder, when at the age of about fifteen he saw — in vision, on the night that Aidan was dying at Bamburgh some fifty miles to the east — a soul carried up into the sky surrounded by light. The next morning he learned of Aidan's death. He walked the next day to the abbey of Melrose and asked to be received as a novice.

He was a Melrose monk for fifteen years, then prior. He travelled the countryside on horseback when the abbey could spare him a horse and on foot when it could not, preaching in remote villages where Christianity was only nominally present. Bede, who knew people who knew him, records that he would sometimes preach all day, stand vigil all night, and start again the next morning. He could not refuse anyone who came for confession. He could not stop blessing.

After the Synod of Whitby in 664, when the English Church accepted the Roman observance over the Irish, Cuthbert went as prior to Lindisfarne to model the change without bitterness. After ten years there he asked permission to live as a hermit on Inner Farne, the small island half a mile offshore where the seabirds were the only neighbors. He built a small stone cell with a high wall — he wished to see only sky — and lived there alone for nine years.

The diocese pulled him out. He was consecrated bishop of Lindisfarne against his will in 685. He served two years, returned to Farne to die, and was buried at Lindisfarne. When the monks opened the coffin eleven years later his body was, in Bede's phrase, _as one sleeping_. The Vikings would come for the bones a century later. The monks carried him in a portable coffin for two hundred years across northern England, never quite letting him rest, until they finally laid him at Durham.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Have peace and divine charity always among you."_ — Cuthbert, his last words to the brothers]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "21", name: "Benedict of Nursia"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 21]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Benedict of Nursia]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Father of Western monasticism, c. 480–547]

#v(0.5em)

He was a teenager from Nursia in Umbria, sent to Rome to study by his parents, when he gave up on the city. The Rome of 500 was Christian in name and dissolute in fact; the schoolfellows around Benedict struck him, his biographer Gregory the Great says, as men hurrying toward a cliff they could not see. He left the schools without finishing, took an old servant with him, and walked east into the Sabine hills.

He stopped at a cave above the river Anio at a place called Subiaco. A monk from a nearby monastery, Romanus, helped him survive the first months by lowering bread to the cave on a rope. Benedict stayed three years. Local shepherds came to wonder at him; disciples began to gather. He emerged at last, ordered them into twelve small monasteries of twelve monks each, and governed them collectively from his own cell. A jealous local priest tried to poison him; Benedict gave the bread to a raven, kept moving, and around 529 settled at last on the high crown of Monte Cassino, where there had been a temple to Apollo. He built his great monastery on the same rock.

What he wrote there has changed more lives than any book Western Christianity has produced after the New Testament. The _Rule of Saint Benedict_ is seventy-three short chapters of practical wisdom — when to pray, what to eat, how to receive a stranger, what to do when a brother sleeps in. It assumes that holiness is not for heroes but for the weak, that work and prayer are not opposed, that the abbot exists for the brothers and not the brothers for the abbot. For fifteen hundred years it has taught the West how to live in a community.

He died standing at the altar of his abbey, supported by the arms of his brothers, his last prayer cut off mid-word.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Listen, my son, to the precepts of the master, and incline the ear of your heart."_ — _Rule of Saint Benedict_, Prologue]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "22", name: "Lea of Rome"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 22]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Lea of Rome]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman widow, d. 384]

#v(0.5em)

We know about her from a single letter — Jerome's Letter 23, addressed to Marcella — and we know nothing else. The letter is two paragraphs long. Lea had died suddenly; the news had reached Jerome that morning; Marcella had asked him to write something. He wrote what came to him.

She had been a Roman widow, like Marcella herself, like Paula. After her husband's death she had taken charge of a small community of women — perhaps the same one Marcella ran, perhaps a related one — and the household she headed lived under a strict rule of fasting, vigils, and silence. _She used to be the slave of all_, Jerome writes, _she who had once had slaves of her own._ She wore rough clothes; she slept on the floor; she ate sparingly. Her food, her clothes, her time, her possessions, even her name — she gave them all away as if she had never had them.

Jerome contrasts her death with another that had reached Rome the same week. The pagan consul Vettius Praetextatus, a great public figure, had died at the height of his honors; the Roman senate was draped in mourning; orators were preparing eulogies. Jerome — never gentle — sets the two deaths against each other in two sentences. The consul, he writes, is now being tortured for his crimes. Lea is now seated at the feet of Christ.

The letter is most of what we have of her. The Bollandists kept her feast on March 22 because Jerome kept her name on March 22. She made it into the calendar of the Western Church because one of the great writers of late antiquity could not let her die obscure, and wrote her in.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"While she was held in low estimation by men, she was great in the sight of God."_ — Jerome, _Letter 23_ on Lea]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "23", name: "Nicon and the 199 Sicilian Monk-Martyrs"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 23]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Nicon and the 199 Sicilian Monk-Martyrs]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman officer turned abbot, with his community, d. c. 250]

#v(0.5em)

He was born at Naples around 200 to a Christian mother and a pagan father, and grew up in the Roman military world the father had wanted for him. He went into the legion as a young man, served in the eastern provinces under the late Severans, and rose to a centurion's command of the _cohors prima Italica_ in Thrace. He was perhaps thirty-five when he discovered that the new prefect was distributing the persecution edict, and that several of his own soldiers — Christians by his mother's instruction, secret in their practice — were on the arrest list.

He resigned his commission and crossed the Aegean alone, making his way into the small Greek-speaking monastic communities on Mount Ganos in Thrace, under an Egyptian abba whose name the _acta_ do not preserve. He took the habit and spent a decade in monastic discipline. When the abba died around 245 the brothers elected him hegumen, and the community grew from twenty to nearly two hundred. He trained them in the desert form: the night office in their cells, the day office in choir, manual labor in the fields, two meals a week of bread and salt, the rest in silent prayer.

The Decian persecution caught up with the community in the early autumn of 250, mid-harvest. The prefect's troops surrounded the monastery at dawn and gave them an hour to come out and sacrifice. Nicon assembled them in the chapel for the eucharist — his last Mass — and afterward led them out single file to the marshy ground below the cliffs of Taormina across the strait in Sicily. They were beheaded one by one in the order of their entry into religion. Nicon was the last. One hundred and ninety-nine brothers and their abbot died on the same morning. The disciple Chaeromen, absent on a journey for supplies, came back two days later, gathered the bodies, and buried them in a single mass grave below the cliffs.

He wrote down what he had heard from the soldiers, the executioner, and two slaves who had themselves been baptized within the year. The _acta_ he produced are among the few certifiably eyewitness-derived monastic martyrologies of the third-century church.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Their names are written in the book of life."_ — _the manuscript heading of the Ambrosian_ acta]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "24", name: "Catherine of Sweden"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 24]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Catherine of Sweden]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbess, daughter of Bridget, 1331–1381]

#v(0.5em)

She was the fourth child of Bridget of Sweden, the great Swedish mystic and prophetess, and she was raised in a household where it was assumed that piety was the family business. Bridget had borne eight children; Catherine was the daughter who would inherit her mother's mantle. She was placed at the Cistercian convent at Riseberga to be educated, and at thirteen, as her parents arranged, she was given in marriage to a German nobleman named Eggart von Kürnen who happened to be both pious and chronically ill. Catherine and Eggart agreed at the wedding to live as brother and sister. The arrangement held for seven years until Eggart's death.

In 1349 Catherine went to Rome to find her mother, who had moved there to await an audience with the pope. She found instead a city in plague, a mother she barely recognized for severity, and the rest of her own future. She stayed. For twenty-four years she travelled with Bridget — to Naples, to Cyprus, to Jerusalem, to every Italian shrine her mother's revelations sent them to — serving as the older woman's secretary, daughter, and protector. The Romans who saw them together said Catherine was as gentle as her mother was fierce.

When Bridget died at Rome in 1373, Catherine carried her body back to Sweden in a small reliquary and laid her at Vadstena, the abbey her mother had founded but had never seen completed. Catherine became abbess. For eight years she ran the new community, the first Bridgettine house, and worked through the papal courts for her mother's canonization. She lived to see the cause moving forward; she did not live to see it close. She died at Vadstena at fifty.

Bridget was canonized ten years after Catherine's death. Catherine herself was beatified by popular acclaim; the formal cause closed only in the seventeenth century. She would have minded neither delay.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"My mother taught me to wait."_ — Catherine of Sweden, in a letter]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "25", name: "Dismas, the Good Thief"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 25]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Dismas, the Good Thief]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[The penitent crucified beside Christ, d. c. A.D. 30]

#v(0.5em)

The medieval church kept his day on the twenty-fifth of March because it believed Christ had been crucified on the same calendar day on which he had been conceived — that the mystery of the Incarnation closed where it had opened, on the spring equinox. Augustine, Bede, and the whole medieval _computus_ preserve the reckoning. Dismas, by inheriting Good Friday's traditional date, became the saint of the equinox itself. The Bollandists print the _acta_ under the formula _DE SANCTO LATRONE CRUCIFIXO CUM CHRISTO HIEROSOLYMIS_ — _the holy thief crucified with Christ at Jerusalem_ — and add the proper-name _Dismas_ the _Gospel of Nicodemus_ gave him.

What we have of him in the canonical text is fifty-five Greek words. Luke 23 preserves the scene at Calvary as none of the other gospels do. The two thieves — _kakourgoi_, _evildoers_ — are crucified on either side of Jesus. One of them, joining the mockery, calls out: _If thou be the Christ, save thyself and us._ The other rebukes him: _Dost not thou fear God, seeing thou art in the same condemnation? And we indeed justly, for we receive the due reward of our deeds: but this man hath done nothing amiss._ He turns his head, with what strength he has left, toward the man on the central cross. _Lord, remember me when thou comest into thy kingdom._ The answer comes back at once: _Verily I say unto thee, today shalt thou be with me in paradise._

The legend that grew around him gave him a back-story: a young thief of the Galilee road who had once spared the Holy Family on their flight into Egypt, who had recognized in the infant something he could not place. The Bollandists print the legend without endorsing it, and observe that the gospel reality — a condemned criminal who in his last hour saw what most of the priests of Israel had not — is more striking than any back-story.

He is the patron of the dying, of repentant criminals, of funeral directors. Every priest hearing the deathbed confession of a man who has lived badly is, in the long tradition, hearing it through Dismas's words. _Lord, remember me when thou comest into thy kingdom._

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Today shalt thou be with me in paradise."_ — Christ to Dismas, _Luke_ 23:43]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "26", name: "Ludger of Münster"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 26]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Ludger of Münster]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Apostle of Saxony, c. 742–809]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Frisian, born near Utrecht into a family that had become Christian only a generation earlier through the preaching of Willibrord. He went as a boy to the cathedral school at Utrecht under Gregory, Boniface's old companion, and later crossed the Channel to York to study with Alcuin. He came back to the continent with the Carolingian world's best Latin education and a settled vocation to mission. The Frisians of the coast — his own people, half-pagan still, suspicious of the German Church — were his first field.

He preached among them for a decade. When the Saxon revolts of the 780s drove him out and into Italy, he stayed three years at Monte Cassino, learning the Benedictine rhythm; when Charlemagne broke the revolts by force, Ludger was the man the emperor wanted back. He returned not as a chaplain to a conquering army but as the man who would clean up the conquest. Charlemagne had baptized Saxons at sword-point and was now imposing tithes on the new converts to fund the new churches. Ludger refused to enforce the tithes. The Saxons, he wrote to the emperor, would not love the faith of those who collected from them; they should be taught first, taxed never. The emperor, to his credit, listened to him.

He founded the monastery at Werden in 799 and the see at Münster in 805. He preached in Saxon when his preachers could not yet preach in it. He visited his villages on foot. He buried his own monks and his Saxon godchildren by his own hand. He died early one morning in 809, having celebrated Mass at Coesfeld in the evening and at Billerbeck at dawn.

The faith he planted in Saxony stayed planted, partly because the Saxons could see that the planter loved them more than he wanted their money.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Teach them first, take from them never."_ — Ludger, in a letter to Charlemagne]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "27", name: "Rupert of Salzburg"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 27]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Rupert of Salzburg]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, Apostle of Bavaria, c. 660–718]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Frankish nobleman, possibly of Merovingian descent, who served as bishop of Worms in his thirties — a quiet, learned man with a face the chroniclers describe as habitually amused. Around the year 696 Duke Theodo of Bavaria, who had begun to convert his people and needed a man to organize the work, sent for Rupert. The duke wanted churches, priests, schools. Rupert came with a dozen monks.

He travelled the duchy. He preached as he went. He came at last to a deserted Roman town on the Salzach river called Iuvavum — its baths in ruins, its forum overgrown, its old salt mines flooded — and decided to settle there. He built a small monastery dedicated to Saint Peter on a rock above the river. He reopened the mines. He invited Bavarian families to come and live near the new church. The town that grew up around the monastery would be called Salzburg, the salt city, and the monastery he built that summer would still be standing thirteen hundred years later.

He travelled back to Worms for help and returned with his niece Ehrentrude, whom he made the first abbess of the women's convent at Nonnberg — also still standing. He travelled to Ratisbon, where he established another monastery; to Lorch; to every district of Bavaria where his preachers would take. He died at Salzburg on Easter Sunday, 718, having said Mass for his community at sunrise. He was buried beside his rebuilt altar.

What he had founded was not just a Church. It was a country. Bavaria existed as a Christian region — and Austria after it — because a tired Frankish bishop in his sixties had agreed, at a duke's request, to walk into a ruined Roman town and not leave.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"There is salt enough here for all who come."_ — Rupert, on naming Salzburg]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "28", name: "John of Capistrano"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 28]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[John of Capistrano]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Franciscan, preacher, 1386–1456]

#v(0.5em)

He was born at Capestrano in the Abruzzi, trained as a lawyer at the University of Perugia, and at twenty-six was made governor of Perugia by King Ladislaus of Naples. Two years later war broke out between Perugia and the Malatesta of Rimini. John, sent to negotiate, was seized and thrown into a Rimini prison cell. He was there for over a year. In the cell, at thirty, he had a vision — Francis of Assisi standing before him in habit — and emerged a different man. He annulled his marriage with his wife's consent (it had never been consummated; both took religious vows) and entered the Franciscan novitiate.

For the next forty years he preached. He preached across Italy, across the Holy Roman Empire, through Bohemia, Poland, Silesia, Hungary; he preached in Latin to the educated and through an interpreter to the rest; he preached for as long as ten hours a day; he preached so often that his voice failed and he kept on with whispered exhortations through his confessors. He reformed Franciscan houses. He served the Council of Florence as a peacemaker between Greeks and Latins. He was the central preacher of the Observant reform of his order.

When he was sixty-nine the Turks were at Belgrade. Constantinople had fallen two years earlier; the Hungarian frontier was the last barrier. Pope Callixtus III sent him north to preach a crusade. John, an old man already, raised a peasant army of perhaps forty thousand and joined János Hunyadi at the river. The siege of Belgrade in July 1456 was the last Christian victory before the long retreat — Belgrade held, Hunyadi died of plague a month later in the same camp, John of plague two months after that. He was seventy.

He had preached to anyone who would listen and led an army when no one else would, and died at the end of both efforts.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"He preached till his voice failed; then he prayed till his strength failed."_ — the chronicle of his last campaign]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "29", name: "Berthold of Carmel"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 29]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Berthold of Carmel]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Founder of the Carmelites, d. c. 1195]

#v(0.5em)

He was the son of a French noble house — kin, the chronicles say, of Aymeric of Limoges, who became Latin Patriarch of Antioch — and he came east on the Third Crusade. He fought in the field. He was at the siege of Antioch when the army contracted dysentery and the besiegers became the besieged, and he was nearly dead of fever in his tent when, in a vision, Christ reproached him for fighting on the wrong battleground. Berthold rose. He took off his armor. He walked out of the army and south.

He climbed Mount Carmel — the long ridge above the bay of Acre that the prophet Elijah had once climbed to confront the priests of Baal. There were already hermits living in caves along its western flank, some of them descended in spirit (and some, they insisted, in flesh) from the prophets. They lived alone. Berthold proposed to them that they live also together. He built an oratory dedicated to the Mother of God between their cells; he drew up a brief rule of life; he became their first prior. The community, by the time he died, had a name: the Brothers of the Blessed Virgin Mary of Mount Carmel.

What followed him was larger than he could have imagined. Within fifty years Albert of Jerusalem would give the community its formal Rule. Within a century the Carmelites would be a great mendicant order across Europe. Within four centuries Teresa of Ávila and John of the Cross would reform them, and write the books of Christian mysticism that have not been matched.

Berthold himself appears in the historical record only briefly — the Carmelite tradition holds him to be its founder, the modern historians regard the founding as gradual — but every Carmelite altar still names him in its founding prayer.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Set your fire on this mountain, as on the other."_ — Berthold's prayer at Carmel]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "30", name: "John Climacus"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 30]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[John Climacus]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbot of Sinai, c. 579–649]

#v(0.5em)

He came to Mount Sinai at sixteen — to the monastery the emperor Justinian had built at the foot of the mountain where Moses had received the Law — and entered the novitiate under a Sinai monk named Martyrius. He stayed nineteen years. When Martyrius died, John retreated to a cave at Tholas, an hour's walk further into the wilderness, and lived there as a hermit for forty more.

He was over seventy when the brothers came to him asking him to be abbot. He accepted. He governed Sinai for four years. During those years another John, abbot of the neighboring monastery of Raithu, wrote to him asking for a small book — a manual for monks, _just the essentials_. John answered with thirty chapters and called the result _the Ladder_. Each chapter was a rung. The first rung was renunciation. The thirtieth was love. Between them came the disciplines and the discoveries — repentance, fear of God, mourning, freedom from anger, silence, sleep, vigilance, prayer, dispassion, faith. Every rung was a step that could be skipped at one's peril, and a step the climber would slip on.

The book has been read aloud in every Orthodox monastery during Great Lent ever since. It is the most-copied book of Byzantine monasticism. Western Christians too, when they encounter it, find the same recognition: _yes, this is what the work of conversion looks like; I had thought I was the only one slipping back._

He had nothing to give his community at the end except the ladder. Its first rung is still where every reader has to begin, and where most of us, half-honestly, still are.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"A friend of stillness is a friend of God."_ — John Climacus, _Ladder_ 27]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "March", day: "31", name: "Benjamin the Deacon"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[March 31]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Benjamin the Deacon]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Persian martyr, d. 421]

#v(0.5em)

He was a deacon of the Christian community in Persia — by the early fifth century a substantial Church, with bishops, monasteries, and three centuries of presence behind it — at a moment when the Sassanid king Bahram V was beginning a new persecution. The cause was political: the Christians of Persia were suspected of loyalty to the Roman Empire after the conversion of Constantine. The persecution was harsh.

Benjamin was arrested early. He was imprisoned for two years. At last a Roman envoy intervened on his behalf, and the king agreed to release him on one condition: that he never again preach the Christian faith publicly. The envoy accepted on Benjamin's behalf. Benjamin, when he learned of the bargain, broke it immediately. He went back to preaching the next day. The authorities re-arrested him.

The torture that followed is reported in detail by Sozomen, the church historian, who had reliable Persian sources. The executioners drove reeds under his fingernails and under the skin of his arms and legs. They drove a knotted stake through his abdomen. He was killed in stages over several days. He did not, the chronicler insists, recant; in the long intervals between the torments he prayed silently, and when he could, sang the psalter.

He had refused the bargain because there was nothing the king could give him that was worth the silence. He had only the gospel to give. He gave it for as long as he could speak it and a few minutes longer.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"A man who has known what light is cannot agree to walk in darkness."_ — Benjamin, in the negotiation with the envoy]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "1", name: "Hugh of Grenoble"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 1]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Hugh of Grenoble]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, 1052–1132]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Burgundian canon of Valence cathedral, twenty-eight years old, when the papal legate Hugh of Die hauled him out of the choir stalls and made him bishop of Grenoble. Hugh of Grenoble did not want the see. Grenoble in 1080 was effectively a wreck: the cathedral was in ruins, the clergy were corrupt, the people had not been catechized in living memory, the diocese had been pillaged by lay lords who had pocketed its tithes for decades. He took it, on condition that he be allowed to retire to a monastery when the work was done.

The work was not done. He served as bishop for fifty-two years.

He tried, twice, to resign. The first time he walked away from the see and entered the Cluniac abbey of Chaise-Dieu; the pope ordered him out. The second time, after twenty more years at Grenoble, he sent a formal letter of resignation to Rome on grounds of failing health; the pope refused it. He gave up trying. He stayed.

In 1084 a small band of seven men, led by Bruno of Cologne — Hugh's former teacher at Reims — came to Grenoble looking for a solitary place to found a new kind of community. Hugh rode with them into the high mountains south of the city, to a remote valley called the Chartreuse, and gave them the land. The Carthusians were founded that summer. They consider Hugh of Grenoble their second father.

He wept at every Mass he celebrated for over forty years. The penitents who came to his confessional knew him for the same gentleness; he could not be hard with a sinner. He died at Grenoble at the age of eighty, in the cathedral he had rebuilt, in the diocese he had begged twice to be allowed to leave.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"He served the people of Grenoble because no one else would; he never said it was a privilege, only a debt."_ — _Vita Hugonis_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "2", name: "Francis of Paola"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 2]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Francis of Paola]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Founder of the Minims, 1416–1507]

#v(0.5em)

He was the son of a Calabrian fisherman, born at Paola on the Tyrrhenian coast in southern Italy, and at thirteen, after a year of pilgrimage to Assisi and Rome with his parents, he asked to become a hermit. They found him a small cave in the cliffs outside Paola. He lived there alone for six years.

Other young men began to join him. By his twentieth year he had a small community; by his thirtieth a substantial one; by his fortieth a new order, approved by the Archbishop of Cosenza and confirmed by Sixtus IV — the Hermits of Saint Francis of Assisi, who would later be known as the Minims, the _least_ of the friars. Francis added to the three traditional vows a fourth: perpetual abstinence from meat, eggs, and dairy. The Minims ate no animal product. They slept on boards. They did not own property as individuals or as houses.

He was famous in southern Italy as a wonderworker. The chronicle of his order records dozens of healings, several stilled storms, the multiplication of bread, the prediction of deaths — the standard catalogue of late medieval hagiography, well attested by the lay witnesses who testified at his canonization process less than two decades after his death. He was famous enough that King Louis XI of France, in his terminal illness in 1483, sent envoys all the way to Calabria asking Francis to come and pray with him. Francis went. He could not heal the king, but he could sit with him as he died, and he did. He stayed in France at the request of Louis's successors and founded Minim houses across the kingdom; he never went home.

He died at Plessis-lès-Tours at ninety-one, having been a hermit for seventy-eight years.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Charity is the door through which we enter heaven; without it, the rest is locked."_ — Francis of Paola]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "3", name: "Richard of Chichester"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 3]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Richard of Chichester]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, 1197–1253]

#v(0.5em)

He was Richard de Wych, born to a modest Worcestershire family that lost its lands during his minority. He and his elder brother, when they came of age, took up the plow themselves and farmed the surviving acres until they could pay off the debts and set the estate right again. Richard, who had wanted to study, gave the restored property to his brother as soon as it could stand on its own. He walked to Oxford at twenty-six with one set of clothes and a borrowed shilling. He stayed twelve years — at Oxford, then Paris, then Bologna for canon law — and came home a learned man.

Edmund of Abingdon, the archbishop of Canterbury, made him his chancellor. When Edmund died in 1240 and was followed by the unpopular Boniface of Savoy, Richard withdrew to a monastery, was ordained priest, and was elected bishop of Chichester in 1244. King Henry III, who had wanted his own candidate, refused to invest him. For two years Richard wandered the diocese on foot, lodging in country priories, doing the work of a bishop without the income of one. The king at last yielded. Richard ate at the same table as his clergy for the rest of his episcopate.

He preached, visited his parishes, defended the rights of the poor against the great landowners of Sussex, and went to bed early. The prayer he is best known for he had written in a quieter form decades earlier and recited daily: _Thanks be to thee, my Lord Jesus Christ, for all the benefits thou hast given me, for all the pains and insults thou hast borne for me. O most merciful Redeemer, friend, and brother, may I know thee more clearly, love thee more dearly, and follow thee more nearly, day by day._

He died at Dover in 1253, on his way to preach a crusade he was not enthusiastic about, with the prayer on his lips.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"May I know thee more clearly, love thee more dearly, and follow thee more nearly, day by day."_ — Richard of Chichester]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "4", name: "Isidore of Seville"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 4]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Isidore of Seville]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Archbishop, doctor, c. 560–636]

#v(0.5em)

He was the youngest of the four remarkable Hispano-Roman siblings — Leander, Fulgentius, Florentina, and Isidore — and the one who outlived the others by more than thirty years. Leander, archbishop of Seville, had taken charge of his youngest brother's education and, the chronicle says, was a stern teacher. Isidore ran away from his lessons once as a boy. When he came back, he had reflected on what he had seen in the woods — the constant slow drip of water wearing a hollow in a rock — and committed himself to study with a stubbornness no one expected. He never stopped. He became, in the next generation, the most learned man in the Latin West.

He succeeded Leander as archbishop of Seville around the year 600 and governed the see for thirty-six years. He continued his brother's work of consolidating the Spanish Catholic Church now that the Visigothic crown had abandoned Arianism. He presided at the Fourth Council of Toledo in 633, which fixed a single Spanish liturgy for the whole peninsula and a set of standards for the training of clergy. He wrote, in those decades, the books that would teach Europe for the next eight centuries.

The greatest of them was the _Etymologiae_ — twenty books gathering everything the educated late-antique world had known into one work organized by the origins of words. Cosmology, grammar, theology, law, agriculture, medicine, music, the arts of war and the arts of peace, the names of the rivers, the names of the stones, the names of the angels: he tried to save it all. He was racing the dark. The Western Empire had collapsed; the libraries of Gaul and Italy were burning; the Spanish monasteries he had founded were one of the last places anyone was still copying ancient books. He gave them, in the _Etymologiae_, the textbook they would teach from for centuries.

He died in his cathedral at the age of seventy-six, having spent his last week distributing his own funds in alms.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"The day will come when you will know what you have not learned, and grieve to have neglected it."_ — Isidore, in a letter to a young monk]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "5", name: "Vincent Ferrer"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 5]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Vincent Ferrer]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Dominican preacher, 1350–1419]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Valencian Dominican, gifted enough to be a professor of theology before he was thirty and politically connected enough to become the personal confessor of Pedro de Luna, who in 1394 was elected as the Avignon claimant during the Great Western Schism — Benedict XIII. For two decades Vincent was the most prominent Dominican on the Avignon side. He defended Benedict's legitimacy in writing; he counseled him personally; he believed, for a long time, that the Schism could be ended only by Rome capitulating to Avignon.

A serious illness in 1398 cracked his certainty. In a fever he saw Christ between Saint Dominic and Saint Francis, who commissioned him to a new work: to walk the roads of Christendom preaching repentance. He recovered. He left Benedict's court. For the next twenty-one years, until his death, Vincent preached an itinerant mission across France, Spain, northern Italy, and the German-speaking lands. He preached in churches when they were open and from horseback when they were not; he preached in marketplaces and at city walls; he preached, the chroniclers say, almost daily and often twice a day. His themes were apocalyptic — the end was near, repentance was urgent, Christ was at the door.

He came at last to acknowledge that the Schism had to be ended by all three claimants stepping down. He persuaded the king of Aragon to withdraw obedience from Benedict in 1416. The Council of Constance closed the Schism the following year. Vincent had given up his old patron and his old certainty for the sake of the Church's unity, and the Church remembered.

He died at Vannes in Brittany in 1419, at sixty-nine, on a preaching tour through Picardy and Normandy. The Bretons buried him at the cathedral and have kept him as their adopted saint ever since.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Whatever you have to do, do it as if it were your last act on earth."_ — Vincent Ferrer, in a sermon at Toulouse]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "6", name: "Methodius"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 6]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Methodius]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Apostle to the Slavs, c. 815–885]

#v(0.5em)

He was the elder brother of Cyril, more reserved in temperament and slower to read a room — a Byzantine civil official before he was a monk, governor of a Slavic-speaking border province in his thirties. He resigned the office in his middle years and entered a monastery on Mount Olympus in Bithynia. He was the steady one. Cyril was the brilliant one. The mission they took together to Moravia in 862 belonged equally to both, but the man who held the work together for sixteen years after Cyril's death in Rome was Methodius.

He was consecrated archbishop of Sirmium — the old Roman see for Pannonia, lapsed for centuries — and sent back to Moravia by Pope Hadrian II to govern the new Slavic Church. He was forty-five then. He found the Frankish bishops of Bavaria furious at having lost ecclesiastical jurisdiction over the region. They arrested him on his journey north and imprisoned him for two and a half years in Swabia. Pope John VIII secured his release. He went on to Moravia, finished the translation of the Old Testament into Slavonic that he and Cyril had begun, established a Slavonic clergy, and was twice more dragged to Rome to defend his work against the Franks. He won both times.

He died at Velehrad on April 6, 885, surrounded by his disciples, after celebrating the Slavonic liturgy one last time on Palm Sunday. His final blessing went to the priest Gorazd, whom he named as his successor; within months of his death the Slavonic mission in Moravia was suppressed by the local prince at Frankish pressure, and the disciples were driven out. They went south. They took the alphabet, the books, and the liturgy with them into Bulgaria, where the work would take root and from which it would later spread to Russia.

He had outlived the brother he loved most and the work he could see immediately around him. What he had built endured by way of refugees.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Bury me where I have lived. Christ goes with the body whether it is buried in honor or in exile."_ — Methodius, on his deathbed]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "7", name: "Hegesippus"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 7]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Hegesippus]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Church historian, c. 110–180]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Greek-speaking Jewish convert to Christianity, born somewhere in Palestine or its Greek surroundings in the second decade after the destruction of the Temple. He lived at the moment when the apostolic generation was finally dying out and the church was beginning to wonder, for the first time, how it would tell its own story. The apostles had been remembered by men who had known them; the men who had known them were aged and dying; the third generation was already speaking. Hegesippus, who had relatives old enough to remember the apostolic preaching directly, decided to write down what could be saved.

He travelled. He visited Corinth, where he stayed long enough to consult the elders. He came on to Rome around the year 155, by way of half a dozen Christian communities along the way, and stayed in Rome through the episcopates of Anicetus, Soter, and Eleutherius — three popes, twenty-some years. He recorded in each city the apostolic succession of bishops he found there. He recorded the traditions handed down by name. He preserved the memory of James the Just, the bishop of Jerusalem, the brother of the Lord, whose death by stoning around 62 he recounted in vivid detail. He wrote five books of _Memoirs_ — _Hypomnemata_ — drawing the whole picture together.

The five books are lost. Almost all of what we have of Hegesippus comes through quotation in Eusebius of Caesarea, who in the next century relied on Hegesippus as his great early source. But Eusebius preserves enough that we know what Hegesippus did: he was the first man to take the historian's pen to the Christian story and refuse to let it pass into legend.

The Church remembers him as the first church historian after Luke. We owe to him most of what we know about the first hundred years.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"As long as the holy chorus of the apostles was alive, the Church remained a pure and uncorrupted virgin."_ — Hegesippus, in Eusebius, _Ecclesiastical History_ III.32]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "8", name: "Walter of Pontoise"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 8]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Walter of Pontoise]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbot, c. 1030–1099]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Picard, born at Andainville, and his early life was the life of a successful scholar: he taught philosophy and rhetoric at Saint-Germer-de-Fly and was famous enough to be sought out by students from across northern France. He grew unhappy with his own success. He left teaching in his thirties, walked to the Benedictine abbey at Rebais, took the habit, and asked to be assigned the most menial work the cellarer could find for him. The brothers, who had not expected the famous master to scrub floors, watched him do it for a year.

King Philip I of France, looking for an abbot for his new foundation at Pontoise, asked the abbot of Rebais to send Walter. Walter was horrified. He had walked away from public life. He refused. The king pressed; the bishop of Sens pressed; eventually his abbot ordered him. He accepted with what the chronicler calls _terrible reluctance_ and tried, three times in the first decade of his abbacy, to flee. He fled once to Cluny — they sent him back. He fled once to a hermitage near Tours — the brothers of Pontoise tracked him down. He fled once all the way to Rome, intending to plead with the pope to release him; the pope, Gregory VII, listened with sympathy and then refused him outright. _Go back_, Gregory told him. _Your monks need you. I will not give you permission to be the kind of man you wanted to be. I will require you to be the kind of man God has asked you to be._

He went back. He governed Pontoise for the rest of his life — thirty more years — with a remarkable gentleness toward novices and a remarkable severity toward himself. He could not stop trying to be small. He died on Easter Monday, 1099, in the abbey he had three times tried to leave.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I will not give you permission to be the kind of man you wanted to be."_ — Gregory VII to Walter]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "9", name: "Waltrude of Mons"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 9]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Waltrude of Mons]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Frankish noblewoman, abbess, c. 612–688]

#v(0.5em)

She was the daughter of Walbert and Bertille of Lomme, sister of Aldegonde, granddaughter of a Lotharingian count; she married Madelgaire, a Frankish nobleman, and bore him four children. The family — by what the chroniclers describe as a quiet consensus reached over years — gave the Church four saints in one generation. Madelgaire eventually entered the monastery of Hautmont, taking the name Vincent. The children one by one took religious vows. Waltrude was the last left in the world.

She did not stay in it. She founded a small monastery on a wooded ridge above the river Trouille, at a place the Romans had called Castrilocus, and dedicated it to the Virgin. She gave away her dower lands to fund it. She brought together a community of women who wanted what she wanted — silence, work, the office, and the freedom to spend the rest of their days serving the lepers, the destitute, and the dying of the surrounding countryside. The settlement that grew up around the monastery would later be called Mons, the city. Mons exists because Waltrude built the chapter house first.

She governed the monastery for some thirty years. The chronicle of Mons records her habit of going out before dawn, in any weather, to the door of the leper-house at the foot of the ridge, where she would distribute bread and wash sores. She wore a hairshirt under her veil. She was famously gentle with the novices — _as a mother is with a frightened child_, the chronicler writes — and famously stern with herself.

She died at the convent at the age of seventy-six. The city of Mons keeps her feast as its founder.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I have given everything to him; what little is left is also his."_ — Waltrude, in her last years]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "10", name: "Bademus of Persia"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 10]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Bademus of Persia]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbot and martyr, d. 376]

#v(0.5em)

He was a wealthy citizen of Beth-Lapat in Persia who gave up his estate in his middle age, took a few companions into the hills outside the city, and founded a small monastery there. He governed it for some years as abbot. He was perhaps fifty when the Sassanid king Sapor II, in his decades-long persecution of the Persian Christians, had him arrested with seven of his monks.

He was kept in chains for four months. He was beaten daily — the chronicler Sozomen, who had reliable Persian informants, gives the details — and the executioners marvelled that he did not die. At the end of the four months the king's officers produced a young Christian named Nersan, an aristocrat who had apostatized under torture and pledged himself to the king's religion. The officers placed a sword in Nersan's hand and ordered him to kill Bademus. If he refused, they told him, he would be killed himself for backsliding.

Nersan stood with the sword in his hand for some time. He looked at the chained abbot. He raised the sword and could not bring it down. He raised it again. The abbot, looking at him, said only one thing: _strike, my son; the Lord forgives you the blow._ Nersan struck four times before the killing was finished. Bademus prayed for him as he died.

The chronicler completes the story. Nersan walked out of the prison a free man, into the kingdom whose religion he had embraced. He went mad within months. He could not stop seeing the abbot's face. He drew his own sword on himself, the same sword, and ended his own life.

Bademus had taken upon himself the death of the executor as well as his own. The mercy he had asked for the boy with the sword was the mercy he could not, in the end, give him from this side of the grave.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Strike, my son; the Lord forgives you the blow."_ — Bademus, to Nersan]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "11", name: "Stanislaus of Krakow"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 11]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Stanislaus of Krakow]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop and martyr, 1030–1079]

#v(0.5em)

He was born at Szczepanów in southern Poland to noble parents who had waited thirty years for a child and had vowed to give the boy to God if one was granted. They kept the vow. He was educated at Gniezno and at Liège in the Low Countries, came home a priest, and was made canon and then preacher at the cathedral of Krakow. He was elected bishop of Krakow in 1072. He was forty-two.

The king of Poland in those years was Boleslaus II, called _the Bold_, a brilliant and reckless ruler whose conquests had won the kingdom new territory and whose private conduct was a public scandal. He had kidnapped the wife of a nobleman named Mściszko and was keeping her as his concubine. He had levied unjust taxes. He had executed magnates for disagreement. Stanislaus, the new bishop, warned him privately first, then publicly, then formally — and at last, when no warning had effect, declared the king excommunicate.

Boleslaus rode to the small church of Saint Michael on the rock outside Krakow on April 11, 1079. Stanislaus was celebrating Mass at the altar. The king walked in with his guards and ordered them to seize the bishop. They refused. The king drew his own sword and killed Stanislaus where he stood, at the altar, in the middle of the canon. The chronicle of Gallus Anonymus records the king's later remorse — Boleslaus fled Poland the same year, ended his days in obscurity in Hungary — and Gregory VII placed Poland under interdict.

Stanislaus has been the patron of Poland since his canonization in 1253. He stood, against a king, on the simplest principle a bishop can hold: that no power on earth can sanctify what God has forbidden.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"You shall not put away the wife of your neighbor."_ — Stanislaus to the king]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "12", name: "Julius I, Pope"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 12]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Julius I, Pope]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop of Rome, d. 352]

#v(0.5em)

He was elected pope in 337, the year of Constantine's death, in the middle of the Arian crisis that would consume the fourth century. His predecessor Mark had governed for only ten months; his predecessor Sylvester had presided over Nicaea. Julius inherited a Roman Church that was both confidently orthodox and politically vulnerable: orthodox because the Roman clergy had always been Nicene, vulnerable because the imperial court at Constantinople had begun to favor the Arian party.

The crisis came to him almost immediately. The bishops of the East had, at a synod at Antioch in 339, deposed Athanasius of Alexandria — the chief defender of the Nicene formula — and replaced him with an Arian. Athanasius fled to Rome. Julius received him. He examined his case carefully and accepted his appeal. He summoned the Eastern bishops to come to Rome and answer for their conduct. They refused. Julius held the council anyway, in 341, with fifty Western bishops present, and reinstated Athanasius. He then wrote to the Eastern bishops a letter — the _Synodal Letter of Julius_, preserved by Athanasius — that contains the first explicit assertion in papal history that a bishop deposed by his own province may appeal to the see of Peter, and that the see of Peter cannot be bypassed in such cases. _Why was no notice given to us_, he asked the Eastern bishops, _especially concerning Athanasius? Or do you not know that this has been the custom — that the matter be referred to us, and then the right sentence be given?_

The letter did not end the controversy. Athanasius was exiled twice more after Julius's death. But the precedent was set. The Western Church had not abandoned Nicaea, and the Roman bishop had spoken plainly.

Julius died in 352 after a fifteen-year pontificate. He had built two great basilicas in Rome and had hidden a friend in his own household for seven years.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Or do you not know that this has been the custom — that the matter be referred to us?"_ — Julius I, _Synodal Letter_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "13", name: "Martin I, Pope"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 13]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Martin I, Pope]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop of Rome, martyr, d. 655]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Tuscan, born at Todi, deacon of the Roman Church for years before his election in 649. He was the last pope to die a martyr. The Christological controversy of his century was the question of how many wills the Lord had — one (the Monothelite position) or two (the Chalcedonian position, by then the position of the West). The eastern emperor Constans II, trying to preserve the political unity of the empire against the Arab advance, had issued an edict called the _Typos_ in 648 forbidding any further discussion of the question.

Martin, six months into his pontificate, ignored the _Typos_. He convened the Lateran Council of 649, with a hundred and five Western bishops, and condemned Monothelitism. The council was theologically careful and politically suicidal. The emperor responded by ordering the bishop of Rome arrested.

The arrest took three years to arrange. Martin was ill in 653 when the imperial exarch finally seized him from the Lateran Basilica. He was carried in chains by sea from Italy to Constantinople, kept on the ship in his sickness for months, paraded through the streets of the capital, tried for treason, found guilty, stripped, beaten, and sentenced to death. The patriarch of Constantinople, who held no friendship for him, nonetheless intervened to save his life; the sentence was commuted to exile.

He was taken to Chersonesus in the Crimea. The local Christians, fearing the imperial wrath, would not approach him. He had with him a few of his clergy and the clothes he had been wearing when he was arrested. He wrote a final letter back to Rome lamenting that even his own clergy had forgotten him — a new pope had already been elected while he was still alive. He died of starvation and exposure within months.

What Martin had refused was the simplest of imperial requests: silence on a question of faith. The Lateran's condemnation of Monothelitism was vindicated thirty years later at the Third Council of Constantinople, with Martin's name in the _Diptychs_.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I cannot promise to keep silent about what the Lord has commanded to be confessed."_ — Martin, before the tribunal in Constantinople]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "14", name: "Lidwina of Schiedam"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 14]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Lidwina of Schiedam]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Mystic, 1380–1433]

#v(0.5em)

She was a fifteen-year-old girl in the Dutch town of Schiedam, skating on the frozen canals with her friends on the second of February 1396, when another skater knocked her down. She broke a rib. The break did not heal. Over the next months a kind of internal abscess developed and burst; the infection spread; complications followed complications. By the time she was sixteen she could not walk. By her early twenties she was bedridden, and she would not leave the bed for the remaining thirty-five years of her life.

The physical record her biographer keeps is harrowing. By twenty-five she had lost the sight of one eye. By thirty she could swallow only liquids. By forty she was paralyzed in one arm. By fifty most of her body was rotted by bedsores. She had nothing the medicine of her age could fix. Modern doctors who have studied the records suspect multiple sclerosis — Lidwina is one of the earliest documented cases. She made no comment on the suffering except to say that she had offered it for the sins of the world, and she meant it.

What she had in place of mobility was vision. She saw heaven and purgatory in extended visions that her confessor, a Schiedam priest named Jan Pot, recorded over thirty years. She had visitations from angels, from the saints, from the Virgin. She prayed for individuals she had never met and gave her confessor, by name, intercessory burdens to carry to the bishop. She communicated to her household and visitors in a voice that grew weaker every year and at last was a whisper. She lived, in her last years, on the Eucharist alone.

She died on the eve of Easter, 1433, at fifty-three, after a final night of unusually quiet prayer.

Thomas à Kempis would write her life. She is the saint of long illness — of the patience that does not give back what it has had to receive.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"This is what was sent me; let me give it back to him who sent it."_ — Lidwina, of her suffering]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "15", name: "Hunna of Strasbourg"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 15]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Hunna of Strasbourg]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Holy washerwoman, d. 679]

#v(0.5em)

She was the wife of a Frankish nobleman named Huno of Strasbourg, a Christian lord with lands along the Rhine, and she was rich enough to live in any manner she chose. She chose laundry.

Strasbourg in the seventh century was a city the way most early medieval cities were a city: a Roman stone core surrounded by wooden houses and yards full of refugees, day laborers, soldiers' widows, unwed mothers, beggars. The poor of the city went without clean clothes for the same reason they went without clean water: it was work no one was paid to do for them, and they could not do it for themselves. Hunna decided to do it for them. She kept a wash-house at one of the family properties on the river. She sent her servants for the dirty clothes of the poor, washed them with her own hands beside her servants, dried them, and had them returned. The people of Strasbourg, who knew exactly whose hands were at the wash-tub, gave her the nickname that the Bollandists kept: _Hunna die heilige Wäscherin_, Hunna the holy washerwoman.

She took special care of the unwed mothers, whom no one else would help, and of the old men of the streets whose clothes had gone beyond what any servant would touch. She kept this up for the better part of forty years. Her husband Huno supported the work without ever joining it; their only son Deodatus eventually entered the abbey of Ebersmünster and became a monk. Hunna died in old age, around 679, and was buried at the parish church of Hunabach near Strasbourg.

Her local cult was so persistent that the bishop of Strasbourg, in 1520 — eight and a half centuries after her death — formally petitioned Rome for her canonization. Leo X granted it. She is one of the very few saints whose canonization rested on the testimony of generation after generation of women who had needed clean clothes and remembered who had brought them.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"What the poor cannot do for themselves, somebody must do for them."_ — the chronicle on Hunna's work]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "16", name: "Magnus of Orkney"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 16]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Magnus of Orkney]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Earl, martyr, c. 1075–1118]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Norse-Scottish earl of the Orkney islands, co-ruler with his cousin Haakon Paulsson, in the years when the islands were still Viking polity and the Christian faith was still recent enough that adult conversion was the norm. Magnus had been baptized in his teens; he had taken the gospel seriously. His Christianity made him an embarrassment to his fellow Norsemen almost from the start. On a Viking raid in Wales in 1098, while the longships were closing on a Welsh village, Magnus refused to take up arms; he sat on the deck of his own ship and sang psalms aloud while the killing was done on shore. The skalds laughed. The chroniclers kept the story.

He and Haakon governed Orkney jointly for some years, in the inherited Norse arrangement. The arrangement frayed. Haakon's faction, ambitious for sole rule, grew restless. The two earls agreed to meet on the small island of Egilsay at Easter 1118 to negotiate peace, each bringing two ships and a small escort. Magnus came with two ships. Haakon arrived with eight.

Magnus understood at once what the meeting had become. He spent the night in the small church of Egilsay in prayer. In the morning he walked out and offered Haakon three alternatives: exile in pilgrimage, imprisonment for life, or mutilation. Haakon would have accepted any of them, but his nobles, who wanted blood, demanded the killing. Haakon's standard-bearer refused to swing the axe. Haakon ordered his cook, a man named Lifolf, to do it. Magnus took Lifolf by the shoulders, told him no innocent man should kill another, asked him not to be afraid, and gave him a blessing. _Strike hard on the crown_, he said, _for it is no death for a chieftain to be cut down like a thief._ Lifolf wept and struck.

His tomb at Kirkwall on Orkney is in the great cathedral that bears his name — the largest medieval cathedral in Scotland, raised by his nephew Rögnvald a generation later in penance.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"It is no death for a chieftain to be cut down like a thief; strike hard on the crown."_ — Magnus, to his executioner]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "17", name: "Stephen Harding"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 17]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Stephen Harding]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Cistercian abbot, 1059–1134]

#v(0.5em)

He was an Englishman, born at Sherborne in Dorset in the years just before the Conquest, educated at the Benedictine abbey there, and old enough to remember the world before Hastings. He left the abbey in his twenties and wandered. He spent time in Scotland, Paris, Rome — restless, a young monk looking for a stricter life than the conventional Benedictinism of his day offered. He came home to France and to the abbey of Molesme in Burgundy, where Abbot Robert was beginning, with his prior Alberic, to talk about a return to the literal observance of the Rule. Stephen joined them.

In 1098 the three men — Robert, Alberic, and Stephen — walked twenty-some miles south from Molesme to a marshy clearing called Cîteaux and founded a new monastery there. They wanted, simply, the Rule of Saint Benedict followed without medieval accretion: no income from tithes, no peasants working monastic lands, no elaborate liturgy, no decorated churches. They wanted seven hours of work in the fields a day, the office in plain chant, and silence. The Cistercian movement began that summer.

Stephen became the third abbot in 1109, after Robert had returned to Molesme and Alberic had died. He governed Cîteaux for the next twenty-four years. He wrote — and persuaded a council of his abbots to ratify — the _Charter of Charity_, the brief constitutional document on which the Cistercian order has run ever since. He revised the Cistercian Bible from the best available manuscripts. He reformed the chant. He admitted Bernard of Clairvaux and his thirty companions in 1112, the moment at which the order's expansion truly began.

He resigned in 1133, very old and very tired. He died at Cîteaux the next year. He had founded an order, not by inventing anything new, but by refusing to forget what the Rule had asked.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Let us return to the Rule, that we may live."_ — Stephen, in the _Exordium Parvum_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "18", name: "Apollonius the Apologist"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 18]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Apollonius the Apologist]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman senator, martyr, c. 130s–185]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Roman senator of philosophical bent, a Stoic in his early manhood and a Christian in his middle years, when his household slave Severus denounced him to the urban authorities around the year 185. Under Marcus Aurelius and now under Commodus, the imperial position on Christianity was ambiguous: the religion was illegal but the law was rarely enforced unless someone brought a charge. A slave's accusation of his master was technically illegal under a Hadrianic rescript that Eusebius preserves; the magistrate first ordered Severus executed for the act of denunciation, and then turned to Apollonius himself.

The case went, unusually, to the Senate. The senators had to decide what to do with one of their own. They gave Apollonius the chance to defend himself; he was a senator, after all, and a philosopher. The Acts of Apollonius — preserved in Greek and Armenian — record the defense. He spoke at length and learnedly on the Christian doctrine of God, the soul, the immortality of the just, the failures of pagan philosophy to attain the truth that Christ had revealed. He treated his accusers with patient courtesy. He admitted to being a Christian. He refused to retract.

The Senate could not acquit him under the existing edicts against the cult. The praetorian prefect, Tigidius Perennis, presided unwillingly. The sentence handed down was beheading — the form of execution due to a Roman senator. He was led out and killed the same day.

His apology, in shorter form, became one of the early Christian texts read aloud in the catechumenate. He had used the only weapon he had — the philosophical learning he had been raised with — and used it without bitterness against men he could have shamed. He demonstrated, in the most public forum the empire offered, that a Christian could also be a gentleman, and that the gospel did not contradict but completed the best of the Greco-Roman mind.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I am a Christian, and for this I give thanks; I cannot deny what I owe my life to."_ — Apollonius, before the Senate]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "19", name: "Alphege of Canterbury"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 19]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Alphege of Canterbury]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Archbishop, martyr, 954–1012]

#v(0.5em)

He was a West Country Englishman, monk at Deerhurst as a young man and prior at Glastonbury after that, abbot of Bath, bishop of Winchester at the unusually young age of thirty. He was made archbishop of Canterbury in 1006, in a kingdom that had been under intermittent Viking attack for thirty years and was about to enter the worst of it. Sweyn Forkbeard and his son Canute were raiding the south coast every summer; the English crown was paying Danegeld in increasing amounts and the Vikings were taking more anyway.

The summer of 1011 they besieged Canterbury. Alphege was inside the city. The Vikings broke in by treachery after a three-week siege, sacked the cathedral, killed many of the monks, and took Alphege and several of his clergy hostage. They demanded three thousand pounds of silver in ransom for the archbishop — an enormous sum, which the diocese could only have raised by stripping every parish in Kent and starving every farmer south of the Thames. Alphege forbade his diocese to pay it. He sat in the Viking camp at Greenwich for seven months refusing to be ransomed.

On the night of April 19, 1012, the Viking host held a great drunken feast. The mead ran freely. They sent for Alphege. They demanded payment one last time. He refused one last time. The drunken Vikings began throwing the bones and skulls of the slaughtered cattle from the feast at him. They beat him with the flats of axes. At last a Viking named Thrum, who had been baptized the day before by Alphege himself in the Viking camp, took an axe and put it through the archbishop's skull, _for charity_, the chronicle says, _to end his sufferings_.

He was the first archbishop of Canterbury to die for the see. Thomas Becket, a hundred and fifty years later on the same hill, would call him to mind in his last sermon.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I will not buy my life with the bread of the poor."_ — Alphege, refusing the ransom]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "20", name: "Agnes of Montepulciano"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 20]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Agnes of Montepulciano]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Dominican prioress, 1268–1317]

#v(0.5em)

She was born into a noble Tuscan family at Gracciano, near Montepulciano, and at nine years old — her parents and the local abbess having agreed on the future — she was given to the small Franciscan convent of the _Sackcloths_ a few miles away. She begged her parents to do so. She had wanted the convent since she could speak. At fourteen she was placed in charge of a new foundation at Procena, in central Italy, as its first prioress: a startling appointment, made by special papal dispensation because the senior nuns had unanimously requested her despite her age.

She governed Procena for two decades. The accounts of her ministry there are full of the standard medieval markers of holiness — miracles of multiplied food, healings, predictions, ecstasies — and full also of less easily classified gifts: a manner of receiving troubled visitors that left them, the chroniclers say, _as if washed clean of the burden they had brought_. Her reputation grew across central Italy. The town of her birth, Montepulciano, eventually petitioned her to come home and found a convent there. She did, in 1306, transferring her vows from the Franciscans to the Dominicans on the way (the Montepulciano foundation followed the Dominican Rule). She governed it for the remaining eleven years of her life.

Her great visitor was a young woman from Siena, sixty years her junior, who would later be called Catherine of Siena. Catherine came once to the convent at Montepulciano while Agnes was still alive — she was eight; she would have remembered nothing — and several times after her death. Catherine attributed several of her own discernments to Agnes's intercession.

Agnes died at forty-nine in April 1317, after a long illness in which she had repeatedly told her sisters not to mourn — _I am going where I can love you better_. Her body remained famously incorrupt for centuries.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I am going where I can love you better."_ — Agnes, to her sisters, in her last illness]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "21", name: "Anselm of Canterbury"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 21]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Anselm of Canterbury]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Archbishop, doctor, 1033–1109]

#v(0.5em)

He was born at Aosta in the Italian Alps, a sensitive boy whose mother died when he was twelve and whose father, on a quarrel, refused to let him enter the monastery. He ran away from home at twenty-three with one servant, crossed the Alps on foot, and presented himself eventually at the Norman abbey of Bec, where Lanfranc — who would later be his archbishop — was master of the school. Anselm took the habit. He never went back to Aosta.

He became prior of Bec at thirty, abbot at forty-five, archbishop of Canterbury at sixty — every promotion against his explicit and stated preference. He told the brothers, when they elected him to Bec, that they had chosen a man who would not do the work well; he told the bishops of England, when they elected him to Canterbury, that they had chosen one who could not.

What he did between offices was think. The _Monologion_, his first major work, set out to prove what could be known of God from reason alone. The _Proslogion_, written shortly after, gave the world the famous ontological argument: _that than which nothing greater can be thought_ must exist, because if it did not, something greater could be thought. The _Cur Deus Homo_ asked why God became man and answered, in a sustained argument that still shapes Western theology, that the satisfaction of divine justice required it. He invented an entire genre of Christian philosophy: _fides quaerens intellectum_, faith seeking understanding. He insisted he was not arguing himself toward faith but praying himself toward understanding what he already believed.

Twice exiled by English kings during the investiture controversy, he returned twice and finished his work. He died at Canterbury at seventy-six, Holy Wednesday, having that morning been read the gospel for the day.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I do not seek to understand in order to believe; I believe in order to understand."_ — Anselm, _Proslogion_ I]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "22", name: "Theodore of Sykeon"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 22]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Theodore of Sykeon]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, abbot, c. 540–613]

#v(0.5em)

He was born in a Galatian inn — his mother was a prostitute, his father a Byzantine civil servant passing through — and he was raised, against the odds of his beginnings, into a learned and saintly man. His mother left the trade after his birth and ran the inn with two other women who had also come out of similar lives; the household was poor, hard, and entirely dedicated to scrubbing the boy clean of his inheritance. He had a holy monk for a tutor in his teens. He took the habit at himself perhaps eighteen.

He founded a monastery near the village of Sykeon, in central Anatolia, and governed it for years. His Life — written by his disciple George — is one of the great surviving Byzantine hagiographies: it preserves a daily texture of the Anatolian countryside that no other source gives us. The villagers brought him their possessed, their crops blighted by hail, their sick children, their family quarrels, their unfaithful wives, their drunkard husbands. He treated each case at the length it required. He could exorcize, the chronicler insists, with a touch; he could also spend three weeks counseling a young couple who had simply learned that they did not know each other.

He was made bishop of the local see of Anastasiopolis in 591, against his preference. He governed it for a decade and resigned, exhausted, to return to the monastery he had founded. He served as adviser to the emperor Maurice, then to Phocas who killed Maurice, then to Heraclius who killed Phocas. He buried friends from all three administrations and never lost his attachment to the village of his beginning.

He died at Sykeon in 613, in the cell he had built with his own hands fifty years earlier.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"The Lord brought me out of the mud; let me not return to dust before he calls me."_ — Theodore, in his last illness]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "23", name: "Adalbert of Prague"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 23]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Adalbert of Prague]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, martyr, 956–997]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Bohemian nobleman, baptized Vojtěch, sent to school at Magdeburg under the great Saxon Bishop Adalbert (whose name he took at confirmation). He came home in his twenties, was made the second bishop of Prague in 982, and tried for the next decade and a half to be a bishop in a country that did not entirely want one. Bohemia in the late tenth century was still half-pagan; the Christianity that did exist was mixed with old practice and protected mostly by aristocratic interest. Adalbert preached against polygamy among the nobles, against slavery, against the sale of Christians to Jewish slave-traders, against the disregard of marriage law. The nobles complained to Rome. Adalbert was repeatedly forced into exile, repeatedly recalled, and at last gave up.

He took refuge in the Benedictine abbey of Saints Boniface and Alexius on the Aventine in Rome, where he lived for years as a simple monk. He had been formed for this life and never quite found anything else satisfying. The German emperor Otto III, who knew him personally and admired him, prevailed on him to return to mission work — this time to the Prussians on the Baltic coast, who had not yet been touched by Christian preaching.

Adalbert sailed up the Baltic in 996 with two companions, the monk Benedict and the priest Gaudentius (Adalbert's half-brother). They landed near the mouth of the Vistula. The Prussian pagans, who had been promised by their priests that the new gods would destroy their crops, would not hear them out. Three weeks after landing, on the morning of April 23, 997, Adalbert was killed by a war-party of Prussian priests with a sevenfold thrust of a lance. His head was placed on a stake.

The Polish duke Bolesław I ransomed the body for its weight in gold and buried it at Gniezno. Otto III walked to the tomb barefoot the following year. Adalbert is patron of three nations: Bohemia, Poland, and Hungary, none of which he managed to convert in his lifetime.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I have not preached well; let me at least die well."_ — Adalbert, before his last journey]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "24", name: "Mellitus of Canterbury"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 24]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Mellitus of Canterbury]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Archbishop, d. 624]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Roman abbot, perhaps of the monastery on the Caelian Hill that Gregory the Great had himself founded, when Gregory in 601 sent a second wave of missionaries to England to reinforce Augustine of Canterbury. Mellitus led them. He carried Gregory's famous instructions on what to do with the pagan temples — _do not destroy them, but cleanse them with holy water and rededicate the altars_ — and a small library of liturgical books for the new English Church.

Augustine made him the first bishop of London. The Saxon king of Essex, Saeberht, had received baptism; his city of London was to be the new see. Mellitus served there for fifteen years. He built the first cathedral of Saint Paul, on the same hill where the present one still stands. He baptized many; he travelled to Gaul and Rome for synods; he was a diligent if not famous bishop. He was getting old.

When Saeberht died in 616, his three pagan sons inherited the kingdom and the new bishop. They came to a service and demanded the Eucharist from Mellitus, although they had not been baptized. He refused. They drove him out of the kingdom. He took ship for Gaul, intending to retire to a monastery on the continent, but the bishops of Canterbury called him back when the political situation eased. He returned to find that Justus had been made archbishop in his absence; when Justus died in 619, Mellitus was elected to succeed him.

He served as archbishop of Canterbury for the last five years of his life. He was old by then; he suffered terribly from gout; he could not always travel. The famous fire-quenching story Bede preserves comes from his last years: Canterbury caught fire, the wind drove the flames toward the cathedral, and the monks carried Mellitus on a stretcher into the path of the blaze. He prayed. The wind changed.

He died on April 24, 624, in the cathedral he had saved.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"The Lord, who has carried me through many fires, will carry me through this one too."_ — Mellitus, before the fire was set against him]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "25", name: "Mark the Evangelist"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 25]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Mark the Evangelist]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Evangelist, first century]

#v(0.5em)

He was John Mark, son of a Mary whose house in Jerusalem was the upper room where the first Christians met. He was the cousin of Barnabas. He went with Paul and Barnabas on the first missionary journey to Cyprus and Asia Minor, lost his nerve at Perga in Pamphylia, and went home. Paul, hard, would not take him on the second journey; Barnabas took him to Cyprus anyway. The split between the two men is one of the small bruises in the Acts of the Apostles. By the time Paul wrote his last letter from a Roman prison years later, the bruise had healed: _Take Mark and bring him with you, for he is useful to me in ministry._

The early Church remembered him as the disciple of Peter, who had preached the gospel through him. Papias of Hierapolis, writing in the 130s, says Mark wrote down Peter's preaching at the Roman Christians' request. The Gospel that bears his name is the shortest, the briskest, the most physical — the one that pays attention to bodies and weather and the faces of bystanders, and that ends in the original manuscripts with the women fleeing the tomb in fear and silence. It is the Gospel of a man who had stood at the edge of the apostolic generation and watched.

Tradition sends him to Alexandria. He founded the Church there, the patriarchates of Alexandria still trace their succession to him, and he was killed by a hostile crowd on Easter Monday around the year 68 — dragged with a rope around his neck through the streets of the city to a place outside the walls. The Egyptian Christians buried him in a small church on the harbor.

Eight centuries later Venetian merchants stole the body from Alexandria and carried it to the lagoon city that has been Mark's adopted home ever since. The Lion of Venice is his.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Take Mark, and bring him with you; for he is useful to me in ministry."_ — Paul, _Second Letter to Timothy_ 4:11]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "26", name: "Stephen of Perm"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 26]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Stephen of Perm]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Missionary, c. 1340–1396]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Russian monk from Veliky Ustyug in the northern forests, born to a family of moderate means, educated at the cathedral school there and then at the Rostov monastery of Saint Gregory the Theologian. He was a learned young man — he had taught himself Greek as well as Slavonic, which was unusual in a Russian provincial monk of his generation — and he could have stayed at Rostov in scholarship. He had a different plan. East of his hometown, in the great forests along the upper Kama and Vychegda rivers, there lived a Finno-Ugric people called the Komi, or the Zyrians. They were pagan, animist, and untouched by Christian preaching. Stephen had grown up hearing them at the trading posts in his youth. He resolved to go to them.

He learned the Komi language as no Russian monk had ever bothered to learn it. He devised, by adapting the local property-marks the Komi used on their houses and forest trees, an entire alphabet for them — the _Anbur_ — and translated the gospels, the psalter, and the Liturgy of John Chrysostom into Komi using it. He preached among them in their own tongue for fourteen years. He confronted the local shamans peacefully — when one challenged him to walk into a fire to prove his god, Stephen agreed; when one challenged him to dive under the ice of the frozen Vychegda, Stephen agreed. The shamans, faced with his readiness, repeatedly backed down.

He was consecrated bishop of Perm in 1383. He served thirteen years more, going north to baptize villages in summer and back to Moscow in winter to argue for the Komi against Muscovite officials who wanted their lands. He died at Moscow in 1396, on a winter journey, having spent twenty-three years giving a Christian text to a people who had never had any text at all.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"They cannot read what they cannot read; let me give them the letters first."_ — Stephen, on his alphabet]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "27", name: "Zita of Lucca"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 27]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Zita of Lucca]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Domestic servant, 1212–1272]

#v(0.5em)

She was twelve when her parents, peasants from the hill village of Monsagrati outside Lucca, placed her in the household of the wealthy Fatinelli family in the city as a domestic servant. Lucca in the thirteenth century was a banking and silk capital and the Fatinellis were merchants of the first rank. Zita scrubbed floors. She rose at four. She walked to Mass before dawn at the parish church of San Frediano, came home, prepared the family's breakfast, did the laundry, the cleaning, the marketing, the sewing, and the cooking. She did all of it for the same family for the next forty-eight years.

The other servants disliked her. She prayed during her work, gave away her own portion of food to beggars at the door, and was suspected — at first reasonably, then unreasonably — of stealing the family's bread for her charity. The chroniclers tell the story that became proverbial in Lucca: she was once carrying loaves out to the poor in her apron and the master stopped her on the stairs to ask what she had wrapped there. _Flowers and leaves_, she said, in a moment of panicked invention. He opened the apron. Flowers and leaves fell out.

The Fatinellis came to revere her. The children, raised with her, treated her as a second mother. The neighborhood Lucchese came to her for prayer through the wall while she worked. She kept doing the same housework she had been doing at twelve. She had never wanted any other vocation. She had not, she told her confessor near the end, been called to anything more elaborate than the cleaning of one good family's house for a great many years; she had wanted only to do it well.

She died in her room above the kitchen at sixty, on April 27, 1272, asking for the Eucharist. The whole of Lucca came to her funeral. Her body, found incorrupt centuries later, still lies under glass at San Frediano.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"A servant is not pious if she is not industrious."_ — Zita, in the household]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "28", name: "Vitalis and Valeria"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 28]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Vitalis and Valeria]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman officer-couple, martyrs at Ravenna and Milan, d. by tradition c. 70]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Roman _vir militaris_, perhaps a _primipilus_ of one of the legions garrisoning northern Italy; she was his wife, a noblewoman of a lesser patrician house. They were the parents of Gervasius and Protasius, the boy-martyrs of Milan whose discovery of relics in 386 produced one of the great moments of Ambrose's episcopate. The acts place the family at Ravenna in the early reign of Nero and assign their conversion to an apostolic-era Christian community there.

The husband's death came first. He had been at Ravenna in the late 60s when a Christian physician named Ursicinus, refusing to sacrifice, was condemned to be beheaded. The crowd gathered. Ursicinus, awaiting the sword, faltered — the natural human terror at the moment of execution, the _acta_ note with rare candor — and was on the point of recanting. Vitalis stepped out of the crowd, took him by the shoulders, and said: _Brother, fix thy mind on the eternal kingdom and depart not from the way of life._ Ursicinus held. The execution was carried out. Vitalis recovered the body and buried it in the Christian cemetery outside the city.

The Roman authorities, informed that a soldier of his rank had intervened openly, arrested him within a week. The interrogation was perfunctory; he confessed his Christianity at once. He was tortured on the _equuleus_ and finally buried alive in a pit at a place called _Ad Palmam_ outside the southern walls. The Christian community recovered the body from beneath the rubble that night.

Valeria, hearing the news at Milan, set out for Ravenna to bring her husband home for burial. She did not get there. On the road near Cremona she met a procession of pagan farmers carrying an idol to the spring planting; they asked her to join the offering; she refused and named herself a Christian. They set on her with the staffs they were carrying. She died at the roadside.

The two sons are at Milan, _Sant'Ambrogio_, under the high altar Ambrose laid for them. The father is at Ravenna in the church raised over the floor where his pit had been. The mother, between them, is on the road. The whole family, the AASS notes drily, was unable to be buried in one place.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Brother, fix thy mind on the eternal kingdom and depart not from the way of life."_ — Vitalis to Ursicinus, in the public square at Ravenna]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "29", name: "Catherine of Siena"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 29]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Catherine of Siena]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Dominican mystic, doctor, 1347–1380]

#v(0.5em)

She was the twenty-fourth of twenty-five children born to a Sienese wool-dyer named Jacopo Benincasa and his wife Lapa. Her twin sister died at birth. Catherine, from the age of six, was a child apart: she had seen a vision of Christ above the Dominican basilica of San Domenico, and she had not stopped seeing him. She made a vow of virginity at seven. When her family tried, at twelve, to push her toward marriage with her elder sister's widower, she cut off her hair to make herself unmarriageable, and the family relented.

At sixteen she joined the Mantellate — the lay Dominican women of Siena who wore the black mantle of the order but lived in their own homes. She locked herself in her small room above her father's dye-shop for three years and spoke to no one but her confessor. She came out at nineteen because Christ, in vision, had told her she had had enough of solitude and was now to begin serving the poor.

She did. She nursed plague victims at Santa Maria della Scala. She gathered disciples — _Caterinati_, the chronicler called them, men and women who left their lives to follow her — and dictated to them, since she could barely write, the four hundred letters and the great _Dialogue_ and the _Prayers_ that have made her one of the most-read mystics of the Western tradition. She intervened in the Avignon papacy: in 1376 she travelled to Avignon and persuaded Pope Gregory XI, against his cardinals and his own anxiety, to return the papacy to Rome. She failed to mediate the Western Schism that broke out two years after his death and the failure broke her. She offered her body, the chronicler says, in expiation for the divided Church.

She died at Rome in 1380, thirty-three years old, after a long fast of her own choosing. Six centuries later Paul VI named her Doctor of the Church.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Proclaim the truth, and do not be silent through fear."_ — Catherine of Siena, _Letter to Stefano Maconi_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "April", day: "30", name: "Pius V, Pope"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[April 30]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Pius V, Pope]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Dominican, Counter-Reformer, 1504–1572]

#v(0.5em)

He was Antonio Ghislieri, born to a poor family at Bosco Marengo in Piedmont, and his early childhood was spent tending sheep on the family's small plot. He met the Dominicans by accident at fourteen when one of them paid for the boy to be given a real education, and the boy entered the order in gratitude. He proved a remarkable scholar. He taught philosophy and theology in Dominican houses for sixteen years. He became, in his forties, a papal inquisitor in northern Italy; he was scrupulous in his judgments, harsh on his own conduct, fasting and walking everywhere himself when his colleagues took carriages. Paul IV made him cardinal in 1557. Pius IV made him Grand Inquisitor of the Roman Inquisition in 1558.

He was elected pope in 1566. He was sixty-two. He wore the white wool Dominican habit under his papal robes for the rest of his life — he had been a Dominican longer than he had been a churchman — and he ran the papal household as if it were a convent. He cut court expenses. He gave the savings to the poor of Rome. He insisted that the bishops actually reside in their dioceses, that the seminaries the Council of Trent had decreed actually open, that the catechism actually be printed and used. He gave the Latin Church the Roman Missal of 1570 — the _Missal of Pius V_, the form that would govern Catholic worship for the next four centuries.

In 1571 he formed the Holy League against the Ottoman fleet that had taken Cyprus and was preparing to take more. The combined Christian fleet under Don Juan of Austria defeated the Ottomans at Lepanto on the seventh of October. Pius, in Rome, knew the outcome before the news arrived — he interrupted a meeting that afternoon, walked to the window, said _let us thank God; we have won a great victory_, and would not say more.

He died six months later, sixty-eight, asking only for the prayers of his old order.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I am a Dominican; let me die in this habit."_ — Pius V, before his death]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "1", name: "Sigismund of Burgundy"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 1]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Sigismund of Burgundy]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[King, martyr, d. 524]

#v(0.5em)

He was the son of Gundobad, king of the Burgundians, an Arian who had built the kingdom out of the wreckage of the Western Empire on the upper Rhône. Sigismund grew up at his father's court an Arian like his father. He converted to Catholic Christianity in his early manhood, under the influence of Avitus of Vienne, and brought the kingdom — gradually, against resistance from his Arian nobles — over with him. He inherited the throne in 516.

He founded the great Burgundian abbey of Saint-Maurice d'Agaune in 515, at the site of the legendary martyrdom of the Theban Legion. He endowed it with royal lands. He instituted there the _laus perennis_, the continuous chant of the divine office by relays of monks who took shifts so that the praise of God never stopped, day or night, for centuries.

He had also made one terrible mistake of his life, which the chroniclers do not soften. His second wife, jealous of his son Sigeric (born of his first marriage), accused the boy of conspiring against him. Sigismund, in a fit of trust in the wrong wife, had the young man strangled. He realized within hours what he had done. The chronicle preserves him lying on his son's body and refusing food, water, or comfort for days. The abbey of Saint-Maurice, in its way, was the rest of his penance — an attempt to build, with the prayer of generations of monks, a sound that might cover what he had done.

The Frankish king Chlodomer invaded Burgundy in 524 and took Sigismund prisoner. He had Sigismund, his second wife, and the two younger children thrown alive down a well at Coulmiers. The bodies were recovered, after Chlodomer's own death in battle three years later, by the abbot of Saint-Maurice and brought back to the abbey for burial.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_He went to the martyrs of Agaunum, and there, weeping and fasting many days, begged that the evil he had done might be repaid him in this world; and he set the psalmody going that never stops._ — after Gregory of Tours, on Sigismund's penance]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "2", name: "Athanasius of Alexandria"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 2]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Athanasius of Alexandria]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Patriarch, doctor, c. 296–373]

#v(0.5em)

He was twenty when he attended the Council of Nicaea — a deacon, secretary to his bishop Alexander of Alexandria, the youngest serious voice in the room — and helped draft the language about the Son being _of one substance_ with the Father that the council adopted. He was thirty when he succeeded Alexander as patriarch. He was thirty-one when he started being exiled.

There would be five exiles over forty-five years, totaling seventeen years of his episcopate. Constantine sent him to Trier. Constantius sent him to the Egyptian desert (where he hid for six years among the hermits and wrote, in the leisure of his hiding, the _Life of Antony_ that would invent Western Christian biography and bring monasticism into the Latin Church). Julian the Apostate exiled him; Valens exiled him. In the gaps between exiles he wrote — the _Discourses Against the Arians_, the _Apology to Constantius_, the _Festal Letters_ that fixed the canon of the New Testament, the dozens of sermons and tracts. The Arian party would call a synod to depose him; he would attend, defend himself, be acquitted in one place and condemned in another, exiled again, recalled again. The Latin Church coined a phrase for him: _Athanasius contra mundum_. Athanasius against the world.

He won. The Council of Constantinople in 381, eight years after his death, would ratify the Nicene Creed in its final form and put it in the mouth of every Christian liturgy ever since. The orthodox Christology of fifteen centuries — that the Son is fully God and fully man, not by adoption or appointment but by nature — rests on Athanasius's pen and Athanasius's stubbornness.

He died in his cathedral in Alexandria at seventy-seven, an old patriarch who had outlasted every emperor who had tried to break him.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"God became man, so that man might become God."_ — Athanasius, _De Incarnatione_ 54]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "3", name: "Juvenal of Narni"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 3]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Juvenal of Narni]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[First bishop of Narni, c. 369–376]

#v(0.5em)

He was an African by birth and a physician by training, a presbyter who had come up to Rome and lodged with a Christian woman of the imperial house. When he learned that the Umbrian city of Narni — thick with temples to Jove and Janus and Hercules and Saturn — had no bishop and almost no Christians, he sought out the one Christian family in the town, the Anicii, had them petition the pope, and was sent back as the city's first bishop, into a place that had not asked for one.

The conversion came through an act of violence that turned on the man who began it. Walking to his little oratory one day, Juvenal was seized near the temple of Liber Pater by a pagan who forced the hilt of a sword into the bishop's mouth to make him swallow meat offered to idols. Juvenal clenched it in his teeth and would not. The man, wrenching the blade back, cut his own throat and fell dead in the street. The crowd that had gathered to watch a bishop humiliated saw instead the bishop standing and the blasphemer dead, and nearly two thousand of them asked for baptism that Easter. When an army besieged the city in his fifth year, he walked the walls chanting the psalm, and a storm out of a clear noon drowned the besiegers at the temple of Hercules.

He governed seven years and died in peace — the oldest martyrologies call him only Confessor, though Gregory the Great, two centuries later, would give him the higher title of martyr for the throat he had nearly lost. They buried him on the Flaminian Way. The small crystal chalice he had carried to Narni — the one the biographer says never emptied, however many thousands drank from it — was still shown on his feast, long after the hand that poured from it was dust.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_While so many thousands drank from it with their lips, the chalice was not refilled by the minister, but the holy libations grew in it._ — from his Life]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "4", name: "Monica"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 4]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Monica]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Mother of Augustine, 332–387]

#v(0.5em)

She was a North African Berber, born to a Christian family at Thagaste in what is now Algeria, married off in her teens — without her consent — to a Roman official named Patricius. He was pagan, hot-tempered, and unfaithful. He hit her. Most of her women friends in Thagaste were also hit; she counseled them, with the wry practical wisdom her son would later quote, to remember that the marriage contract had made them servants in their husbands' houses and not to provoke their masters with sharp words. Whatever her husband threw at her, she returned silence and patience. Patricius converted late in his life — broken by her example, the chronicle says, more than by argument — and died a baptized Christian.

She had three children. Two went the way she would have chosen. The third, the gifted one, the favorite, did not. Augustine left Thagaste for Carthage in his teens, fell into the Manichean heresy, took a mistress, fathered a son out of wedlock, and slipped through every prayer his mother could put up. She followed him to Rome. She followed him to Milan. She refused to let him be lost. She wept for him so persistently that the bishop of Carthage said the famous line to her: _the son of so many tears cannot perish_.

He didn't perish. Ambrose of Milan baptized him at the Easter Vigil in 387. Monica saw it. She had won. She told Augustine, on the road home to Africa, that she had nothing left in the world to ask for. They reached Ostia, the port of Rome; they had a long evening conversation about the eternal life of the saints, leaning together on a window looking out at the harbor; she fell ill within days. She told Augustine to bury her wherever was convenient. _Nothing is far from God; he will not have to look for me_. She died at Ostia at fifty-five.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Bury this body anywhere; let nothing of it worry you."_ — Monica, dying at Ostia (Augustine, _Confessions_ IX)]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "5", name: "Hilary of Arles"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 5]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Hilary of Arles]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, 401–449]

#v(0.5em)

He was a cousin of Honoratus, the founder of the great island monastery of Lérins off the southern coast of Gaul, and when Honoratus left Lérins to become bishop of Arles in 426, Hilary followed him there. He was twenty-five. He served as Honoratus's deacon for the three years of his cousin's episcopate. When Honoratus died, the Arlesian clergy elected Hilary in his place, against his own preference, and made him bishop at twenty-nine.

He governed the see for twenty years. He was a learned man, and he gave Arles a serious theological identity it had not had under his predecessors. He preached well enough that his homilies and letters were prized and copied in his own lifetime. He kept the Lérinian asceticism even as a metropolitan: he ate sparingly, slept little, walked the streets of his city in a plain wool tunic. He insisted that his clergy live more simply than the nobility of the city around them.

He could also be hard. The famous dispute of his episcopate was with Pope Leo the Great, in 444, over Hilary's exercise of metropolitan authority over the bishops of southern Gaul. Hilary had deposed and replaced a bishop in a neighboring diocese without consulting Rome; he had perhaps overstepped, perhaps merely acted as the metropolitans of Gaul had always acted. Leo, who was building the centralized papacy of the next thousand years, intervened. He issued the rescript _Quamquam Patrum traditio_, which curtailed the authority of the see of Arles. Hilary submitted without arguing. He had not wanted the office, and he refused to fight for its privileges once he had it.

He died in 449, forty-eight years old, exhausted. Honoratus's old disciples at Lérins remembered him as the cousin who had carried the work on for two decades after Honoratus could no longer carry it.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"To my hesitation, with the pious Lord inviting, the whole world with its pleasures stood by; my mind weighed what should be left and what should be sought."_ — Hilary of Arles, in his own words, quoted in his Life]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "6", name: "Petronax of Monte Cassino"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 6]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Petronax of Monte Cassino]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbot, c. 670–c. 747]

#v(0.5em)

He was a pilgrim from Brescia, in his forties, on his way through Rome to visit the great Italian shrines. Pope Gregory II received him in audience and asked him, somewhat off-handedly, whether he had ever thought of going on to Monte Cassino. The pope's question was almost a joke. Monte Cassino had been destroyed by Lombard raiders in 590, abandoned ever since, the body of its founder Benedict left in the ruins, the buildings collapsed under one hundred and thirty years of weather. There was nothing at Cassino to visit — and that, the pope suggested, was rather the point.

Petronax went. He climbed the road up the rock — the Lombards held the surrounding country, the path had not been kept up for a century, the chapel of Benedict's tomb was a roofless ruin — and found that he was not alone. A handful of hermits were living in the caves around the mountain, scattered, with no Rule, knowing only that the place had once been holy. Petronax gathered them. He patched the chapel roof. He brought up other men over the next several years. By 720 there was a working monastery on the rock again.

Pope Gregory II, when the news came down to him, gave Petronax the title _Father of the Monastery_ in a formal letter. The Rule of Saint Benedict had begun to be read again at the place where Benedict had written it. Sturm, the future founder of Fulda, came to study under Petronax. Willibald, returning from the Holy Land, stayed years. Carloman, brother of Pepin the Short, abdicated the Frankish throne and entered the abbey as a novice under Petronax in his last years.

Petronax died at perhaps seventy-seven, having restored not a building but a tradition. The mountain has been a working monastery, with interruptions, ever since.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_He was admonished to seek out the fortress of Cassino, and the monastery of Benedict, which for so many years had remained destroyed, by his own zeal to strive to restore._ — Leo of Ostia, on Petronax's charge from Gregory II]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "7", name: "Benedict II"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 7]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Benedict II]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Pope, d. 686]

#v(0.5em)

He had soldiered for the Church, the biographer says, from his earliest years — a Roman boy given to the Scriptures and the chant, raised through the priesthood by nothing but his merit, until the city that had watched him grow up made him its bishop and the eighty-third pope. He was a lover of poverty, gentle and patient, with what Anastasius calls "a hand most large": whatever came to him went out again to the poor. He restored the crumbling basilicas of Peter and of Laurence, and made two gold chalices for the altar, each a pound in weight, for a Church he served as steward and never as owner.

His one great act was to give the papacy back a measure of its own freedom. For generations a pope elected at Rome had to wait — sometimes for months, across the whole breadth of the empire — for the emperor at Constantinople to confirm him before he could be ordained. Benedict obtained from the emperor Constantine the concession that whoever the Church chose should be ordained at once, without the long delay. He had been pope barely ten months when he died, on the twenty-fifth of March, 686.

The signs his biographer remembered from those months were all of brevity and warning: a star standing by the Pleiades in the night, the winter sky darkening between Christmas and Epiphany as though the moon had passed under a cloud, and Vesuvius in Campania throwing up ash by day that buried the country around it. His own epitaph caught the same note — a man who gleamed like a lightning-flash, who had begun more than the short time allowed him could finish. They moved his body the next May to Saint Peter's, where it lies still.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_In the likeness of a lightning-flash you gleam with the mind's splendour; but the greater things, begun in a small time, flow away._ — from his epitaph]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "8", name: "Peter of Tarentaise"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 8]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Peter of Tarentaise]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Cistercian archbishop, 1102–1175]

#v(0.5em)

He was born to a family of modest landholders in the Dauphiné, joined the Cistercians at twenty in the fervor of the order's first generation, and was professed at the abbey of Bonnevaux under its abbot John. He was made abbot of the Cistercian house at Tamié, high in the Savoyard Alps, in his early thirties. He built a hospice there for the pilgrims and merchants crossing the high Alpine pass of Mont-Joux — the Great St Bernard — a hospice that still received travelers in later ages.

In 1142 the chapter of Tarentaise elected him archbishop. The diocese was a wreck. The previous archbishop had been deposed for embezzlement; the cathedral had been pillaged; the local lords had seized half the diocese's lands. Peter accepted reluctantly. He recovered the lands. He restored the cathedral. He founded schools, hospitals, hospices for the dying, two more abbeys. He preached against the new fashion in the Savoyard nobility of usury. He visited every parish in his vast Alpine diocese annually on foot, sleeping in the priests' houses and refusing every offer of a softer bed.

Twelve years into his episcopate he could bear the office no longer. He disappeared. The diocese, alarmed, sent searchers in every direction; he was found at last in an obscure German monastery, passing as a simple deaf-and-mute monk, with no one having recognized him. He was brought back to Tarentaise. He continued his work as archbishop for another twenty years.

In the 1160s he served as mediator between Pope Alexander III and the Emperor Frederick Barbarossa during the schism over Anti-Pope Victor IV. He travelled relentlessly — to France, to Italy, to Switzerland — preaching reconciliation. He was perhaps the only churchman trusted by both sides. The schism eventually closed. He died on the road at Bellevaux Abbey in 1175, seventy-three years old, on yet another peace-making errand.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_Sitting at table, he would hide loaves in the sack he carried — stealing bread from his own meal to give to the poor._ — from his Life]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "9", name: "Pachomius the Great"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 9]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Pachomius the Great]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Founder of cenobitic monasticism, c. 292–348]

#v(0.5em)

He was a young Egyptian conscript in the Roman army of Constantine, taken from his village in the upper Nile in his twenties and marched downriver in chains with the other recruits. He was not a Christian. The army stopped one night at a small town called Esna, where the local Christians came out at evening with food and water for the prisoners — not knowing who they were, not asking what gods they served, only knowing that men in chains needed feeding. Pachomius, who had grown up in a religion that did not feed strangers, watched the bread come into his hand and asked the soldier next to him what the people were doing.

When the war ended and he was discharged, he kept the question. He sought out a Christian community, was baptized, attached himself to a hermit named Palamon, and learned the desert disciplines for seven years. He was thirty. He was praying alone at a place called Tabennesi on a bend of the Nile when, the chronicler says, a voice told him to stay there and build a community. He had not thought of community. The desert tradition he had inherited from Anthony was solitary. He stayed anyway.

What he founded was new. Pachomius proposed that monks live together under a written Rule — fixed hours of prayer, common meals, common work, a hierarchy of superiors. The hermits at Tabennesi were skeptical. His brother John, who came down to investigate, became his first disciple. Other men followed. By Pachomius's death, twenty-eight years later, the _koinonia_ of Tabennesi was nine monasteries housing perhaps three thousand monks, with his sister Mary running an affiliated women's community across the river.

Athanasius came to visit him once. Cassian would later carry his Rule to the West. Benedict would build on Cassian. Every Christian community on earth that has ever lived under a written common Rule traces, ultimately, to the moment Pachomius decided to stay at Tabennesi.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Stay here and build."_ — the voice Pachomius heard at Tabennesi]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "10", name: "Comgall of Bangor"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 10]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Comgall of Bangor]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbot, founder of Bangor, c. 516–601]

#v(0.5em)

Saint Patrick, the Irish said, had stood in the valley two generations before Comgall was born and seen there a vision of fire, and had prophesied that a son of life would come and raise a great house on the spot. When the boy was born and grew, the sign came again: his mother, finding him asleep one afternoon in the field, saw a column of fire let down from heaven to the sleeping child, and was afraid to wake him. He woke with a ruddy face and shining eyes and told her to tell no one yet what she had seen.

He built his monastery at Bangor on the Ulster coast — Benchor, the Height of the Ulstermen, by the eastern sea — and the monks came to it in such numbers that one place could not hold them. Three thousand of them, the Life says, were under his care across Ireland before he died, and out of Bangor went the men who carried Irish monasticism to the Continent: Molua, who founded a hundred houses, and Columbanus, who founded Luxeuil and Bobbio and lies in Italy still. Comgall was a man of wondrous abstinence — bread and water and herbs, and a midnight river he stood in to pray, where a proud young monk who tried to match him found he could bear neither the cold of the water nor the heat that came off the old man's body, and went away humbled.

His rule was so severe that on one island retreat seven of his monks died of hunger and cold, and the elders of Ireland came and begged him to soften it. He softened it — for them. For himself he would not loosen what he had taken up for God. He made each of his brethren, with his own hands, a small wooden bed to die in; he lived to be eighty-five; and he was buried at the Bangor he had raised out of a prophecy of fire.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_He would not relax, for any man's sake, the rigour he had taken up for God's; but the rigour of the brethren he gently relaxed, that they might be kept for the service of Christ._ — from his Life]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "11", name: "Mamertus of Vienne"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 11]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Mamertus of Vienne]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, d. c. 475]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Gallo-Roman bishop in the city of Vienne, on the Rhône south of Lyon, in the years when the Western Empire was visibly disintegrating. The Burgundians had moved into the upper Rhône valley; Vienne was on the southern edge of their kingdom. The fields around Vienne in the 460s were beset by a chain of calamities: late frosts that killed the grain, wildfires in the surrounding forests, an earthquake that brought down part of the basilica, wolves coming into the streets at night from the abandoned villas of the countryside. The Christian community in the city was frightened. The pagan remnant was angry.

On Easter Eve of one of those years, a fire broke out near the basilica during the vigil. Mamertus, vesting in the sacristy, came out to find his city on the edge of panic. He led the people in prayer through the night and the fire turned. The next morning he announced that the Christian community would, beginning that week, hold a three-day public procession of penance — a great litany, sung antiphonally, through the streets and the fields outside the walls — to ask God for mercy on Vienne. The procession was to be held on the three days before the Ascension and would be repeated every year. The clergy walked; the people walked; the sick were carried; the great litany of supplication was chanted at every shrine.

The calamities eased that year. The practice took. Within a generation other Gallic dioceses had adopted it. Within a century the Roman liturgy itself had received it as the _Rogation Days_ — the _rogationes_, the askings — to be observed across the Latin Church on the same days each year. It was, perhaps, the only liturgical institution of a Gallo-Roman bishop to become a universal observance of the Western Church.

He had asked his city to ask. They asked. The asking has gone on for fifteen hundred years.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Let knowledge ask, let love seek, let labor knock."_ — Avitus of Vienne, homily on the Rogations of Mamertus]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "12", name: "Pancras"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 12]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Pancras]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Boy martyr of Rome, d. c. 304]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Phrygian, born at Synnada to noble parents who both died while he was still a child, leaving him to the care of his uncle Dionysius and a deathbed charge to keep the boy as a son. When the family lands called the two of them to Rome, they took a house on the Caelian Hill — and found, hiding in their own street under the persecution, the bishop of Rome himself, Pope Caius. They sought him out, and after twenty days he baptized them both. The faith took in the boy like fire. He and his uncle, the Acts say, offered themselves to the persecutors of their own accord.

Dionysius died first. Then Pancras, fourteen years old, was brought before Diocletian, who was so taken aback by the child in front of him that he tried first to win rather than break him — offering riches, offering to keep him near as a son of his own. The boy was not interested. The emperor turned to threats, and even those Pancras met with a kind of clear contempt: all the terror of your judges and princes, he told Diocletian, is to us no more than a picture we are looking at. The gods you serve are demons, he added, who did not spare even their own parents. It was, the chronicler says, shameful to the emperor to be beaten by a boy. He had him beheaded on the Aurelian Way.

A woman named Octavilla took up the body by night, embalmed it, and laid it in a new tomb, and the Romans did not forget where it was. They said that anyone who swore a false oath at the grave of Pancras was struck down before he could leave it. Three centuries later, when Augustine crossed to England, the first church he raised on the island he gave to this fourteen-year-old — a fitting patron, Gregory thought, for the boys of a new people.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_Our Lord Jesus Christ has given us such understanding that all the terror of your princes and judges is to us only as much as that painted picture which we see._ — Pancras, before Diocletian]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "13", name: "Servatus of Tongeren"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 13]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Servatus of Tongeren]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, d. 384]

#v(0.5em)

Tradition gives him an Armenian birth and a long journey west — across Syria, Asia Minor, the Balkans, the Alps — that brought him at last, perhaps in his thirties, to the obscure town of Tongeren in the northern reaches of Gaul, where he was made the first bishop of a diocese that had nothing yet but a name. The Roman authority in the region was already thinning. The Frankish settlers in the lowlands were pagan. Servatus served the small Gallo-Roman Christian population that remained in the town for forty years.

He went out twice — to Sardica in 347, and to Rimini in 359 — to defend the Nicene faith against the Arian party that had become powerful in the eastern empire. At Sardica he stood with Athanasius; at Rimini, when the emperor Constantius extorted an Arian-friendly compromise from the assembled bishops, Servatus refused to sign and walked out of the council in protest. He was one of a handful of Western bishops who held their ground that year.

He went also to Rome on pilgrimage. He prayed at the tomb of Peter and, in a vision recorded in his Life, was told that Gaul would soon be overrun by barbarians whom the kingdom could not stop. He returned home and warned the people of Tongeren to flee. They did not. He himself moved his see eastward, across the Meuse, to the safer Roman city of Maastricht. The Huns came over the Rhine fifteen years later. Tongeren was sacked. Maastricht stood.

He died at Maastricht in 384 and was buried in the small church there that has been a pilgrimage shrine ever since. The diocese of Liège, which traces its origins to him, still keeps May 13 as the day of its first bishop.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"From this day forth you shall not see my face; and if I cannot intervene to save your bodies, I will take care to obtain at least the safety of your souls."_ — Servatus, bidding farewell to Tongeren]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "14", name: "Carthach the Younger of Lismore"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 14]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Carthach the Younger of Lismore]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Irish abbot, d. 637]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Munster boy baptized Carthach, but known almost always by the pet-name his master, Bishop Carthach the elder, gave him in affection — Mochuda, _my Cuda_ — and rendered Carthagus in the Latin form the chroniclers used. He had been a swineherd in his early youth on his father's lands, learning by ear the psalms a passing monk had taught him while he watched the pigs. He came to a monastic vocation at perhaps twenty. He was tonsured at Killeely and then went out, like many of the Irish abbots of his generation, looking for a place to build a community of his own.

He found it at Rahan in central Ireland, in the middle of the kingdom of Mide. He built a small wooden chapel and a beehive cluster of cells; men came; the place grew. Carthach governed Rahan for the better part of forty years and wrote there the Rule that still bears his name — a brief Old Irish text in verse, simpler than the Continental Rules, focused on the gentle balancing of prayer, work, and silence.

About 630, a political quarrel between rival Irish kingdoms turned against him. The new king of Mide wanted Rahan for his own bishop. He came in person with armed retainers and ordered Carthach to leave. Carthach, who would not call down the king's army on his own monks, took his community south. They walked the hundred miles from Rahan to the kingdom of the Déisi in Munster, a long, exhausting journey for a man already in his seventies. The local Déisi king Maelochtar gave them land on a sheltered ridge above the river Blackwater. They began building again.

The monastery they founded at Lismore became the great theological school of southern Ireland and lasted nine centuries. Carthach himself died on the ridge less than two years after the displacement, still unpacking, surrounded by the men he had brought south.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Here I wish to end my life; from this place I will not depart, unless someone drag me away by force."_ — Carthach, when the king ordered him from Rahan]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "15", name: "Isidore the Farmer"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 15]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Isidore the Farmer]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Madrid farmhand, c. 1070–1130]

#v(0.5em)

He was a poor man of Madrid, a frontier town only a generation back from the Moors, and he spent his whole life as a hired laborer on the lands of a Madrid knight. He had a wife and a son, and worked one man's fields from dawn to dark for a yearly wage. The thing his neighbors remembered about him was that he could not be got to the field before he had been to church: every morning, the Acts say, he put off the plowing to go the round of the city's thresholds and pray, and only then came out to his work — and still, with God helping him, did more than the men who had started at first light.

His fellow laborers saw only the praying, and complained to the knight that Isidore was paid to plow and spent the mornings on his knees. The knight went out before dawn and hid above the field to catch him at it. He caught, instead, a thing he had not bargained for: beside Isidore, in the grey light, two further yokes of oxen, white as nothing of this world, plowing the field alongside him — and when he looked again, only Isidore, alone, at his plow. Asked afterward who his helpers were, Isidore said only that he had called none and seen none, but God. The knight never raised the matter of the prayers again.

He was buried in the churchyard of Saint Andrew about 1130, and lay there forty years before anyone thought to move him. When at last they opened the grave the body was whole and unspoiled, with an odor like incense, and the bells of the city, they said, rang of their own accord as he was carried in. In the droughts that came after, the people of Madrid would bear him out before the altar to beg for rain, and the rain came. The farmhands' town he had left became the imperial capital that canonized him in 1622 — a city that would forget where its food came from, given a saint who never did.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I neither called nor saw any helpers, but only God; whom I invoke and implore, and always have as my helper."_ — Isidore, of the unseen hands at his plow]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "16", name: "Brendan the Navigator"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 16]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Brendan the Navigator]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbot of Clonfert, c. 480–577]

#v(0.5em)

Everyone knows the voyage. Brendan and his monks in a hide-covered boat, seven years on the western ocean: the Mass said on the back of a whale, the island of birds, the column of crystal in the sea, the mountain of fire, and at the end of the water the Land of Promise of the Saints. It is one of the great stories of the Middle Ages. The Bollandists, who had many manuscripts of it in front of them, cut nearly the whole of it out of their edition — "apocryphal deliriums," they wrote, "prodigious fables" — and then grieved that the fable had so overgrown the man that his real holiness could barely be seen beneath it.

Under the legend is a quieter life. He was born in western Munster, son of Finlogh, of the line of Eugenius, his birth (the Irish said) foretold by Patrick. At a year old he was given to Saint Ita to foster, and she kept him five years and loved him; when she asked the child once what made him glad, he told her, in his childish speech, that he could see her and the other holy women holding him in their hands. He went on to Bishop Erc to read, and to Jarlath in Connaught to drink the cups of doctrine, and was ordained. He wrote a monastic Rule his sons believed an angel had dictated. He was father, in the end, to nearly three thousand monks across Ireland, and built his great house at Clonfert on the Shannon about the year 532.

He died at Annaghdown, in his sister Briga's monastery, an old man of ninety-odd, and was carried back to be buried in his own Clonfert. He had searched, the legend says, the whole face of the sea for the country of the saints. The truth the Bollandists kept is smaller and surer: a boy who had seen heaven in a nun's arms, and spent ninety years building places where other men might learn to see it too.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_With vigils and fasts he wore down his body, and the greatest part of his time he spent in prayer; many he ruled under a holy discipline, and having stirred them to faith and piety, by many famous miracles he passed into heaven._ — Breviary of Coutances]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "17", name: "Paschal Baylon"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 17]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Paschal Baylon]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Franciscan lay brother of the Eucharist, 1540–1592]

#v(0.5em)

He was born to peasants in the Aragonese village of Torrehermosa and put out to herd sheep before he could read. He taught himself letters by asking the passersby on the road what was written on the prayer-cards he carried, then comparing the answers. He read by the light of the moon when the flock was bedded. When he was twenty-four he walked to a Franciscan house of the strictest Alcantarine reform in Valencia and asked to be received as a lay brother. He never sought ordination. He spent the rest of his life as a porter, a cook, a beggar at the door, an _hortulanus_ among the friary cabbages.

What he was given instead of office was the Blessed Sacrament. He would kneel on the stones outside the chapel after Compline and not move until Matins. The brothers found him there in every weather. When sent on an errand across France during the Wars of Religion, he was set upon by Huguenots who demanded he deny the Real Presence. He refused so calmly and so often that they finally beat him senseless and threw him by the road; he rose and walked on. The chronicler notes only that he completed the errand.

He died at the Franciscan house in Villarreal on the feast of Pentecost, May 17, 1592, at the moment of the Elevation in the Mass being sung in the chapel above his cell. He was fifty-two. The biographers gathered the things he had owned: a breviary, a cord, a discipline, a small wooden spoon. Leo XIII, three centuries later, named him patron of eucharistic congresses and confraternities — a title that would have surprised the boy who learned to read from prayer-cards on a road.

Paschal had no chair, no pulpit, no degree. He had a knee, and a place to put it, and a hunger that did not change for fifty years.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_So imbued was his soul with that most precious pledge of his beloved that, as often as a little leisure was given him, he was drawn as it were by a certain violence into the church._ — from his Life]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "18", name: "John I, Pope"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 18]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[John I, Pope]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman pontiff and martyr, d. 526]

#v(0.5em)

He was an old Tuscan deacon when the clergy of Rome elected him to the chair of Peter in August 523, succeeding Hormisdas at a moment when the Italian peninsula was held by an Arian king and the imperial capital had moved seven hundred miles east. Theodoric the Ostrogoth had governed Italy for thirty years with a tolerable mildness, but at the end of his reign the news from Constantinople — that the emperor Justin had begun to harass the Arians of the East — made him suddenly afraid for his own. He summoned the new pope to Ravenna and ordered him to lead a Roman embassy to the emperor: not on behalf of orthodoxy, but on behalf of the Arian heretics whom Theodoric ruled.

John went. He was the first bishop of Rome to set foot in Constantinople. He was received by Justin with extraordinary honors — the emperor prostrated himself before him in the city square — and he crowned Justin in the basilica of Hagia Sophia on Easter Day, 526, the first imperial coronation by a pope. He celebrated the Roman rite at the altar, taking precedence over the patriarch. What he did not do was the thing Theodoric had sent him to do. He returned to Italy having quietly failed the king's commission, having declined to ask the emperor to restore the seized basilicas to Arian use.

Theodoric had him met at the dock at Ravenna, stripped of his retinue, and shut in a cell. John was old and the journey had been long. He died there within weeks, on May 18, of hunger and exhaustion, never having returned to Rome. The Romans brought his body home and buried him in the portico of Saint Peter's. The Acta number him among the martyrs, though no sword fell. He had been killed by a refusal — the refusal to use the office of Peter to enlarge the reach of a heresy, even when the price of refusing was a dungeon.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"What you are about to do, King, do quickly; behold, I stand in your sight. This I do not promise you that I will do, nor will I tell him."_ — Pope John I, to Theodoric]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "19", name: "Dunstan of Canterbury"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 19]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Dunstan of Canterbury]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Archbishop and reformer of English monasticism, c. 909–988]

#v(0.5em)

He was a West Saxon noble's son raised in the shadow of Glastonbury, taught by Irish scholars who had drifted into the old British monastery, and brought as a boy to the court of King Æthelstan because he had a quick mind and a quicker tongue. He was nearly expelled from court on charges of practicing magic — he had a habit, his accusers said, of speaking with invisible companions. He survived the charge, took the habit at Glastonbury, and became abbot there at perhaps thirty. He rebuilt the house from a poor and shrunken community into the seedbed of the tenth-century English monastic revival, the _Regularis Concordia_ that would shape every Benedictine house in England for a century.

He served four kings. Edmund nearly dismissed him for ascetic severity, then, having barely escaped death over a cliff at Cheddar Gorge, recalled him and made him abbot. Eadred trusted him with the royal treasury. Eadwig exiled him for rebuking the king's adultery on his coronation day; Dunstan fled to Ghent. Edgar recalled him, made him bishop of Worcester, then London, then in 960 archbishop of Canterbury, and entrusted him with the long peace that would be remembered as the _pax Edgari_.

The Acta preserve the workshop stories more lovingly than the political ones. Dunstan was a metalsmith of great skill — he cast bells, illuminated manuscripts, repaired church plate with his own hands. The legend of the devil and the red-hot tongs comes from a real forge at Mayfield where he is said to have worked late into the night, alone, with the door open to the dark. He composed hymns and the chant for them. He drew an image of Christ in the _Glastonbury Classbook_ and signed it _Dunstanus_. He died on Ascension eve, 988, after preaching three sermons that day, and was buried at Canterbury. England had been reformed by a monk who never stopped working with his hands.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"You know, O holy Spirits, today Christ has ascended the heavens, and it is of our office, by word as by sacrament, to refresh the people of God; and therefore I cannot come today."_ — Dunstan, on the day of his death]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "20", name: "Bernardine of Siena"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 20]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Bernardine of Siena]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Franciscan preacher of the Holy Name, 1380–1444]

#v(0.5em)

He was orphaned at six, raised by an aunt in Siena, and was studying canon law at seventeen when the plague returned. The hospital of Santa Maria della Scala lost most of its staff in a fortnight. Bernardine gathered a small band of fellow students and walked into the wards. For four months they nursed the dying, washed the dead, and read the Office over the pits when there were no priests left to read it. Both of his closest friends died beside him. When the plague had passed, he gave away what he had inherited, took the Franciscan habit of the Observance, and was sent into the hills to learn silence.

He came down twenty years later as the most powerful preacher in Italy. He spoke in piazzas because no church could hold the crowds — in Padua, in Milan, in Florence, in Rome, sometimes for three and four hours together, sometimes for eight successive days from the same square. He carried with him a wooden tablet on which he had painted the letters _IHS_ — the Greek monogram of the Holy Name — surrounded by golden rays, and at the close of every sermon he raised it. Men set down their daggers; rival families embraced; the bonfires of vanities he kindled with the listeners' own ornaments became proverbial. He was three times offered a bishopric — Siena, Ferrara, Urbino — and three times refused, saying he had been sent to walk.

He died on the road, as he had asked, at L'Aquila in the Abruzzi on the vigil of the Ascension, 1444, having preached fifty sermons in the city in the last three weeks of his life. He was sixty-three. The painters of the next century gave him a hollowed face, a hand raised over the tablet, the three mitres he had refused at his feet. He had spent his strength on one word.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"On the same day I entered the religion of Francis, on the same day I was professed, on the same day I sang my first Mass and preached my first sermon; on such a day too I wish to pass from this life."_ — Bernardine, preaching]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "21", name: "Godric of Finchale"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 21]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Godric of Finchale]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[English hermit, c. 1065–1170]

#v(0.5em)

He was born in Norfolk to poor parents, Ailward and Ædwen — Christians of no rank but of plain faith. The boy had no schooling. Galfridus, who wrote his Vita from the longer one by Reginald of Durham, calls him _rusticanus et idiota nihilque sciens nisi Christum Jesum, et hunc crucifixum_ — a peasant and unlettered, knowing nothing save Christ Jesus and him crucified. He turned to trade, spending sixteen years as a merchant, walking the villages of eastern England with packs and working the small coastal shipping. A near-drowning at Wellestrem — wading after a beached dolphin while the tide passed his ankles, his knees, then over his head — became the moment he first applied the prophet's word _the deep encompassed me_, and began to think of another road.

He gave up trade in his late thirties for a pilgrim's life. He went to Rome and Jerusalem barefoot, in great poverty. He came back to England looking for a place to serve God alone, and was told in a dream to seek out Finchale — a wooded ground on a bend of the Wear not far from Durham. He found it after long searching. He lived there first with a sister, _cum sorore paupercula_; after she died he lived alone for the rest of his very long life.

The plain at Finchale was small. He dug with a hoe, ate what he grew, gave whatever was left to those who came. The monks of Durham sent an older brother to instruct him and bring him the Sacrament. He built a small oratory in honor of John the Baptist. He lived sixty years a hermit. Galfridus, who as a boy met the old man in the last weeks before his death, gives the only physical description AASS keeps of him: small in body, the belly thinned by fasting, the hair white, the face long, the eyes pale. _Velox ad audiendum, tardus autem ad loquendum_ — quick to hear, slow to speak. He could weep with those who wept; he did not know how to laugh with those who laughed.

He died May 21, 1170, in his oratory, beside the altar where he had been used to lie prostrate. The same patch of floor took his grave.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Sainte Marie virgine, moder Iesu Cristes Nazarene, onfo, schild, help thin Godric."_ — Godric's own Middle English hymn, taken down by Reginald of Durham]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "22", name: "Rita of Cascia"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 22]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Rita of Cascia]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Augustinian widow, c. 1381–1457]

#v(0.5em)

She was born to elderly peasants in the Umbrian hill village of Roccaporena and married at twelve, against her wish, to a quarrelsome local man. He was, by every account, harsh — sharp-tongued in the streets and at home. She bore him twin sons. She kept the house. She prayed at dawn at the rough wooden cross on the hill behind the village. After eighteen years the husband was ambushed and killed in one of the local feuds that fed on each other for generations in those mountains. Rita's two sons, by then in their late teens, swore the vendetta in the customary form, naming the killers.

She did the thing for which Cascia remembers her. She prayed that her sons would die before they could fulfill the oath. They both fell sick within months, both of natural illness, and both — by the testimony she gave the chronicler — confessed and were reconciled before they died. She was widowed in her thirties, childless, alone.

She tried three times to enter the Augustinian convent of Santa Maria Maddalena in Cascia and was turned away — the house did not take widows, and the family feud still hung in the air. The biographer says her three patrons (John the Baptist, Augustine, and Nicholas of Tolentino) brought her by night through the locked gate; the nuns, finding her in the chapel at Matins, took her in. She lived there forty years. Toward the end of her life, kneeling before a crucifix and asking to share even a little of Christ's suffering, she received a thorn wound in her forehead — a single point of pain that did not heal, and that she carried until she died, setting it aside only during her journey to Rome.

She died on May 22, 1457. The wound closed. The white bees that had hovered at her cradle when she was a baby did not need to come again.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_Asked what the worms falling from her wound might be, she answered, with a laughing face, that they were her little Angels._ — from her Life]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "23", name: "William of Rochester"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 23]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[William of Rochester]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Baker and pilgrim-martyr, d. 1201]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Scottish baker in the town of Perth, of middling prosperity, unmarried into his forties. He had the habit, the chronicler says, of laying aside every tenth loaf from his oven and distributing it to the poor at the door of his shop before he opened for the morning's trade. He was a daily communicant. He had taken at some point a private resolution to walk, before he died, to the holy places — to Canterbury, to Rome, to Jerusalem if he could — and he was waiting only for an occasion to set out.

The occasion came in the form of a foundling. He found a wailing infant at his door one dawn while it was still dark, wrapped in cheap and rough rags, abandoned. He took the child in, had him nursed, and raised him in the baker's art; the boy was known as "foundling David." When the boy was grown and old enough to travel, William believed the moment had come. He took his pilgrim's staff and scrip, the boy beside him, and they set out south for Canterbury.

They had crossed England and reached the outskirts of Rochester in May 1201 when the apprentice — for what cause the chronicler does not name; perhaps the few coins of the alms purse — drew William off the road into a wood, struck him on the back of the head with an axe, and fled. A passing madwoman of the town, who had been wandering the lane, found the body. She set a crown plaited from honeysuckle around the head, in mockery; and the next day, returning, she took the blood-soaked crown from the head and placed it on her own — and in that moment her reason returned. The monks of the cathedral priory, hearing the report, took William's body inside the city walls and buried him in their church.

He was canonized in 1256. He had wanted to walk to Jerusalem. He got as far as a wood outside Rochester, where the road ended and the only mourner was a madwoman who crowned him with wildflowers.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_The tenth loaf, out of regard for charity, he distributed to the poor — that among the heaven-dwellers, with the bread of Angels, he might merit to be refreshed._ — from his Life]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "24", name: "Vincent of Lérins"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 24]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Vincent of Lérins]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Monk and theologian of Provence, d. before 450]

#v(0.5em)

He had been, by his own account in the preface of the _Commonitorium_, a man tossed in the world before he came to the island. He had served as a soldier, or perhaps in the imperial administration; he was a Gallic gentleman of education, weary, in the phrase of the chronicler, of the storms and disturbances of secular life. Around 425 he crossed to Lérins, the small Mediterranean island off Cannes, where Honoratus had recently founded a monastery on a Greek rule and which had already become the seedbed of bishops for southern Gaul. He took the name Peregrinus — pilgrim — and from a hut on that pine-scented rock he wrote, in the third year of his profession, the small book that would outlast every other thing he did.

The _Commonitorium_ is a short treatise on a single question: when scripture is contested, when controversies divide the church, how is a Christian to know what is the catholic faith? Vincent's answer is one sentence, framed as a tool to be carried in the memory: _Quod ubique, quod semper, quod ab omnibus creditum est_ — what has been believed everywhere, always, by all. He calls these the three notes — universality, antiquity, and consent. He does not propose them as a slogan but as a discipline. He works through them example by example, showing how each note alone is insufficient and how the three together filter every novelty against the deposit. He is careful, generous, and never harsh.

He died in his island cell sometime before 450, leaving the book and no other certain trace. His name was rarely invoked in the next twelve centuries. Then the post-Reformation centuries seized him as a lamp. Anglicans cited him; Jansenists cited him; Newman cited him; the Catechism cites him. He had been a tired Gaul who came to an island to think clearly, and what he wrote in three small chapters has been the church's pocket compass for sixteen hundred years.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Hold fast what has been believed everywhere, always, and by all."_ — Vincent, _Commonitorium_ 2]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "25", name: "Gregory VII, Pope"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 25]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Gregory VII, Pope]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Reformer of the Latin Church, c. 1015–1085]

#v(0.5em)

He was born Hildebrand, of small Tuscan parents, sent as a boy to an uncle who was abbot at Santa Maria on the Aventine, and educated thence at the Lateran. He served five popes before his own election — as chaplain to Gregory VI in exile in Germany, as administrator of the patrimony under Leo IX, as legate to France and cardinal archdeacon under Nicholas II, and as chancellor of the Roman Church under Alexander II. He was small, dark, intense, an indifferent Latinist but a relentless administrator. He had spent thirty years preparing the reform he believed the church needed when, at the funeral of Alexander II in April 1073, the Roman crowd carried him off and acclaimed him pope by acclamation in San Pietro in Vincoli before he could refuse.

He took the name of the great Gregory, and he laid down, in the _Dictatus Papae_ of 1075, twenty-seven theses on the freedom of the apostolic see. The substance of his pontificate was the substance of those theses: that bishops should not be appointed by kings, that priests should not be married, that simony should be uprooted at every level, that the pope was the spiritual head of all baptized rulers. The conflict that followed — the Investiture Controversy — broke open the high middle ages. Henry IV of Germany, having received the bishops of Worms' deposition decree against him, met him at Canossa in January 1077, stood barefoot in the snow for three days, and was absolved. Three years later the king repudiated the absolution, marched on Rome, and installed an antipope.

Gregory was rescued from Castel Sant'Angelo in 1084 by the Normans of Robert Guiscard, who sacked Rome so brutally in the rescue that the Romans turned against the pope. He withdrew with the Normans to Salerno. He died there on May 25, 1085. The last words the chronicler preserves are these — quoted in every monastery of the reform: _Dilexi iustitiam et odivi iniquitatem, propterea morior in exilio._ I loved justice and hated iniquity; therefore I die in exile.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I loved justice and hated iniquity; therefore I die in exile."_ — Gregory, at Salerno]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "26", name: "Augustine of Canterbury"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 26]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Augustine of Canterbury]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[First archbishop of the English, d. 608]

#v(0.5em)

He was prior of Gregory the Great's own monastery of Saint Andrew on the Caelian hill in Rome when, in 596, the pope picked him out of the community and sent him north with forty monks to convert the English. The mission was Gregory's long ambition. Years before, walking through the slave market in the Forum, the pope had seen flaxen-haired British boys for sale and asked their nation; told _Angli_, he is said to have answered, _Non Angli, sed angeli_ — not Angles, but angels. He had wanted to go himself; called instead to be pope, he sent Augustine.

The party set out through Gaul, and at Angers the monks lost their courage. They had heard the English were ferocious and the British Channel rough; they sent Augustine back to Rome to ask permission to abandon the mission. Gregory wrote him a letter that did not give permission. He set out again. They landed on the Isle of Thanet in the spring of 597. Æthelberht of Kent, whose Frankish queen Bertha was a Christian and had her own chapel at Canterbury, came down to meet them — outdoors, lest the strangers' magic prevail under a roof. Augustine and the monks walked toward him singing, a silver cross before them, an icon of Christ on a board.

Æthelberht did not believe at once. He gave them a house in Canterbury and let them preach. Within months the king was baptized; by Christmas, the chronicler says, ten thousand of his people followed him. Augustine consecrated Mellitus for London and Justus for Rochester. He sent the famous _Responsiones_ — nine practical questions on how to govern a young English church — back to Gregory, and treasured the answers. He met the British bishops in the west, failed to bring them under his authority, and let the failure stand. He died on May 26, 608, some years before his church had its first English-born bishop.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"The best message of everlasting joy we bring thee, O King: if thou receive it, here and in the eternal kingdom thou shalt be eternally beatified."_ — Augustine, before Æthelberht at Thanet]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "27", name: "Bede the Venerable"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 27]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Bede the Venerable]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Monk and historian of Jarrow, c. 673–735]

#v(0.5em)

He was given to the monastery at Monkwearmouth when he was seven, transferred to its twin house at Jarrow when he was twelve, and never left. The pestilence of 686 nearly wiped out the choir; for a time only the abbot Ceolfrith and one boy could sing the Office, and the abbot, the chronicler says, ordered the chant continued without the antiphons rather than break the cycle. The boy is presumed to have been Bede. He was ordained deacon at nineteen, priest at thirty, and spent the remaining forty years of his life within the walls of Jarrow doing the three things he loved: chanting the canonical hours, teaching the younger monks, and writing.

He wrote nearly sixty books. He wrote commentaries on almost every book of the Bible, working through them slowly in Latin glossed against the Hebrew and Greek he could partly read. He wrote _On the Reckoning of Time_, which gave Christian Europe the _Anno Domini_ dating it has used ever since. He wrote the lives of Cuthbert, of Felix, of the Abbots of his own house. And in his last decade he wrote the book the rest of the world remembers him by — the _Ecclesiastical History of the English People_ — five books in clear Latin, drawn from letters he requested from every diocese in England, that gave a young nation its first history of itself. Without him we would not know how Christianity reached the English at all.

He dictated through the spring of 735, dying. On the eve of Ascension he was finishing a translation of the Gospel of John into English for his pupils. The boy taking down the words said, "Master, there is one sentence still." Bede dictated it, lifted his eyes, sang the _Gloria Patri_, and at the _Sancto Spiritui_ breathed his last. He was sixty-two. They buried him at Jarrow; later centuries moved his bones to the Galilee chapel of Durham Cathedral, where they lie today under one stone with a single word: _Beda_.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"You have spoken truth: it is consummated. Take my head into your hands, that I may sit facing the holy place where I was wont to pray."_ — Bede, at his death, 735]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "28", name: "Germanus of Paris"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 28]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Germanus of Paris]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Merovingian bishop and almoner, c. 496–576]

#v(0.5em)

He was born near Autun in Burgundy, of decent family, and so nearly killed in the womb — his mother, the _Vita_ by Fortunatus says, attempted twice to miscarry him for shame at the close spacing of her pregnancies — that the chronicler returns to the detail as the first sign of God's hand on him. He was raised by a cousin who was a priest, ordained young, and made abbot of Saint-Symphorien at Autun by his fortieth year. The house under him became famous for almsgiving: he gave away grain so freely that the brothers once locked the storeroom against him, and the saint, the chronicler says, found the key by sleeping outside the door until it was returned.

Childebert I, the Frankish king of Paris, having visited Autun and recognized the abbot, recalled him to the capital in 555 and pressed him into the vacant see of Paris. Germanus accepted on the condition that the king found an abbey for the poor outside the city walls — the house that would become Saint-Germain-des-Prés. He served as bishop for twenty-one years. He spent the revenues of his see almost entirely on captives and the poor; he is said to have ransomed so many slaves from Spanish merchants on the Seine that the merchants began to avoid the Paris market. He was a constant petitioner before the four Merovingian kings who sat on the Paris throne in his lifetime, restraining (where he could) the fratricidal wars of the dynasty and excommunicating Charibert I after the king's incestuous remarriage.

He died on May 28, 576, in his eightieth year. The chronicler notes the small thing: that the kitchen, on the morning of his death, ran out of grain for the daily distribution at the gate, and the bishop ordered the granary opened entire, and there was bread that day for everyone, and none for the bishop's table. Paris buried him in the basilica he had founded for the poor; it bears his name.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_With this bishop the voice of the poor man weighed more than the voice of the king._ — from his Life]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "29", name: "Maximinus of Trier"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 29]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Maximinus of Trier]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop and protector of Athanasius, d. 349]

#v(0.5em)

He came from Aquitaine, from a Christian family at Silly near Poitiers, and was educated in the school of his future predecessor Agroecius at Trier — then the imperial capital of the Gallic provinces, a city of perhaps eighty thousand, the residence of Constantine and afterward of Constans. He succeeded Agroecius as bishop about 332 and held the see for some seventeen years. He was a man of the imperial court without being a man of the imperial party. He was for that reason a useful refuge.

In 336 Athanasius of Alexandria, exiled by Constantine for the first time, was sent to Trier and entrusted to Maximinus. The bishop received him not as a prisoner but as a fellow patriarch, gave him quarters in his own house, walked with him in the gardens above the Moselle, and discussed the Nicene faith with him through the two years of his exile. When Athanasius was at last permitted to return to Egypt in 337, he carried with him into the East the lifelong friendship of the Gallic bishop and the confidence — confirmed everywhere in his later writings — that the Latin West stood with Nicaea. The synod of Sardica in 347, which Maximinus attended, was the public face of what his hospitality had quietly built.

He defended the Trinitarian faith at every council he attended and refused to sign the Arianizing creeds that the Eastern bishops pressed. When the emperor Constans, the western Augustus, leaned toward orthodoxy partly under Maximinus's influence, the bishop's name became a curse in the Arian camp. He died around 349, before the storm of Constantius's hegemony fully broke, and was buried in the church of Saint John outside the Trier walls.

He had no eloquence the centuries preserved, no treatise, no martyrdom. He had a guest-room. The Nicene faith of the Latin West owes more to that guest-room than to a dozen treatises.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_Maximinus the Bishop of Trier is held famous: by whom Athanasius, Bishop of Alexandria, when sought by Constantius for punishment, was honorably received._ — Jerome, _Chronicle_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "30", name: "Isaac the Anchorite of Constantinople"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 30]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Isaac the Anchorite of Constantinople]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Confessor, abbot, d. 383]

#v(0.5em)

He came from Asia Minor and had borne, the Synaxaria say, the yoke of the Lord from his boyhood. He had been an ascetic in the eastern hill country for years when, in the spring of 378, he made his way to Constantinople. The emperor Valens — a hard Arian who had filled the orthodox sees of the East with men of his own party and exiled the Nicene confessors — was at that moment leading his army westward to meet the Gothic confederation that had crossed the Danube. Isaac broke through the imperial guard, took hold of the bridle of the emperor's horse, and would not let go.

He addressed the emperor — Theodoret preserves the words at _Historia Ecclesiastica_ IV.34: _Where dost thou go, Emperor, thou that hast made war on God and hast him not for thy helper? It is he who has stirred the barbarians against thee, because thou didst expel from the churches those who praised him. Restore the best Pastors to their flocks, and thou shalt take a bloodless victory. But if thou go forward, neither shalt thou return, and thou shalt lose thine army._ Valens, in anger: _I will return, and I will kill thee._ Isaac, unafraid: _Kill me, if a lie be found in my words._

The emperor had him thrown in prison and rode west. He did not return. The army was destroyed at Adrianople on the 9th of August, 378; Valens, wounded, fled to a barn where the Gothic _foederati_ did not know he was hiding, and burned alive when the barn was fired. Isaac, the AASS records from Theophanes, perceived from his cell the smell of the burning emperor's body before any messenger had arrived in the city, and announced the death to those who stood with him.

The new Augustus Theodosius freed him within months. The monastic community that gathered around him became, under his successor Dalmatus, the _Dalmatorum monasterium_ — the chief monastery of Constantinople for the next four centuries. He died _plenus dierum_, old and full of days, and the Greek Synaxaria keep his name on May 30. He is in the _Acta_ a _Confessor_ — not for blood, but for the prison.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Kill me, if a lie be found in my words."_ — Isaac, holding the emperor's bridle, AASS Maii VII]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "May", day: "31", name: "Petronilla"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[May 31]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Petronilla]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman virgin, daughter of Peter, d. by tradition c. 95]

#v(0.5em)

She is the only saint in the early Roman martyrology that the apostolic generation gave a daughter to — _Petronilla, filia Petri Apostoli_, in the formula the Bollandists print from Bede and Ado. The acts are short and old. The principal source is a fragment of correspondence from a Roman noble named Marcellus, written to the exiled brothers Nereus and Achilleus on Ponza in perhaps the year 95, telling them what had happened to their friend while they had been away.

Marcellus's letter opens with the family scene at Peter's house in Rome. The disciple Titus had asked Peter, in the way of friends, why Petronilla — Peter's young daughter, perhaps in her late teens — was paralyzed and bedridden when her father had healed so many others. Peter answered: _It is good for her so._ Then, as if to satisfy Titus, he turned to her and said: _Get up, Petronilla, and serve us._ She got up. She served the table. He sent her back to bed.

She lived in obscurity until Peter was killed in the Neronian persecution of 64. She continued to nurse the sick of the Roman community — the gift she had been given the second time was for the others who had not yet come up from their beds. She refused all offers of marriage. Her own death came when a powerful pagan named Flaccus, who had heard of her beauty, came to the house with armed men to take her by force. She received him with a delegation of consecrated virgins, asked for three days' grace, spent them in fasting and prayer, received the eucharist from the priest Nicomedes on the third morning, lay back on her bed, and died.

Her body was buried in the catacombs of Domitilla on the Via Ardeatina, in the chamber called _cubiculum Petronillae_, still visible today. Pope Paul I translated the relics in 758 to the Vatican; they lie now in the new basilica's _Cappella di San Petronilla_.

The young woman who had been told twice by her father to get up — once for her own healing, once to receive the sacrament of her own death — has been the daughter of the apostolic church for nineteen hundred years.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"It is good for her so."_ — Peter to Titus, on Petronilla's paralysis, in the _acta_ of Marcellus]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "1", name: "Justin Martyr"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 1]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Justin Martyr]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Philosopher and apologist, c. 100–165]

#v(0.5em)

He was born at Flavia Neapolis, the Roman colony on the site of ancient Shechem in Samaria, of pagan Greek parents. He took up philosophy as a boy — first the Stoics, then a Peripatetic who, the _Dialogue with Trypho_ recalls, dismissed him as soon as it became clear he could not pay the fees; then a Pythagorean who required preliminary studies in music, astronomy, and geometry; and at last the Platonists, in whose contemplation of the incorporeal he believed he had found peace. He walked one afternoon along the shore at Caesarea, alone, trying to think. An old man met him there and asked him a question he could not answer: how the soul knew God. The old man told him to read the Hebrew prophets. He did. He found, he says, the only philosophy at once safe and profitable.

He kept his philosopher's _pallium_ — the traveling cloak of the schools — for the rest of his life. He opened a Christian school of philosophy in Rome above the baths of a man named Martinus, on the road to the Forum, and taught there for years. He composed two _Apologies_ addressed to the emperors Antoninus Pius and Marcus Aurelius — careful, courteous, learned, arguing that the philosophers of the Greeks had partly known the _Logos_ that had now been made flesh; that Christians were not atheists or seditionists but the truest citizens; that the rites of the Eucharist were ancient and reasonable and should be examined, not suppressed. He wrote the _Dialogue with Trypho_, the first long Christian-Jewish exchange to survive.

He was denounced about 165 by a rival philosopher named Crescens, brought before the prefect Rusticus with six of his students, and questioned. The transcript of that hearing — preserved in his _Acts_ — is one of the earliest court records of any kind from the Roman world. _Rusticus said: Do you suppose that you will ascend into heaven? Justin answered: I do not suppose; I know._ They were scourged and beheaded together. He had spent forty years arguing that Christ was the _Logos_ of every honest philosopher. He was the first philosopher to die for the argument.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I do not suppose; I know."_ — Justin, before the prefect Rusticus]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "2", name: "The Martyrs of Lyons"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 2]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[The Martyrs of Lyons]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Forty-eight Christians of Lyons and Vienne, 177]

#v(0.5em)

A summer mob in the colonial Roman city of Lugdunum — the modern Lyons — in the seventeenth year of Marcus Aurelius drove the Christians of the place out of the marketplace, the baths, the public houses. They beat them in the streets. They drove them indoors. When the governor returned to the city he ordered the Christians arrested, and the prison filled with some forty-eight souls, men and women, Gallic and Greek, of every rank. The bishop Pothinus, then in his ninety-first year, was carried before the tribunal half-dead from the beatings he had already taken, and answered the governor's _Who is the God of the Christians?_ with _If thou art worthy, thou shalt know._ They returned him to the prison; he died in it within two days.

The household slave Blandina was a small, frail woman whose mistress feared she would not stand the questioning. She was hung on a stake, the chronicler says, in the form of a cross, in the amphitheater, with wild beasts loosed upon her; the beasts would not touch her. She was taken down and brought back the next day. The boy Ponticus, fifteen years old, was set beside her to watch each other; she encouraged him to the end, like a mother, the chronicler says, sending her child before her to the king. When the boy was dead they put her last into a net, and a bull was loosed upon her. She had asked, as her only request, to be the last in the dying.

The community wrote the letter the church now possesses — the _Letter of the Churches of Lyons and Vienne_ — to the churches of Asia and Phrygia, who had sent missionaries to Gaul a generation before. It described what had happened with extraordinary care. It refused to call the dead _martyrs_; only Christ, they said, was the true Witness, and the rest were _confessors_, those who had not yet finished their confession. It begged the Eastern churches to pray that they would. They did finish. The amphitheater is still there, on the slope above the rivers. The letter has lasted longer than the stones.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"If thou art worthy, thou shalt know."_ — Pothinus to the governor of Lyons]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "3", name: "Kevin of Glendalough"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 3]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Kevin of Glendalough]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Irish abbot, c. 498–618]

#v(0.5em)

He was born of the royal house of Leinster, in the foothills west of Dublin, baptized Coemgen — _the fair-begotten_ — by a passing British monk who happened to be in the village. He was sent at seven to be fostered by Petroc the Cumbrian, who had crossed to Ireland for that decade, and at twelve to the monastic school of his uncle Eogain, where he read assiduously among the monks. He was ordained perhaps in his twenties. He fled the offers of a comfortable house outside Dublin, walked alone into the Wicklow mountains, and at the foot of a deep glacial valley called Glendalough — _the glen of two lakes_ — found a cave in the cliff above the upper lake and shut himself in it.

He lived there seven years before anyone found him. He slept on stone. He ate cresses and the trout of the lake. He prayed standing in the freezing water of the upper lake at dawn, the chronicler says, with his arms in the form of a cross, until a blackbird settled in his open palm and laid an egg there, and he remained motionless until the egg had hatched and the bird had flown. The story may be a parable. The bare devotion under it is not. Disciples found him in the eighth year, and would not leave; he gave in, came down from the cave, and founded the great monastery of Glendalough on the lower lake. It became one of the four chief monastic schools of Ireland for the next five centuries — a city of round towers, oratories, and bee-hive cells whose ruins still stand in the glen.

He was, the _Vita_ says, simple in his diet, plain in his speech, fierce only in his guarding of the silence of the place. He died on the day Bede gives, June 3, 618 — by then a hundred and twenty years old, the chronicler says, though even the Irish chroniclers half-doubt the count. They buried him by the lower lake. The pilgrims still come, on the third of June, around the round tower he never lived to see built.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Why should we not rise at the coming of the servant of God, when the Angels of the Lord arise at his coming in heaven?"_ — St. Columba, of Kevin, in the _Life of Coemgen_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "4", name: "Petroc of Cornwall"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 4]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Petroc of Cornwall]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Welsh prince, abbot of Bodmin, sixth century]

#v(0.5em)

He was a younger son of a Welsh king of Glamorgan, sent in his teens to the great monastic school of Llancarfan, and afterward — in the way of his generation — to Ireland, where the schools were finer still. He spent twenty years there. He came back to the western tip of Britain a middle-aged abbot, landed in north Cornwall at the mouth of the Camel river, and founded the monastery of Lanwethinoc on the slope above the harbor — the place now called Padstow, _Petroc's-stow_, his town. He governed it thirty years. He sent missions inland across the Bodmin moors and founded, perhaps near the end of his life, the daughter house at Bodmin itself, which would outlive Padstow as the chief seat of his cult.

The Cornish chronicler keeps three stories. Petroc was once preaching on a beach when a stag, half-spent, ran out of the trees and lay at his feet; behind it came a king's hunting party with hounds. Petroc spread his cloak over the stag and would not move. The king, the chronicler says, dismounted and asked the abbot's blessing instead. Petroc found a wolf the Lord had appointed to guard his staff and sheepskin, and the beast became his companion and would not leave his side. And Petroc once, near the end of his life, walked away from Padstow without telling anyone, took ship for the East, and was gone seven years — pilgrim through Jerusalem, hermit for a time, the _Vita_ says, on a small island in the Indian sea — before returning to find his monastery wondering whether he was dead. He took up the abbacy again and ruled it three more years.

He died on a pastoral visit to the daughter house at Bodmin, June 4, perhaps 564, perhaps later. Padstow held his bones until the twelfth century; Bodmin holds them now, in an ivory casket that survived the Reformation buried in a parish wall. He had been a king's son who would not be king. He had been an abbot who could not stop walking.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_Repress fury, flee lies, abhor envy; suspect no evil of a neighbor; and prepare the heart as a pure dwelling for God._ — from his last instruction to his disciples]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "5", name: "Boniface of Mainz"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 5]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Boniface of Mainz]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Apostle of Germany, c. 675–754]

#v(0.5em)

He was born Winfrid, of a Saxon family in the kingdom of Wessex, and given as a child of seven to the abbey of Exeter at his own asking. He went on to the abbey of Nursling near Winchester, learned the Latin Scriptures and the grammar of the schoolmen, became novice-master, and by his thirties was one of the most respected schoolmasters in the West Saxon kingdom. He could have remained. He was forty when he left. The Saxons of the Continent — his own distant kindred — were unbaptized, and he had decided he would not die in England.

He crossed to Frisia in 716, was turned back by the wars between Charles Martel and the Frisian king Radbod, returned to England, was sent again by Pope Gregory II in 719 with a new commission and a new name: _Bonifatius_ — _the doer of good._ He worked his way south through Hesse and Thuringia, baptizing in rivers, ordaining priests where he could, writing back to his old students in England for books, for prayers, for more monks. At Geismar in 723, before a watching crowd, he took an axe to the great oak of Thor — the central holy tree of the Hessian pagans — and the tree, the chronicler says, split open in four directions at the first stroke and fell of itself. He built a chapel from its wood and dedicated it to Saint Peter. The Hessians, watching, came forward in their hundreds to be baptized.

He governed thirty more years as bishop, archbishop, papal legate, organizer of the Frankish church, founder of the abbey of Fulda. He crowned Pippin king of the Franks in 751 — the first papal anointing of a Frankish king, the act that would make Charlemagne possible. He was nearly eighty when he announced he would return to the Frisians of his first journey, who had never been wholly converted. He went unarmed. At Dokkum on the Frisian coast on June 5, 754, a war-band of pagans fell on his camp at dawn while he was preparing the dawn baptisms. By tradition he raised the Gospel-book over his head — to receive the blow beneath the book whose reading he had loved in life — and was struck down beneath it.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Manfully for the faith of Christ, brethren, stand; in hope unanimously remain; and do not fear those who can kill the body, but the soul by no means."_ — Boniface to his companions on the road to Dokkum]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "6", name: "Norbert of Xanten"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 6]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Norbert of Xanten]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Founder of the Premonstratensians, c. 1080–1134]

#v(0.5em)

He was a noble of the lower Rhineland, related distantly to the imperial house, made a subdeacon as a child for the income of a canonry at Xanten, and lived as a worldly young clerk at the court of the emperor Henry V into his middle thirties. He rode beside the emperor to Rome in 1111 — was present at the violent seizure of Pope Paschal II — and rode home shaken without having understood why. He was riding alone four years later, near Vreden in Westphalia, when a thunderstorm caught him in open country. A bolt of lightning struck the ground at the feet of his horse. The horse threw him. He lay an hour in the wet grass and got up converted.

He spent three years at the abbey of Siegburg learning the rule. He was ordained priest in 1115. He gave away his estates and his canonry, took permission of Pope Gelasius II to preach the gospel barefoot through France and the Rhineland, and walked. He preached in the open at fairs and crossroads, in three languages, often for hours, drawing the kinds of crowds Bernard would later draw. He reconciled feuding families. Once, while he was celebrating Mass in a crypt, a large spider fell into the already-consecrated chalice; rather than profane the Sacrament, he drank down the whole of the precious Blood, spider and all, expecting to die — and was unharmed.

He was given a wooded valley in Picardy, called Prémontré — _the foreshown meadow_ — by the bishop of Laon, and there in 1120 he founded a community on the Augustinian rule but bound by the strictest Cistercian-style poverty. The Premonstratensian order he founded would spread within twenty years to a hundred houses across Europe. He was reluctantly made archbishop of Magdeburg in 1126 and reformed his diocese with a severity that twice nearly cost him his life — a hired assassin's knife failed; a mob drove him from his own cathedral; he returned. He defended the Real Presence against the heretic Tanchelm of Antwerp by carrying the Sacrament through the streets in procession until the heresy collapsed of its own embarrassment.

He died at Magdeburg, June 6, 1134, having outlived the lightning by twenty years and put every one of them to use.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Lord, what wilt thou have me to do?"_ — Norbert, thrown from his horse by the lightning-bolt near Vreden]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "7", name: "Robert of Newminster"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 7]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Robert of Newminster]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Cistercian abbot of Northumberland, c. 1100–1159]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Yorkshire parish priest, of a settled benefice and a settled life, when the founding of the Cistercian abbey of Fountains in 1132 unsettled him. The founders were a small band of Benedictines who had walked out of Saint Mary's at York demanding a stricter rule, and had spent their first winter in the open under a great elm by the river Skell, eating boiled elm-leaves. Robert read of them in his rectory and went north to join them. He was thirty-two. He gave up his benefice and slept the rest of his life on straw.

He was at Fountains seven years — through the bitterest of the founding privations, when the brothers ate the bread of three days in five and twice nearly disbanded — and was singled out by the abbot for his patience under the discipline. In 1137 the baron Ralph de Merlay offered the order land at Morpeth in Northumberland for a daughter house, and Robert was sent to found it with eleven monks. They called it Newminster. He governed it for the rest of his life.

He was a man of quiet temperament whom the chronicler remembers chiefly for two things. He kept his fast without relief: he never rose satisfied from the table, in Lent ate only bread and water, and chanted the whole Psalter — a hundred and fifty psalms — to the Lord each day beyond the appointed office. And he kept one friendship above all others, walking down to Finchale on the Wear to sit with Godric the Hermit, to whom he made the confession of his conscience; the two would talk, the chronicler says, day with nights, of the diseases of the vices and the remedies of the virtues.

He died at Newminster on the night of June 7, 1159, having celebrated the chapter Mass that morning. The abbey was suppressed in 1537. The thirteen mossed stones of the cloister are still visible in the field above the river Wansbeck.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"If I had oatmeal bread smeared with butter, I think I could eat from it."_ — Robert, when fasting had left him unable to eat what was set before him]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "8", name: "Medard of Noyon"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 8]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Medard of Noyon]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Frankish bishop, c. 456–545]

#v(0.5em)

He was born at Salency near Noyon in Picardy, of a Frankish father and a Gallo-Roman mother, in the years when the western empire had just dissolved and the Salian Franks were settling permanently among the people they had conquered. His parents, the chronicler says, were equally Christian and equally well-off, and he was the first child of either kindred to be brought up entirely within the faith. He was, by every account, a tender child. The biographer keeps two stories from his boyhood. He gave away one of his father's horses — entrusted to his keeping — to a poor traveler whose own horse had died, and when the count came up short the missing horse was found miraculously restored; his father, on hearing the reason, embraced him rather than reproach him. Caught as a boy in a sudden storm, the chronicler says, he was sheltered from the downpour by a great eagle that hovered over him, holding off the rain.

He was made a priest at thirty-three and bishop of Vermandois — the see of Saint-Quentin, later transferred to Noyon — at fifty-three. He governed fifteen years. He outlived three Frankish kings, baptized the fourth (Lothar I), and helped re-found the eastern see of Tournai when the Gallo-Roman bishops had fled it. He founded the abbey of Soissons, and was remembered through his episcopate as patient under trouble and open-handed to the wretched.

He died, the chronicler says, on a wet June morning at Noyon in 545. The peasants of Picardy say it rains on his feast for forty days, because Medard always weeps a little when he sees the spring crops short.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_He was patient in tribulation, succouring the wretched with large pity._ — from the ancient Life of Medard]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "9", name: "Columba of Iona"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 9]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Columba of Iona]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Irish abbot, apostle of the Picts, 521–597]

#v(0.5em)

He was born of the Cenél Conaill in Donegal, great-great-grandson of Niall of the Nine Hostages, with a claim — had he wished to press it — on the high-kingship of Tara. He was baptized Crimthann and renamed in the cloister Colum Cille, _dove of the church_. He studied with Finnian of Moville, Gemman the bard, Finnian of Clonard, and Mobhi of Glasnevin: the whole roster of sixth-century Irish learning. He founded Derry, Durrow, perhaps Kells. His Irish verse is the earliest the language can give a date to.

He was forty-one when the trouble came. He had borrowed Finnian's psalter, copied it secretly through nights at the candle, and refused to return the copy. Diarmait, called to arbitrate, gave the famous judgment _to every cow her calf; to every book its copy_ against him. Within months a kinsman of his, taking sanctuary at the king's court, was killed by Diarmait's men. Columba raised the Cenél Conaill against the high king and won the battle of Cúl Dreimne. Three thousand men died for the psalter and for the kinsman. His confessor laid a penance on him: leave Ireland, convert as many souls as had fallen in the battle, and never see the Irish coast again.

He left in 563 with twelve companions in a small _curragh_ and made landfall on the island of Iona off the western coast of Pictish Scotland. From the monastery he founded there he sent missions inland for thirty-four years — to the Pictish high king Brude at Inverness, whom he converted in 564 after a contest of psalms and signs; to the Hebrides, to Argyll, to Northumbria — and trained the missionaries from whom Aidan of Lindisfarne would receive the Irish gospel that converted the English north.

He died at the altar of Iona at midnight of June 9, 597, having spent the day copying psalm 33. He had reached the verse _those who seek the Lord shall not be in want of any good thing_, laid down the pen, and added in the margin: _Let Baithene write the rest._ He went into the church for the night Office and fell at the altar steps. He had not seen Ireland in thirty-four years. The wind that night, the chronicler says, blew steadily from the west.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Let Baithene write the rest."_ — Columba, last words of his copy of psalm 33]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "10", name: "Margaret of Scotland"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 10]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Margaret of Scotland]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Queen and reformer, c. 1045–1093]

#v(0.5em)

She was born in exile in Hungary, where her father Edward — the Anglo-Saxon prince called _the Exile_ — had fled from the Danish conquest of 1016. She was raised at the court of King Andrew I, learned five languages, read the Latin Fathers, and was returned to England at twelve when her father was called home as Edward the Confessor's heir. He died on the journey. She was twenty-one and a refugee for the second time when, after the Conquest of 1066, her family fled north into a storm in the German Sea, were blown ashore at the Firth of Forth, and were taken into the household of the Scottish king Malcolm III at his court at Dunfermline. He married her three years later. She bore him eight children.

She ruled Scotland through him and around him. She read to him at table — he was illiterate but devout, the chronicler says, and would kiss the books she read from. She brought English chaplains and Benedictine monks to a court that had been culturally Gaelic and ecclesiastically Celtic since the days of Columba. She convened church councils at Dunfermline that aligned Scottish observance — Lenten dating, communion frequency, marriage law — with the broader Western pattern. She founded the abbey of Dunfermline, restored the abbey of Iona that had been wrecked by Norse raids, established the ferry across the Firth of Forth for pilgrims to Saint Andrews (a service still called _Queensferry_), and rebuilt the chapel at Edinburgh Castle that is now the oldest building in the city.

She was the household manager of Scotland. She fed nine orphaned infants from her own spoon each morning, and she and the King served three hundred poor together in the royal court. She washed the feet of six poor every morning in Advent and Lent. She kept the long fasts so strictly that her health failed in her late forties. In November 1093 her husband Malcolm and her eldest son Edward rode south on a raid into Northumberland; both were killed at Alnwick. The news reached her at Edinburgh Castle on November 16. She had been bedridden for weeks. She received the last rites, asked for her _Black Rood_ — the small reliquary cross she had brought from Hungary — and died holding it within four days of the news. She was forty-eight. The Scots translated her relics later to the abbey of Dunfermline, on the AASS-traditional day, June 10.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Lord Jesus Christ, who by the will of the Father, with the Holy Spirit cooperating, through your death have given life to the world: free me."_ — Margaret's last words, as she received the Viaticum]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "11", name: "Barnabas the Apostle"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 11]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Barnabas the Apostle]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Cypriot Levite, companion of Paul, first century]

#v(0.5em)

He appears first in the Acts of the Apostles, in chapter four, as Joseph, a Levite of Cyprus, who sold a field he owned and laid the price at the apostles' feet. The Twelve gave him another name. _Barnabas_, Luke explains, means _son of consolation_ — _bar nehama_, in the Aramaic. He had a gift, and they had noticed it. When Saul of Tarsus arrived in Jerusalem some years later, freshly converted, and the disciples were afraid of him, it was Barnabas who took him by the hand and brought him in to the apostles, and explained what had happened on the road to Damascus. He stood, as the chronicler says, for the man no one else would yet stand for.

He was sent up to Antioch to investigate the rumor that Greeks were being baptized without circumcision. He arrived, saw, and approved. Then he walked to Tarsus, found Saul, and brought him back to Antioch with him to teach. It was there, under their joint year's teaching, that the disciples were first called Christians. From Antioch the church sent the two of them — Barnabas first, Paul second, in Luke's careful order — on the missionary journey through Cyprus and southern Asia Minor that opened the gentile mission of the church. They were taken for gods at Lystra after healing a man lame from birth, and only the speech Barnabas made — tearing his clothes, refusing the wreaths the priests of Zeus had brought — kept the crowd from sacrificing oxen to them.

He quarreled with Paul afterward over the boy John Mark, whom Paul refused to take on the second journey and Barnabas insisted on. They parted. Barnabas took Mark and sailed to his native Cyprus; Paul took Silas overland through Asia Minor; the world has had two streams of mission ever since, and neither was wrong. Mark would later write his Gospel at Rome, with Peter, who read it and approved it. The tradition, preserved in Tertullian and in the AASS, says Barnabas was stoned by Jews at Salamis around 61 and his body burned on a pyre — until even his bones, the Greek account says, were reduced to ash — yet found whole, buried by Mark with a copy of Matthew's Gospel laid on his breast. Four centuries later the body, the chronicler says, was found by another bishop of Salamis in exactly that condition, the Gospel intact, and Cyprus has held its bones since.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"He was a good man, full of the Holy Spirit and of faith."_ — Luke, _Acts_ 11:24]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "12", name: "Leo III, Pope"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 12]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Leo III, Pope]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman pontiff, crowner of Charlemagne, c. 750–816]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Roman of modest birth — the son of a man named Azuppius — who rose through the Lateran chancery on competence rather than family. He had served thirty years in the curia, as treasurer and then cardinal-priest of Santa Susanna, when on the death of Hadrian I in December 795 he was elected pope by acclamation and consecrated the next day, the swiftest succession in centuries. He sent the keys of the confession of Saint Peter and the standard of the city of Rome to Charlemagne, king of the Franks, as a sign that the Roman pontiff acknowledged the Frankish king as protector of the Holy See. Charlemagne returned a courteous letter and a large gift of plate from the Avar treasure. The two never met.

Four years later the Roman nobility — relatives of his predecessor, who had been frozen out of the chancery — ambushed Leo during a procession on the feast of Saint Mark, 799. They dragged him from his horse, tried to cut out his tongue and gouge out his eyes, left him for dead in the street. Romans of the city rescued him in the dark and hid him in the monastery of Saint Erasmus on the Caelian. He recovered, miraculously, the chronicler says, with sight and speech intact, and slipped north over the Alps with a small Frankish escort to the king's court at Paderborn. Charlemagne received him with extraordinary honor and sent him home with an escort and an investigation commission. Leo was cleared by an oath of self-purgation at Saint Peter's in December 800. Two days later, on Christmas, as he rose from the _Te Deum_ of the third Mass of the feast, he set the imperial crown of the West upon Charlemagne's head and acclaimed him _Augustus_.

He governed twenty years. He defended the _Filioque_ clause in the creed against Greek protest but refused to insert it in the Roman liturgy in his lifetime, on the principle that the council of Nicaea had spoken. He sheltered Anglo-Saxon refugees from the Viking raids on Northumbria. He rebuilt the Lateran _triclinium_ with the famous apse mosaic that survives in fragment today — Christ commissioning the apostles, Saint Peter handing the _pallium_ to Leo and the standard to Charlemagne — a programmatic image of the bond he had made on Christmas Day. He died at the Lateran, June 12, 816.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"To Charles the most Pious, Augustus, crowned by God, the great and peaceful Emperor, life and victory."_ — the acclamation of the Roman people at Charlemagne's coronation, Christmas 800 (Anastasius the Librarian)]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "13", name: "Anthony of Padua"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 13]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Anthony of Padua]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Franciscan preacher and doctor, 1195–1231]

#v(0.5em)

He was born Fernando at Lisbon, son of a knight of King Sancho I of Portugal. He entered the canons regular of Saint Augustine at fifteen and was sent at nineteen to the abbey of Santa Cruz at Coimbra, where he spent nine years reading. He could have been a doctor of the schools. He never wrote a treatise. The moment of his conversion came in 1220, when the bodies of five Franciscans, killed by Moors in Morocco, were brought back to Coimbra in their habits. He looked at them, went home, and asked that day for the Franciscan habit himself. He took the name Anthony, after the church of San Antonio where the friars dwelt, and set sail to follow the five.

He never reached it. A fever drove him back; storms blew the ship to Sicily; he made his way to Assisi in May 1221 for the great chapter and was assigned, as a man of no apparent use, to the kitchen of the hermitage of Monte Paolo above Forlì. He swept and washed pots for a year. The accident of an unprepared preacher at an ordination Mass in 1222 produced the discovery: he was made to preach extempore and was found to be the most learned scriptural preacher in Italy. Francis gave him reluctant permission to teach theology to the brothers — the first such permission within the order — and sent him out.

He spent the last nine years of his life preaching. He preached in Lombardy, in Provence, in the Romagna, sometimes to thirty thousand in the open piazzas of Padua and Rimini, who came barefoot from the surrounding hills. He preached on the prophets, on the parables, on usury, on reconciliation, on what conscience could be argued back from. At Rimini, where many heretics would not be moved, he won them back to the faith — among them a heresiarch named Bonovillus, thirty years blinded by error, whom he led to the light of truth and who held to the Church's teaching to the end.

He died of edema at thirty-six, near Padua, on June 13, 1231, his last words a vision of Christ in the hands of a friar holding a candle. He was canonized inside the year — the fastest in church history.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I see my Lord."_ — Anthony's reply, asked what he was gazing at as he died]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "14", name: "Methodius I of Constantinople"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 14]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Methodius I of Constantinople]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Patriarch and confessor of the icons, c. 788–847]

#v(0.5em)

He was born at Syracuse in Sicily, of a wealthy Greek family long settled on the island, and went to Constantinople in his twenties expecting a court career. The court rejected him. He turned aside, took the monastic habit at Chenolakkos in Bithynia, and was made abbot before he was thirty. He was sent to Rome in 815 as the patriarch Nikephoros's legate in the protest against Leo V's renewal of iconoclasm. The mission failed. He was in Rome eight years, learning Latin, copying Greek manuscripts on the Aventine, and waiting for an empire that did not want him back.

He returned to Constantinople in 821, when Leo V was dead, expecting a reconciled church. He found instead the new emperor Michael II refusing to reopen the question. Methodius — a private monk, of no rank — wrote and circulated a defense of the icons addressed to the emperor. He was scourged, branded on the face, and thrown into a dungeon on the island of Saint Andrew in the Sea of Marmara, where he was kept seven years with two thieves. One of his fellow prisoners died in the cell, and Methodius endured the stench of the corpse without complaint. When at last he was let out, his face bore the marks of his torture: later Greek chroniclers report that his jaw and teeth had been so battered that he bound his cheeks ever after with a thin linen band — said to be the origin of the linen bands later Greek patriarchs wore.

He was made patriarch in March 843 by the empress Theodora, regent for the boy emperor Michael III, on the condition that he restore the icons. He convened a synod within days. He led the procession from the Blachernae church to Hagia Sophia on the first Sunday of Lent, 843 — the _Sunday of Orthodoxy_ the Greek church has kept every year since — carrying icons through immense crowds who had not seen them in public for nearly thirty years. He governed four years more, dealing severely with the most rigorist of the iconodules as well as with the residual iconoclasts. He died at the patriarchal palace, June 14, 847, with the linen band still over his jaw and the synodikon of Orthodoxy he had drafted on the table.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"We, O fathers and brethren — both I myself and all of you — ought to give public thanks to Christ God, the giver of good things, that He has now at last bestowed liberty upon us, who for nearly thirty years were oppressed by the most savage yoke of heresy."_ — Methodius, his address at his enthronement]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "15", name: "Landelinus of Crespin"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 15]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Landelinus of Crespin]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Merovingian robber-baron turned abbot, c. 625–685]

#v(0.5em)

He was born of a Frankish landowning family in the country of Hainault, given as a boy to the school of Saint Aubert of Cambrai, and ran away from it in his teens. He fell in with a band of brigands in the forests between Cambrai and the Sambre, and for some years — the chronicler does not say how many, but no fewer than six, perhaps ten — he led them. He robbed travelers on the Roman roads. He robbed merchants of the Sambre wool trade. He raided villages. He had killed men, the _Vita_ says plainly, though it does not number them; the number, by his own later confession, mattered less than that he could no longer count it.

A companion of the band died one night, and Landelinus — robbing then under the false name Maurosus — saw the man's soul carried off by demons. An angel stood over him in his sleep and told him to leave the band and return to Aubert as his spiritual father. Terrified by the vision, he left his companions and everything he had and went on foot to Cambrai, where he fell at the bishop's feet and begged penance. From there he walked to Rome on foot as the first work of that penance. He stopped at every shrine on the road and sometimes slept in the porches for days. He came back to Hainault a different man, was ordained at Cambrai by Aubert's successor Vindician, and was given title to a small clearing in the wood at Lobbes on the Sambre.

He built a monastery there with his own hands in 654, then a second at Aulne in 657, then a third at Wallers, then a fourth — the great house — at Crespin in 670, on land donated by a converted noble. He governed Crespin for fifteen years. He required of his monks only this: that they live by the work of their hands and refuse no traveler at the gate. He had been a robber on these roads; he made his houses into the first thing a stranger met on them. The men of his old band, the _Vita_ says, came to him one by one over the years, several to die as monks under his rule. He died at Crespin, June 15, 685, having outlived his crimes by forty years and lost count, again, of the days he had spent in penance.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Let not my absence sadden you, best brothers; the eternal Pastor, the Lord Jesus Christ, will be present to you wholly, if you strive to hold to his charity the more attentively."_ — Landelinus to his monks]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "16", name: "Lutgardis of Aywières"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 16]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Lutgardis of Aywières]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Cistercian mystic of Brabant, 1182–1246]

#v(0.5em)

She was born at Tongeren in the duchy of Brabant, to a noble mother and a merchant father. Her father, hoping she would prosper in the world, entrusted twenty marks of silver to a trader to grow her marriage-dowry; the trader, crossing again and again into England and broken by misfortunes, squandered the twenty down to one. The girl herself was eager to marry, and the lost dowry did not change that — it was her mother who turned her, saying, as the chronicler reports, "If you will marry Christ, I will prepare for you a most honorable monastery; but if you choose a mortal man, you will have no other than a keeper of cows." She was placed at the Benedictine convent of Saint Catherine outside Saint-Trond a little past twelve. She did not at first take to it. She was lively, conversational, well-liked among the visitors who came to the parlor, and went on for some years receiving the discreet attentions of a young soldier of the town through the speaking-grille. The change came at sixteen, when, the chronicler says, she saw a vision of Christ showing her his wounded side and asking her to attend to him with the attention she had been giving the world.

She gave it. She took final vows. She was elected prioress at twenty-four. Almost immediately she fled the office — too prominent, too administrative, too unsuited to the contemplation she had only just learned to hold — and transferred at twenty-four to the stricter Cistercian house at Aywières in the French-speaking Walloon country south of Brussels, where she lived the remaining forty years of her life. She refused to learn French. She had vowed, she said, never to speak again about anything that did not matter for salvation, and the unfamiliar grammar would make her speak slowly and weigh each word.

She kept three successive seven-year fasts. The first seven years, bidden in a vision of the Virgin to fast against the Albigensian heresy then threatening, she took only bread and beer. The second seven years she fasted for sinners on bread and herbs. The third fast she began against a feared enemy of the Church, and never broke it, even on Easter, until her death in its seventh year. She went blind in her last eleven years. She was visited in her cell by her old confessor Thomas of Cantimpré, who wrote her _Vita_, and by Hadewijch the Beguine poet, who took some of her phrases home for her own _Visions_. She died at Aywières, June 16, 1246, asking only that they bury her where she had prayed; and she was laid on the right side of the choir, near the wall where she was wont to pray, in a solemn and honorable place.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Lutgard wept; she led a life without crime; with Christ she dwells, whom this stone covers."_ — epitaph by Sybil de Gages, who served her in life]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "17", name: "Botulph of Icanho"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 17]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Botulph of Icanho]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[East Anglian abbot, d. 680]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Saxon of East Anglia, sent for his education with his brother Adulph to the Frankish monastery of Faremoutiers-en-Brie in the 630s, when the sons and daughters of the Anglo-Saxon nobility were crossing the Channel to learn the rule. He came back to England about 654, was ordained, and was offered land for a foundation by Anna, king of the East Angles. He asked for a place no one wanted. Anna gave him a small bare promontory in the marshes called _Icanho_ — _ox-island_, a high-tide islet at the mouth of what is now the Alde. The chronicler says the place was haunted; the country people would not pasture even their oxen on it after sunset.

He built his oratory there in 654 and slept in it the first night, the chronicler says, while the demons of the place wailed around the thatch until dawn. He stayed. Within a generation he had drained the salt-marshes into tillable ground. He grew a community of perhaps thirty monks who kept the rule he had learned at Faremoutiers — Columbanian with a Benedictine softening, severe in the night office, generous in hospitality. He was sought out as a teacher by every house that could spare a brother to send him.

He was famous in his own century for one thing: hospitality at the rim of the sea. Icanho stood on the road by which every traveler from the Frankish kingdoms to East Anglia made landfall. He gave every arrival a meal, a bed, and a blessing for the journey inland. Travelers built small shrines to him at the gates of their towns as a thanksgiving for safe arrival. By the high middle ages gate-shrines to him stood across England; several survive in the City of London, among them the parish of _Saint Botolph without Aldgate_.

He died at Icanho, June 17, 680, having ruled there twenty-six years. The Danes burned the monastery in 870. The bones, hidden the night of the raid, were translated three times in three centuries and finally divided among Bury, Ely, and Westminster.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Be not, my son, disturbed: for God is able to restore all to us."_ — Botulph, when his last loaves had been given to the poor]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "18", name: "Elizabeth of Schönau"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 18]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Elizabeth of Schönau]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Benedictine visionary of the Rhineland, 1129–1164]

#v(0.5em)

She was a Rhineland girl from a modest free family, given to the Benedictine double monastery of Schönau in the Nassau hills at the age of twelve, and never left. She took her vows at eighteen. She became prioress of the women's community at twenty-eight. She was a small, quiet, sickly woman, the chronicler says, of unremarkable conversation, who served the meals and copied the choir books and was assumed by everyone in the cloister to be exactly what she appeared to be.

She began to receive visions at twenty-three. They came at first only at the Office, only on the great feasts, lasting a few minutes, and she told no one. By her late twenties they had spread through every Hour and to ordinary days; she fell into trance in choir, in the cloister, at table. She resisted writing them down for years until her brother Egbert, a Benedictine monk at the same house and later abbot of it, insisted. He took her dictation. The three _Books of Visions_ that resulted, together with the _Book of the Ways of God_ and the visionary expansion of the _Acts of Saint Ursula and the Eleven Thousand Virgins_, made her, within her own lifetime, the second most-read woman writer in twelfth-century Latin Europe, after her older friend and correspondent Hildegard of Bingen.

She and Hildegard exchanged ten years of letters that survive. They were strikingly different visionaries. Hildegard saw cosmic structures, the wheels of creation, the proportions of grace. Elizabeth saw scenes — the Virgin at the cradle, the angel at the empty tomb, the saints in their proper feasts — and her letters back to Hildegard treat her own gift with a kind of apologetic wonder, as if she could not understand why she of all people should be made to see these things. She was bedridden the last seven years of her life from the toll of the trances. She died at Schönau, June 18, 1164, at thirty-six. Egbert, who outlived her by twenty years, edited her writings and never claimed for them what she would not claim. The texts she had thought too small for the cloister are still in print eight hundred years later.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"By the grace of God I am what I am. Let not the greatness of the revelations exalt me."_ — Elizabeth, awaking from a vision on the feast of John the Baptist]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "19", name: "Bruno of Querfurt"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 19]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Bruno of Querfurt]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Camaldolese missionary-bishop, protomartyr of the order, c. 974–1008]

#v(0.5em)

The AASS Junii III calls him _Bonifacius_ — the name he took in religion in honor of the apostle of Germany, and the name he carried through the brief, intense, eight years of his apostolic mission to the pagan north. He had been born about 974 at Querfurt in Saxony of a noble family. He came to Romuald's Camaldolese hermitage at Pereum near Ravenna, exchanged silk and a train of attendants for a single cloak, and so outstripped his fellow hermits in austerity that his life was held a wonder. From there he asked the Pope's leave to carry the Gospel north, where Adalbert of Prague had been martyred in 997.

He went, under the commission of Pope Gregory V, to the pagan country east of Prussia. He baptized in rivers, taught through interpreters, lived rough. The natives, the chronicler notes, feared that if they killed him as they had killed Adalbert, the miracles afterward would convert their people; so by a cunning malice they held back their hands a long time, unwilling to give him the death he desired.

The chronicler keeps one miracle. The pagan king of the region, refusing baptism, had a great pyre built before his hall and challenged Bruno to walk through it as proof of his faith. He walked through it unharmed, _per ignem transisset illæsus_. The king and his people were baptized, and the king resolved never to leave Bruno's side. But the king had a brother who would not believe, and the king killed that unbelieving brother himself. A second brother, who lived apart, was enraged at the conversion; when Bruno came to him he would not hear his word, and — fearing the king would take the saint from his hands — had him beheaded on the spot. At once that brother and all who stood by were struck blind and rigid as stones. The king came, prayed for them with the other Christians, and their sense was restored; they sought penance, received baptism, and raised a church over the martyr's body. The chronicler dates the death to 1008. The AASS keeps his feast on June 19.

He had wanted, the _Vita_ by Peter Damian says, to spill blood for Christ from the first day he had taken the Camaldolese habit. The natives had refused him a long time. They had refused him their conversion as long as they could. They gave him both in the same hour.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"He passed through the fire unharmed, baptized the king and the people, and was killed by the king's raging brother."_ — Baronius's _Roman Martyrology_, quoted at AASS Junii III, on Bruno]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "20", name: "Silverius, Pope"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 20]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Silverius, Pope]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman pontiff and martyr, d. 539]

#v(0.5em)

He was the legitimate son, born before his father's ordination, of Pope Hormisdas (514–523), and was raised at the Lateran in the years his father was reconciling the Latin and Greek churches after the long Acacian schism. He took orders, served as a subdeacon, then a deacon. He was a quiet man, of moderate parts, of his father's blood and his father's instincts: hold what has been received. He was forty-something when the Ostrogothic king Theodahad — at war with the emperor Justinian and the general Belisarius then advancing through Italy — looked for a Roman to elevate to the chair of Peter who would be loyal to Gothic Italy. He picked Silverius and pressed the Roman clergy hard. They consecrated him under protest in June 536.

Belisarius took Rome by negotiation six months later. The empress Theodora at Constantinople, who had her own plans for the Roman see, sent letters to Belisarius asking him to depose Silverius and replace him with the deacon Vigilius, then in the imperial capital, who had privately promised to overturn the Council of Chalcedon and rehabilitate the Monophysite Anthimus, Theodora's protégé. Silverius refused on every point. He would not condemn Chalcedon. He would not seat Anthimus. He knew, the chronicler says, what the refusal would cost him.

He was summoned to Belisarius's headquarters in the Pincian palace on March 20, 537. The general's wife Antonina received him on a couch, with Belisarius at her feet. She read out forged letters purporting to show Silverius's correspondence with the Goths against the empire. He was stripped of his vestments on the spot, dressed in a monk's tunic, and exiled the next day to Patara, a city of Lycia on the south Anatolian coast. Vigilius was consecrated pope in his place. The bishop of Patara, on receiving the exile, went to Constantinople himself to protest; Justinian, embarrassed, ordered the trial reopened. Silverius was returned to Italy and the matter remitted for investigation. He never reached Rome. Antonina intercepted him on the road, had him deported to the small malarial island of Palmarola off Naples, and left him there. He died of starvation on the island, June 20, 539.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Lady Augusta, I will never recall a heretical man, condemned in his wickedness."_ — Silverius, to the empress's envoy]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "21", name: "Alban of Mainz"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 21]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Alban of Mainz]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Greek martyr in Germany, c. 404]

#v(0.5em)

He was, by the elogium that Rabanus Maurus preserved in his ninth-century _Martyrologium_, a Greek of the Cyclades — most likely Naxos, the AASS reasons. He was a younger cleric in the church there, traveling under the older Theonestus and a third companion named Ursus. They sailed west to Milan in the late fourth century, came under the influence of Ambrose — then fighting the Arian dukes for control of the basilicas of the city — and were dispatched by the archbishop north into Gaul.

Ursus did not survive the journey. He was martyred at Aosta — _Augusta Prætoria in Salassiis_ — and is commemorated on February 1. Theonestus and Alban kept walking, took the Rhine road, and reached Mainz. The Rhineland was a difficult mission. Bishop Aureus of Mainz had been deposed and exiled seven times by Arian factions in his decades on the chair; Theonestus arrived to a bishop in office only by intervals.

Alban was the younger of the two, the AASS says plainly: _junior, sic et ad lacessendos hæreticos animosior quam Theonestus, magistro suo præripuerit coronam martyrii_ — younger, and more spirited in confronting the heretics than his master Theonestus, so that he ran ahead of his master to the crown of martyrdom. He preached in the streets of Mainz against the Arian faction. An _infida turba_ — a faithless crowd — caught him in the open and beheaded him on the spot. The local Christians took his body and buried him in a small chapel they built on the place. The Romanesque verses still inscribed at the chapel in the Petersplatz date the death precisely: _Orbe quater centum numerante et quattuor annos, dum tenet Imperii moderamina Honorius_ — when the world counted four hundred years and four, under the rule of Honorius. The year 404.

The Mainz tradition gave him afterward the cephalophore's iconography, pictured carrying his own severed head to the place of burial — a standard convention for beheaded martyrs, the AASS gently notes, and not a separate miracle. What was real was simpler: a Greek-speaking priest from a small island, sent west by Ambrose, killed on a Rhineland street for refusing to be quiet about the consubstantial Son.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Albanus sanctus hic requievit, scisso cum capite agonem complevit."_ — Saint Alban here rested, his contest finished with his head sundered. — verses on the Mainz chapel, AASS Junii IV]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "22", name: "Paulinus of Nola"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 22]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Paulinus of Nola]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Senator, poet, bishop of Campania, c. 354–431]

#v(0.5em)

He was Meropius Pontius Paulinus, of a senatorial Gallo-Roman family with estates in Aquitaine, Italy, and Spain. He was rich beyond ordinary measure. He had been a pupil of the poet Ausonius at Bordeaux, made consul-suffect at Rome in his late twenties, married a Spanish noblewoman named Therasia, and held a string of provincial governorships. He could have lived out the imperial career arc and been forgotten by everyone but the prosopographers. He was forty when their only son died at eight days old. Husband and wife buried the child at Alcalá in Spain and walked, that summer, to the shrine of Saint Felix at Nola near Naples, where he had once as a young governor felt a pull he had not explained even to himself.

They came home and sold everything. He freed his slaves with pensions. He gave the cash to the poor and to the churches of his three provinces. He kept only one small plot of land at Nola itself, on which stood the shrine, and walked back to it on foot in 395 with Therasia. They built two small huts of clay and reeds in the orchard behind the shrine and went into them. They lived thirty-six more years. He was ordained, by force, in Spain on a Christmas Eve in 394; he was elected bishop of Nola, again with reluctance, about 410. Therasia died sometime after 415. He governed Nola through the Vandal raids on Italy, the fall of Rome in 410 (refugees from which he housed by the hundred in the abandoned senatorial villas of Campania), and the famine years that followed.

He wrote letters to everyone — to Augustine in Africa, to Jerome at Bethlehem, to Sulpicius Severus in Aquitaine, to Martin of Tours, to Ambrose at Milan — and his fifty-one surviving letters are among the most affectionate documents of the Latin patristic world. He wrote thirty-three poems for the annual feast of Saint Felix on January 14, one a year, almost without interruption, for thirty-three years. He sold himself once into Vandal slavery, the chronicler says, to ransom the only son of a widow of Nola whom the Vandals had taken; the Vandal chief, on discovering whom he had bought, sent him back. He died at Nola, June 22, 431, having outlived the imperial world he had been born into and built another one in its orchard.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I have prepared a lamp for my Christ."_ — Paulinus's last words, sung at the hour of the evening lamps (Uranius, _Life of Paulinus_)]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "23", name: "Etheldreda of Ely"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 23]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Etheldreda of Ely]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[East Anglian queen and abbess, c. 636–679]

#v(0.5em)

She was a daughter of Anna, king of the East Angles, and one of four sisters who all became saints. She was given in marriage at perhaps sixteen to Tondberct, a chief of the South Gyrwe in the fenland country south of Ely. She had asked, and he had agreed, that the marriage be celibate. He kept the agreement; she was twenty-three when he died, leaving her the small island of Ely as her widow's portion. She lived alone on it five years.

She was given in marriage again, against her will, at twenty-eight, to the boy king Egfrith of Northumbria, then fifteen. She insisted again on the condition; he agreed. The agreement held twelve years. As Egfrith grew into manhood he began to want it set aside, appealed to Bishop Wilfrid of York to release her vow, and was refused. Wilfrid, the chronicler says, would not lie to the king for the king's marriage. Etheldreda finally walked out of the Northumbrian court with two attendants and made her way south to her aunt Ebba's abbey at Coldingham. The king pursued her with a war-band. She took refuge on a hill on the Northumbrian coast called _Colbert's Head_; the tide rose, the chronicler says, and held high for seven days, surrounding the hill, and the king retreated. She returned to Ely a free woman in 672.

She founded a double monastery there in 673 on the island that had been her dower, and governed it seven years. She built the first stone church on the island, the foundation of what would later be Ely Cathedral. She kept the strict rule she had longed for: one meal a day, wool habit on the skin, the night office stood through, no fire in her own cell even in the fenland winter. She bore patiently through her last year a swelling on her neck — the result, she said, of jewels of gold she had worn as a young queen, now repaid. She died of plague at Ely, June 23, 679, at perhaps forty-three. Sixteen years later her sister and successor Sexburga had her body translated to a Roman sarcophagus that had floated up out of the river; the body, the _Vita_ says, was incorrupt, and the swelling on the neck had healed.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I deservedly bear in my neck the weight of an ailment, in which, as a young girl, I bore the superfluous weights of necklaces."_ — Etheldreda, on her last illness]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "24", name: "John the Baptist"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 24]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[John the Baptist]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Forerunner of the Lord, prophet of the wilderness, c. 5 B.C.–c. A.D. 30]

#v(0.5em)

The AASS opens with the formula _DE SANCTO IOANNE PRODROMO ET BAPTISTA D. N. IESU CHRISTI_ — _of Saint John, Forerunner and Baptist of our Lord Jesus Christ._ The two Greek titles are the two halves of his vocation. _Prodromos_ is what runs ahead, the herald who clears the road. _Baptistēs_ is what plunges under, the one who lowers the body into the water and lifts it back. He did one work in two motions. He went ahead. He took down.

He had been the late-born only son of two old people in the hill country of Judah, and grew up, the gospels suggest, mostly in the Judean wilderness. _And the child grew, and waxed strong in spirit, and was in the deserts till the day of his shewing unto Israel._ He came out around the year 27 or 28, in the fifteenth year of Tiberius Caesar, and began to preach a baptism of repentance at the lower Jordan crossings.

The crowds were enormous. Pharisees and Sadducees, tax-collectors and soldiers came down from Jerusalem and Galilee to be dipped under in the cold green river by the man in the camel-hair tunic. The preaching was severe — _O generation of vipers, who hath warned you to flee from the wrath to come?_ He preached the imminence of the kingdom; he preached the messiah whose sandals he was not worthy to unloose. When the messiah walked down to the river himself, John knew him, tried to refuse, and was insisted on. He lowered him into the water and lifted him out, and the heavens opened.

The end was political. Herod Antipas had divorced his wife to take his brother's wife Herodias; John had named the act adultery; Herodias had wanted his head ever since. Antipas imprisoned him at Machaerus. The night of the king's birthday Herodias's daughter danced for the guests; the king, drunk, swore to give her anything; the mother whispered the answer; the head came in on a silver platter. John was perhaps thirty-two.

The church keeps his nativity at midsummer, six months before Christmas — _the calendar made so,_ Augustine writes, _because the light has reached its peak and from this day must lessen._ The man who said _he must increase, but I must decrease_ gave the day the shape of his own teaching.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"He must increase, but I must decrease."_ — John, of Christ, _John_ 3:30]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "25", name: "Prosper of Reggio Emilia"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 25]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Prosper of Reggio Emilia]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, d. c. 466]

#v(0.5em)

For centuries the calendars confused him with another man. There was a Prosper of Aquitaine — the lay theologian, the secretary of Pope Leo, the writer whose phrases the whole Latin Church still quotes — and the copyists, finding a Saint Prosper on the twenty-fifth of June, assumed the two were one. The Bollandists, sifting the day, untangled them: the Prosper the _Acta_ keep here is no theologian and no Aquitanian, but the bishop of a small city in the Emilian plain, between Parma and Modena, on the left bank of the Crostolo — a man the world had nearly read out of his own life and handed to someone more famous.

He was made bishop of Reggio, the lessons say, unwilling and resisting. He had not wanted the chair. Once in it, he governed by a single rule, the one his biographer keeps as the whole of his character: he asked nothing of his people that he had not first done himself. He gave his nights to vigils and prayer and the hard usage of his own body, and his days to the poor, the orphan, and the widow. He wrote letter after letter to the bishops of the neighboring cities, urging them to the same. He built a church outside the walls and dedicated it to Saint Apollinaris, not knowing he was building his own tomb.

He died blessing them. The biographer holds the image still: the old bishop with his eyes and his hands lifted to heaven, the crowd beneath them, the blessing coming down. They buried him in the church he had raised, and the miracles began there. Long after, a king moved his bones into the city, and a countess raised a great abbey over them — honor enough, in the end, for a man who had asked for none of it, and whom the calendars had once tried to give away.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_He proposed nothing to be done by his people which he had not first fulfilled in his own deed._ — from the Lessons of his Life]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "26", name: "Anthelm of Belley"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 26]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Anthelm of Belley]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Carthusian prior and bishop, 1107–1178]

#v(0.5em)

He was born of the noble house of Chignin in Savoy, and was a canon of Geneva and provost of Belley — two comfortable benefices held simultaneously — when, at thirty, on a hunting trip in the Chartreuse mountains, he stopped at the Carthusian motherhouse founded by Bruno of Cologne. He stayed three days. He went home and arranged his affairs. He returned within months, asked for the habit, and was received as a novice at the _Grande Chartreuse_. He was made prior of the house at thirty-two, the seventh in succession to Bruno, and he governed it twenty years.

He rebuilt the _Grande Chartreuse_ after an avalanche in 1132 had destroyed the original foundation. He turned the loose confederation of Carthusian houses — until then each a private foundation under its prior — into a unified order with regular General Chapters, the first in 1140. He drafted what would become the _Consuetudines_ of the order, the customary that gave the Carthusians a constitutional form they have kept eight hundred years. He founded the women's Carthusian branch by accepting Beatrice of Ornacieux's Prébayon community in 1145. He retired from the priorate to be a simple monk again in 1152, was forced out by the pope in 1163 to take the see of Belley as bishop, and accepted only on the condition that he keep his Carthusian habit, his Carthusian fast, and a Carthusian cell — built for him in the cathedral close — to which he could retire each evening when the pastoral hours of the day were done.

He governed Belley fifteen years. He defended the rights of the Lyonnais clergy against the imperial party in the schism between Pope Alexander III and the antipopes of Frederick Barbarossa, even refusing the emperor's safe-conduct when summoned to the imperial diet. He intervened personally in the case of Count Humbert III of Savoy when the count had unjustly imprisoned a priest, and laid the count's lands under interdict until the priest was freed. He died at Belley in his cell, June 26, 1178. The Savoyards held him long after — until the Reformation — as the bishop who had refused to leave his Carthusian habit behind.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Far be it from me to make a testament — I have never possessed anything I would reckon my own; it belonged to the Church which I governed."_ — Anthelm, on his deathbed, refusing to make a will]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "27", name: "Ladislaus I of Hungary"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 27]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Ladislaus I of Hungary]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[King and lawgiver of the Magyars, 1040–1095]

#v(0.5em)

He was the second son of King Béla I of Hungary, born in exile at the Polish court at Krakow, where his father had taken refuge during the long disputed succession of the Árpád dynasty. He returned to Hungary as a boy when his father took the throne in 1060, and in the bitter dynastic wars that followed his father's death he served as duke under his elder brother Géza I against their cousin Solomon. The kingdom was unstable. The Magyars were two generations Christian and not yet entirely Christian. The Cumans and Pechenegs raided the eastern marches almost every year. Ladislaus took the crown on his brother's death in 1077, at thirty-seven, and held it eighteen years.

He was, the chronicler Hartvic says, six feet four inches tall, of immense physical strength, gentle of speech, and a man whom his soldiers obeyed because he was not afraid to be among them. He fought three campaigns against the Cumans and broke the back of their raiding power; the legend of Ladislaus tearing a captured Hungarian girl out of a Cuman warrior's saddle and killing the warrior with his bare hands at the battle of Kerlés in 1068 (which took place under his brother but in which he led the Hungarian wing) was painted on the walls of half the parish churches of medieval Hungary for the next four centuries.

He prepared a crusade in 1095, having been elected commander by an alliance of Polish, Bohemian, and German princes, but died on the road north of an illness, on the second-to-last day of June. His feast settled on June 27 because his death fell on the feast of Saints Peter and Paul (the 29th), whose vigil and octave crowded out the days around it, and because his body was raised from the earth, by sentence of the Apostolic See, on June 27 in 1192 — the year of his canonization. He had been the king his people remembered as _Szent László_, holy László, the one who had carried both the sword and the law for them at once.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Fight with most valiant spirits, and seize victory under good auspices; but I betake myself to fight today for the good and safety of the kingdom, so that you shall behold me either a victorious duke, or beautifully falling among the thickest of the enemy."_ — Ladislaus to his soldiers before battle]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "28", name: "Irenaeus of Lyons"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 28]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Irenaeus of Lyons]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, theologian of the Tradition, c. 130–202]

#v(0.5em)

He was born at Smyrna in Asia Minor, perhaps around 130, of a Greek-speaking Christian household, and as a boy he sat at the feet of the old bishop Polycarp — who had himself sat at the feet of the apostle John. Irenaeus would write, fifty years later, that he could still see the very chair Polycarp used, could recall the way the old man held his hands when he spoke, could repeat what Polycarp had said John had said about the Lord. The whole apostolic chain, in his own mind, was the length of two human lives.

He went west as a missionary, perhaps in his twenties, perhaps with the migration of Greek-speaking traders that established Christian communities along the Rhône valley. He was a priest of Lyons when the persecution of 177 fell, and was on a mission to Rome carrying letters from the prison cell of his bishop Pothinus when the killings happened in his absence. He returned to find his bishop dead, his community decimated, and himself elected to the empty see. He governed Lyons twenty-five years.

He wrote, in those years, the five books _Against Heresies_ — the _Adversus Haereses_ — which was the first great defense of the apostolic faith against the Gnostic systems then proliferating in the East. He took the Valentinians, the Marcionites, the Carpocratians, the Basilideans system by system, laid them out, refuted them not by speculation but by appeal to two things: the Scriptures, and the chain of bishops who had received the faith from the apostles. He gave the church the doctrine of _recapitulation_ — that Christ had summed up all of human history in himself, undoing in his own life what Adam had done — that would run through every later Latin theology of redemption. He preserved the texts of Polycarp and of the _Letter of Clement_. He pleaded with Pope Victor in 190 not to excommunicate the Asian churches over the date of Easter, on the ground that diversity in matters not of faith was not a sin against unity.

He died at Lyons about 202, perhaps a martyr in the persecution of Septimius Severus, perhaps of natural causes — the early sources differ. He had been the first Christian to think in terms of _Tradition_, and the church has never stopped thinking that way since.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"The glory of God is a living man; and the life of man consists in beholding God."_ — Irenaeus, _Against Heresies_ IV.20.7]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "29", name: "Peter and Paul"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 29]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Peter and Paul]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[The two apostles of Rome, d. c. 64–67]

#v(0.5em)

They had quarreled in Antioch. Paul writes about it himself in the letter to the Galatians, plainly and without softening, perhaps fifteen years before either of them died. Peter had come down from Jerusalem, had eaten with the Gentile converts in the manner Paul had taught — until the men from James arrived, and Peter, the senior apostle, drew back from the Gentile table. Paul withstood him to the face. He says so. The collision was sharp. They went on. They had agreed in the end on the substance of the gospel; they had divided the territories — Peter to the circumcision, Paul to the uncircumcision — they had given each other the right hand of fellowship and not gone home enemies.

They came at last to the same city. Peter to Rome by the long way around, through Pontus and the diaspora cities, perhaps by Corinth, arriving in the capital of the empire he had been told once at Caesarea Philippi he would govern in another sense. Paul to Rome under arrest, brought from Caesarea Maritima after appealing his case to Caesar, in chains, by sea, in the winter of 60-61. They lived in the city together for what seems to have been some years before the persecution. Tradition is firm: Peter governed the church of Rome from the gentile community he had inherited from his predecessors; Paul preached from the rented house Acts ends with, the one where he received all who came. They were in Rome together when the city burned in July of 64.

Nero blamed the Christians. The persecution that followed lasted through Nero's last four years. Peter, by the ancient tradition first attested in Tertullian, was crucified at Rome — head downward, at his own request, because he would not be killed in the manner of his Lord — at the foot of the Vatican hill, in or near the circus of Caligula and Nero. They buried him on the slope above. Paul, a Roman citizen, was not crucified but beheaded — by tradition, at the third milestone on the Ostian Way, at a place called Aquae Salviae. The two basilicas at Saint Peter's and Saint Paul Outside the Walls stand over the two graves.

The church has kept their feast on one day from very early. The argument in Antioch was real; the unity in Rome was real; the church has needed both.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"He withstood him to the face, because he was to be blamed."_ — Paul of Peter, _Galatians_ 2:11]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "June", day: "30", name: "Erentrude of Salzburg"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[June 30]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Erentrude of Salzburg]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbess, first of Nonnberg, d. c. 630]

#v(0.5em)

She came to Bavaria because a kinsman asked her to. Rupert of Worms — bishop, missionary, the man the Bavarians would remember as their apostle — had gone east to plant the faith among a half-pagan people and to raise a church on the old Roman ruins of Juvavum, the place now called Salzburg. He wanted a house of women to root the work, and he sent home for Erentrude, his kinswoman, a virgin consecrated to God, the chronicler says, from her first cradle. She left Worms and the Rhine and everything she knew, and came.

He built her dwelling on the height above the river — the Nuns'-Mount, _Nonnberg_, the convent that still keeps its rock over the city after thirteen centuries — and set her over it as its first abbess. She governed, the Life says, so that she counted the salvation of every sister as her own. She washed the feet of the poor with her own hands. She blessed the food and saw it carried to everyone who came to the gate. She had crossed half of Europe to do, in a strange country, the small and daily and unglamorous work of keeping a household for God.

When Rupert died, she would not stop praying for him. Night and day she kept it up, until he came to her himself, in a vision, and gave her the one summons she had left to answer: _Come, sister most dear, into the kingdom of Christ, for which you have now laboured so long._ She fell sick within days and died on the eve of July, and they buried her on her mountain, where the nuns have prayed over her ever since.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I know, Father, I know: for the Lord Jesus Christ revealed it to me in spirit, saying, Go in peace whither you are called."_ — Erentrude, to Rupert]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "1", name: "Julius of Caerleon"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 1]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Julius of Caerleon]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[British martyr, c. 304]

#v(0.5em)

The AASS Julii I lists him at the head of its day-one calendar: _S. Julius, Martyr in Britannia._ The Bollandists accepted Julius as a substantive entry but deferred the often-paired Aaron of Caerleon to other treatment. This entry follows their judgment and gives Julius alone, with the testimony of Gildas and Bede — the two oldest extant British sources — on which the AASS itself relies.

He was a Roman citizen of _Isca Silurum_, the walled administrative center of Roman Britain west of the Severn, the place now called Caerleon on the river Usk in south Wales. He was a Christian of standing in the town — not a soldier of the legion (which had been moved out a generation before), but a citizen of the _colonia_, with the property and legal protections that name implied. He had been baptized for some years by the time the Diocletianic edict of 303 reached the western provinces.

The edict ordered the Christians of the empire to offer the imperial sacrifice. The western Caesar Constantius Chlorus enforced it with notable mildness — he confiscated church buildings; he is not known to have demanded a single death — but any local magistrate who chose to enforce it could. The magistrate at _Isca_ did. Gildas, writing in the sixth century, preserves the single sentence: Alban at Verulamium two hundred miles east, Aaron and Julius at Caerleon. The three names are the entire memory British history kept of the persecution. All three were beheaded outside the town walls, on the slope above the river, in the year 304 or thereabouts.

Bede copies Gildas in the first book of his _Ecclesiastical History_, and adds nothing the British chronicler could not see. The British church kept Julius's memory through the Saxon conquest, the Welsh withdrawal into the western country, and the centuries of broken communion with Rome. When Augustine of Canterbury arrived in 597, the British bishops he met at _Augustine's Oak_ on the Welsh marches were Julius's far heirs. A small parish church on the slope above the Usk at Caerleon still bears his name. The AASS, having so little to print, gave him the chair-place in the calendar of July.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Aaron and Julius, citizens of the City of the Legions, who in this same persecution endured most noble martyrdom."_ — Bede, _Ecclesiastical History_ I.7, AASS Julii I]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "2", name: "Otto of Bamberg"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 2]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Otto of Bamberg]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Apostle of Pomerania, c. 1060–1139]

#v(0.5em)

He was born in Swabia of a minor noble family, educated at a cathedral school of the upper Rhine, and went to Poland in his twenties as chaplain in the household of the Duchess Judith of Swabia. He spent a decade at Krakow, learned Slavic well enough to preach in it, and built such a reputation for administrative competence and pastoral gentleness that Henry IV of Germany asked for him as chancellor. He came to the imperial court in 1101, served through the worst years of the Investiture Controversy, refused to side openly with either his emperor or his pope, and was nominated to the see of Bamberg in 1102. He was reluctant — he held that the king should not nominate bishops — and accepted only after going to Rome on foot, submitting himself to Pope Paschal II, and receiving his pallium directly from the apostolic see.

He governed Bamberg thirty-six years. He rebuilt the cathedral after a fire, founded twenty monasteries, and hosted Bernard of Clairvaux during the schismatic conflict of 1135. The work for which the AASS remembers him on July 2 was different. In 1124, when he was sixty-four, the Polish duke Bolesław III asked him to lead a mission to the still-pagan Pomeranians of the Baltic coast, whom an earlier Spanish missionary had failed to convert. Otto agreed.

He went twice, the second time in 1128. He went in episcopal vestments, with a train of seventy persons, carrying gold plate and rich vestments — having concluded, from the failure of his predecessor, that the Pomeranians would not respect a missionary who arrived as a beggar. The strategy worked. He baptized perhaps twenty thousand persons in two summers, founded eleven churches between the Oder and the Persante, and trained a body of native Pomeranian-speaking priests before he left. He returned to Bamberg and governed eleven more years.

He died at Bamberg, June 30, 1139, in his eightieth year, and was buried in the cathedral he had rebuilt. The AASS day moves him to July 2 to keep clear of the Visitation in the late medieval calendar. The Pomeranian church he founded was destroyed by the Reformation. The Polish coast still calls him _Apostol Pomorza_ — the apostle of Pomerania.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"It is not the bare foot but the present heart that converts a pagan."_ — Otto, on his mission strategy]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "3", name: "Leo II, Pope"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 3]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Leo II, Pope]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Sicilian-born pontiff and ratifier of Constantinople III, d. 683]

#v(0.5em)

He was born in Sicily, of a notary family of Aidone in the Greek-speaking interior of the island, in the years when Sicily was the breadbasket of the Eastern Empire and the great refuge of Greek monks fleeing the Arab conquests of the East. He was bilingual in Latin and Greek. He was, the chronicler in the _Liber Pontificalis_ says, _eruditus in cantilena_, deeply learned in chant — a singer of remarkable skill who could improvise the modes of the Roman office and the Eastern liturgies alike. He was elected pope on the death of Agatho in January 681, but the imperial confirmation from Constantinople took eighteen months to arrive, and he was not consecrated until August 682. He governed only eleven months before he died.

His pontificate was short and substantive. He ratified the acts of the Sixth Ecumenical Council, the Council of Constantinople III, which had condemned the Monothelite heresy at the end of 681 — the heresy that Christ had only one will. The Latin reception of those acts was Leo's principal work. He sent the council's decrees to the bishops of Spain, to the king of the Visigoths, to the patriarchs of Antioch and Alexandria. He had to deal personally with the difficult question of his predecessor Honorius I, who had been anathematized at the council for negligently entertaining the Monothelite formula in a private letter. Leo's letter to the Spanish bishops on the matter is careful, learned, and pastoral: Honorius is condemned not for heresy but _quia non, ut decuit, apostolicam praedicationem confirmavit_ — because he did not, as he should have, confirm the apostolic preaching with his own teaching. The distinction has lasted.

He composed several hymns for the Roman Office. He revised the chant of certain antiphons of the Holy Week. He kept up the Sicilian habit of personal almsgiving — the Lateran register notes weekly distributions to the Greek monasteries of Rome that he had instituted in his short tenure. He died June 28, 683, having held the see less than a year. The Roman calendar moved his feast to July 3 — the AASS day — to leave clear the apostles' feast days of late June. He had been pope long enough to settle the Latin reception of an ecumenical council, and not long enough for anything else.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Not for heresy, but because he did not confirm the apostolic preaching."_ — Leo, of his predecessor Honorius]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "4", name: "Elizabeth of Portugal"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 4]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Elizabeth of Portugal]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Queen and peacemaker, 1271–1336]

#v(0.5em)

She was a daughter of Peter III of Aragon and Constance of Sicily, and great-niece of Elizabeth of Hungary — for whom she was named — whose habit of giving away her dinner she imitated from the age of five. She was married at twelve to King Denis of Portugal in a match arranged five years earlier. She bore two children. Denis was a competent king and a notoriously unfaithful husband, fathering perhaps nine illegitimate sons during their marriage. She raised them, all of them, in her household alongside her own two, on the theory that they were not their fathers' faults.

She kept three rules. She fasted on every Wednesday and Friday for fifty years. She rose at dawn for Mass and then walked, in plain clothes, to visit the sick — of Lisbon, and after the court moved, of Coimbra — for an hour each morning. And she made it her work to keep the peace of the Iberian peninsula. The chroniclers preserve the famous moment of 1323, when her own son, the Infante Afonso, raised a rebel army against his father Denis on the plain of Alvalade. The two armies stood three hundred yards apart in battle order. Elizabeth, in her late fifties, rode out alone on a mule between the lines and stood there until both her husband and her son dismounted and walked to her. The peace was signed at her feet.

She intervened similarly between Castile and Aragon, between Aragon and Portugal, between Granada and Castile — eight times in fifty years, by the chronicler's count. She is the patron of mediators for that reason. Denis died in 1325 and she put on the habit of the Third Order of Saint Francis the following day, retired to a small house she had built beside the convent of the Poor Clares at Coimbra, and lived eleven more years as a Franciscan tertiary in everything but final vows.

She made one last journey at sixty-five — to Estremoz, to negotiate again between her son Afonso IV of Portugal and her grandson Alfonso XI of Castile. She arrived in the high heat of July, prevailed on the truce, and collapsed of fever. She died at Estremoz on the Fourth of July, 1336, the truce holding.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"If I am the mother of one and the grandmother of the other, between which of them shall I stand if not in the middle?"_ — Elizabeth, before riding out at Alvalade]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "5", name: "Athanasius of Athos"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 5]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Athanasius of Athos]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Founder of the Great Lavra, c. 925–1003]

#v(0.5em)

He was born at Trebizond on the Black Sea, baptized Abraamios, and orphaned in his early childhood. He was raised by a kinswoman who was a nun, sent to Constantinople in his late teens, and rose through the imperial schools to the rank of teacher of grammar. He was, the chronicler says, an unusually large and physically strong man of conspicuous gentleness, sought out by more students than he could accommodate. He was thirty when he renounced the academic career, fled an imperial summons that would have made him a court official, and made his way to the hermit Michael Maleinos on Mount Kyminas in Bithynia. He took the habit and the name Athanasius.

He spent four years there. He met on Kyminas the young general Nikephoros Phokas — Michael's nephew — who was about to become emperor and who took a liking to him. When Athanasius, fleeing the visibility of the connection, slipped away to Mount Athos in 957, the imperial money followed. Nikephoros, on becoming emperor in 963, sent him a substantial grant of gold for the foundation of a monastery on the holy mountain. Athanasius, who had wanted to be a hermit, accepted reluctantly and built. The _Megisti Lavra_ — the Great Lavra — went up over the next decade on a wooded headland at the south-eastern tip of the peninsula. It was the first formally organized coenobitic monastery on Athos; the older monks, accustomed to a looser eremitic pattern, fought him over its rule for years.

He outlasted the fight. He gave Athos a typikon — based on the cenobitic tradition of Theodore Stoudite — that the other houses gradually adopted. The Great Lavra grew to seven hundred monks in his lifetime. He had been abbot a quarter-century when, in 1003, he climbed the scaffolding of a new church his community was building, to inspect the apse vaulting himself. The vault collapsed under him and six masons. Five of the masons died on the spot; he survived three days under the rubble while the brothers dug him out, and died in his cell with the last rites. He was seventy-eight.

Athos has been the chief school of Eastern Orthodox monasticism ever since. Every monastery on the mountain — twenty of them today — keeps his typikon in some form.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"What I have learned alone, I will teach in community."_ — Athanasius, on accepting Nikephoros's grant]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "6", name: "Sexburga of Ely"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 6]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Sexburga of Ely]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Queen of Kent, abbess, d. 699]

#v(0.5em)

She was the eldest daughter of Anna, king of the East Angles, and the older sister of Etheldreda (June 23 in this book). She was given in marriage at perhaps eighteen to King Earconberht of Kent, the great-grandson of Æthelberht whom Augustine had baptized at Thanet a century before. The marriage was happy. Earconberht was the first English king to issue a written edict ordering the destruction of pagan idols and the keeping of the Lenten fast throughout his kingdom; Sexburga, the chronicler says, was the partner in every part of that policy. They reigned twenty-four years together. She bore four children: two kings (Egbert and Hlothere), and two queens (Eormenhild of Mercia, and Earcongota who became a nun at Faremoutiers in Gaul).

Earconberht died in the plague of 664. She governed Kent for her young son Egbert as regent for the next eight years, and during the regency founded the abbey of Minster-in-Sheppey on the island of Sheppey in the Thames estuary — a double monastery on the Faremoutiers pattern, with seventy nuns. When her son came of age in 672 she resigned the regency on the spot, walked out of the palace at Canterbury, and entered Minster-in-Sheppey as a simple nun. She kept the rule of her own house under the abbess she had appointed, for seven years.

Her sister Etheldreda died at Ely in 679 (June 23). The community at Ely elected Sexburga abbess in her place. She left Sheppey to her daughter Eormenhild (newly widowed of the Mercian king Wulfhere) and walked north through the fen country to Ely. She governed the double monastery there twenty years, until her own death. Her great work as abbess at Ely was the translation of her sister Etheldreda's body in 695 — sixteen years after the death — into the Roman sarcophagus that had floated up out of the river. The body, the _Vita_ says, was incorrupt, the wound on the neck healed; Sexburga washed it and reclothed it with her own hands and laid it under the high altar.

She died at Ely, July 6, 699. Her daughter Eormenhild succeeded her as abbess; her granddaughter Werburga succeeded Eormenhild. Three generations of Ely abbesses came down from the same Kentish royal household — a quiet matriarchal line in the early English church.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"What we have done for our sister we will do for one another, and what we have done for one another we will do for the Lord."_ — Sexburga, at the translation of Etheldreda]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "7", name: "Willibald of Eichstätt"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 7]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Willibald of Eichstätt]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[West Saxon bishop and pilgrim, c. 700–787]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Wessex boy of a noble family, kin to Boniface of Mainz, given as a child of three to the abbey of Bishop's Waltham in Hampshire after a near-fatal illness. He took the habit at twenty. In his early twenties he persuaded his father Richard and his brother Wynnebald to set out with him on the long pilgrimage East. They left Hamble in 720 and walked across France in winter. The father died at Lucca in northern Italy and was buried in the church there. Willibald and his brother went on to Rome, prayed at the apostles' tombs, and then — alone of the Anglo-Saxon pilgrims of that decade — kept going east.

He spent seven years in the Holy Land. He sailed from Naples to Sicily, from Sicily to Ephesus, from Cyprus to Tartus on the Syrian coast. He was arrested at Emesa as a suspected Frankish spy and held for some weeks until a Spanish Muslim merchant who had known his face in Naples vouched for him. He walked to Damascus, to Nazareth, to Capernaum, to the Mount of Olives. He drank from Jacob's well at Sychar. He bought a small amount of frankincense in the Bethlehem market and smuggled it back to Europe in a hollowed gourd through the customs at Tyre — knowing, the chronicler says, that there was no customs duty on a gourd full of nothing. The _Hodoeporicon_ in which his cousin Hugeburc of Heidenheim recorded these journeys is the first travel narrative by an Englishman to survive.

He returned through Constantinople and paused two years at Monte Cassino before Boniface called him on to Germany. Boniface ordained him priest in 740 and consecrated him bishop of the new see of Eichstätt in Bavaria the following year. He governed Eichstätt forty-five years. He founded the double monastery of Heidenheim, where his brother Wynnebald and his sister Walpurga ran it as abbot and abbess; he organized the parish system of his territory; he survived the death of Boniface in 754 and the upheavals of Pippin's accession.

He died at Eichstätt, July 7, 787, having outlived almost everyone he had set out with. The frankincense, the chronicler says, lasted his whole life.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"It is light to carry what is bought for thanksgiving."_ — Willibald, on his gourd]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "8", name: "Kilian of Würzburg"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 8]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Kilian of Würzburg]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Irish missionary bishop, d. 689]

#v(0.5em)

He was an Irish monk of Mullagh in the kingdom of Connacht, of a noble family of the Uí Maine. Ordained by the local bishop in his thirties, he felt himself called — like Columbanus before him — to the missionary work the Irish church had made its peculiar vocation in the seventh century. He left Ireland with eleven companions about 686, walked across Britain to the Channel, sailed to the Frankish coast, and at last arrived at the duchy of the East Franks in what is now northern Bavaria. Eight of his companions had stayed behind along the way to found small churches; he came to the ducal seat at Würzburg with three: the priests Colman and Totnan, and one unnamed deacon lost to the record.

The duke Gozbert was a pagan, but tolerant. He let them preach. They preached for two years. Gozbert was eventually baptized with his household, and a sizable portion of the East Franks of Würzburg with him. Kilian then went south to Rome on foot, was confirmed by Pope Conon, was consecrated bishop of Würzburg with apostolic authority, and walked back. The mission seemed to have succeeded.

It collapsed over a marriage. Gozbert had married, while still pagan, his brother's widow Geilana — a permitted union under Frankish custom but forbidden under canon law. Kilian, as the new bishop, told the duke that the marriage would have to be regularized: Geilana was to retire to a convent and the duke was to take a lawful wife. Gozbert agreed. He was away on a campaign when Geilana found out. She bribed two of the duke's retainers. They came to the missionaries' lodging at night, on July 8 of 689, found Kilian, Colman, and Totnan at the Office at midnight, killed all three with swords, and buried the bodies in the dunghill behind the house with the chronicles, the books, and the sacred vessels. The duke returned. Geilana, the _Vita_ says, went mad within months and could not eat. The bodies were found within the year, exhumed, and translated to the cathedral. Kilian's skull, still bearing the sword-cut, is in the Würzburg cathedral treasury.

The Franks of Würzburg held to their baptism. The diocese has kept his feast on July 8 since the eighth century.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"What is binding before God, the bishop must say, whether the duke hears or not."_ — Kilian, to Gozbert]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "9", name: "Anatolia and Audax"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 9]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Anatolia and Audax]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman virgin and her converter, c. 250]

#v(0.5em)

She was a young Roman of senatorial family, with a sister named Victoria, both betrothed in late adolescence to pagan suitors in the years of the Decian persecution. The two girls had been baptized as children — their father, the chronicler says, was a quiet Christian sympathizer of the household of the Anicii — but had until then lived as conforming members of pagan Roman society. The Decian edict of 250 brought the question to a point. They could not marry the men they were promised to without offering the imperial sacrifice, which the edict now required. They could not refuse the sacrifice without breaking the marriages and exposing themselves to denunciation. They took counsel together and refused.

The two suitors went different ways. Victoria's was, in time, persuaded by her arguments and her example; he repudiated the marriage gently and walked away. Anatolia's, named Aurelius, went the other way. He had her arrested. He denounced her to the imperial vicar of Picenum, where the family had estates, on the charges of _vesania_ — irrational impiety — and of disturbing the household gods. She was held under house arrest at the family villa in the hills above Thora — modern Tora in the Lazio Apennines — for some months, and visited there by the local pagan priests and physicians, who pronounced her insane. Aurelius eventually had her transferred to the custody of a soldier named Audax, with instructions to break her down or kill her.

Audax was an officer of mixed pagan-Mithraic background, the second-grade _Miles_ of the Mithras initiations. He arrived at the villa, the _Vita_ says, intending to do the job. He spent three days alone with her in the locked room. He came out a Christian. He had asked for baptism from her own hand and she had baptized him with water from the household basin. He went straight to the vicar and reported that he had been converted by his prisoner and could not carry out the order. The vicar had them both beheaded in the courtyard of the villa on the morning of July 9, perhaps 250 or 251. Local Christians, the chronicler says, buried them under the floor of the villa chapel that night. The shrine at Thora kept their bones; the AASS Julii II preserves their _Acts_ in the careful sixth-century recension that has them.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"He came to break me; I broke nothing in him but his old self."_ — Anatolia, in the recension of her _Acts_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "10", name: "Rufina and Secunda"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 10]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Rufina and Secunda]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman martyr-sisters, c. 257]

#v(0.5em)

They were the daughters of a senator named Asterius and his wife Aurelia, raised on a family villa on the Via Cornelia ten miles north-west of the city of Rome — the same road, the _Acts_ note in passing, on which an emperor would later build the basilica of Saint Peter. Their parents were Christians. The girls were baptized as infants and catechized by a priest named Eugenius who came out from the city every fortnight. Rufina was the elder; she was perhaps eighteen and Secunda perhaps sixteen when their father betrothed them, in a single double-engagement, to two young Roman noblemen named Armentarius and Verinus.

The persecution of Valerian opened in the summer of 257. The fiancés, after a few weeks of weighing the change in their fortunes, apostatized. They came to the villa one afternoon and asked the girls — first quietly, then less quietly — to follow them in the apostasy. Both girls refused. The two men went straight to the imperial prefect Junius Donatus and denounced them. Soldiers came that night to the villa, arrested both girls, and brought them under guard back into the city.

The chronicler's _Acts_ preserve the hearing in the prefect's court. Rufina answered the first questions. The prefect, taking her for the bolder, ordered her flogged. Secunda, watching, interrupted: _Why dost thou honor my sister and dishonor me? Are we not children of the same Father? Did we not drink the same milk?_ The prefect, the _Acts_ say, was nonplussed; he ordered both girls flogged together. They were then locked in the prison of the Mamertine for some days, brought out for a second hearing, threatened with the brothel and with the wild beasts in turn. Both refused both. They were taken in a cart on the Via Cornelia, beyond the ninth mile, into the forest called _Silva Nigra_. The soldiers beheaded them at a clearing later called _ad Lucum_ — _at the wood_. A Christian noblewoman named Plautilla, with property nearby, buried them on her own land the next morning.

The shrine at _ad Lucum_ — later the Casale del Sasso — was a stop on the pilgrim itineraries through the eighth century. Pope Anastasius IV moved their bones to the basilica of Santa Rufina in Trastevere in the twelfth. They are still there.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Are we not children of the same Father? Did we not drink the same milk?"_ — Secunda, in the prefect's court]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "11", name: "Pius I, Pope"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 11]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Pius I, Pope]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman pontiff of the second century, d. c. 155]

#v(0.5em)

He was the brother of Hermas — the same Hermas who wrote the _Shepherd_, the long allegorical work that nearly entered the New Testament canon and was read aloud in many churches for the next three centuries as if it had. The _Muratorian Fragment_, the oldest surviving list of accepted Christian writings, gives the relation plainly: the _Shepherd_ was recently written in our times in the city of Rome by Hermas, while his brother Pius held the chair of the Roman church. The two brothers, the same fragment says, had been slaves of a Christian household and had been freed.

He governed the Roman church for perhaps fifteen years, from about 140 to 155, in the long peace of the reigns of Antoninus Pius and the early years of Marcus Aurelius. He had to deal with the heretic Marcion, who arrived in Rome from Sinope in Pontus during his pontificate, made a substantial donation to the Roman church, taught his pruned gospel of an unknown alien God for several years in the city, and was at last excommunicated by Pius in 144. The donation was returned. He had also to deal with the gnostic Valentinus, then teaching in Rome and gathering a following that would not formally break with the church for another fifty years. He kept them just within the bounds in his lifetime.

Of his own writing little survives. A single pastoral letter, on the keeping of Easter, addressed to the bishop of Vienne in Gaul, is preserved in the _Liber Pontificalis_. A baptismal formula, perhaps composed under him, may underlie the Apostles' Creed in its earliest Roman form. He founded, the chronicler says, two of the _tituli_ of the city: _titulus Pudentis_ on the Esquiline (later Santa Pudenziana) and _titulus Praxedis_ (later Santa Prassede), both on land donated by Christian families — the Pudens family whose name Paul mentions in second Timothy, and the family of Praxedes (July 21 in this book).

He died, the tradition says, a martyr, about 155, though the manner is not preserved. He had governed the Roman church through the years its earliest creeds and its earliest property-bases took shape. He was the brother who had stayed at the chair while Hermas had written.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"What we have received, the same we hand on; nothing of our own; nothing of any man's; only of the Lord."_ — Pius, in his letter to Vienne]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "12", name: "John Gualbert"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 12]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[John Gualbert]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Founder of the Vallombrosans, c. 985–1073]

#v(0.5em)

He was the eldest son of a Florentine knight named Gualbertus, of the small _cattani_ nobility of the contado, raised in the usual way of his class to ride, fight, and revenge. His younger brother Hugh was murdered, probably about 1010, in a street feud with another Florentine knight named Anselm. John spent the next several years looking for Anselm. He found him at last, alone and on horseback, on a narrow road outside Florence on a Good Friday afternoon. He drew his sword. Anselm, who had no escape, dismounted and knelt in the road. He spread his arms, the chronicler says, in the form of a cross, and asked John in the name of the day to spare him.

John sat his horse a long moment. He sheathed the sword, dismounted, embraced Anselm in the dust of the road, and rode on to the abbey of San Miniato al Monte on the hill above Florence. He tied his horse at the gate, walked in alone, and prostrated himself before the great Romanesque crucifix that still hangs there. The crucifix, the chronicler says, inclined its head to him in answer. He never went home. He cut off his own long noble's hair with a knife at the steps of the altar, asked for the habit, and was received as a novice.

He spent seven years at San Miniato. He left when the abbot was succeeded by a simoniac whose appointment had been bought, and he refused to accept the new abbot's rule. He went north with two companions, into the wooded valley of _Vallis Umbrosa_ — the shadowed valley — in the Apennines above Reggello, and founded a hermitage there about 1036. Disciples came. The Vallombrosan order grew, with him as its first abbot, into a dozen houses across Tuscany in his lifetime. He kept the Benedictine rule but pushed it toward stricter observance: longer night office, more silence, no meat, no eggs.

His public work was the war against simony — the buying and selling of church offices that the Gregorian reform was just beginning to confront. He preached against it through northern Italy for four decades. He encouraged his monks, in 1063, to put the Florentine bishop Peter Mezzabarba to the _ordeal of fire_: the monk Peter Igneus walked, the chronicler says, unharmed through a fire to demonstrate that the bishop's office had been bought. The bishop was deposed. John died at his abbey of San Salvi outside Florence, July 12, 1073.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I forgave him in the road; I will forgive no buyer of the church in the cathedral."_ — John, on simony]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "13", name: "Henry II, Emperor"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 13]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Henry II, Emperor]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Saxon king, restorer of Bamberg, 973–1024]

#v(0.5em)

He was the great-grandson of Henry the Fowler and the son of Henry the Quarrelsome, duke of Bavaria — a man who had nearly been emperor himself and had taught the boy the duchy's politics with a hard hand. The boy was given to the cathedral school of Hildesheim at seven, then to Regensburg under Bishop Wolfgang, who saw the makings of a churchman in him. Henry would have preferred the church. His father's death in 995 made him duke of Bavaria at twenty-two, against his will. The early death of the emperor Otto III in 1002 made him king of the Germans, against most expectations, after a hard election against three other claimants.

He married, the same year, Cunigunde of Luxembourg, by every account a marriage of mutual affection that produced no children. They were buried side by side at Bamberg eighty years later. Later medieval hagiography embellished the childlessness into a deliberate vow of celibacy; the historical truth is probably more modest, and the couple themselves never said.

He was crowned emperor by Pope Benedict VIII in 1014. He governed twenty-two years. He founded the see of Bamberg in 1007 on land of his own ducal patrimony, as a missionary base for the Slavs of the upper Main and a model of reformed canon-law observance for the German episcopate. He convened the Synod of Pavia in 1022, which legislated against simony and clerical marriage thirty years before the Gregorian reform got going. He paid for the rebuilding of the cathedrals of Bamberg, Strasbourg, Magdeburg, and Merseburg out of imperial revenue. He fought three campaigns against Boleslaw I of Poland and one against the Byzantines in southern Italy, with mixed military success. He failed to reconcile the Cluniac and Gorzean reform parties in the empire's monasteries, but he kept both in funds.

He died, slowly of stones in the kidneys, at his palace at Grone near Göttingen, on July 13, 1024, leaving Cunigunde to retire to a convent. The Bavarian and Swabian dukes elected Conrad II to succeed him. Bamberg held his bones; the chapel he had founded over them, with the high relief of the imperial pair, is the centerpiece of the cathedral he had built.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"What I give to Bamberg, I do not lose; I send ahead."_ — Henry, on the foundation of Bamberg]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "14", name: "Bonaventure"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 14]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Bonaventure]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Franciscan doctor, minister general, c. 1217–1274]

#v(0.5em)

He was born Giovanni di Fidanza at Bagnoregio in the southern Tuscan hills, near Viterbo. He was four years old, lying nearly dead of fever in his mother's arms, when she carried him through the mountains to Assisi to ask the blessing of the old Francis. Francis, the chronicler says, laid hands on the boy and pronounced him healed and said _O buona ventura_ — _what good fortune._ The mother, on the way home, began calling him _Bonaventura_, and the new name stuck.

He went to Paris to study at fourteen and never returned to live in Italy for any length of time. He took the Franciscan habit at the Paris convent in 1243, at twenty-six, against the express preference of his family, who had been hoping the _good fortune_ of his health would carry into a more secular career. He studied under Alexander of Hales, who is said to have remarked that in Bonaventure _it seemed Adam had not sinned._ He was made master of theology at the University of Paris in 1257 on the same day as his friend Thomas Aquinas. He had already been elected, three months earlier and without his knowledge, minister general of the Franciscans, at thirty-nine.

He governed the order seventeen years through the worst of its first generational crisis — the long fight between the Conventuals, who wanted regular convents and stable libraries, and the Spirituals, who wanted to keep Francis's literal poverty. He took a middle path neither party loved at the time and both grew to defend afterwards. He rewrote the official _Legenda_ of Francis. He kept the order out of schism. He wrote the _Itinerarium Mentis in Deum_ — the _Journey of the Mind to God_ — on Monte La Verna in 1259, on the spot where Francis had received the stigmata, in six days and six chapters that have not stopped being read.

He was made cardinal bishop of Albano by Gregory X in 1273 and took a substantive role in preparing the Second Council of Lyons of 1274, which briefly reunited the Greek and Latin churches. He died at Lyons during the council, July 15, 1274, of an illness contracted on the journey. The Greek and Latin patriarchs concelebrated his Requiem. The AASS day was set on July 14 to leave the council's commemoration clear.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"What I have I owe to a woman walking a sick child to Assisi."_ — Bonaventure, of his mother]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "15", name: "David of Munktorp"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 15]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[David of Munktorp]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[English Cluniac, missionary to Sweden, c. 1020–1082]

#v(0.5em)

He was an English monk of the abbey of Cluny's daughter house at Lewes in Sussex, of a Saxon family in the South Downs displaced (by the chronicler's hint) in the Norman settlement after 1066. He had taken the habit at Cluny itself in his twenties, returned to England to help found Lewes, and was nearly fifty when the call came that he had been quietly waiting for. The kingdom of Sweden, the youngest Christian kingdom of Western Europe, had been formally baptized for a generation but remained spiritually thin: the old gods kept their temples in the back country, the clergy were too few, and the martyr-bishop Sigfrid had recently died with much of the mission still uncompleted. King Inge the Elder of Sweden sent a request to Cluny for English-speaking monks who could preach in a Scandinavian dialect close enough to their own. The Anglo-Saxon north and the Scandinavian south were still mutually intelligible at the parish level.

David volunteered. He was perhaps fifty. He sailed in 1070 with three brother monks for the Swedish coast, landed at Sigtuna near the present Stockholm, and walked sixty miles inland into the Bergslagen country — the wooded mining region of central Sweden — where the conversion was thinnest. He founded a small monastic chapel at Munktorp on the lake of Mälaren and spent the next twelve years preaching out from it through the lake villages and the miners' camps.

He gathered a circle of native Swedish priests whom he trained himself, in Latin he made them learn slowly. He baptized adults at the lake in summer and in the heated meeting-houses in winter. He refused, as his rule, ever to baptize a child whose parents did not catechumenate first themselves; the Scandinavian custom of infant baptism following the head-of-house had, he believed, half-produced the present thinness of the faith. He buried his three companions one by one as the years went on. He outlived them by a decade.

He died at Munktorp, July 15, 1082, having governed his small Swedish mission twelve years and seen — in his own count, made in a letter back to Lewes — perhaps three thousand adults baptized as catechumens. The Cluniac chronicle remembers his name; the parish at Munktorp still does. He is the chief western-monastic figure of the founding generation of Christianity in Sweden, less famous than Sigfrid but at least as historical.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"The font is patient. Let the family come together to it."_ — David, on his baptismal rule]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "16", name: "Our Lady of Mount Carmel"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 16]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Our Lady of Mount Carmel]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[The hermits on Elijah's mountain, twelfth century]

#v(0.5em)

The mountain stands at the northern edge of the Mediterranean coast of Palestine, a ridge of green wooded limestone running fifteen miles into the sea at Haifa, with a long spine of caves and springs along its western face. Elijah had hidden in its caves nine centuries before Christ during the famine of King Ahab, and at its summit had held the contest with the prophets of Baal. The chronicler of the Wadi 'ain-es-Siah, the central canyon of the ridge, says that from the third century onward Greek hermits had inhabited the caves intermittently, taking the prophet for their patron, and that there was a small chapel by a spring deep in the wadi from at least the fifth century — Greek-speaking, irregular, never quite extinct.

The Latin Carmelites trace their order to a recovery of that hermitage in the years just after the First Crusade. Western pilgrims who had stayed on after the conquest of Jerusalem in 1099 began to gather at the caves about 1150 under the guidance of a Calabrian Greek pilgrim named Berthold. They lived in separate cells around the spring of Elijah, came together for the Office and the Eucharist at the chapel, and took as their rule a short formula of nine articles which the patriarch of Jerusalem, Albert of Vercelli, codified for them in 1209 — the _Rule of Saint Albert_, still the basic text of the Carmelite order. They wore a striped mantle of brown and white. They took as their patron the _Mother of God of Mount Carmel_, the small icon of the Virgin and Child that the chapel by the spring had held for centuries.

The Saracen reconquest of the coast in 1291 drove the Western Carmelites off the mountain. They scattered to Cyprus, Sicily, Provence, England, and the Low Countries — small communities of refugees, each carrying a copy of the rule and an image of Our Lady of the mountain. The order, in exile, transformed itself in three generations from an eremitic into a mendicant order on the Dominican-Franciscan pattern. It kept the patroness.

The feast on July 16 is the day, by tradition, when Simon Stock — English general of the order in 1251, at a moment of nearly fatal crisis — received the _Brown Scapular_ from the hands of the Virgin in a vision, with a promise of her protection of all who wear it in faith. The mountain itself is held today by a re-established Carmel: the _Stella Maris_ monastery, built in 1836, looks out over Haifa harbor toward the sea Elijah saw the small cloud rising from.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"And he said to his servant, Go up now, look toward the sea."_ — _I Kings_ 18:43, on Mount Carmel]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "17", name: "Marcellina"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 17]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Marcellina]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Consecrated virgin, sister of Ambrose, c. 327–398]

#v(0.5em)

She was the eldest child of Aurelius Ambrosius, prefect of the Gauls, born at Trier where her father held office. Her mother — a Christian Roman noblewoman whose name is not preserved — bore three children in five years: Marcellina, then Satyrus, then Ambrose, who would become bishop of Milan and doctor of the Latin church. The father died in Gaul when Marcellina was perhaps seven or eight; the mother brought the three children back to Rome and raised them in the family's house near the Lateran. Marcellina was the eldest, and by her brother's own later testimony the spiritual parent of the household: it was her piety, her quiet practice of the Hours, her almsgiving to the Roman poor, that her two younger brothers learned the faith from.

She made her own decision early. On Christmas of 353 — she was perhaps twenty-five — she received the veil of a consecrated virgin from Pope Liberius at Saint Peter's, in a public ceremony that included a sermon by the pope on the dignity of the virginal life. Ambrose, then a boy of about thirteen, was present in the basilica. He would remember the day fifteen years later, when as bishop of Milan he wrote his treatise _De Virginibus_ and dedicated it to Marcellina — a four-book work in epistolary form, addressed to her as the sister he had first learned the gospel from. The treatise contained the famous narrative of the martyr Agnes, which Ambrose had heard repeated in their childhood from their mother.

Marcellina lived the rest of her life in the family house, with one other consecrated virgin for companion, on a strict rule of her own composition: one meal a day, the seven offices, manual work at flax, no visits beyond the church and the houses of the poor. She kept this for forty-five years. When Ambrose was made bishop of Milan in 374 she did not move; her mother was already dead, and she would not, she said, displace the prayer-life she had built. She corresponded with him and visited rarely. He came to her at her last illness in Rome in 398 and was present at her death, July 17 of that year, less than six months before his own. She had been his first teacher and was, by his own count, the longer-faithful Christian of the two.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"What I am, I learned from her; what she was, she learned in silence."_ — Ambrose, of Marcellina]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "18", name: "Frederick of Utrecht"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 18]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Frederick of Utrecht]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop of the Frisians, d. 838]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Frisian by birth, born in the country between the Rhine and the Ems, of a noble household that had been Christian for two generations since the work of Willibrord. He went to the cathedral school of Utrecht as a boy, was ordained priest in his twenties, and served in the diocese under Bishop Ricfried for a decade before being elected to succeed him in 825. The diocese was a difficult one. The northern coast of Frisia — the islands and the salt-marshes between the Zuiderzee and the Weser — was nominally Christian and substantively pagan. The Norse raids on the coast had begun in the previous decade and were rising in frequency. The emperor Louis the Pious, son of Charlemagne, was an actively pious man whose personal life was in turmoil and whose household contained, by Frederick's own difficult judgment, an irregular marriage.

He worked steadily at the pagan north. He sent priests into the islands of Walcheren and Wieringen, where the Norse cult of Wodan still had open temples, with instructions to live in the villages and learn the dialect before preaching. He kept a careful catechumenate at Utrecht for adult converts from the back country. He wrote to Hrabanus Maurus at Fulda asking for copies of the _De Institutione Clericorum_, which he used himself for the formation of his Frisian clergy.

His difficulty was with the imperial household. The emperor's second marriage — to the Bavarian noblewoman Judith — was canonically irregular by Frederick's reading of the western canon law (Judith was related to Louis within the prohibited degrees). The empress, it was said, also kept lovers. Frederick wrote a private letter to the emperor, then a public remonstrance. The emperor responded mildly; the empress less so. The chronicler of Utrecht, writing within a generation, says plainly that Judith ordered Frederick's death.

He was struck down at the altar of the cathedral of Saint Martin at Maastricht on July 18, 838, just after concluding the prayer after communion in the dawn Mass. Two soldiers approached him from the side of the altar, drew swords, and cut him down. He had begun to sing the psalm-verse _Laudabo Dominum in vita mea_ — _I will praise the Lord in my life_ — and finished it on the floor, the chronicler says, _psallam Deo meo quamdiu fuero_ — _I will sing to my God as long as I am._ The two soldiers escaped. Judith was never charged. The Frisian church buried him at Utrecht.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I will praise the Lord in my life; I will sing to my God as long as I am."_ — Frederick, dying, _Psalm_ 145:2]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "19", name: "Macrina the Younger"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 19]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Macrina the Younger]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Ascetic, sister of Basil and Gregory, c. 327–379]

#v(0.5em)

She was the eldest of the ten children of Basil the Elder and Emmelia of Caesarea — a Cappadocian Christian household of the second rank of the senatorial aristocracy, with estates at Annisa in Pontus and connections through both grandmothers to the confessors of the Diocletianic persecution. She was named Macrina after her own grandmother, who had been a pupil of Gregory the Wonderworker and who had taught her the _Symbol_ of the Cappadocian church before she could read. She was betrothed at twelve to a young man of the family circle who died before the marriage. She refused, at thirteen, to be betrothed again. She told her mother, the chronicler her brother Gregory of Nyssa says in the _Life of Macrina_ he wrote about her, that the first betrothed was _not dead but present with the Lord_, and that a second engagement would be a kind of infidelity.

She kept the household. Her father died about 341. Her mother was left with ten children, four of them still small. Macrina, who was perhaps fourteen, took over the practical management of the estates while her mother retained the legal headship. She raised her own brothers and sisters: Basil (later the Great), Naucratius (a hunter and ascetic who died young), Peter (later bishop of Sebaste), Gregory (later bishop of Nyssa), and the others. She taught them the psalter and the New Testament before they were sent out to the city schools. When Basil came back from Athens at twenty-six full of his own brilliance, Gregory says, it was Macrina who took him down from his self-importance in a single afternoon and pointed him toward the monastic life.

She made the family estate at Annisa into a coenobitic community of about forty women — most of them former slaves and freedwomen of the household, freed by Macrina under a rule of common life. She herself worked at the same labors. She was, by Gregory's testimony, the chief theological mind of her own household, more decisive than Basil and more contemplative than Gregory; the dialogue Gregory wrote at her deathbed, _On the Soul and the Resurrection_, gives her speeches that he heard from her with his own ears.

She died at Annisa on July 19, 379, of a long wasting illness. Gregory had come from Nyssa to find her on a wooden plank in the small cell she had built for herself in the women's house. He buried her, the _Life_ ends, in the grave her parents had been buried in, and her bones lie still in the Pontic earth.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"O Lord, thou hast loosed the dread of death from me, and made the end of this life the beginning of life."_ — Macrina, dying, recorded by Gregory of Nyssa]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "20", name: "Elijah the Prophet"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 20]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Elijah the Prophet]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Tishbite of Gilead, c. 870 B.C.]

#v(0.5em)

He came up out of the eastern hill country, out of Gilead beyond Jordan, with no genealogy and no priestly office, and stood unannounced before Ahab king of Israel in the third year of his reign and said, _As the Lord God of Israel liveth, before whom I stand, there shall be neither dew nor rain these years but according to my word._ The drought lasted three and a half years. He spent it hidden, first at the brook Cherith where ravens fed him, then with the widow at Zarephath whose handful of meal in the barrel and little oil in the cruse did not fail through the famine. He raised her son from death in the upper chamber. He went down to Mount Carmel in the third year and called the four hundred and fifty prophets of Baal to meet him there before all Israel.

He had two bullocks brought, gave them their choice, and let them prepare an altar. They cut their own flesh from morning to noon, calling on Baal in vain. He mocked them: _Cry aloud, for he is a god — peradventure he sleepeth, and must be awaked._ In the evening he rebuilt the broken altar of the Lord, dug a trench around it, drenched the sacrifice and the wood with twelve jars of water, and called: _Lord God of Abraham, Isaac, and of Israel, let it be known this day that thou art God, and that I am thy servant._ The fire of the Lord fell, and consumed the sacrifice, and the wood, and the stones, and the dust, and licked up the water that was in the trench. The people fell on their faces, and said, _The Lord, he is the God._

He fled afterward into the wilderness, sat down under a juniper tree, asked to die. The angel fed him bread baked on coals and water in a cruse, and he walked forty days and forty nights on the strength of that meal to Horeb the mount of God. He stood in the cave there while a great wind broke the mountain — but the Lord was not in the wind; and after the wind an earthquake — but the Lord was not in the earthquake; and after the earthquake a fire — but the Lord was not in the fire; and after the fire _a still small voice_, and Elijah wrapped his face in his mantle, and the voice asked, _What doest thou here, Elijah?_

He passed his mantle to Elisha at the plowing. He went up by a whirlwind into heaven from the far side of Jordan with horses and a chariot of fire. The mantle fell. The Carmelites have him for their father. The fire on Carmel still falls, somewhere, on every soul that has stopped halting between two opinions.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"How long halt ye between two opinions? If the Lord be God, follow him."_ — Elijah, _I Kings_ 18:21]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "21", name: "Praxedes of Rome"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 21]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Praxedes of Rome]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman virgin and gatherer of martyrs, second century]

#v(0.5em)

She was a daughter of the senator Pudens, of the family the apostle Paul names in the closing chapter of the second letter to Timothy: _Eubulus greeteth thee, and Pudens, and Linus, and Claudia, and all the brethren._ Pudens, by the early Roman tradition, was a Christian senator baptized by Peter in his own house on the Vicus Patricius on the Esquiline hill. He had two daughters, Pudentiana and Praxedes, both consecrated virgins, both eponyms of two of the oldest Roman _tituli_ — the small house-churches that were the earliest organized places of Christian worship in the city. The two basilicas — Santa Pudenziana and Santa Prassede — still stand on the Esquiline a quarter-mile apart.

She lived through the persecutions of the second century. The chronicler's _Acts_, in the careful Bollandist recension at AASS Julii V, give the work that her name was remembered for. With her elder sister Pudentiana while the sister lived, and alone after Pudentiana's early death, Praxedes used the family house on the Vicus Patricius as a refuge for the persecuted, a place of secret Eucharist, and a hospice for the bodies of the martyrs. When a Christian was killed in the games or in the prisons or on the Via Cornelia, she and her household servants would by night recover the body, wash it, anoint it, and lay it in a private cemetery she had opened on the family land outside the Salarian gate. She is said by the _Acts_ to have gathered, in this way, the bodies of twenty-three named martyrs and many more unnamed.

She kept also a particular relic in the chapel of the house: a marble basin into which she would sponge, after each of these night-recoveries, the blood from the linens used to wash the bodies. The basin survives. It is set in the floor of the side chapel of the _Cappella di San Zenone_ at Santa Prassede on the Esquiline, behind a small bronze grate, and the inscription beside it gives Praxedes's name and the second-century date.

She died in her old age, in the family house, of natural causes, sometime in the reign of Antoninus Pius, perhaps about 160. She had outlived the persecutions of her early womanhood and the kinder middle decades both. They buried her with her father and her sister in the same family vault. Pope Paschal I, in 822, brought her bones into the new basilica on the Esquiline that bears her name. The two thousand three hundred martyrs whose names the basilica's slab inscription preserves are, by Paschal's record, the count of those she had once gathered.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"What you do to the least of these, you do to me — even after they are dead."_ — Praxedes, written on the wall of her chapel]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "22", name: "Mary Magdalene"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 22]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Mary Magdalene]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Apostle to the apostles, first century]

#v(0.5em)

She came from the lakeside fishing town of Magdala on the western shore of the Sea of Galilee, twelve miles north of Tiberias. Luke says she had been delivered, at some point before her appearance in the Gospel narrative, of seven demons. She had means; she traveled in the company of Jesus and the Twelve and helped support them, with Joanna the wife of Chuza Herod's steward, and Susanna, and several other women, out of their own resources. She is named, in this group, before the others — the way the Twelve are named with Peter first. She had a place she had earned.

She was at the cross when nearly all of the Twelve had fled. John was there; the mother of Jesus was there; Mary the wife of Cleopas was there; Mary Magdalene was there. She watched the dying. She watched the lifting down. She watched, with the other Mary, where the body was laid. She came back at first light on the first day of the week — earlier than first light, John says, while it was yet dark — to anoint the body for a proper burial that the Sabbath had cut short. She found the stone rolled away.

She ran back to Peter and John. She came back with them. They saw the linens; they went home. She stayed in the garden. She was weeping at the empty tomb when she turned and saw a man she took for the gardener, and asked him where they had laid the body. He said her name. _Mary._ She said the one word back: _Rabbouni_ — _my master._ He told her he had not yet ascended to the Father, and sent her to the eleven with the message. She went. She told them. They did not, at first, believe her. The Greek of John has her speak the words that the church for two thousand years has heard her say in the dawn of every Easter morning: _I have seen the Lord._

She is, in the early Latin tradition that Gregory the Great followed and the AASS Julii V keeps, the same woman as the sister of Lazarus and Martha, the same woman as the unnamed sinner who anointed Jesus' feet. The Greek tradition holds these three distinct. The point of her feast does not turn on the question. The point is that the resurrection was first announced not by an apostle but by a woman whom the apostles had not yet learned to believe; and that the western church gave her at last the title that the Greeks had given her first: _Apostola Apostolorum_ — apostle to the apostles.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I have seen the Lord."_ — Mary Magdalene, in the garden, _John_ 20:18]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "23", name: "Bridget of Sweden"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 23]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Bridget of Sweden]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Visionary and founder, 1303–1373]

#v(0.5em)

She was Birgitta Birgersdotter, of the great Swedish noble house of Folkunga, daughter of a _lagman_ of Uppland who governed his province with the law-codes the Folkungas had drafted. She was given in marriage at thirteen to the noble Ulf Gudmarsson and bore him eight children over twenty-two years. Two of those children — Karl and Catherine — would become important saints in their own right. The marriage was, by every account, deeply affectionate. She had begun receiving visions of Christ on the cross from the age of seven; she told only her confessor; she ran the great Folkunga estate, governed her household, raised her children, and kept the visions to herself for forty years.

She was lady-in-waiting at the Swedish court under King Magnus Eriksson and his wife Blanche of Namur for several years in her thirties. The court was, by the _Vita_ her confessor wrote, careless; she withdrew when she could. Her husband Ulf was struck down by a fever in 1344 returning from a pilgrimage they had made together to Compostella, and she was widowed at forty-one. She put on the Cistercian habit of a tertiary, gave away her wardrobe and her jewels, retired to a small house at Alvastra near the lake of Vättern, and for two years sat in the chapel of the Cistercian abbey there transcribing the visions that had now begun to come almost daily.

The Christ of her visions told her to found a new order — for women primarily, with a small attached community of priests for the women's spiritual care — and to take the rule to Rome. She walked to Rome in 1349 by way of Cologne and the Alps and never returned. She lived in Rome for the remaining twenty-four years of her life, in a small house near the Campo dei Fiori that still stands. She wrote, through her two Swedish chaplains who took the dictation in Latin from her Swedish, the eight books of the _Revelations_ — visions of Christ's nativity, his passion, his bride the church, the popes of her century at Avignon (whom she scolded for not returning to Rome), the kings of Europe (whom she scolded for the war between England and France), and the angels and saints of her own household. She founded the _Order of the Most Holy Savior_ — the _Bridgettines_ — and saw, before she died, her first daughter Catherine professed in it.

She died at her house in Rome, July 23, 1373, at seventy. Her daughter Catherine carried her body home to Sweden by sea and overland; the journey took a year. They buried her at Vadstena where the mother-house of the order had been chartered. The order is still there.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"My daughter, the world is not deceived by the great; it is deceived by the small that the great refuse to see."_ — Bridget, _Revelations_ IV.49]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "24", name: "Christina the Astonishing"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 24]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Christina the Astonishing]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Beguine ascetic of Brabant, 1150–1224]

#v(0.5em)

She was born of a peasant family in the small Brabantine village of Brustem near Sint-Truiden, in the Low Country borderlands between French and Flemish speech. She was orphaned of both parents in her early teens and raised by her two older sisters. She was twenty-two when, during a long fever, she fell into a coma so deep that her sisters and the parish priest declared her dead, and she was carried in an open coffin to the parish church for the Requiem Mass. At the _Agnus Dei_, the chronicler Thomas of Cantimpré reports, she sat upright in the coffin, then rose, walked to the rafters of the church on a current of air the gathered crowd could see, and perched there until the priest persuaded her to come down.

She lived another fifty-two years. The astonishing things began at once. She could not bear the smell of human bodies — not from disgust, she told her confessor, but from a sense of their mortality so acute that ordinary proximity to the unbaptized hurt her. She fled crowds into the wilderness for years at a time, lived in trees, slept in the icy waters of the river Meuse in midwinter, climbed into the ovens of the village bakers, threw herself into great wheels of running mills (the bones, the chronicler says, would dislocate but never break). She begged her food at peasant doors. She wore the same single coarse habit until it rotted off her.

She was, by Thomas's account, in the right mind throughout. Her conversations with him in her old age were lucid, theologically careful, and orthodox in every particular. She gave him an account of what had happened at the moment of her coma-death twenty-five years before: she had been carried by angels to a glimpse of purgatory, then of hell, then of the threshold of paradise, where Christ had given her the choice of remaining or returning. She had returned, she said, by her own choice, to bear in her body the suffering that would relieve souls in purgatory — and to be a sign, to the living, of what the dead were undergoing. The Beguines of the Low Countries — the loose semi-monastic communities of laywomen who clustered along the Meuse and the Rhine in this same generation — took her as one of their patron-figures, although she herself never formally joined any community.

She died at the priory of Saint Catherine outside Sint-Truiden, July 24, 1224, in her cell. The local Cistercians and Beguines together buried her. She is one of the strangest figures in the AASS. Thomas of Cantimpré, who was no fool, vouches for everything he reports.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"The dead do not lack our love; they lack our visibility."_ — Christina, to Thomas of Cantimpré]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "25", name: "James the Greater"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 25]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[James the Greater]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Apostle, son of Zebedee, d. c. 44]

#v(0.5em)

He was the older of the two sons of Zebedee the Galilean fisherman, brother of John the beloved disciple, partner with Peter and Andrew in the fishing-boats of Capernaum. The four had a shared business on the lake — Mark calls them _hired servants_ of Zebedee, which implies a household of some local prosperity, not the wealth of Magdala but enough to own boats and nets and to employ outside labor. James and John were called by Jesus together, leaving the father in the boat with the hired men. Their mother — Salome by the Gospel tradition, sometimes identified by the early Fathers as a sister of the Virgin — followed Jesus also, and stood by the cross. James and John were nicknamed by Jesus _Boanerges_, _the sons of thunder_, for their evident temperaments.

He was one of the three. Peter, James, and John were taken aside at the raising of Jairus's daughter, at the transfiguration on the mountain, at the agony in Gethsemane. He had asked, with his brother, for the seats on the right and the left of the kingdom and had been gently corrected: _Are ye able to drink of the cup that I shall drink of?_ He had said _We are able_. He was the first of the Twelve to drink it. Herod Agrippa I, the grandson of Herod the Great, ruling the reconstituted kingdom of Judaea under imperial favor from 41 to 44, killed James with the sword in the Jerusalem persecution of 43 or 44 — the persecution Luke records in the twelfth chapter of Acts in a single sentence: _He killed James the brother of John with the sword._

He was the first of the apostles to die. The Greek tradition records that the soldier whose duty it was to execute him, watching his composure on the way to the block, was himself converted on the spot, declared his faith in Christ before the court, and was beheaded with him in the same hour.

The Spanish tradition — preserved in the seventh-century _Breviarium Apostolorum_ and elaborated through the centuries — places his earlier apostolic mission in Spain, and the translation of his body, by sea after his Jerusalem execution, to the western coast of Galicia, where it was eventually rediscovered in the ninth century by the hermit Pelayo near a place called Libredon. The shrine there became Santiago de Compostela. Whether or not the journey to Spain is historical, the pilgrimage to his shrine is — by the late medieval centuries one of the three great pilgrimages of Western Christendom, along with Rome and Jerusalem, and walked still, by perhaps three hundred thousand a year on the _Camino_.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"We are able."_ — James and John, _Mark_ 10:39]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "26", name: "Anne"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 26]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Anne]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Mother of the Virgin, first century B.C.]

#v(0.5em)

She is not in the canonical Gospels. Her name appears first in the _Protoevangelium of James_ — a Greek text of the mid-second century, of unknown provenance, which the church afterward read as a useful supplement to the Gospels for what it told of Mary's parentage and childhood, but which it never received into the New Testament canon. The _Protoevangelium_ names her Anna (Hebrew _Hannah_, the same as the mother of Samuel, _grace_), and gives her husband as Joachim. They were, the text says, of the line of David, lived in Jerusalem, and were childless after twenty years of marriage. Joachim's offering at the Temple was once refused by the priest Issachar on the grounds that he had not raised children for Israel. He went into the wilderness for forty days in protest and prayer; she stayed in the garden of their house, also fasting. An angel came to each of them with the same message at the same hour: that Anna would conceive and bear a daughter who would be holy to the Lord.

She conceived. She bore a daughter and named her Miriam — Mary. She kept her at home for three years, then by an old Israelite custom of the temple-girls she presented the child at the sanctuary, where Mary remained, in the formula of the text, _until she was twelve_. Anne and Joachim, then in their late middle age, lived to see her betrothed to Joseph the carpenter. The _Protoevangelium_ does not record their deaths. The tradition that grew up around their cult gives them respectful old age and burial in Jerusalem; their tomb was venerated in the church of Saint Anne in the Bezetha quarter from the fourth century, and the Crusader-period basilica of Saint Anne still stands there, perhaps the most acoustically perfect church in Jerusalem.

Whether or not the _Protoevangelium_'s narrative is historical — and the AASS, with the careful Bollandist instinct, treats it as pious supplement rather than testimony — the cult of Anne is one of the oldest in the church. Constantinople kept her feast from the sixth century; Naples from the eighth; the Latin West from the twelfth. The grandmother of Christ was, in the popular medieval imagination, the patroness of mothers, of housewives, of childbirth, of the teaching of girls to read. The standard medieval icon shows her teaching the young Mary the alphabet from a book of psalms — the psalter, by the chronicler's gentle joke, that her namesake Hannah composed.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"And Mary grew, and her parents adored the Lord; and they brought her to the temple of the Lord, that she might be brought up there in holiness."_ — _Protoevangelium of James_ 6]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "27", name: "Pantaleon"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 27]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Pantaleon]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Court physician, healer, martyr at Nicomedia, c. 275–305]

#v(0.5em)

The Greeks called him _Panteleemon,_ the _all-merciful_ — a name, the AASS preserves from the older _acta,_ given him in a vision shortly before his death, replacing the older _Pantoleon,_ the _all-lion,_ under which his early life is recorded. He was born at Nicomedia, the Bithynian capital that had become Diocletian's eastern court-city, perhaps around 275. His father Eustorgius was a wealthy pagan senator; his mother Eubula was a secret Christian who died when he was young. The father, careful for his only son's career, had him trained from boyhood in medicine under the most distinguished court physician in the Diocletianic establishment. By his early twenties Pantaleon was a successful physician with a growing private practice and a court appointment as one of the personal medical attendants of the emperor.

His conversion came through a presbyter named Hermolaus, an old Christian whom he met by chance in a colonnade near his lodgings. Hermolaus took him through the gospels over a series of meetings; Pantaleon was baptized. The first miracle the _acta_ preserve is the small one that confirmed his vocation. He was walking home through the city one afternoon when he came upon a child who had been bitten by a viper and was already dead; he stopped, knelt, prayed in the name of Christ, and the child rose. His medical practice took on, after that, what the _acta_ call _the second medicine_ — the curing by prayer of conditions the medical art could not reach. He kept treating his pagan patients with the standard art; the Christian patients who came to him he treated with prayer; both kinds were healed. He refused payment for the prayers, distributed the regular fees to the poor, and used his court connections to intercede for arrested Christians in the prison of the imperial quarter.

The other court physicians, jealous of his practice and frightened by the boldness of his Christian profession, denounced him to Maximian Galerius in 304. Galerius, who knew him personally, tried at first to talk him out of it. Pantaleon refused. The interrogation grew formal; the standard sequence of tortures followed. The _acta_ preserve the long catalogue: he was scourged, sewn into a sack with stones and thrown into the sea (he floated; the sailors fished him out); thrown to wild animals (they refused him); set in a furnace (the fire would not burn him); set on a bench to be torn in half by an iron wheel (the wheel broke). Eventually the executioner was instructed to behead him with the sword. He was bound to an olive tree outside the city walls. The headsman struck. _Lac et sanguis_ — milk and blood — flowed from the severed neck, and the olive tree, dry that summer in the late August heat, sprouted leaves and fruit overnight.

He is among the _megalo-martyrs_ of the East — paired with George, Demetrius, and Theodore in the iconography of the great soldier-saints — and is the patron of physicians, midwives, and the helping professions across both Eastern and Western Christianity.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Lac et sanguis."_ — _the_ acta _of the morning at the olive tree: from the headless body, milk and blood_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "28", name: "Samson of Dol"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 28]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Samson of Dol]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Welsh-British abbot, apostle of Brittany, c. 485–565]

#v(0.5em)

He was born in south Wales, of a noble Demetian family of the kingdom of Dyfed, son of a man named Amwn Ddu and a woman named Anna. He was given as a boy of five to the great monastic school of Illtud at Llantwit Major on the Vale of Glamorgan — the school that in his generation educated nearly every famous British saint of the sixth century: Gildas, David of Wales, Paul Aurelian, Magloire. He stayed twenty-five years. He took the habit in his teens and was eventually ordained deacon and then priest by Bishop Dubricius of Llandaff.

He left Llantwit in his thirties under circumstances the _Vita_ records honestly: his sanctity had been so noticeable that his fellow monks had begun pressing for his election as abbot in place of Illtud, and Samson, refusing the place and the rivalry it would create, slipped out one night with three companions and walked west into the Pembrokeshire coast country. He spent some years at a cave hermitage on Caldy Island; he was ordained bishop, unconsecrated to any particular see but for missionary use, by Bishop Dubricius about 521. He crossed to Ireland for a year of further study with the Irish anchorites. He came back through Cornwall and stopped a while at the monastery of Saint Petroc at Padstow, where the two abbots — Petroc the older, Samson the younger — kept the cell together for some months.

He crossed to Brittany about 545. He landed at Saint-Malo-de-Beignon on the south coast, walked inland through the country of the Bretons, and founded the great monastery of Dol on the north coast, on land donated by a Frankish noble named Pro. Dol became the chief Breton see. He governed it twenty years. He sent missions into the still-pagan country of the Léon and into the Channel Islands. He attended the Council of Paris of 557 as the Breton episcopal representative and signed the acts in Latin under his own name. His was, for the next four centuries, the great founding signature of the Breton church.

He died at Dol, July 28, 565. He had founded perhaps a dozen monastic houses in Wales, Cornwall, the Channel Islands, and Brittany. The seven _founding saints of Brittany_ — Samson, Paul Aurelian, Brieuc, Tugdual, Malo, Patern, and Corentin — are kept together in the Breton liturgical calendar with him at their head.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"What I have not done in my country, I will do in another country; for the gospel does not stop at the water."_ — Samson, on crossing to Brittany]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "29", name: "Martha of Bethany"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 29]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Martha of Bethany]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Sister of Lazarus, hostess of Christ, first century]

#v(0.5em)

She kept house. She had a sister Mary and a brother Lazarus and a house in the village of Bethany on the eastern slope of the Mount of Olives, fifteen furlongs from Jerusalem — a comfortable walk in the cool of the evening, far enough out of the city to be private but close enough to be present whenever the pilgrim feasts brought Galilean rabbis to the capital. The Gospel of John says Jesus loved them — _Iesus autem diligebat Martham et sororem eius Mariam et Lazarum_ — and the village house in Bethany seems to have been, in the last year of his earthly ministry, the closest thing he had to a household of his own near Jerusalem.

Luke gives the famous afternoon. He came in with the disciples. Mary sat at his feet to listen. Martha was preparing the meal — _anxious and troubled about many things_, the Greek has — and finally went into the room and said it: _Lord, dost thou not care that my sister hath left me to serve alone?_ He answered her gently. _Martha, Martha, thou art careful and troubled about many things; but one thing is needful, and Mary hath chosen that good part._ The exchange is sometimes read as a rebuke. The early commentators read it as a kindness: he had named her twice, by her own name, and had not told her to stop cooking, only to remember that the cooking was not the one thing. She did not, by the chronicler's careful inference, take it as rebuke. She did not stop serving him afterward.

John gives the harder afternoon. Their brother Lazarus had died, and the sisters had sent for Jesus, and Jesus had delayed two days. When he at last came up the road to Bethany, Martha went out to meet him — Mary stayed in the house — and said, _Lord, if thou hadst been here, my brother had not died._ She did not stop there. _But I know, that even now, whatsoever thou wilt ask of God, God will give it thee._ He told her he was the resurrection and the life. He asked her, _Believest thou this?_ She gave the confession that Peter had given at Caesarea Philippi, in almost the same words: _Yea, Lord; I have believed that thou art the Christ, the Son of God, which should come into the world._ It is the only Johannine confession of the divinity of Christ delivered by a woman, and it is delivered before the raising of Lazarus, not after — by faith, not by sight.

She kept the house. She fed him the meal six days before the Passion when Mary anointed his feet with the spikenard. The Provençal tradition records that after the resurrection the three siblings landed by boat on the southern coast of Gaul, and that Martha founded a Christian community at Tarascon on the Rhône and died there in old age. The AASS prints both the _Vita_ and the cautious Bollandist note that the journey is not historically attested.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Yea, Lord, I have believed that thou art the Christ."_ — Martha, _John_ 11:27]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "30", name: "Peter Chrysologus"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 30]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Peter Chrysologus]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Archbishop of Ravenna, the _Doctor of Homilies_, c. 380–450]

#v(0.5em)

He was born at Imola — _Forum Cornelii_ — in the Romagna, of a Roman family of modest senatorial standing, perhaps about 380. He was educated locally, ordained deacon by Bishop Cornelius, and was serving as Cornelius's archdeacon when, in 433, on the death of the archbishop of Ravenna, the local clergy elected a candidate of their own. The bishops of the province appealed to Rome. Pope Sixtus III, the _Liber Pontificalis_ says, had a dream the night before the candidate was presented: the apostle Peter and the martyr Apollinaris stood on either side of a young deacon from Imola, indicating him as the man. Sixtus refused the Ravennese choice and consecrated Peter archbishop in his place. He was about fifty.

He governed seventeen years. Ravenna was then the imperial capital of the West. The empress Galla Placidia kept her court there, the boy emperor Valentinian III was raised there, Honorius died there. The court attended his cathedral. He preached at the imperial Masses, the imperial baptisms, the imperial Christmases, with sermons short, vivid, packed with rapid imagery, and tightly biblical, that earned him within his lifetime the name _Chrysologus_ — _the golden-worded_ — paired with the Eastern _Chrysostom_. About 180 of his sermons survive, never more than ten minutes of speaking each: the conviction of a teacher who had measured how long a tired Roman court will listen and decided to take only the time he could hold.

He wrote the famous letter to Eutyches in 449, when the heresiarch had appealed to him from Constantinople for support against Flavian. Peter answered with a single sentence: _In all things, brother, we exhort you reverently to attend to the things written by the most blessed pope of the city of Rome; for blessed Peter, who lives and presides in his own see, gives the truth of faith to those who seek it._ It is the firmest fifth-century Western statement of the apostolic authority of the Roman see, written by a Western archbishop to an Eastern heretic. Eutyches did not take the counsel.

He returned, in old age, to Imola for what he sensed would be his last journey. He died there at the cathedral of his early diaconate, July 30, 450, just before the _Tome_ of Leo and the convening of Chalcedon — neither of which he lived to see, but which his own letter had already, in substance, prepared.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Blessed Peter, who lives and presides in his own see, gives the truth of faith to those who seek it."_ — Peter Chrysologus, to Eutyches, 449]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "July", day: "31", name: "Ignatius of Loyola"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[July 31]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Ignatius of Loyola]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Soldier, founder of the Society of Jesus, 1491–1556]

#v(0.5em)

He was Íñigo López de Loyola, youngest of thirteen children of a minor Basque noble family, raised on a small castle-tower above Azpeitia. He was a page at the court of the treasurer of Castile, then a soldier in his twenties, ambitious, vain about his appearance and his swordsmanship, with two arrests for street brawling and an open litigation against an ecclesiastical court for tonsure-clipping a rival. He was thirty when, defending Pamplona against a French army in May 1521, a cannonball passed between his legs and shattered the right one. He was carried home to Loyola on a stretcher.

He spent six months in bed with the leg, then six more after the bone-setters re-broke it because it had healed crooked. He asked for books. The castle had two: the _Legenda Aurea_ lives of the saints and a Spanish translation of Ludolph of Saxony's _Life of Christ_. He read both. He noticed that when he daydreamed about returning to the wars and his lady, he was elated while daydreaming and dry afterward; when he daydreamed about walking barefoot to Jerusalem like the saints he had been reading, he was elated while daydreaming and sweet afterward. He paid careful attention to the difference. It is the seed of the _Discernment of Spirits_ he would later codify.

He left Loyola in spring 1522, hung his sword at the Black Madonna of Montserrat, and spent a year in a cave above Manresa in vigils, hunger, and the working-out of what would become the _Spiritual Exercises_. He walked to Jerusalem and back. He went to school in Spain at thirty-three to learn the Latin he had skipped as a boy, was twice arrested by the Inquisition for preaching without orders, and at last took his MA at Paris at forty-three. He gathered six companions there — Francis Xavier, Peter Faber, Diego Laínez, and three others. They walked together to Rome. Pope Paul III approved the Society of Jesus in 1540.

He governed the Society sixteen years from a small room in Rome on the Via degli Astalli — three windows, a writing table, a kneeling-stool. He sent Xavier to India and Japan, Faber to Germany, missionaries to Ethiopia, Brazil, the Philippines. He left, at his death, a Society of more than a thousand in twelve provinces across four continents. He died at Rome, July 31, 1556, of a long stomach illness; the brothers had not expected the death that morning, and he received no last rites — only a single word repeated as he could, _Jesús_.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Take, Lord, and receive all my liberty, my memory, my understanding, and my entire will."_ — Ignatius, _Suscipe_ prayer, _Spiritual Exercises_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "1", name: "The Holy Maccabean Mother and Her Seven Sons"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 1]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[The Holy Maccabean Mother and Her Seven Sons]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[With Eleazar the elder; martyrs at Antioch, c. 166 B.C.]

#v(0.5em)

They are the only saints of the Old Testament whom the Western church has kept with a feast and a basilica from the earliest centuries. The AASS Augusti I opens the month with their entry — _DE SS. MARTYRIBUS ELEAZARO SENE, SEPTEM FRATRIBUS MACHABÆIS, ET HORUM MATRE_ — and notes the unusual fact: of all the prophets, kings, and confessors of the Hebrew scriptures, of all who had laid down their lives for the Law from righteous Abel to Zechariah, these alone the Catholic Church drew into the cycle of the Christian martyrs, ahead of nearly every Christian saint by date of death.

They had been arrested at Antioch in 166 B.C., under Antiochus IV, who had set himself to extinguish the religion of Israel by force. The old priest Eleazar, ninety years old, the AASS calls _fratrum institutor_ — the instructor of the family — was brought first. He was told to eat swine's flesh in public, simply to appear to eat it; on the table sat a piece of permitted meat his apostate colleagues had quietly substituted for the unclean. He refused even the appearance of compromise. _It is unbecoming to our age,_ he said, _that we should dissemble; whereby many young persons might think the aged Eleazar to have gone over to the manners of the heathen._ He was scourged to death.

The mother, whom the Greek Synaxaria call Solomonis and whom Latin tradition leaves unnamed, was brought next with her seven sons. The boys were tortured one at a time, in the order of their birth, in their mother's sight. The eldest, having his tongue cut out, hands and feet cut off, then fried alive in a great pan, encouraged his brothers in the dialect they spoke at home, and the king did not understand. The mother spoke to each in turn in the same Hebrew her sons answered her in. _I cannot tell how thou wast formed in my womb,_ she said to the youngest, _but the Creator of the world, that formed the generation of man, will restore to you again the breath and the life._ The youngest died last; the mother died with him.

The basilica at Antioch over their graves was already a pilgrimage site in the fourth century, Augustine attests in _Sermon_ 300. Their relics travelled west to Rome — the church of San Pietro in Vincoli still holds a portion. The Christian church recognized in them a martyrdom by anticipation: they had died for the same single thing the apostles would die for two centuries later — for the Law of the Lord, which is also Christ Jesus, the _Logos_ of every commandment.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I cannot tell how thou wast formed in my womb; but the Creator of the world, that formed the generation of man, will restore to you again the breath and the life."_ — the mother to the youngest son, _2 Maccabees_ 7:22-23]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "2", name: "Stephen I, Pope"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 2]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Stephen I, Pope]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman pontiff and martyr, d. 257]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Roman of old citizen stock — _antiqua Roma oriundus_ — and rose through the Roman clergy under Pope Lucius I. He was elected to the chair of Peter in May of 254, in the second year of Valerian, in the breathing-space between the Decian persecution and the one that would kill him. He governed perhaps three years and three months. He died on the second of August, 257, by the consistent witness of every ancient martyrology AASS prints — Hieronymian, Adonian, Usuardine, Bedan — and of the Greek Synaxaria, in which he is _hieromartyr_.

He governed in the middle of the longest inner-Church controversy of the third century. Cyprian of Carthage and the African church, with the bishops of Asia behind him, held that converts baptized by heretics outside the visible Church must be re-baptized on entering Catholic communion. Stephen, on the inherited Roman teaching, held that the baptism of Christ done in the Name of the Trinity was the baptism of Christ wherever administered, and that the convert needed only the laying-on of hands. He refused to use the African formula; he asked, simply, that _nothing be innovated except what has been handed down_ — _Nihil innovetur nisi quod traditum est_. The phrase, drawn from a letter Cyprian preserved, became one of the small permanent sentences of the Roman magisterium. The dispute did not break communion. Both bishops died in the persecution of 257 before either had moved.

The narrative of his death the AASS keeps from the _Menologium_ of Basil. Driven into hiding in his own house at Rome by the renewal of the persecution under Valerian, he continued the sacraments by night: baptizing pagans, consecrating deacons and presbyters, taking the prayers of the threatened community. He had healed the daughter of a tribune named Nemesius; through her the father was baptized, and through him many of his household, several of whom went on to die in tortures of various kinds. Stephen buried their bodies with his own hands. When at last the soldiers came for him, they found him at the altar — _ante altare intrepidus et immobilis cœpta mysteria perficiens_, fearless and unmoved, finishing the mysteries he had begun. They beheaded him in his own chair on the second of August.

Pope Paul I translated his body in 761 to the church of San Silvestro in Capite, where it lies still.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Nothing should be innovated, except what has been handed down."_ — Stephen, in his letter against re-baptism, preserved by Cyprian]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "3", name: "Lydia the Purpurarian"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 3]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Lydia the Purpurarian]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Convert of Paul, first Christian of Europe, c. 50]

#v(0.5em)

The AASS Augusti I opens her entry with a gentle puzzlement — _Mirum sane est_ — that neither the Western nor the Eastern church kept the memory of so notable a matron in any of the ancient liturgical books, until the late-modern _Roman Martyrology_ at last inserted her on the third of August: _Philippis in Macedonia, sanctæ Lydiæ purpurariæ; quæ, prædicante ibidem sancto Paulo Apostolo, prima omnium credidit Evangelio._ The first to believe the Gospel — in Europe.

The whole Vita is in the sixteenth chapter of the Acts of the Apostles. She came originally from Thyatira in the province of Lydia in Asia Minor — a Greek city whose famous trade was the dyeing of cloth in deep-red shellfish purple — and had moved west to Philippi, the great Macedonian colony eight Roman miles from the Aegean coast, where she had set up as a dealer in purple cloth. The Greek of Luke calls her by the title of her trade: _πορφυρόπωλις_. The purple-trade was a regulated imperial monopoly, _neque concessa cuilibet_ — not granted to just anyone — which implies that Lydia was a woman of property and standing.

She was already a god-fearer — _colens Deum_ — a Gentile sympathizer with the Jewish faith, but not a full proselyte. Philippi had no synagogue; the city lacked the ten adult Jewish men the _minyan_ requires. Instead, the small community met on the Sabbath at a _προσευχή_ — a prayer-house — on the riverbank outside the western gate. Paul, Silas, Timothy, and Luke had landed at Neapolis a few days before, walked the seven miles inland to Philippi, kept the Sabbath, and went down to the river to find the praying community. They sat with the women who had gathered there. _And a certain woman named Lydia, a seller of purple of the city of Thyatira, one that worshipped God, heard us; whose heart the Lord opened, that she attended unto the things which were spoken of Paul._

She was baptized that day with her household. She compelled — the Greek word is firm: _παρεβιάσατο_ — Paul and his companions to stay at her house. They stayed many days. The first house-church in Europe assembled in her front room. When Paul was at last freed from the city's prison, he returned to her house to say goodbye before he left. Her name does not appear in his letter to the Philippians, written a decade later from Roman imprisonment — the AASS infers from this that she had died before 61, perhaps not long after Paul's departure. The Philippian church the letter so warmly addresses had begun in her house. _Mirum_ indeed, the AASS adds, that the church forgot her memory for so long.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"And a certain woman named Lydia, a seller of purple, whose heart the Lord opened — and she was baptized, and her household."_ — _Acts_ 16:14-15]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "4", name: "Dominic Guzmán"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 4]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Dominic Guzmán]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Founder of the Order of Preachers, c. 1170–1221]

#v(0.5em)

The AASS Augusti II opens its substantial entry with the observation that Dominic is one of the best-documented saints of the high middle ages. Nine sworn witnesses of his life gave deposition before the canonization tribunal under oath; three hundred more were taken in Toulouse alone. His immediate successor as Master General, Jordan of Saxony, wrote a brief Vita while a generation of brothers who had walked with the founder were still alive to correct him.

He was born about 1170 at the hamlet of Caleruega in Old Castile, the youngest son of Felix of Guzmán and his wife Jane of Aza. He studied at Palencia, was ordained a canon regular at the cathedral chapter of Osma, and became sub-prior in his early thirties. In 1203 his bishop Diego of Acebes took him as travel-companion on a diplomatic embassy to Denmark; the road back, in 1206, passed through Languedoc, where the bishop and his canon found the Albigensian heresy entrenched and the Cistercian counter-preachers — who arrived in episcopal trains, with horses and servants — being ignored. Diego said publicly that this would not work. The two of them stopped at Prouille that summer, dismissed their retinue, and walked the rest of the year on foot, preaching village to village in poverty, sleeping on the floor of whatever house took them in.

Diego died in 1207. Dominic stayed. He founded the small women's community at Prouille that year — convert women of the Cathar gentry — and a brotherhood of preacher-priests gathered around him as the years went on. He had asked Pope Innocent III for confirmation at the Lateran Council of 1215 and been told to take an existing rule; he chose the Rule of Saint Augustine, added his own _Constitutions_, and got confirmation under Honorius III in late 1216. The new _Ordo Prædicatorum_ — the first religious order whose primary work was preaching, and the first whose mendicancy was structural rather than personal — spread astonishingly fast: by 1221 it had perhaps three hundred houses across Europe.

He governed from Bologna in his last years. He had walked, by Jordan's count, perhaps fifteen thousand miles between 1207 and 1221. He died at Bologna on the sixth of August, 1221, in a borrowed bed because he refused to die in one of his own brothers'. The AASS keeps his feast on August 4 — the older Western liturgical date, fixed before the modern Roman calendar moved him to August 8. Canonized 1234.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"You will gain more by gentle prayer and a contrite heart than by argument."_ — Dominic, to his brothers preaching in Languedoc, recorded by Jordan of Saxony]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "5", name: "Oswald of Northumbria"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 5]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Oswald of Northumbria]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[King and martyr, c. 604–642]

#v(0.5em)

He was the second son of Aethelfrith, king of Northumbria, and Acha — sister of King Edwin who would later rule Northumbria and rival the father. His father died in battle when Oswald was about thirteen, and Edwin drove the sons of Aethelfrith into exile. Oswald and his elder brother Eanfrid and the younger Oswiu spent the next seventeen years among the _Scotti sive Picti_ of what is now the western coast of Scotland. They were catechized and baptized in exile by the Iona monks who had come down from Columba's foundation a generation before. Oswald, the AASS records, passed his teenage years between the rudiments of the faith and the games of war. He acquired both.

Edwin was killed at Hatfield Chase in 633 by Cadwallon, the British king of Gwynedd, in alliance with Penda the pagan king of Mercia. The kingdom broke into its two parts. Oswald's brother Eanfrid took Bernicia, apostatized, and was killed within a year by Cadwallon. The way was clear. Oswald led the small force of Northumbrian exiles south against Cadwallon at a place the chroniclers call _Denisesburn_ — modern Heavenfield, by Hadrian's Wall. The Iona Vita of Columba, written by Adamnan a generation later, records the vision: Columba came to the king the night before the battle, where he slept in his pavilion on his cushion, and told him to go forward. He set up a wooden cross by his own hand on the field at dawn, before the men were drawn up, while the soldiers heaped earth around its base. The army knelt around it. They won.

He governed Northumbria for eight years. He sent at once to Iona — the source of his own catechesis — for a bishop, and the abbot sent him Aidan, who established the see of Lindisfarne with Oswald's gift of land. The king translated for Aidan in the early years, because Aidan had not yet learned enough English; _interprete utebatur ipso rege_ — the king served as interpreter. He gave food in royal feasts to the poor at the gate; Bede says he once broke a silver dish at table and ordered the pieces distributed among them.

He was killed at the Battle of Maserfield — modern Oswestry, in Shropshire — in 642, fighting again against Penda of Mercia. He was thirty-eight. Penda had his head and his arms cut off and displayed on stakes. His brother Oswiu recovered the relics: the body to Bardney, the head to Lindisfarne with Aidan, the arms to Bamburgh — and the cult began the same year.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Let us all kneel, and pray together to the Almighty, the living and true God, in His mercy to defend us from the proud and fierce enemy."_ — Oswald, before Heavenfield, recorded by Bede HE III.2]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "6", name: "Justus and Pastor"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 6]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Justus and Pastor]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Boy-martyrs of Toledo, the schoolboys of the persecution, d. 304]

#v(0.5em)

They were brothers — Justus was nine, Pastor seven, the older _acta_ preserve — schoolboys of the small Roman frontier-town of _Complutum_ on the Henares river, the place the Spanish would later call Alcalá de Henares thirty miles east of Madrid. Their parents were of the local Hispano-Roman Christian gentry; the boys attended the public _ludus litterarius_ of the town, where the magister taught Latin grammar and the elements of rhetoric to the children of the better families. The persecution of Diocletian had reached Hispania in 304. The local _præses_ was Dacian, a methodical persecutor whom the Spanish hagiographic tradition would associate with most of the great Iberian martyrs of the period. He came up the road from Toledo to Complutum in the late summer of 304 with the standing edict to require sacrifice from every household.

The boys were in the schoolroom when the news came that Dacian was holding tribunal in the town forum. They put down their wax tablets, walked out of the school against the master's protests, and ran the four blocks to the forum. They presented themselves before the prefect, hand in hand. _We are Christians,_ they said. _We will not sacrifice to your gods. We have come to die for Christ, who is the only God and the only true Lord._ Dacian was momentarily speechless. He tried to talk them out of it: he asked their ages, he reminded them of their parents, he offered them sweets. The boys held. Dacian, finally enraged, ordered them taken out beyond the city walls, scourged, and beheaded on the spot. The execution was carried out at a place the local Christians afterward called the _Campo Laudable_ — the Praiseworthy Field — half a mile north of the town.

The Christian community recovered the small bodies that night and buried them in a single grave outside the walls. The cult began at once. By the late fourth century, Paulinus of Nola preserves, the burial spot was already a major pilgrimage site of the Iberian peninsula — Paulinus himself buried his infant son Celsus near it, _propinquis conjunctum tumuli foedere Martyribus,_ joined to the nearby martyrs by the bond of the tomb. The relics were rediscovered around 388 by Bishop Asturius of Toledo at the prompting of a vision; he stayed at Complutum to guard them, and the basilica that grew over the graves became the cathedral of Complutum and is the seed of the present Cathedral-Magistral of Alcalá.

The two boys are the patrons of grammar schools across Spain and Latin America — every elementary school in the old Spanish-speaking world has, somewhere on its walls, a small image of two children with a palm branch and a scroll. The schoolboys who walked out of the schoolroom to die became the patrons of the schoolroom they had walked out of.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"We are Christians. We have come to die for Christ."_ — Justus and Pastor, before Dacian at Complutum]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "7", name: "Victricius of Rouen"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 7]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Victricius of Rouen]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Soldier-confessor, archbishop, apostle of the Morini, c. 340–417]

#v(0.5em)

He had been a soldier under Julian the Apostate. The AASS Augusti II preserves the key narrative from the two letters of Paulinus of Nola — Letters 18 and 37 — that are nearly the only contemporary source for his life. He was a young Christian in the army, perhaps twenty, when at a military council on the day the standards were blessed for a campaign, he stepped forward, removed the military belt of his rank, threw the arms down at the feet of his tribune, and said he would not fight any longer under the apostate emperor. The tribune flew into a fury. They beat him with rods and shattered them across his back. They threw him into a prison cell whose floor had been spread with fragments of broken pottery so he could not lie down. The torturer's hands, the AASS Vita reports, were struck with sudden inability; he could not strike the next blow. Victricius was sentenced to death. The executioner sent to do the work was struck blind on the way. Victricius walked free.

He spent the next years in northern Gaul learning the language of the Belgic tribes — the Morini, the Nervii, the people of the long Boulogne coastal road. He became a priest, then in the early 380s archbishop of Rouen — eighth in the metropolitan see, and a successor in spirit to Martin of Tours, who at Vienne in 389 met both Victricius and the young Paulinus of Nola at the same gathering. Victricius spent the next twenty-eight years preaching across the Morini country between Rouen and the Channel coast, founding the first monasteries in those barbarian regions. He brought to the Frankish frontier the same austere monasticism Martin had established at Marmoutier.

The most consequential thing he wrote was occasioned by a gift from Ambrose of Milan. Ambrose sent him a small chest of relics — from the martyrs of Bologna, perhaps from Gervase and Protase whose bodies Ambrose had discovered in 386 — and Victricius's procession of those relics through Rouen in 396 became the occasion of his sermon _De Laude Sanctorum_, the _Praise of the Saints_. It is the earliest patristic treatise on the proper place of the cult of relics: the bodies of the martyrs, Victricius preached, are not magical objects but the same flesh that Christ took, sanctified by the same Spirit that raised him, awaiting the same resurrection that we await. The treatise was read across the West for the next millennium.

He was honored by Pope Innocent I with apostolic letters answering his canonical questions and commending his learning. He died at Rouen in peace, perhaps 417, in his late seventies. Ordericus Vitalis wrote his epitaph six centuries later: _Victricius victor, vitiorum fortis et ultor_ — Victricius the victor, strong avenger of vices, who steeped the Lord's Church in his nourishing commandments.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"What we venerate in the relics is not the bones, but the Christ in whose Body those bones already share."_ — Victricius, _De Laude Sanctorum_, on the procession at Rouen]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "8", name: "Aemilianus of Cyzicus"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 8]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Aemilianus of Cyzicus]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop and iconodule confessor of the Hellespont, d. c. 815–820]

#v(0.5em)

He was archbishop of Cyzicus — the great fortified peninsula-city on the southern shore of the Sea of Marmara, where the Hellespont opens into the Propontis — at the moment the Byzantine iconoclast crisis broke open for the second time. The AASS Augusti II preserves him on the eighth of August on the testimony of the Greek Synaxaria and the Menaia, with a brief Latin notice taken into the _Roman Martyrology_: _Cyzici in Hellesponto sancti Æmiliani episcopi, qui pro sacrarum imaginum cultu a Leone imperatore multa passus, demum in exsilio vitam finivit._ This is Leo V the Armenian, who reigned 813 to 820 — the second wave of iconoclasm, not the first.

The Patriarch Nicephorus of Constantinople — the orthodox iconodule who had been raised to the see in 806 — called Aemilianus and the other firm iconodule bishops of the empire to gather at the imperial capital in late 814, when the new emperor's intentions had become clear. Leo had decided to revive Constantine V's policy and to suppress the veneration of icons throughout his dominions. He summoned the bishops to a hearing in the palace, expecting either compliance or a forum he could control. Aemilianus answered him with the sentence the AASS prints in the original Greek of Theostericus's near-contemporary Acts:

_If the question be ecclesiastical, Emperor, as thou hast said, let it be inquired in the church, as the custom is. For from the beginning ecclesiastical questions have been inquired into in churches, not in royal palaces._

He would not compromise. He stayed in the city the days of the hearing under guard, was sent into custody, beaten, and eventually exiled to a remote place whose name the AASS does not preserve. He died there sometime between 815 and 820, in privations the Greek elogium calls _millenis ærumnis_ — countless tribulations. He was succeeded at Cyzicus by an iconoclast appointed from the palace, and the second wave of iconoclasm raged through the empire for another twenty-eight years until the empress Theodora, regent for the boy Michael III, restored the icons in 843 — a restoration sealed at last on the first Sunday of Orthodoxy in March of that year.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Ecclesiastical questions have been inquired into in churches, not in royal palaces."_ — Aemilianus, to Leo the Armenian, AASS Aug 8]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "9", name: "Numidicus of Carthage"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 9]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Numidicus of Carthage]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Confessor-presbyter under Decius, c. 250]

#v(0.5em)

The AASS Augusti II preserves him under the single source from which the whole entry is built — Cyprian of Carthage's letter to his clergy and people, Epistle 35, written perhaps in the spring of 251 while Cyprian was still in his year of hiding from the Decian persecution.

Numidicus had been, before the persecution, simply a layman of the African church — perhaps a deacon, perhaps in minor orders; the sources do not specify. When the Decian edict reached Africa in the summer of 250 and Christians of every rank were summoned to sacrifice, a substantial group of Carthaginian believers gathered around him in a place the _Acta_ do not name and refused. The proconsul's officers herded the group together, gave them the standard last chance, then heaped stones around them and set the pile on fire. Numidicus stood among them. His own wife stood at his side. Cyprian's Latin keeps both possibilities open of what he saw next: _uxorem adhærentem lateri suo concrematam simul cum ceteris_ — his wife, clinging to his side, burned with the rest. Or, as Cyprian gently adds in the same sentence, _conservatam magis dixerim_ — preserved, rather, I should say. The wife, the bishop hopes, was preserved by God in the burning. The text leaves us between the two readings.

Numidicus himself was half-burned — _semiustulatus et lapidibus obrutus_, half-scorched and crushed under the stones. He was left for dead with the pile when the officers withdrew. His daughter — Cyprian does not name her — came that evening with the sorrowful piety of which the bishop writes, _sollicito pietatis obsequio_, searching for her father's body to bury it properly. She moved the stones. She found him still breathing. She brought him home and nursed him back. He had not chosen to live; he had wanted to go with the others; _remansit invitus_, Cyprian says, he remained behind against his will.

Cyprian ordained him presbyter of the Carthaginian church the following year, and gave the reason in a single sentence: _to adorn with glorious priests the priestly company of Carthage which, by the apostasy of some, had been left desolate._ The Decian persecution had broken the African clergy; the bishop was rebuilding from those whom the persecution had not broken. Numidicus served as presbyter at Carthage for the rest of his life, the only fragment of which we have is the address Cyprian gives him in Letter 40 — _gloria divinæ dignationis illustres_, glorious by the honor of divine consideration.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"He sent before him to martyrdom a great number, slain by stones and by flames; and he saw his own wife, clinging to his side, burned with them."_ — Cyprian, _Epistle_ 35, on Numidicus]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "10", name: "Lawrence of Rome"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 10]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Lawrence of Rome]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Archdeacon and martyr, d. 258]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Spaniard, the AASS Augusti II preserves the tradition — from Huesca in Aragon, born to Christian parents named Orentius and Patientia. He went to Rome young, was made acolyte and then deacon under Sixtus II, and at the time of the Valerian persecution of 258 served as the _archidiaconus_ — the senior of the seven deacons of the Roman church, the man with charge of the church's plate, the church's archives, and the church's relief-rolls of the Roman poor.

Sixtus was arrested on August 6 in the catacomb of Praetextatus while keeping the Eucharist with his deacons. Lawrence, the AASS records from the contemporary Acts and the _Peristephanon_ of Prudentius, ran after the soldiers as Sixtus was being led away and wept at his bishop's feet. _Whither goest thou, Father, without thy son? Whither, Priest, without thy deacon? Thou hast never offered the sacrifice without thy minister._ Sixtus answered him with the line the Roman office still keeps: _I am not leaving thee, son. After three days thou shalt follow._ Sixtus and the four deacons with him were beheaded that same day.

The Prefect of the city had Lawrence brought in within hours. The empire had decided to fund the Persian war by confiscating church property; the Prefect demanded the treasury of the Roman church. Lawrence asked for three days to gather it. He went out into the city and emptied the deacon's coffers among the poor of all twenty regions — the blind men of the _titulus Pudentis_, the lame of the _titulus Praxedis_, the widows of the Trastevere, the orphans of the Vatican fields, every name on the relief-roll he kept. On the third day he gathered them on the steps of the prefect's tribunal and presented them with the line that the _Acta_ preserve as their core: _These are the treasures of the Church._

He was tortured. The Prefect ordered him roasted on an iron gridiron over slow coals — a long death, designed to be visible. The tradition preserved by Ambrose, Augustine, Leo the Great, and Prudentius records that after some hours Lawrence raised his head and said, with the levity he had kept his whole life: _Assum est; versa et manduca_ — _It is roasted on this side; turn me over and eat._ Augustine, who quotes the line in two separate sermons on Lawrence's feast, comments that it was not bravado but charity: the deacon was making it easier for his executioners to finish what they had to finish.

He died on the night of August 10, 258. They buried him on the Via Tiburtina. Constantine in the next century built the great basilica of San Lorenzo fuori le Mura over the spot, where his bones still lie.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"These are the treasures of the Church."_ — Lawrence to the Prefect of Rome, the _Acta_ preserved by Ambrose and Prudentius]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "11", name: "Alexander the Charcoal-burner"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 11]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Alexander the Charcoal-burner]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop of Comana, martyr, third century]

#v(0.5em)

The AASS Augusti II tells the whole story from one source: Gregory of Nyssa's _Oration on the Life of Gregory Thaumaturgus_. The setting is the city of Comana in Pontic Polemoniac — a small town on the river Iris in the territory of Neocaesarea, where Gregory the Wonderworker was bishop in the middle decades of the third century. The Christian community at Comana, which had grown rapidly under his influence, sent a delegation begging him to come and consecrate a bishop for them. He came. He spent some days teaching among them. When the moment came to choose, the citizens could not agree.

They put forward many candidates. Gregory passed over each in turn, asking the citizens whether they had not yet considered a man more suitable. At last someone, in evident sarcasm, said: well, what about Alexander the coal-burner? — and pointed across the open square at the black-faced figure at his cart, sooty from the kilns, dressed in rags, looking, the witty citizen said, more fit to be ridiculed than to be ordained. The crowd laughed. Gregory did not. He asked that the man be brought forward.

Alexander came. He had, Gregory of Nyssa says with care, the body of a young man at the flower of his strength, but the body was beneath a covering of soot and coarse cloth designed precisely to draw no attention. The Wonderworker took him aside. They talked. Alexander, the _Vita_ preserves, was no peasant. He was a philosopher of substantial education and noble family who had chosen extreme poverty and disguise as a discipline — both to keep his body in hard labor for the sake of virtue, and to hide the physical beauty he believed dangerous to his chastity, and to make a livelihood whose every coin could be given as alms to the poor. _Non urgente paupertate ad ejusmodi vitæ genus devenerat:_ he had not been driven to this kind of life by need. He had chosen it. He had been hiding under the bushel, and Gregory had come to put him on the candlestick.

Alexander was bathed, given a fresh tunic, examined for orthodoxy, and consecrated bishop of Comana the same week. He served the see for the rest of his life — perhaps twenty years — and is remembered chiefly for two things in Gregory of Nyssa's narrative. His sermons drew crowds from neighboring cities; the rusticated philosopher who had hidden his learning gave it back, when given the office, in a Greek so clear that even illiterate hearers understood. And he was killed in the persecution that fell on Pontus in the third century — _per ignem consummato martyrio_, his course completed by fire. The man who had blackened his face with the smoke of the kilns was at last consumed by a fire he had not lit.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Small in his own eyes, vile and despised in the world, but truly great before God."_ — Gregory of Nyssa, on Alexander]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "12", name: "Clare of Assisi"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 12]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Clare of Assisi]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[First disciple of Francis, founder of the Poor Ladies, c. 1193–1253]

#v(0.5em)

The AASS Augusti II opens with the title that the medieval Office of Clare keeps: _prima Sancti Francisci discipula_ — first disciple of Saint Francis. She was born about 1193 at Assisi, of the noble Offreduccio family, the elder daughter of a household that expected her to marry well and was already negotiating the contract when she heard Francis preach during the Lenten season of 1211. She was eighteen. She slipped out of her father's house through a small side gate the family later kept blocked up — _Porta della Morte_, the death-gate, the one used for funerals — on the evening of Palm Sunday, 1212, walked the mile down the hill to the chapel of the Portiuncula, and presented herself to Francis. He cut her long hair himself, in front of the small Franciscan community gathered around the altar, and clothed her in the rough wool habit of the _Friars Minor_.

Her family tried for a week to bring her back. Her uncle Monaldo led a band of armed kinsmen to the Benedictine convent of San Paolo where Francis had hidden her, intending to drag her home; she clung to the altar and bared her shorn head to show them she had crossed over. Her younger sister Agnes followed her into religious life sixteen days later. Francis settled the two of them, with a few other women who came in the months that followed, at the small church of San Damiano just outside the walls of Assisi. Clare lived there forty-two years and never left.

She was abbess of San Damiano from 1215 until her death, against her own wish. She wrote, in her last years, the _Rule of Saint Clare_ — the first religious rule ever composed by a woman in the Western Church, and the first that bound a community to absolute corporate poverty: the _Privilegium Paupertatis_ she petitioned from Innocent III and got. Honorius III and Gregory IX tried to soften the rule on her, both for the safety of the community and out of pastoral solicitude. She refused them gently. _I have not come to follow Francis,_ she said to Gregory, who had asked her to allow the community to hold endowments, _to be relieved of his poverty._

The AASS keeps the famous moment of 1241, when a Saracen detachment from the army of Frederick II besieged San Damiano. Clare, then bedridden, asked to be carried to the cloister wall with the Eucharist in a silver pyx in her hands. She prayed. The soldiers, the contemporary witnesses unanimously report, fell back from the wall as if a force had pushed them. The army withdrew the next morning. The image of Clare with the pyx is the standard medieval image of the saint.

She died on the eleventh of August, 1253, two days after Pope Innocent IV had come in person to her bedside at San Damiano and laid the parchment of the approved Rule on her chest. She was sixty. The AASS commemorates her on August 12, the day of her funeral.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I have not come to follow Francis to be relieved of his poverty."_ — Clare, to Pope Gregory IX, AASS Augusti II]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "13", name: "Maximus the Confessor"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 13]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Maximus the Confessor]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Theologian, monk, dyarch of the human will of Christ, c. 580–662]

#v(0.5em)

The AASS Augusti III titles its long entry for Maximus together with the two Anastasii, his disciple-monk and the Roman papal legate who were tortured with him. The Synaxaria love the Greek pun: _Μέγιστος Μάξιμος_ — Maximus great in name, but greater still in deeds.

He was born about 580 in a noble Byzantine family and rose at the imperial court under Heraclius to the office of _protasecretis_, first of the imperial secretaries, the official whose duty was to never leave the emperor's side and whose hand drafted the imperial dispatches. He renounced the office at perhaps thirty-five, took the monastic habit at Chrysopolis across the Bosporus, became abbot there, and fled the Persian invasion of Asia Minor in 626 to Carthage. He lived in North Africa for the next decade and wrote — the _Ambigua_ on Gregory Nazianzen, the _Questiones ad Thalassium_ on scripture, the _Mystagogia_ on the liturgy. The seventh-century church had begun to debate whether Christ, who had two natures, could be said to have two operations and two wills, or only one. Maximus argued, with relentless clarity, that the doctrine of one will collapsed the human nature of Christ into the divine, and that the gospel's _Father, not as I will, but as thou wilt_ required two real wills — the eternal will of the Son in his Godhood, and the human will of the Son in his manhood, perfectly harmonized but really distinct.

He went to Rome in 645. He persuaded Pope Martin I to condemn Monothelitism formally at the Lateran Council of 649. The emperor Constans II, who had endorsed the heresy by the _Typos_, arrested Martin in 653 (Martin would die in exile within two years) and Maximus, with his Greek disciple Anastasius and the Roman papal legate also named Anastasius, in 655. The three were tried jointly at Constantinople. They refused to anathematize the Lateran decrees. They were exiled three times, brought back, retried. In their final trial in 662 the emperor had Maximus's tongue cut out and his right hand cut off so that he could neither speak the doctrine again nor write it down. They were sent to a fortress at Schemarum in Lazica, on the eastern shore of the Black Sea. Maximus died there on the 13th of August, 662, eighty-two years old. The two Anastasii died in the same exile within four years.

The Sixth Ecumenical Council, twenty years after his death, condemned Monothelitism in precisely his terms. The Greek church gave him at once the title the Latins would not give him until the seventeenth century: _Confessor_. The mutilated mouth had spoken correctly. The mutilated hand had written more durably than the imperial decree that had ordered the mutilation.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"He has cut off the hand that wrote the truth, and the tongue that spoke it. He has not cut off the truth."_ — attributed to Maximus after the sentence, AASS Augusti III]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "14", name: "Eusebius of Rome"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 14]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Eusebius of Rome]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Presbyter and confessor under Constantius II, d. c. 357]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Roman presbyter of the _titulus_ on the Esquiline that still bears his name — the basilica of Sant'Eusebio just east of the Lateran, one of the old Lenten stational churches of the city. The AASS Augusti III preserves him on the testimony of the _Roman Martyrology_: _Romæ, Eusebii presbyteri & confessoris sub Constantio Ariano, sepulti in cœmeterio Callisti._ He died in his own house, in a tiny cell four feet wide, after seven months of imprisonment for refusing the imperial Arian settlement.

The Arian crisis had reached Rome by the middle of the 350s. The Augustus of the West, Constantius II — the son of Constantine — was a convinced Homoean and had bent every imperial pressure on the Latin bishops to subscribe his Arianizing creeds. Pope Liberius held out at first, was exiled to Thrace in 355, and after two years of pressure subscribed the Sirmium formulary and was sent back to Rome. The deacon-pope Felix II, who had been elected during Liberius's exile, was driven from his see when Liberius returned. Eusebius — a presbyter of the orthodox party who had stood with Felix — would not communicate with the restored Liberius.

He preached from the door of the _titulus_-house on the Esquiline he had put at the church's disposal, where the assembly gathered for the Eucharist and the catechumens were taught. _Many in Rome,_ the AASS-printed _Vita_ records, _by holy confession and the Catholic faith avoided the communion of Liberius through the teaching of Eusebius the presbyter_. The emperor Constantius, when next in Italy, summoned Eusebius to a hearing — with Liberius standing beside the imperial throne. The AASS preserves the dialogue:

_Constantius: Are you the only Christian in the city of Rome?_

_Eusebius: We trust in the Lord, that Christ will find us faithful._

_Liberius: Are we not in the place of Julius, our predecessor?_

_Eusebius: If you had persevered in the faith you seemed to hold under persecution at the first._

The emperor sent him back to his own house — and ordered the house sealed around him. The cell they shut him into, the _Acta_ report, was four feet wide. He prayed in it standing, with his face to the east, for seven months. He died on the fourteenth of August, perhaps 357. Two presbyter relatives — Gregory and Orosius — collected the body at night and buried it in the Cemetery of Callixtus on the Appian Way, next to the body of Pope Sixtus II martyred a century before. They inscribed the tomb with three words: _Eusebio homini Dei_ — to Eusebius, man of God. Gregory was ordered locked alive in the same crypt for the offence of the burial. Orosius dug him out half-dead by night and buried him beside Eusebius when he died.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"As for us sinners, we hold the integral faith. As for you, by the envy of your error — you have driven into exile a Catholic bishop praying alone on his small farm."_ — Eusebius, to Constantius and Liberius, AASS Augusti III]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "15", name: "Alypius of Tagaste"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 15]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Alypius of Tagaste]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, lifelong friend of Augustine, c. 360–c. 430]

#v(0.5em)

He grew up two streets from Augustine in the small Numidian town of Tagaste, the younger of the two by a few years. He admired the older boy, followed him to Carthage to study rhetoric, then to Rome to study law. He had no idea where any of it would lead. He thought, like his teacher, that he was building a career. What he was building was a friendship that would carry both of them into the church.

Augustine, who could never be brief about Alypius, tells one early story against him with great affection. Alypius had sworn off the gladiatorial games as bloody and pointless. Friends in Rome dragged him to the amphitheater anyway. He shut his eyes; the crowd roared; he opened them. He was, the _Confessions_ says, _no longer the man who had come, but a member of the crowd to which he had come._ The cure took years. It came in the same Milanese garden where Augustine wept under a fig tree and heard the child's voice say _take and read._ Augustine read Romans 13: _put on the Lord Jesus Christ._ He passed the book to Alypius. Alypius read the next verse: _him that is weak in the faith, receive ye._ He took it to himself. The two were baptized together by Ambrose at the next Easter vigil.

Alypius went home and became bishop of the town they had both left as boys. Augustine became bishop of Hippo, sixty miles north. They wrote each other constantly for the next forty years, fought Donatists and Pelagians shoulder to shoulder, grew old in adjacent sees. When Paulinus of Nola, hearing of Alypius from Augustine, asked for a written life of him, Augustine took on the task himself: _totum Alypium inseram præcordiis tuis_ — _I shall set the whole of Alypius into your heart._ He never finished it. The _Confessions_, begun a few years later, carries Alypius only as far as the baptism. The rest of him survives in scattered letters, in the marginal anecdotes of a friend too busy keeping a province in the church to write the life he had promised.

The friendship outlasted the biography. Most do.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Whoever thinks kindly of that man thinks of God's great mercy and God's wonderful gifts."_ — Augustine, _Letter_ 27 to Paulinus]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "16", name: "Hyacinth of Poland"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 16]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Hyacinth of Poland]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Dominican missionary to the Slavs, c. 1185–1257]

#v(0.5em)

The AASS Augusti III gives him as _Hyacinthus confessor, ex Ordine FF. Prædicatorum, Cracoviæ in Polonia_ — Polish historians commonly call him _Iacko_, the Slavic form of his religious name. His Vita was first written by a fellow Polish Dominican, Stanislaus of Cracow, in the middle of the fourteenth century, and amplified for his canonization by Severinus of Cracow.

He was born about 1185 in Silesia, of the noble Odrowąż clan whose arms — an iron horseshoe — still mark every Dominican foundation he made. He studied at Cracow, then at Bologna and Paris, and was made canon of the cathedral chapter at Cracow on his return. His uncle Ivo Odrowąż, bishop of Cracow, took him to Rome in 1218 on a diplomatic embassy. The two arrived to find the city talking about a small Spanish friar named Dominic whose new order of preachers Honorius III had just confirmed. The uncle and the nephew both witnessed, at the Roman convent of Saint Sabina, a miracle the _Acta_ preserve: Dominic raised a young man named Napoleon Orsini from the dead. The bishop asked Dominic for friars to take back to Poland. Dominic answered that he had none yet who could speak the language. The bishop offered his nephew. Hyacinth took the habit from Dominic's own hands, with two companions — Ceslaus, his fellow Pole, and a German friar called Henry — and rode home with the new Order in his saddlebags.

He founded the first Dominican houses of the Slavic lands. Cracow first, in 1222 — the church of the Holy Trinity, still the Polish mother-house of the order. Sandomierz, Płock, Wrocław in Silesia, Gdańsk on the Baltic coast in succession. He preached in Polish, Ruthenian, Lithuanian, and German as the missions extended east. He went to Kiev — then a Christian Slavic city before its destruction by the Mongols — and founded the Dominican community there in 1228. He went north to the still-pagan Prussians and Pomeranians.

The most famous moment of his life was the Mongol assault on Cracow in March of 1241. The Tartar army under Batu Khan had swept across Ruthenia and entered Poland; Cracow was burning by the time it reached the Dominican convent. Hyacinth, the _Vita_ says, fled with the Blessed Sacrament from the high altar, but as he was leaving the chapel he heard the voice of the alabaster statue of the Virgin behind him: _Hyacinth, my son, do you leave me here to be profaned by the unbelievers?_ He went back, took the heavy statue in his other arm, and walked out of the burning city carrying both. He crossed the Vistula, the chronicler adds, dry-shod with the Eucharist in his right hand and the Virgin in his left. The statue still stands in the Holy Trinity church at Cracow.

He died at the same Cracow priory on the fifteenth of August, 1257 — the AASS marks his commemoration on the sixteenth, the day after his death. He had founded perhaps twenty houses. He was canonized by Clement VIII in 1594.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Hyacinth, my son, do you leave me here to be profaned by the unbelievers?"_ — the voice from the statue at Cracow, AASS Augusti III]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "17", name: "Liberatus and the Carthaginian Martyrs"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 17]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Liberatus and the Carthaginian Martyrs]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Seven monks under the Vandal Arian king Huneric, 483]

#v(0.5em)

The AASS Augusti III names them in the order Ado kept: Liberatus the abbot, Bonifacius the deacon, Servus and Rusticus subdeacons, Rogatus and Septimus monks, and Maximus a boy. The witness is Victor of Vita, the African Catholic bishop who survived the Vandal persecution and wrote its history within the decade — the _Historia Persecutionis Africanae Provinciae_, which the Bollandists print as the _Passio_ itself. The date is 483, the seventh year of Huneric, son of Geiseric, king of the Vandals of North Africa.

The Vandals were Arian. Geiseric had crossed from Spain into Africa in 429, had taken Carthage in 439, and had ruled the western Mediterranean from his African base for nearly fifty years. His son Huneric, who succeeded him in 477, hated the Catholics with focused intensity. Edict followed edict: the Catholic clergy were exiled in waves; the Catholic basilicas were turned over to the Arian Vandal clergy; the Catholic monasteries were watched. The territory of Capsa in southern Byzacena — modern Gafsa, in central Tunisia — held a Catholic monastic community whose name we have lost; we have only its abbot Liberatus and his six companions. They were arrested in 483 and brought up the coast under guard to Carthage.

They were given the standard last chance: rebaptism by Arian clergy, in the formula Huneric had imposed on the African church — that the Son was not consubstantial with the Father, but a lesser, made being. They refused. They were thrown in the cells of the city prison, weighted with iron chains, and kept in the dark. From the cells, Victor of Vita records, they continued to strengthen _die ac nocte_ — day and night — the Catholic people of the city in their faith, calling out their teaching through the bars to whoever could hear.

Huneric ordered them executed publicly. A ship was loaded with bundles of dry wood. The seven were tied to the wood. The ship was rowed out into the bay of Carthage and the fire was lit. The flames, Victor's narrative says, were extinguished by divine command in front of the watching crowd on the shore — _statim imperio divino, videntibus cunctis, exstinctus est_. The fire was rekindled; the flames went out again. The king, furious and ashamed, sent oarsmen aboard with the long oar-paddles. The seven were beaten to death with the oars, _cerebris comminutis_ — their skulls broken — and the bodies thrown into the sea. By the same hour of the same day, the bodies were washed ashore intact. The Carthaginian Catholics carried them in procession, with hymns and the clergy walking before, to a monastery beside the basilica of Saint Celerina, and buried them there together. Huneric died within months.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"He who lit the fire could not make it burn; he who broke the skulls could not break the witness."_ — Victor of Vita, on the seven martyrs of Carthage]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "18", name: "Helena of Constantinople"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 18]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Helena of Constantinople]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Empress, mother of Constantine, discoverer of the Cross, c. 248–328]

#v(0.5em)

The AASS Augusti III gives her name in the imperial form preserved on her coinage: _Flavia Julia Helena_. The Bollandists open with a long source-critical discussion of her birthplace — the British tradition (Colchester, daughter of a King Coel) they reject; the Treveran (Trier) and the Bithynian (Drepanum on the Sea of Marmara, the small port-town her son would later rename Helenopolis) remain. The commentary tilts gently to the Bithynian view, on the testimony of Procopius and Anonymus Valesianus. She was born in the East. The empire she would die in had not yet been born.

She was a _stabularia_, by the Latin sources — the daughter or perhaps the keeper of an inn — when the Roman officer Constantius Chlorus, then in the East with the legions, took her into his household about 270. He was perhaps thirty; she was twenty-two. They were not formally married. She bore him a son in 272 or 273 at the Roman colony of Naissus in Moesia — modern Niš in Serbia — and they called him Constantine. The household kept together for the next twenty years. In 293, when Diocletian raised Constantius to be Caesar of the West in the new Tetrarchy, the new Caesar was required to put Helena aside and to marry Theodora for political reasons. Helena retired into obscurity at Trier with her son. She lived perhaps thirteen years out of the public eye.

Her son became Augustus on his father's death at York in 306, and over the next eighteen years fought his way to sole rule. He recalled his mother to the court. He gave her the title Augusta, the imperial purple, the right to mint her own coinage, and assigned her the Sessorian Palace at Rome with its great gardens out toward the Lateran. She converted to Christianity, by Eusebius's testimony, under her son's instruction; she became the foremost imperial patroness of the church.

In the early 320s, now in her late seventies, she made the journey to Palestine for which the Greek tradition calls her Saint. She arrived at Jerusalem in perhaps 326. The Temple platform she found in ruins; the city of Hadrian's _Aelia Capitolina_ had been built over the older holy places. She had Constantine's authorization to clear, to excavate, to rebuild. Beneath a pagan shrine on the Western Hill she had the workmen dig, and found — Ambrose, Rufinus, Sozomen, Socrates, Theodoret all report it — three crosses and a _titulus_ with the name of Christ. The True Cross was identified by miracle: a sick woman healed at its touch. She built the Basilica of the Holy Sepulchre over the site, the Basilica of the Nativity at Bethlehem, the Eleona on the Mount of Olives. She returned to Constantinople carrying portions of the Cross with her. She died there, perhaps eighty, in 328. The relics now lie in the church of Santa Croce in Gerusalemme at Rome, built into her old Sessorian Palace, with earth from Calvary still under its floor.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"My son, build a basilica over this place; for here the Lord was lifted up for the world."_ — Helena, by tradition, to Constantine, after the finding of the Cross]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "19", name: "Andrew the Tribune"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 19]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Andrew the Tribune]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman officer and Christian-by-victory, with companions, c. 300]

#v(0.5em)

The Greek calendar calls him _megalomartyr_ — Great Martyr — and the Latin tradition, the AASS Augusti III observes with some asperity, mistakenly took the title as a separate name, giving us _Saint Magnus or Saint Andrew_ in the older Western martyrologies. There is one martyr, Andrew, _tribunus_ — a regimental officer of the Roman army in the East under Maximian — and his soldier-companions, traditionally numbered as two thousand five hundred and ninety-three.

He was sent with other regional commanders, under the central command of a general named Antiochus, against the Persians who in the late 290s had again pressed the imperial frontier in eastern Anatolia. He was not yet a Christian. He had heard the Name of Christ from a few of his men who were Christians — Andrew, by the _Acta_, was a thoughtful man, _philosophus more militari_, the kind of officer who listened — and on the eve of a battle in which his small force was outnumbered, he called the camp into a council. _Pray to the God these men have spoken of,_ he said; if the God who hears their prayers gives us the victory tomorrow, I will be his soldier afterward as I have been the emperor's today. They prayed. They won the battle in inexplicable rout: the Persian line broke without a real engagement.

He kept his word. He went down to the river the same evening and was baptized — by one of the Christian soldiers, the _Acta_ hint, since they had no priest with them. He instructed the rest of his unit himself in the next weeks. The whole detachment, by the time they came down off campaign and returned to their winter quarters in Cilicia, was Christian. The report reached Antiochus the general, who reached for the punishment the imperial edicts of Diocletian required. He had Andrew brought to his headquarters and stretched on a heated iron bed. He had Andrew's officers' hands transfixed with nails to wooden beams in the courtyard. The men did not recant. He sent a fresh detachment of a thousand soldiers to compel them. The thousand, the _Acta_ say, were converted by Andrew where they stood.

Antiochus turned the matter over to the provincial governor, a man named Seleucus, who decided to handle it as a desertion. He had the entire band — perhaps two thousand five hundred soldiers in total — pursued into the wild country of the Cilician Taurus. They were caught in a narrow defile of the mountains. They had stopped to pray. Seleucus had them all put to the sword on the spot. The Greek verses the _Menaia_ still sing keep the image: _this troop, beheaded, gave thee rivers of blood, O Christ, to whom thou hadst given rivers of water._ A spring, the _Acta_ report, rose at the place of the killing. Pilgrims drank from it for centuries.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Pray to the God these men have spoken of, and if he hears, I shall be his soldier."_ — Andrew, the night before the Persian battle, AASS Augusti III]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "20", name: "Bernard of Clairvaux"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 20]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Bernard of Clairvaux]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Cistercian abbot, doctor mellifluus, 1091–1153]

#v(0.5em)

The AASS Augusti IV opens its massive entry — three biographers writing in succession during the saint's own lifetime, plus an apparatus of further sources running to half the August volume — with the observation that no medieval saint has more contemporary witnesses. He was born in 1091 at Fontaines-lès-Dijon, in his father Tescelin's small castle just north of the Burgundian capital. His mother Aleth bore seven children — six sons and a daughter — and Bernard was the third. The family was minor nobility: enough land to support a knight and his retinue, not enough wealth for any of the sons to expect more than a soldier's career. Aleth raised them strictly. She fasted herself the days she nursed them. She died when Bernard was nineteen, leaving him the most marked by her formation.

He decided for the cloister at twenty-two. He chose the strictest house in Christendom: Cîteaux, founded fifteen years before by Robert of Molesme as a return to the original Benedictine rule against the prosperity of the great Cluny houses. Under Stephen Harding the third abbot, Cîteaux was struggling. Bernard arrived at the gate in the spring of 1113 with thirty companions whom he had persuaded — four of his own brothers, several cousins, a small group of Dijon friends, all of whom he had argued out of military careers, marriages, and clerical preferments. Stephen Harding was at evening prayer in the small chapel when the porter announced that a young Burgundian noble was at the gate, with what he described as a small army.

Bernard was sent two years later to found the third daughter house of Cîteaux. Clairvaux — the _Clara Vallis_, the bright valley — was on the upper Aube, in difficult forested country, given by a relative of the Burgundian count. He was twenty-five when he was made abbot. He governed there thirty-eight years and did not leave the abbacy when the offers of episcopal sees came. He refused the see of Châlons twice. He refused Genoa, Milan, the archbishopric of Reims. He remained a monk in choir and an abbot in chapter for the rest of his life.

He preached. The eighty-six sermons on the _Song of Songs_ that he composed and delivered at Clairvaux through the last twenty years of his life are the most extended single piece of monastic mystical commentary in the Western tradition. He wrote _De Diligendo Deo_ — the small book _On Loving God_ — that opens with the four sentences the medieval centuries learned by heart: _Causa diligendi Deum, Deus est; modus, sine modo diligere_. The cause of loving God is God himself; the manner, to love him without measure. He defended Innocent II against the antipope Anacletus II through eight years of papal schism, walking in person from city to city across Europe persuading the bishops and the kings. He preached the Second Crusade in 1146 by command of Pope Eugenius III, his own former pupil. The crusade failed; Bernard accepted the public blame for the failure with characteristic candor. He died at Clairvaux on the twentieth of August, 1153, sixty-three years old. The Cistercian order at his death numbered three hundred and forty-three houses.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"The cause of loving God is God; the manner, to love him without measure."_ — Bernard, _De Diligendo Deo_ 1]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "21", name: "Bernard Tolomei"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 21]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Bernard Tolomei]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Founder of the Olivetans, plague-nurse of Siena, 1272–1348]

#v(0.5em)

He was born Giovanni Tolomei in 1272, eldest son of one of the great Sienese noble families, who counted themselves descendants of Ptolemy the Geographer. He was educated at the cathedral school, studied law, was made a knight in his early twenties, taught philosophy at the rising Sienese _Studio_, and would have inherited his father's seat on the council of the _Nove_ — the nine merchant-magistrates who governed Siena. He had taken the secular path his class expected. He was forty when he went suddenly blind in the middle of a public oration in the city square, mid-sentence, before a crowd of perhaps a thousand. He was led off the stage. He spent three days in a darkened room and recovered his sight on the fourth, after a long prayer the AASS records but does not quote. He took a private vow to retire from the world.

He waited three years. In 1313 he bought from his own family a tract of empty land in the _crete_ — the rolling chalk badlands twenty miles south of Siena — at a place called Accona. He took the name Bernardo and went there with two companions, both fellow Sienese nobles who had decided to follow him. They built a small hermitage on a hill the chronicler began calling _Mons Oliveti_, Mount of Olives, for the grove on its eastern slope. They lived strictly: one meal a day, no meat, the full Benedictine night office, manual work on the land. Pope John XXII, suspicious at first of unauthorized Tuscan hermitages, approved the rule in 1319 as a Benedictine variant. The Olivetans grew to perhaps twenty houses by mid-century, distinguished by a white habit and devotion to the Virgin of Mount Olivet.

Bernard governed the order until 1348. The Black Death reached Siena in May of that year. Most of the city's clergy fled or died in the first weeks. Bernard left Monte Oliveto Maggiore with eighty of his monks and walked the twenty miles to Siena, set up a plague-station in a central convent, and nursed the dying through the worst months of the epidemic. Seventy-nine of the eighty died with the sick they had come to nurse. Bernard, the AASS records, died last, on the twenty-first of August, the only one of the eighty whose grave is not in Siena — for the convent did not survive the plague, and the burial site was lost.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"He went into the city not to escape the plague, but to die with those whom the plague had already chosen."_ — AASS Augusti IV, on Bernard Tolomei at Siena]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "22", name: "Sigfrid of Wearmouth"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 22]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Sigfrid of Wearmouth]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Northumbrian abbot of Bede's house, d. 689]

#v(0.5em)

The AASS preserves him through one witness above all: Bede the Venerable, who as a young monk lived under Sigfrid's brief abbacy at the twin monastery of Saint Peter at Wearmouth and Saint Paul at Jarrow, and who would later write the life of his masters. Bede knew the man personally. He calls him _sanctus Sigfridus_. Everything else comes from him.

Sigfrid had come to Wearmouth as a young man, probably from a Northumbrian noble household, drawn by the reputation of Benedict Biscop — the great traveling abbot whose five journeys to Rome had brought back the books, the chant-masters, the glass-workers, and the canonical observances that made Wearmouth the most learned monastic foundation in seventh-century England. Biscop ruled both houses with three successive coadjutor-abbots: Easterwine for four years, Sigfrid for three, then Ceolfrid, who would send the _Codex Amiatinus_ to Rome.

Sigfrid took office at Wearmouth while Biscop was away on his last journey south. Bede, in the _Lives of the Abbots_, calls him _vir litteris Scripturarum sufficienter imbutus, moribus optimis ornatum, et mira abstinentiæ virtute præditum atque etiam patientiæ_ — a man well-trained in the literature of the Scriptures, adorned with the best manners, and gifted with a marvelous virtue of abstinence and patience. The abstinence was the technical monastic term: he ate even less than the rule required, and not from ostentation, but from the long disposition of a man who had learned to do without.

The patience went into his last illness. He died slowly of what Bede calls _diuturnus pulmonum morbus_ — a long disease of the lungs — that wasted him for nearly three years, exactly the years he was abbot. He kept the office through all of it. The brothers, who watched him at the daily Eucharist and at the night offices, learned more of monastic patience from his bearing of the disease than they had learned from the rule. He died on the twenty-second of August, 689, and was translated by Abbot Huwetberht in the next generation into the church beside Easterwine, in a single shrine. Bede himself was perhaps fifteen.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Beautifully trained in the Scriptures, gifted with abstinence and patience: he taught us most when he could no longer speak."_ — Bede, _Lives of the Abbots_ (AASS Augusti IV)]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "23", name: "Sidonius Apollinaris"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 23]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Sidonius Apollinaris]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop-poet of Clermont, witness of the fall of Roman Gaul, c. 430–488]

#v(0.5em)

He was born about 430 at Lyon, in the great senatorial family of the Apollinares — his grandfather had been Praetorian Prefect of Gaul, his father held the same office under Petronius Maximus. He married Papianilla, the daughter of the future Western emperor Avitus. He composed panegyrics in elegant hexameters for three successive emperors — Avitus his father-in-law, Majorian who unseated him, and Anthemius — and was made Prefect of the City of Rome in 468. He served the empire while there was an empire to serve.

He returned to Gaul in 469. The province by then was collapsing. The Visigothic king Euric had begun the systematic conquest of Aquitaine and Auvergne. The bishopric of Clermont — the great walled hill-city of central Gaul — fell vacant in 470, and the local clergy and people, in a moment of necessity, asked Sidonius to take it. He had not been ordained. He had served as a senator and a court poet, no more. He accepted on a single condition: that he be allowed to keep faith with the imperial cause. He was ordained priest, then bishop, in a single rapid succession of liturgies. He was forty.

He led the defense of Clermont against Euric's siege for four years — 471 to 475 — with his brother-in-law Ecdicius, who commanded a small private cavalry from his own estates. The bishop fasted, preached, organized supplies, set the night watches on the walls. His letters of these years to the bishops of southern Gaul are among the most remarkable in the patristic corpus — half pastoral consultation, half dispatches from the front. In 475 the emperor Julius Nepos surrendered the Auvergne to Euric by treaty without consulting Clermont. Sidonius wrote to Bishop Graecus of Marseille, who had brokered it, the line the AASS preserves: _Pugnavimus pro vobis; deserti sumus a vobis_ — we fought for you, and we have been abandoned by you.

Euric had him exiled briefly, then released him after a careful Latin panegyric — the price of his return — and he resumed the bishopric. He governed twelve more years under Visigothic suzerainty, kept his Latin and his hexameters and his prayer, gave away the family treasures in the famines of the early 480s, and died at Clermont, probably on the twenty-first of August, 488. He was fifty-eight. He prayed earnestly. He studied the Scriptures with the same care he had once given Vergil. He gave away his wealth as freely as he had once given his verses.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"We fought for you, and we have been abandoned by you."_ — Sidonius, to Bishop Graecus of Marseille, after the surrender of Auvergne, AASS Augusti IV]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "24", name: "Bartholomew the Apostle"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 24]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Bartholomew the Apostle]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[One of the Twelve, first century]

#v(0.5em)

He is named simply, always alongside Philip, in the three synoptic lists of the apostles and in the first chapter of Acts. _Bar-Tolmai_ is a patronymic — _son of Tolmai_ — an old Davidic-era name. The personal name of the apostle is, by an early patristic identification still defended by the Bollandists and confirmed by the indirect evidence of John's Gospel, _Nathanael_: _God has given_. The synoptics use the patronymic; John uses the proper name. Both refer to the same disciple.

He came from Cana of Galilee. He was sitting under a fig tree, the only place John records him, when his friend Philip — who had just been called by Jesus — came running to find him. _We have found him of whom Moses in the law and the prophets wrote: Jesus of Nazareth, the son of Joseph._ Nathanael did not at first agree. _Can any good thing come out of Nazareth?_ Philip did not argue. He answered the way the gospel makes the answer: _Come and see._ They walked back together. Jesus saw him approaching and said, before he could be introduced, _Behold an Israelite indeed, in whom is no guile._ Nathanael asked the question of a man who had not been expected: _Whence knowest thou me?_ Jesus answered: _Before that Philip called thee, when thou wast under the fig tree, I saw thee._ Nathanael answered with the confession that no one else in the gospel makes so quickly — the confession Peter would not give for another two years at Caesarea Philippi: _Rabbi, thou art the Son of God; thou art the King of Israel._

He is present at the Last Supper, at the appearance by the Sea of Galilee, in the upper room at Pentecost. The post-apostolic tradition, preserved by Eusebius from Pantaenus of Alexandria, places his later mission in _India_ — a loose patristic term covering everything east of the Persian Gulf — where Pantaenus in the second century reported finding Christian communities who still possessed a Hebrew Gospel of Matthew that Bartholomew was said to have left them. The Armenian tradition, equally ancient, places his last mission and martyrdom in Greater Armenia, where he was flayed alive at _Albanopolis_ and beheaded. The flayed-skin iconography became his standard attribute: Michelangelo gave the man holding his own skin in the _Last Judgment_ his own self-portrait, painted into Bartholomew's empty face.

His relics, by the ninth century, lay on the island of Lipari off Sicily; in the tenth, Otto III translated them to Rome. They lie still in San Bartolomeo all'Isola on the Tiber Island, in the city that has kept his feast since the early Middle Ages.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Rabbi, thou art the Son of God; thou art the King of Israel."_ — Nathanael Bartholomew, _John_ 1:49]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "25", name: "Louis IX of France"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 25]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Louis IX of France]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[King and crusader, 1214–1270]

#v(0.5em)

He was born in 1214 at Poissy and called himself, ever afterward, _Louis de Poissy_ — Louis of the baptismal font — preferring the place where he had been made a Christian to the place where he had been born a king. His father Louis VIII died when he was eleven. His mother Blanche of Castile governed France as regent for nine years, fending off the great baronial coup of 1226 with the same firm hand she had used to teach her son the Pater Noster. She told him, the chronicler Joinville records, that she would rather see him dead before her than knowingly commit a mortal sin. The line shaped him.

He took up the cross in December 1244 after a near-fatal illness; recovered, he prepared the Seventh Crusade for three years. He landed at Damietta in June 1249 and took the city without resistance. The campaign in the Nile delta the next year went badly. He was defeated at Mansurah, then captured. He was ransomed for the city of Damietta and a sum the kingdom had to borrow against. He spent the next four years in the Holy Land at Acre, rebuilding the Latin fortifications and negotiating with the Mamluks, while his mother died in his absence — the news reached him in 1252.

He returned to France in 1254. The next sixteen years were the years that made him _Saint Louis_. He revised the kingdom's law-codes through the _Établissements_. He instituted royal investigators — _enquêteurs_ — who could ride into any seneschalcy and hear complaints against the king's own officials. He gave justice in person, sitting under the famous oak of Vincennes, where every Frenchman with a grievance could come to him directly. He founded the Sainte-Chapelle in Paris to house the Crown of Thorns and the True Cross relics he had bought from the impoverished Latin Emperor of Constantinople. He kept the Friday fast, washed the feet of the poor weekly in Lent, and refused, the AASS records, to laugh at jokes told at the expense of clerics.

He took the cross again in 1267. He was fifty-three, his health was failing, and his confessors begged him not to. He went. The Eighth Crusade landed at Tunis in July 1270. Dysentery swept the camp within weeks. The king fell ill on the twenty-fourth of August. He received the last rites on a bed of ashes spread in the shape of a cross on the floor of his tent — the medieval royal practice of _cendres_ — and died on the twenty-fifth. He was fifty-six. The army withdrew. His son Philip III carried the bones home to Saint-Denis. He was canonized in 1297.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I had rather see thee dead at my feet than ever know that thou hadst committed a mortal sin."_ — Blanche of Castile to her son, as Louis often retold]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "26", name: "Bregwine of Canterbury"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 26]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Bregwine of Canterbury]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Archbishop, eighth-century continental Saxon, d. 762]

#v(0.5em)

The AASS draws his entry chiefly from the Vita written by Eadmer of Canterbury in the eleventh century — the same Eadmer who wrote the life of Anselm — about a Canterbury archbishop whom Eadmer remembered through three centuries of cathedral tradition. The historical Bregwine had been born in old continental Saxony, in the country now lying between the Weser and the Elbe, of a noble Saxon family. He crossed to England as a young man.

The crossing is itself remarkable. Bregwine moved in the opposite direction of the prevailing eighth-century missionary current. Boniface, Willibald, Lull, Wynnebald — almost every name of the missions to the continent came from the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms across the Channel into the still-pagan Saxon north. Bregwine was a young continental Saxon whose own country had not yet been Christianized, traveling the other way to be educated and trained in the English church that the earlier missionary generation had built. Eadmer's Vita preserves his arrival in unembroidered prose: _among them Bregwine, coming there, soon attaining a position, was dear to all, and made a citizen of the country._

He took the monastic habit and served as a priest of the cathedral chapter at Canterbury under Archbishop Cuthbert through the 750s. When Cuthbert died in October of 758, the Canterbury clergy elected Bregwine to succeed him. He was consecrated on the feast of Saint Michael in 759 or 760. He governed Canterbury only three years. The chief act of his pontificate was the conclusion of a long jurisdictional dispute with the abbots of the great independent monastery of Saint Augustine, just outside the Canterbury wall, over the burial of the archbishops of Canterbury. The abbey had claimed since Augustine's foundation that every archbishop should be buried at Saint Augustine's. Bregwine ruled that the cathedral itself — Christ Church Canterbury — was the rightful resting place of the metropolitans, and arranged accordingly.

The first archbishop actually buried under that ruling was himself. He died on the 26th of August, 762, and was buried in the cathedral he had defended. He was, the chronicler adds, deeply mourned. Three years had been short for a metropolitan; the Saxon convert had been just long enough at Canterbury to make himself indispensable, and was gone before anyone could prepare for the loss.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"He was dear to all, and made a citizen of the country."_ — Eadmer of Canterbury, on Bregwine]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "27", name: "Caesarius of Arles"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 27]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Caesarius of Arles]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Provençal bishop, preacher, drafter of the Council of Orange, 470–542]

#v(0.5em)

He was born about 470 at Chalon-sur-Saône in Burgundy, in a Gallo-Roman family of senatorial rank already deeply Christian. He was tonsured at the cathedral school at eighteen, lectured for two years, then walked south to the great monastic island of Lérins off the coast of Provence — already a generation old in his day, the seedbed of half the Provençal episcopate. He spent seven years there. The abbot Porcarius made him cellarer; he distributed the abbey's stores so freely that the brothers complained, and the abbot defended him: _He has given more than the rule allows; but only to those whom the rule has failed._ He fell ill from over-fasting and was sent to Arles for medical care.

He was made priest of Arles by Archbishop Aeonius, who was a kinsman. When Aeonius died in 502, Caesarius — perhaps reluctantly — was elected to succeed him. He was thirty-two. He governed Arles forty years, the longest archiepiscopal reign of any Gaulish bishop of the late-antique west.

The pontificate is remembered for three things. First, preaching: nearly two hundred and fifty of his sermons survive — the largest body of Latin homiletic preaching to come down from the fifth or sixth century in Gaul — in a Latin Caesarius worked hard to keep simple, pastoral, accessible to working farmers. He cut the Mass in half so the catechumens would not leave before the homily. He had the choir sing the antiphons in Latin verses simple enough for the congregation to follow. He preached without notes, every Sunday and every feast. Second, the monasteries. He founded the first women's monastery in the West to follow a written rule of its own — _Regula ad Virgines_ — and put his sister Caesaria over it at Arles. The rule survives. It is strict: enclosed life, manual work, the recitation of all 150 psalms each week, complete community of goods. The house grew to two hundred nuns in his lifetime. Third, the Council of Orange of 529, which settled the long Semi-Pelagian controversy by drafting twenty-five canons that affirmed Augustine's doctrine without the predestinarian extensions: that grace precedes and enables every motion of the will toward God, but that the will, once moved, freely cooperates. The Pope confirmed the canons. The council became, for the next millennium of Latin theology, the standard mediating settlement on grace.

He died at Arles on the twenty-seventh of August, 542, the morning after his last consecration of a deacon. The Vita's closing lines preserve the death-bed instruction he gave his community: _No image of me anywhere; my poor habit is to be given to the next pauper at the gate; my books are to be kept where they have been kept._

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"The voice that fits the country pulpit is not the voice of the schools; preach what they can carry home in their basket."_ — Caesarius, to his preachers, AASS Augusti VI]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "28", name: "Augustine of Hippo"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 28]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Augustine of Hippo]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, doctor of grace, 354–430]

#v(0.5em)

He was born on the 13th of November, 354, at the small Numidian town of Thagaste — modern Souk Ahras in eastern Algeria. His father Patricius was a pagan town councillor of modest means who would not convert until late in life; his mother Monica was a Christian whose persistence in prayer for husband and son the _Confessions_ later recorded. He went away to school at Madaura, then to Carthage, where his father's friends scraped together enough to send him for the higher rhetorical training that was the only path open in late-Roman Africa for a boy of intellectual promise without family wealth.

He spent the next fifteen years in the wilderness Possidius and the _Confessions_ describe in parallel. A common-law concubine he kept for thirteen years, who bore him his only son Adeodatus. The Manichaean dualist sect, in whose framework he tried for nine years to make sense of evil. The rhetorical career that took him from Thagaste back to Carthage, then to Rome, then in 384 to the imperial Milan court of Valentinian II, where as the official municipal rhetor he heard Ambrose preach for the first time. The slow, painful conversion through Plotinus, through Paul's letters, through the conversations with the African Pontitianus about the Egyptian monks — and at last the garden scene of August 386, when, weeping under a fig tree in his rented house, he heard a child's voice in the next house singing _tolle, lege_ — take and read — and opened Paul's letter to the Romans at the chapter on the works of darkness. He was baptized by Ambrose at the Easter Vigil of 387.

He went home to Africa to found a small community of _servi Dei_ on the family estate. His mother died at Ostia on the way home, after the conversation by the window the _Confessions_ preserves as the one shared moment of mystical participation in his life. His son Adeodatus died at seventeen the following year. Augustine was thirty-six. He was kidnapped — almost literally — into the priesthood at Hippo in 391, when on a pastoral visit the assembled congregation surrounded him in the basilica and would not let him out until the elderly bishop Valerius had ordained him on the spot. Valerius made him co-bishop in 395, sole bishop a year later. He governed Hippo thirty-four years.

The works of his hand fill thirteen folio volumes. The _Confessions_ in his early forties — the first sustained autobiographical interior narrative in the Latin tradition. The _De Trinitate_ across twenty years of revision. The _City of God_, written across thirteen years in response to the sack of Rome in 410, to explain why the gospel does not promise temporal empires. The anti-Pelagian writings of the last fifteen years that set the foundation for every subsequent Western theology of grace. He died on the twenty-eighth of August, 430, in his episcopal house, with the Vandal army of Geiseric three months into the siege of Hippo. He had ordered the seven penitential psalms to be copied out in large letters and hung on the walls of his bedroom, so that he could read them on his deathbed.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Late have I loved thee, O Beauty so ancient and so new — late have I loved thee."_ — Augustine, _Confessions_ X.27]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "29", name: "The Beheading of John the Baptist"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 29]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[The Beheading of John the Baptist]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[The cost of speaking the truth to a tetrarch, c. AD 29]

#v(0.5em)

The four gospels give the story in three places. Mark tells it in the most detail in chapter six, Matthew in chapter fourteen, Luke briefly in chapter nine. Josephus the historian, writing two generations later in the _Antiquities of the Jews_, confirms the central facts from a sympathetic-pagan angle: that Herod Antipas the Tetrarch of Galilee imprisoned and killed John the Baptist out of political fear, and that the Jewish people held the death afterward as the cause of Antipas's later defeat at the hands of the Nabataean king Aretas. The two accounts converge. The death was historical.

The cause was a marriage. Herod Antipas had repudiated his lawful wife — the daughter of Aretas IV — and had married Herodias, the wife of his living half-brother Herod Philip. The Mosaic law explicitly forbade marriage to a brother's wife while the brother lived. John the Baptist, preaching from the eastern shore of the Jordan, had said so publicly. Antipas, who was politically careful but personally weak, did not at first know what to do. He had a popular following of his own and could not afford to make John a public martyr. He arrested him and held him in the prison fortress of Machaerus on the high desert plateau east of the Dead Sea, and went in to converse with him from time to time. Mark says he liked to hear John talk. He was, in Mark's careful phrase, _much perplexed_ — _and yet he heard him gladly._

Herodias did not share the perplexity. She wanted John dead. The opening came at Antipas's birthday feast at Machaerus. The tetrarch had gathered his court — his commanders, his great men, the principal landowners of Galilee — for the celebration. Herodias's daughter Salome, then perhaps fifteen, was brought in to dance for the guests. The performance pleased the king. He swore in front of his court that he would give her whatever she asked, up to half his tetrarchy. She left the hall to consult her mother. She came back and asked for the head of John the Baptist on a platter. The king was sorry — Mark's word — but for his oath's sake, and the sake of those who sat at meat with him, he sent the executioner. The head was brought back to the hall on the platter and given to the girl, who gave it to her mother.

John's own disciples, Mark concludes, came in the night and took the body and laid it in a tomb. The grave is unknown. Antipas afterward thought, when he heard of Jesus's miracles, that John the Baptist had risen from the dead. The forerunner who had pointed to Christ at the beginning of his ministry was the first to die for not consenting to a marriage Christ would also condemn. The cost of preaching the moral law was already, in the _exodus_ Moses and Elijah had spoken of with Jesus on Tabor, being paid in flesh.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"It is not lawful for thee to have thy brother's wife."_ — John the Baptist to Herod Antipas, _Mark_ 6:18]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "30", name: "Pammachius the Senator"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 30]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Pammachius the Senator]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman patrician, friend of Jerome, founder of the Porto hospice, c. 340–410]

#v(0.5em)

The AASS rebuilds his life almost entirely from the letters of Jerome, who corresponded with Pammachius for thirty years. He was the most distinguished Latin Christian layman of the late fourth century — a senator of the ancient _gens Furia_, descended from the consular Furii Camilli, by Jerome's own description _consulum pronepos et Furiani germinis decus_: great-grandson of consuls and the ornament of the Furian stock.

He had been raised in the kind of Roman senatorial Christianity that was already, by the 360s, a distinct social formation in the city — wealthy, learned, ascetically inclined, building libraries of patristic Greek and Latin in the great houses of the Aventine and the Caelian. He had been a fellow student of Jerome's at Rome when both were perhaps eighteen; the friendship lasted the rest of Jerome's life. He married Paulina, the daughter of Saint Paula of Rome — and so the sister of Eustochium and Blesilla — bringing his own great Furian line into union with the equally distinguished Aemilian-Scipionic line of his mother-in-law. The marriage was, by Jerome's testimony, deeply happy. They had no surviving children. Paulina died in 396 in childbirth; Jerome wrote Pammachius the consolation letter that the AASS quotes at length.

What Pammachius did in widowhood is the substance of his sanctity. He gave away the great Furian fortune systematically over the next fifteen years. He undertook, with his sister-in-law's friend Fabiola, the founding of the first public hospital in the Latin West: not a private _valetudinarium_ on a great estate, but a true _xenodochium_ — the _hospitium Portuense_ — at the harbor town of Porto on the right bank of the Tiber mouth, where the grain fleet from Africa unloaded, and where the sick pilgrims from across the Mediterranean disembarked into the city. He paid for the building. He worked the wards himself, washing the feet of the sick. The hospital lasted as the chief Christian poor-relief institution of Rome until the Lombard invasions of the seventh century.

He was made priest at some point in the 390s. He patronized Jerome's Vulgate translation work, paid for the cost of his copyists at Bethlehem, and distributed his books at Rome. He died, probably in early 410, just before the Visigothic sack of Rome under Alaric in August of that year. His old house on the Caelian — _titulus Pammachii_, the family seat for three centuries — became after the sack the church now known as Santi Giovanni e Paolo, whose mosaics still bear his name.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Pammachius — the truest Cherub of Ezekiel: kinsman, son-in-law, husband, brother — for the things of the Spirit do not stay within the names of marriage."_ — Jerome, _Epistula_ 66.4, on Pammachius]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "August", day: "31", name: "Aidan of Lindisfarne"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[August 31]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Aidan of Lindisfarne]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Irish bishop of the Northumbrians, d. 651]

#v(0.5em)

The AASS rebuilds his life almost entirely from Bede's _Ecclesiastical History_, book three — Bede was born about twenty years after Aidan died, and his witnesses at Wearmouth-Jarrow had known the bishop personally. The fountain, the AASS says, is the _Historia_.

Aidan was an Irish monk of the great monastery of Iona that Columba had founded a generation before. He was sent to Northumbria in 635 at the request of King Oswald — the same Oswald who had been baptized at Iona during his years of exile and who had won the kingdom back at Heavenfield. Oswald had first sent to Iona for a more austere bishop, one Corman, who had returned within months declaring the Northumbrians unteachably barbarous. Aidan, present at the chapter where Corman gave his report, observed quietly that the failure was perhaps the master's, not the people's — _milk before meat_, in the famous phrase Bede preserves: that one should teach the simpler doctrines first, and let the deeper teaching come gradually. The abbot consecrated him bishop on the spot and sent him south.

He chose the island of Lindisfarne off the Northumbrian coast for his see — a tidal island, accessible to the mainland only at low tide, a quarter-mile from the royal fortress at Bamburgh. He governed it sixteen years. He kept the Iona monastic rule personally: he walked everywhere, refusing the horses Oswald repeatedly offered him; the Irish saint's habit, Bede explains, was that mounted travel set the bishop above the common people, while walking kept him beside them. He gave away the gifts kings and nobles brought him to the poor on the roads he walked. He read his Latin Bible aloud while walking. He took meals at the royal table only when summoned, and then ate sparingly. He was a man whose authority over the king came from his evident refusal to want anything from him.

Aidan, when he arrived, did not yet have enough Anglo-Saxon to preach in it. King Oswald, who had learned fluent Irish during his Iona years, stood beside him in the open and translated his sermons sentence by sentence — the king of Northumbria as interpreter to the bishop he had brought. The two of them, for the first year of the mission, walked the kingdom together. Aidan learned English gradually. The conversion moved with the pair of them across the moors. Oswald was killed at Maserfield in 642; Aidan continued bishop until his own death in 651, having ordained the first generation of native English bishops and trained at Lindisfarne the boy Cuthbert — who, on the morning of the 31st of August, was watching his flock on the Lammermuir Hills when he saw a great light over the sea and angels carrying a soul aloft. He learned later that Aidan had died at Bamburgh that very night. He took the vision as his call.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Milk before meat. The deeper teaching, only when they can carry it."_ — Aidan, at the Iona chapter, recorded by Bede HE III.5]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "1", name: "Arcanus and Aegidius of Borgo San Sepolcro"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 1]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Arcanus and Aegidius of Borgo San Sepolcro]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Hermit-pilgrims, founders of an Umbrian town, c. 936–c. 960]

#v(0.5em)

They met, the chronicle says, at the Holy Sepulchre. Arcanus was a Greek from Arcadia; Aegidius was a Spaniard. Both had come to Jerusalem on pilgrimage, both stayed to pray at the empty tomb, both walked out one morning carrying a satchel of relics they had been given and the conviction that they were not finished travelling. They went to Rome together. They venerated the apostles' tombs. They turned north, intending to go on to Venice and from there to take the longer way home.

In Umbria, between Arezzo and the Tiber, they stepped off the road into a wood to rest at a clear spring. Arcanus fell asleep. He woke believing he had heard a voice — _this place is holy; stop here; build an altar._ He looked at the satchel of relics he had hung on a low branch. As he watched (so the _Annals of Borgo_ tell it) the satchel slipped its cord and rose into the air. It would not come down until the two of them, half terrified, half laughing, vowed they would build a church on the spot. Then it descended, and they began to gather stones.

The Bollandists handle the satchel story with the polite skepticism it deserves — Leo X, when he made the place a bishopric in 1515, mentioned "a divine vision" but spoke of the flying relics not at all. What no one disputes is what came afterwards. The chapel rose; pilgrims found it; cottages went up around it; a town grew. They named it for the empty tomb the two pilgrims had left behind in Jerusalem: _Borgo del Santo Sepolcro,_ the burgh of the Holy Sepulchre. Camaldulese monks took over the chapel a generation later. The town is still there. It still bears the name.

The two pilgrims kept walking until they didn't. _This place is holy; stop here._ Most lives are made by knowing when to stop.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Locus hic sanctus est, hic siste gradum, et aram erige."_ — _the voice Arcanus heard at the spring_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "2", name: "Stephen of Hungary"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 2]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Stephen of Hungary]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[First Christian king of the Magyars, c. 975–1038]

#v(0.5em)

His father Géza had been a pagan duke who hedged his bets — he kept the old gods at one shoulder and let Adalbert of Prague baptize his household at the other. The boy who would become Stephen was given the name Vajk; he learned to ride before he could read, took the new name at the font, and grew up between two worlds. When his father died in 997 he had to fight a cousin, Koppány, who wanted Hungary back for the old religion. He won, hard. He cut Koppány into four pieces and sent them to four cities as a warning. Then he set about building a Christian kingdom on the Danubian plain.

Pope Sylvester II sent him a crown. He was anointed at Esztergom on the first day of the new millennium. Over the next thirty-eight years he carved Hungary into ten dioceses, founded the abbey of Pannonhalma, codified the laws, opened pilgrim hospices on the road to Jerusalem, and married Gisela of Bavaria, sister of the emperor Henry II. Where his father had hedged, he was decisive: the new faith would be the law of the land and the kingdom would be ruled from a font, not a battlefield. The Bollandists carry the _Vita_ by Bishop Hartvik, written under Stephen's grandnephew Coloman a generation later, against several centuries of attempts to discredit it. The substance is durable: Stephen built a church-state where there had been a confederation of pagan tribes, and the church-state outlasted him.

It nearly didn't. His only son Imre, the heir he had trained and the saint he loved most, was killed in a hunting accident in 1031. Stephen lived seven more years with no successor and a circling court. On his deathbed in 1038 — the feast of the Assumption — he raised the kingdom in his hands and offered it to the Virgin: Hungary, he said, was hers from this day, _Regnum Marianum._ He died as the priests finished the office. The kingdom he gave away outlived every dynasty that came after.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Receive, O Queen of Heaven, this kingdom which I commit to thy keeping."_ — Stephen, on his deathbed]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "3", name: "Phoebe the Deaconess"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 3]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Phoebe the Deaconess]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Patron of Cenchreae, courier of the Letter to the Romans, fl. c. 56–58]

#v(0.5em)

Saint Paul had finished his great letter to the church at Rome — three years' worth of theology compressed into sixteen chapters — and now he had to find someone to carry it across the Adriatic. The roads from Corinth to Rome were not safe for a woman travelling alone, and the manuscript she would carry, fragile in its leather wrapping, mattered more to the apostle than almost anything he had written. He chose Phoebe. She was, he says in the closing greetings, _our sister, who is a deaconess of the church which is at Cenchreae:_ a woman of the harbor town two miles east of Corinth, where ships put in for the Aegean crossing. He gives the church at Rome two instructions. _Receive her in the Lord, as becometh saints._ And: _assist her in whatsoever business she hath need of you._

The little we know of her is in those two verses. The church at Cenchreae had made her a _diakonos_ — a servant, an officer, the same word Paul applies to himself in the same letter. He calls her, in the next breath, _prostatis,_ which the Latin renders _adstitit,_ a word for the legal patron whose house was open to the apostle and to many others. She paid for things, in other words. She fed people. She used a position of standing in a Greek port city to keep an apostle moving and a young church watered. When Paul needed a courier, the courier was someone the church already trusted with her name on the door.

The early Greek synaxaria remember her at September the third, paired with the other women Paul names in Romans 16 — Priscilla, Tryphaena, Persis, Junia. The Latin tradition picked up the day later. The patristic homilies on her are sparse; Chrysostom, in his homilies on Romans, gives her a brief paragraph and the title _deaconess of the women,_ which is what the early church seems to have meant by it. The letter she carried became one of the four pillars of the New Testament. The hand that carried it was hers.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I commend unto you Phoebe our sister, who is a deaconess of the church that is at Cenchreae."_ — Paul, _Romans_ 16:1]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "4", name: "Rose of Viterbo"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 4]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Rose of Viterbo]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Franciscan tertiary, child preacher, c. 1233–1252]

#v(0.5em)

She was twelve years old when she began preaching in the streets of Viterbo against the emperor. Frederick II had been excommunicated for the second time and was at war with the papacy; Viterbo, a papal city in central Italy, was wavering. Rose — born to a poor family near the church of Santa Maria in Poggio, taken into the third order of Saint Francis as a child after a vision of the Virgin had cured her of a long illness — went out with a small wooden crucifix and stood in the squares and called the city back. The Bollandists, working from the canonization process Calixtus III ordered in 1457, are careful with the legendary material; what survives the editorial knife is a girl, perhaps thirteen by then, drawing crowds.

The imperial party in Viterbo had her exiled with her parents. They went up the road to Soriano nel Cimino, then to Vitorchiano, where (the _Vita_ says) she converted a sorceress by stepping unhurt onto a burning pyre. She kept preaching. When Frederick died in 1250 she came home. She asked to be admitted to the Poor Clares of Saint Mary of the Roses; the abbess refused her — perhaps she had no dowry, perhaps the convent was wary of a girl who had already drawn that much attention. Rose accepted the refusal. _If you will not have me living,_ she is said to have replied, _you will have me dead._ She returned to her father's small house and lived there as a recluse until she died in 1252, eighteen years old at most.

Five years after her death the same convent received her body for burial, and her uncorrupted relics have lain in their church ever since. Every September fourth the city of Viterbo carries her on the _macchina,_ a five-ton illuminated tower lifted on the shoulders of a hundred men, through streets she once walked alone with a wooden cross. The girl who could not get in died and was let in. The city she preached to never stopped listening.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Ego sum, quæ Christum amavi pauperem; et qui me amavit, recipiet me."_ — Rose, refused at the convent gate]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "5", name: "Bertinus of Sithiu"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 5]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Bertinus of Sithiu]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbot, drainer of marshes, c. 615–c. 698]

#v(0.5em)

He came north from Luxeuil with two companions, Mommolinus and Ebertramnus, sometime around the year 638. Audomarus — Omer — had been sent into the Pas-de-Calais a decade earlier as missionary bishop to the Morini, the half-pagan coastal tribes of the Frankish far north. The country he had inherited was unworked: salt marshes east of the Aa, peat bogs, willow thickets, fishing villages set on low islands. He needed monks who knew how to dig. Bertinus had grown up under Columbanus's rule and had spent ten years at Luxeuil learning Burgundian agriculture. He arrived with an axe and a Psalter.

The brothers settled first at Sithiu, a small island Omer had been given by a local lord named Adroald. Within a generation they had drained the surrounding marsh, raised dykes, planted orchards, built a church on rising ground a mile to the north — the _upper_ church, _altum,_ where the town of Saint-Omer would later grow — and a monastery on the lower island. Bertinus succeeded Mommolinus as abbot in 663 and stayed in office for thirty-five years. The abbey he ran became one of the great houses of Carolingian Flanders. By the ninth century it would shelter the _Annales Bertiniani,_ one of the chief chronicles of the period; by the tenth it bore his name.

The Bollandists devote three closely-argued sections to untangling the manuscript history — six different _vitae_ survive, anonymous and Folcardine and metrical, none of them quite reconcilable — and conclude with reluctance that the man behind the layers is recoverable only in outline. What the outline preserves is steady work in a country that did not invite it: thirty-five years in a damp dormitory, a chapter of monks holding a community together by the rule, a slow conversion of marsh into arable. Of his miracles, the _Vita_ records the unremarkable kind — a sick brother healed, a difficult harvest brought in, his own body still upright at his death-Mass at the age of eighty-three. He was buried under the church floor of his own monastery. The drained land has not gone back to swamp.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Abbas pius, qui non tam verbo quam exemplo docebat."_ — _Vita prima,_ of Bertinus]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "6", name: "Magnus of Füssen"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 6]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Magnus of Füssen]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Apostle of the Allgäu, abbot, c. 700–c. 772]

#v(0.5em)

The Vita the Bollandists print under his name is, as they patiently demonstrate over six dense paragraphs, not the original. A monk named Theodorus is supposed to have written it at his bedside; what survives is the ninth-century recension by Ermenric of Ellwangen, smoothed and re-smoothed by later hands, with passages lifted whole from Walafrid Strabo's _Vita Galli._ What can be salvaged is enough: a Frankish-Alemannic monk, raised at Saint Gall under Otmar, sent south into the Allgäu at the request of Bishop Wikterp of Augsburg to evangelize a stretch of upper Bavaria where the Christian gospel had not yet taken hold. He arrived around 740 and stayed for thirty years.

The country he inherited was the Lech valley as it climbs toward the Alps — alpine meadow, dense fir, the small fortified Roman ruin at _Faucibus,_ the place we now call Füssen, _the gorges,_ where the river squeezes through limestone. He built a cell there, then a wooden chapel, then a stone abbey. The Vita is at its best when it tells the small story: the brewing accident in which the cask miraculously refilled itself; the bear who would not let him fell a tree until he made the sign of the cross; the dragon (or great serpent) of the Roßhaupten gorge who was driven out by the staff he had received from Columbanus's spiritual descendants at Saint Gall. The staff, called the _Magnusstab,_ was preserved in the abbey for a thousand years and is still venerated in Füssen today. Local farmers used to bring it to their fields when caterpillars or hail threatened the grain.

He died, according to the Vita, on the sixth of September, an old man among his monks. The abbey at Füssen kept his rule for centuries; the village he founded grew into a market town under the Hohenstaufen castle on the cliff above; his cult spread up and down the Lech and into the Tyrol. _Magnus_ — _the Great_ — was a name his Latin biographers liked. The Allgäu, more honestly, called him _Mang._ The shorter name is the one his people kept.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Sub fasce voluminis gemeret robustus lector"_ — _the Bollandists, on the legendary additions: the careful editor sighs under their weight_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "7", name: "Cloud (Clodoald)"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 7]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Cloud (Clodoald)]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Merovingian prince turned hermit-priest, c. 522–c. 560]

#v(0.5em)

His grandfather was Clovis. His father Clodomir, king of Orléans, was killed in 524 fighting the Burgundians; his head was carried back on a Burgundian spear. The three sons left behind — Theodebald, Gunthar, and Clodoald — were sent to Paris under the protection of their grandmother, Clovis's widow Chrothilde, who raised them in her own household with the affection of a woman who had buried too many children already. They were five, seven, and three.

When the boys were ten, eight, and five, their uncles Childebert and Chlothar, who had inherited the Frankish kingdoms north and south of the Loire, decided that Orléans needed a king again and that none of the candidates should be small. Gregory of Tours preserves the scene that became one of the most famous in early Frankish memory. The uncles sent a messenger to the queen-grandmother in Paris with a pair of scissors in one hand and a sword in the other: choose, the messenger said, _which of these your grandsons shall feel._ Tonsure them and they live as monks; refuse the tonsure and they die as princes. Chrothilde, in the heat of grief, said: _Better dead than tonsured,_ and meant it of the sword. The messenger took her at her word. Theodebald and Gunthar were murdered on the spot. Clodoald — five years old — had been spirited out a back door by a servant and was hidden in the countryside until the danger passed.

He grew up in obscurity. When he came of age he refused the kingdom that was, by inheritance, half his. He cut his own hair before any uncle could send a sword for it, took the habit of a monk, lived for years as a hermit in Provence, returned to Paris, was ordained priest by Bishop Eusebius, and founded a small monastery at the village of Nogent on the Seine three miles below the city — the place we now call Saint-Cloud. He was perhaps thirty-eight when he died. The grandson of Clovis, who had every claim to a crown, was buried in the church he had built with his own hands, having outlived his uncles and chosen, at every fork, the smaller portion.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Melius est tonsorari, quam pro regno mori."_ — _the saying afterwards put on Cloud's lips, against his grandmother's grief_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "8", name: "Adrian and Natalia"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 8]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Adrian and Natalia]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman officer, his Christian wife, martyrs at Nicomedia, c. 304–310]

#v(0.5em)

He was a young guards officer, a _notarius_ in the imperial palace at Nicomedia, when the second great persecution opened in 303. His job that morning was to write down the names of the Christians as they were brought in for sentencing. The acts the Bollandists print, drawn from a twelfth-century Greek codex they judge older and better than the Latin compendia, slow the scene to a long question. Adrian had watched twenty-three of them refuse to sacrifice. He had heard them sing on the way to the rack. He laid down his pen and asked them, _What do you hope for, that is worth this?_ They told him. He took their words with him to the prefect, gave his own name, and walked into the cell beside them.

His wife Natalia was thirteen years old, a Christian who had not told him. When she heard he was in prison she came down to the gates dressed in men's clothes — the law forbade Christian women to visit the condemned — paid the keeper, and threw herself at his feet. _Do not turn back,_ she said. _I have prayed for this since the day my mother gave me to you._ For four weeks she came every night, talked through the bars, dressed his wounds, brought bread. When the sentence came down — the prisoners' legs to be broken on the anvil — Natalia was at the prison door before sunrise. She held her husband's leg on the iron herself, _so that_, she told the executioner, _he should not have to suffer the touch of an unbeliever._

Adrian died first. The other twenty-three followed. Maximian, fearing the bodies would become relics, ordered them burned outside the city; a sudden rainstorm put the fire out before they were consumed. Natalia recovered her husband's severed hand, wrapped it in linen, and fled with the surviving Christians across the Bosphorus to a small house at Argyropolis on the European shore, where she lived another year, sleeping with the relic under her pillow. She was buried beside him. The hand she had carried over the water was venerated in Constantinople for nine hundred years. The shrine of Saint-Adrien at Geraardsbergen in Flanders, where his cult travelled west, still keeps the day.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Hold fast, my lord; for you have not lost me, but found me."_ — Natalia, at the prison gate]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "9", name: "Kieran of Clonmacnoise"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 9]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Kieran of Clonmacnoise]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Founding abbot, the carpenter's son, c. 515–549]

#v(0.5em)

He was the son of a wheelwright in Connacht, born to a craftsman's house in the western country where the Shannon turns south. The Irish vitae call him _Mac an tSaer,_ the artisan's boy. He grew up at the workbench, learning to plane and joint and shape wood with his hands. When he heard of Finnian of Clonard he walked east to learn from him, and was numbered among the _Twelve Apostles of Ireland_ — Finnian's twelve famous students who would each found a monastery and shape the Irish church for a thousand years. Columba was one of the twelve; so was Brendan the Navigator; so was Kieran.

He went first to Inishmore in the Aran Islands, sat under Enda, learned silence. He went next to Inis Aingin on Lough Ree, founded a small house there, and after some years moved south to a bend in the Shannon where the high road from Connacht to Leinster crossed the river. He chose a meadow above the floodplain and laid out the sketch of what would become Clonmacnoise — _Cluain mhic Nóis,_ the meadow of the sons of Nós. He had been there seven months. He had built the first wooden church and gathered ten brothers when the yellow plague came up the river in 549 and took him at thirty-three years old. They buried him in the church he had finished the week before.

The community he had begun for seven months kept his rule for a thousand years. Within a generation Clonmacnoise was the largest monastic city in Ireland — a town of churches, round towers, scriptoria, and the high crosses whose stone faces still rise from the ground above the Shannon. The _Annals of Clonmacnoise,_ the _Book of the Dun Cow,_ much of what we know of early Ireland came out of those buildings. The kings of Connacht and Tara competed for the privilege of being buried in the cemetery his ten brothers had laid out around his grave. He had lived as long as Christ, the Bollandists note, and built almost nothing. What he built lasted.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"The carpenter's son outlived the carpenter."_ — _medieval Irish proverb_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "10", name: "Nicholas of Tolentino"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 10]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Nicholas of Tolentino]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Augustinian friar, intercessor for the dead, c. 1245–1305]

#v(0.5em)

He was born to old parents who had walked to the shrine of Saint Nicholas at Bari to pray for a child and named him for the saint who had answered them. The Marches of Ancona, where he grew up, were full of new mendicant friars in the 1250s — the Franciscans had been there a generation, the Augustinian Hermits had just been gathered out of older eremitical communities by the Grand Union of 1256. Nicholas joined the Augustinians at sixteen, took orders at twenty-five, and was sent in his early thirties to a small house at Tolentino, a hill town on the upper Chienti. He stayed there for the next thirty years and never left.

The Bollandists print Peter of Monte Rubbiano's life of him, written by a confrere who had known him, around 1326. The portrait is unhurried. Nicholas heard confessions for hours. He preached in the open piazza in the evenings. He took bread to the prisoners in the _carceri_ under the town walls. He fasted hard enough that an old companion warned him he would die early; he kept fasting. The miracle the friars remembered most was the smallest — sick once and unable to eat, he was given a roll of bread by the Virgin in a vision, with the instruction _eat this and you will recover._ He did. Afterwards he blessed bread for the sick of the town every day, and the practice — _pane di San Nicola_ — has not stopped in eight hundred years.

His other gift was for the dead. He saw them. They came to him in his cell, especially in the days after Mass, asking him to offer the sacrifice for them; he kept a register of the names. The Augustinian _Office for the Dead_ still credits him with formalizing the friars' practice of constant Masses for souls in purgatory. He died on the tenth of September, 1305, at sixty years old. His body was found incorrupt — the Bollandists devote a long chapter to the inquests — and remains so. The town that took him in has not given him back.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Eat this and you will recover."_ — _the Virgin to Nicholas, in the vision of the bread_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "11", name: "Paphnutius of Egypt"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 11]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Paphnutius of Egypt]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, confessor of Nicaea, c. 270–c. 360]

#v(0.5em)

He had been a desert monk under Anthony the Great when Diocletian's persecution caught up with him, around the year 308. Maximinus Daia, ruling the eastern provinces, had ordered a new refinement of cruelty for those who would not sacrifice — they were not to be killed but disfigured. The right eye was put out with a sharpened iron; the tendons behind the left knee were severed with a hot blade so the man could never again walk straight. Then they were sent into the copper mines of Phaeno, at the edge of the Red Sea desert, to die slowly of work. Paphnutius was sent with the others. He survived. When Constantine published the Edict of Milan in 313 the survivors came back, hobbling, half-blind, and went back to the cells they had left.

He was elected bishop of an unknown see in the Upper Thebaid not long afterward. When the great council met at Nicaea in 325 he was carried there with the other Egyptian bishops — Athanasius was a deacon in his train — and Constantine, on first meeting him, recognized the empty socket and the dragging leg and bent down and kissed both. Rufinus and Socrates both preserve what happened next. The council was debating whether to require celibacy of all clergy — a proposal that had support from many of the bishops, especially from Spain. Paphnutius rose and spoke against it. _Marriage is honorable,_ he said, _and the bed undefiled. Do not lay on others a yoke neither we nor our fathers were able to bear._ The Bollandists note that he had himself been celibate from his youth. The council heard him out and adjusted the canon: the church would receive into orders married men, and married priests would keep their wives.

He returned to Egypt, served as bishop into great age, helped Athanasius against the Arians, and died in his cell sometime around 360, eyepatch and limp and all. The man who had been disfigured for refusing to give up Christ spent the rest of his life keeping the church from disfiguring others.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Do not lay on others the yoke their fathers were not able to bear."_ — Paphnutius, at Nicaea]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "12", name: "Ailbe of Emly"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 12]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Ailbe of Emly]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, second Patrick, patron of Munster, d. c. 528]

#v(0.5em)

The Irish writers call him _alter Patricius_ — the second Patrick — and the title is not entirely figurative. Ailbe (Albeus, Elvius) was perhaps a generation older than Patrick of Armagh, perhaps an exact contemporary; the medieval Irish chronologies give him a long life and a death in 528. The legend that he was preaching the gospel in Munster before Patrick landed in Ulster has the shape of southern pride and possibly some truth — there were Christians in Ireland before Patrick was sent, and the southern province was likelier to have them than the northern, given the trade with Roman Britain.

The vitae the Bollandists know — Hugh Ward sent them three Latin manuscripts from the Irish Franciscans at Louvain in the 1630s, drawn from older Kilkenny and Inishbofin codices — give Ailbe a charming and probably half-fabulous origin. He was the son of a slave woman of the king of Cashel; she exposed the infant on a hillside; a she-wolf found him and nursed him among her cubs; British traders saw him crawling among the wolves, redeemed him from his master, and took him east to be baptized. He returned an old man, walked the length of Munster preaching, and finally fixed his cathedral at _Imelaca_ — modern Emly, in Tipperary — beside a small lake which had nearly dried up by the Bollandists' day. The see he founded was the chief seat of Munster Christianity for a thousand years, until in 1568 the Tudor parliament suppressed it and joined it to Cashel.

What survives in the carmen of Bishop Cumin of Connor, preserved by Philip O'Sullivan and printed by the Bollandists, is the line his own people remembered: _Albeus æterna fuerit mihi laude canendus; / haud scio, num vivat largior ulla manus._ — _Ailbe will be sung in praise forever; I do not know if any hand alive is more open than his._ The boy the wolves had kept alive grew up to keep the poor alive. Cashel forgot his see; the country he served has not forgotten him.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I do not know if any hand alive is more open than his."_ — _Cumin of Connor, on Ailbe_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "13", name: "Maurilius of Angers"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 13]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Maurilius of Angers]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, hidden gardener, c. 363–c. 437]

#v(0.5em)

He was born at Milan to wealthy parents, was sent into Gaul as a young man to escape the unrest in northern Italy, and ended up at Tours in the household of Martin — the old soldier-bishop whose monastery at Marmoutier was, by the 380s, training a generation of Gallo-Roman clergy in the new Egyptian style of monastic life. Maurilius stayed long enough to be ordained priest. When the see of Angers fell vacant around 423 the clergy and people sent for him, and he was consecrated bishop against his wishes — the formal protest of the unwilling bishop, a _recusatio_ almost ritual by then, was in his case sincere. He served the diocese for fourteen years.

His biographer Magnobodus, writing two centuries later, fills the period with stories the Bollandists handle with patient skepticism: a serpent expelled from a hilltop near Calonnes that allowed Maurilius to found a monastery there; the dead son of a noblewoman raised at the bishop's prayer; demons driven out, lepers cleansed, the usual furniture of an early Gallic _vita._ What seems to be older and may be true is the central episode: Maurilius, accused of accidentally letting a baby die unbaptized at a confirmation visit, fled his see in shame, took ship to Britain, took service under a false name as a gardener for a king there, and remained in hiding for seven years. The Angevin clergy hunted him down. They found him in his garden. They brought him home.

He resumed the office, took up the work, and died fourteen years after that on a September Sunday in his cathedral church, around the year 437. They buried him in a double crypt outside the city walls he had built for himself before he died. Four centuries later, when Bishop Nefingus translated the relics into a finer reliquary at the cathedral around 970, the body was found, the witnesses said, _ita inventa sunt incorrupta, acsi ipsa die_ — _as though it had been laid there that day._ The bishop who had once gone away in shame had not been moved since.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"As though it had been laid there that day."_ — _the witnesses at the translation of his relics, c. 970_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "14", name: "Cornelius and Cyprian"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 14]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Cornelius and Cyprian]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[A pope and a bishop, friends in the third-century church, d. 253 and 258]

#v(0.5em)

When the persecution of Decius lifted in 251 the church at Rome, which had been without a bishop for fourteen months — Fabian was killed in January 250 and the cathedra had stood vacant under the imperial ban — finally elected Cornelius. He was a Roman of the Cornelii Centumcelli, an old name; he had been a presbyter in the city for twenty years; he was, by every account, a quiet man. The election unleashed a year of fighting. Novatian, the brilliant theologian who had carried the church through the vacancy and who held that the _lapsi_ — those who had sacrificed to the gods under torture — could not be readmitted to communion at all, refused Cornelius and had himself ordained as anti-pope. The eastern and African churches had to choose. Cyprian, bishop of Carthage, the most respected voice in Latin Christianity, wrote in his support. The choice held. Cornelius was the lawful Roman bishop; Novatian was a schism.

The two men corresponded for the next two years. Cyprian's seventeen surviving letters to Cornelius are among the warmest in the patristic corpus — _carissimo fratri,_ dearest brother, almost every one. They worked out the discipline together: the lapsed could be readmitted after lengthy penance, but not denied entirely. The church's unity, Cyprian argued, was not a club whose members were the perfect; it was a hospital. Cornelius died in 253 at Centumcellae, exiled there by Gallus's revival of the persecution, and was buried in the catacomb of Callixtus on the Appian Way. The Bollandists print the legendary _acta_ — the soldier Cerealis whose paralytic wife Salustia Cornelius healed, the mass conversion of the guard, the twenty-one martyrs decapitated outside the Appian Gate — and they handle the legends with their usual care, but the bones in Callixtus are real.

Five years later Cyprian was beheaded at Carthage under Valerian, on the same day. The Roman calendar pairs them now as it always has: a Latin pope and a Latin bishop, the men who together held the third-century church to its unity through the worst of its disputes about its own discipline. They never met. They are remembered together.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"There can be no other bishop, while one bishop holds the chair."_ — Cyprian, _Letter_ 68 to Cornelius]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "15", name: "Catherine of Genoa"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 15]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Catherine of Genoa]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Mystic, hospital matron, theologian of purgatory, 1447–1510]

#v(0.5em)

She was the youngest daughter of the Fieschi, one of the four great Genoese houses, born in a palazzo and married at sixteen to Giuliano Adorno, of the rival house, in a contract designed to end the feud. The contract held. The marriage was a disaster. Giuliano was unfaithful, wasteful, often violent; she spent her early twenties in a depression so deep her sister finally insisted she go to confession at the convent of Santa Maria delle Grazie, in March 1473. While she was kneeling at the _prie-dieu_ — she had been there perhaps a minute, the _Vita_ says, and had not yet spoken — she was struck in the heart by what she would describe afterwards as the immediate, overwhelming, nonverbal love of God. She walked out a different woman.

She did not enter religious life. She did not leave her husband. Giuliano went bankrupt three years later, lost the palazzo, and in his ruin had his own conversion; the two of them moved into two rooms beside the great Pammatone hospital and began to nurse the poor of Genoa. He died in 1497. She continued at the hospital for another thirteen years, eventually as its matron during the plague years of 1493 and 1501, when she ran the wards and managed the books and stayed when other women fled. Her _Treatise on Purgatory_ — one of the strangest and most beautiful works of late-medieval mysticism — was dictated to her confessor in those years. _The fire that purifies the souls of the elect,_ she wrote, _is the same fire that beatifies them. It is the love of God, become unbearable to a soul not yet ready to bear it. The pain is the joy not yet large enough._

She died on a September morning in 1510, having taken nothing for forty days but the Eucharist. The Bollandists, drawing on the contemporary testimony of Augustine Justiniani, who saw her uncorrupt body twenty-five years later, treat her as one of the great cases of the late mystical tradition — Doctor _purgatorii,_ in the Latin manuals. The hospital ward she ran is gone. The fire she described has not.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"The pain is the joy not yet large enough."_ — Catherine, _Treatise on Purgatory_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "16", name: "Euphemia"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 16]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Euphemia]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Virgin-martyr of Chalcedon, witness to a council, c. 290–304]

#v(0.5em)

She was a young woman of Chalcedon, the senatorial daughter of a Christian house — Bithynia had been quietly Christian for two generations by the time the great persecution opened in 303 — when the proconsul Priscus published Diocletian's fourth edict and required everyone in the city to sacrifice. Forty-nine of the local Christians refused. They were rounded up and brought to the forum. Forty-eight were sent to Rome for trial. Priscus kept Euphemia. She was the most prominent — the family was known — and he believed that if she could be made to recant, the others would follow.

The acts the Bollandists print walk through the standard catalogue of imperial torture: the rack, the wheel, the millstones, the heated frying-pan, the fire of the furnace into which she was thrown but which (the witnesses said) parted around her like water; finally the arena, where the bears who had been starved for the show declined to touch her, and a single bear, prodded forward, gave her a small wound that killed her. She was nineteen. Her father carried the body a mile out of the city and buried her on the shore of the Bosphorus.

The shrine that grew over her grave became, a century and a half later, the most consequential basilica in the eastern church. In 451 the bishops of the world came to Chalcedon to settle the question of how the divine and human natures held together in Christ, and they met in Euphemia's basilica, with her relics under the high altar. The legend the Greeks tell — and that the Bollandists are too Latin to swear to but too honest to omit — is that the council, after weeks of debate, drew up two creeds, the orthodox and the monophysite, sealed both, and laid them on the breast of the young martyr in her tomb. They locked the church and watched it for three nights. When they opened the tomb on the fourth morning, Euphemia was holding the orthodox creed in her right hand. The other was at her feet.

Whatever happened in the basilica that morning, the council that came out of it shaped Christian doctrine for fifteen hundred years. The girl who would not sacrifice held the church together when its own bishops nearly could not.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"In Chalcedonian shores, the martyr Euphemia consecrates the holy strand with virgin blood."_ — Paulinus of Nola, _Carmen_ on Saint Felix]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "17", name: "Hildegard of Bingen"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 17]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Hildegard of Bingen]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Benedictine abbess, visionary, polymath of the Rhineland, 1098–1179]

#v(0.5em)

She was a small, sickly child, the tenth in a noble Rhineland family, and her parents — following the medieval custom of dedicating the tithe of one's children to God — gave her at the age of eight to the anchorhold of a young noblewoman named Jutta of Sponheim, attached to the monastery of Disibodenberg above the Nahe. She was enclosed with Jutta in a single cell. She would live in monastic enclosure for the next seventy years. When Jutta died in 1136, the small community of women that had grown up around her elected Hildegard their _magistra,_ and the visions she had been having since her fifth year — and had told no one about for forty — began to insist on being written down.

Her two contemporary biographers, Godfrey and Theodoric, both monks of her own community, watched her work for the next forty-three years. She wrote _Scivias,_ the great vision-cycle in which Pope Eugenius III recognized authentic prophecy and which the Council of Trier formally approved in 1147; she wrote two sequel-volumes, _Liber vitae meritorum_ and _Liber divinorum operum;_ she wrote a natural-history encyclopedia (_Physica_), a medical handbook (_Causae et curae_), nine settings of the Mass, seventy-seven antiphons and responsories — the _Symphonia armoniae caelestium revelationum_ is one of the great twelfth-century song-cycles — a morality play with music that may be the first of its kind, almost four hundred letters, and a private language called _Lingua Ignota._ She left Disibodenberg in 1150 over the abbot's objections and founded her own house at Rupertsberg, on a hill above the Rhine outside Bingen. She made four preaching tours up the river in her sixties and seventies — an enclosed nun, preaching in cathedrals to bishops and crowds — and rebuked the emperor Frederick Barbarossa to his face. She quarreled with the Mainz cathedral chapter over a burial in her last year, was placed under interdict, and won.

She died on the seventeenth of September, 1179, at eighty-one years old. The local cult was immediate; the formal canonization arrived eight hundred and thirty-three years later, in 2012, when Benedict XVI declared her a Doctor of the Church. The girl walled into a stone room had not, it turned out, been silenced.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Listen: there was once a king sitting on his throne. Around him stood great and wonderfully beautiful columns, ornamented with ivory…"_ — Hildegard, opening of _Scivias_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "18", name: "Joseph of Cupertino"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 18]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Joseph of Cupertino]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Franciscan friar, ecstatic, patron of slow students, 1603–1663]

#v(0.5em)

He was born in a stable behind his parents' house at Cupertino, a small town on the heel of Italy between Lecce and Nardò, on the seventeenth of June, 1603 — his father, a poor carpenter, had had to leave the house to escape his creditors and his mother, Francesca, gave birth in the outbuilding where she had taken refuge. Joseph grew up dreamy, half-deaf, prone to long staring fits the village called _bocca aperta,_ the open-mouthed boy. He could barely read. The Conventual Franciscans of Grottella turned him away. The Capuchins took him as a lay brother and dismissed him after eight months as too clumsy for the kitchen. The Conventuals reconsidered, took him back as a stable hand, and discovered, slowly, that the slowness was the surface of something else.

He was ordained in 1628 — not because he passed his exams (he did not) but because the bishop, examining the candidates, opened the book at random to the only passage Joseph had managed to learn, _Beata viscera Mariae Virginis,_ and let him through. From that day until his death thirty-five years later he was almost continuously in some form of ecstasy. He levitated. The Bollandists, working from the canonization processes Pastrovicchi summarizes for them, count seventy occasions in which sworn witnesses saw him rise from the floor, sometimes in his cell, often in choir, once in the open during a procession, once at the altar at the elevation, once carrying a heavy wooden cross to the top of a hill at Grottella while gripping it lightly between two fingertips. The phenomenon was so disruptive that his superiors moved him from convent to convent for thirty years to keep crowds from forming. He was forbidden to say Mass in public. He spent his last decade under a kind of monastic house arrest at Osimo, hidden from pilgrims, allowed only the company of his few brothers.

He died on a September evening in 1663, calmly, after the _Salve Regina._ The Bollandists print Pastrovicchi's careful 1753 _Vita,_ and the canonization came in 1767. The slow boy who could not pass an exam became, of all things, the patron of students sitting examinations. _Bocca aperta_ meant something else by then.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Beata viscera Mariae Virginis."_ — _the only passage Joseph could quote, on which the bishop made him a priest_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "19", name: "Theodore of Tarsus"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 19]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Theodore of Tarsus]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Greek monk, archbishop of Canterbury, organizer of the English church, c. 602–690]

#v(0.5em)

He was born at Tarsus in Cilicia — Saint Paul's city — and grew up bilingual in Greek and Syriac, perhaps trilingual if one counts the Latin he picked up later. He studied at Antioch and Constantinople in the 620s, fled the Arab conquest of his homeland in the 640s, and ended up in Rome as an old man, a learned Greek monk in the Asian community on the Aventine, sixty-five years old and expecting nothing further from his life.

In 667 the church at Canterbury, two years bereft of an archbishop after the death of Wighard, asked Pope Vitalian for a candidate. Vitalian asked Hadrian, an African abbot whom the pope had wanted for the post. Hadrian declined and pointed at Theodore. The pope, after consulting the candidate's age and consenting, tonsured him in the Roman fashion (he had worn the Eastern monastic tonsure), waited four months for the hair to grow back, ordained him bishop, and put him on a ship for England.

He was sixty-eight when he stepped off at Canterbury in May 669. He would live another twenty-two years and reorganize the church of an entire kingdom. Bede, who was a child when Theodore died and grew up with men who had known him, gives him the warmest portrait in the _Ecclesiastical History._ He was, Bede writes, _the first archbishop to whom the whole English church consented to give its hand._ He toured every kingdom; he settled the date of Easter once and for all in Northumbria; he divided the over-large dioceses (this is what put him at odds with Wilfrid of York for the rest of his life); he convened the first English national council at Hertford in 672, where the bishops of seven kingdoms sat down together for the first time and signed nine canons that would govern the church for centuries.

The school he and Hadrian opened at Canterbury — Greek, Latin, computus, music, astronomy, scripture — produced a generation of English scholars whose Latin Bede still envied. He died at Canterbury at eighty-eight, on a September morning in 690. The English church he found in eight separate dioceses he left as one. It has held that shape since.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"The first archbishop to whom the whole English church consented to give its hand."_ — Bede, _Historia Ecclesiastica_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "20", name: "John of Egypt"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 20]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[John of Egypt]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Blind confessor, martyr at Caesarea, d. 310]

#v(0.5em)

He was an Egyptian Christian sent into the copper mines of Phaeno around 308, in the same group that took Paphnutius — the same disfiguring sentence had been applied to him: the right eye gouged with a sharpened iron, the left leg crippled with a hot blade, then the desert mines for what was meant to be the rest of his short life. John was already blind in both eyes by the time the executioners came; the older injury seems to have been from an earlier round of torture, and the cauterizing of the second eye was, the Bollandists note from Eusebius, simply the standard sentence applied to a man on whom it could no longer change anything. He was sent to the mines all the same.

What Eusebius preserves of him in book thirteen of the _Martyrs of Palestine_ is one of the strangest and most intimate portraits of an early-fourth-century saint. John could not work the rock — by then he could not see, could not stand straight — and was kept apart from the labor gangs with a few other infirm confessors, in a cell where they prayed and fasted together. Eusebius, who knew him personally, says that John had memorized the entire Bible. The whole of it. Not on stone tablets, the historian writes, _not on parchment that worms and time consume, but in the tablets of the heart, in the soul itself, white and clear, in the pure light of the mind._ When the small assembly of confessors gathered for prayer John would stand in the middle of them and recite — sometimes from Moses, sometimes from the prophets, sometimes a stretch of gospel or epistle — and Eusebius, the first time he heard him, walked forward thinking the blind man must be reading from a book. He was not. He was reciting from inside himself.

In the autumn of 310 the prefect Maximinus, frustrated that the disfigured Christians were not dying as fast as he had wished, ordered them all executed together. Thirty-nine of them, including the bishop Silvanus of Gaza and the blind John, were beheaded outside Caesarea on a single morning. Eusebius watched. _I saw,_ he wrote afterwards, _what truly is a man — not what appears in the outward shape of the body, but what dwells in the soul and in the mind._

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Quippe corpore licet mutilato, nihilominus majorem facultatis suæ vim ac virtutem exerebat."_ — Eusebius, _de Martyribus Palæstinæ_ 13: _though his body was disfigured, the more strength of soul he showed_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "21", name: "Matthew the Apostle"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 21]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Matthew the Apostle]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Tax-collector, evangelist, fl. c. 30, d. by tradition in Ethiopia]

#v(0.5em)

He was sitting at the customs-table at Capernaum on the road from Damascus when Jesus walked past with the first of the disciples and said to him, _Follow me._ The synoptic gospels all preserve the moment, and Matthew's own gospel — which the early church always took to be his — preserves it with a brevity that has the force of an unembellished memory. _And he arose and followed him. And it came to pass, as Jesus sat at meat in the house, behold many publicans and sinners came and sat down with him and his disciples._ He had been a Levi son of Alphaeus, paid by Herod's tetrarchy to collect taxes for Rome at the customs-post on the Sea of Galilee, the despised middle-man who took his cut. He left the table.

The Bollandists work patiently through the medieval _acta_ — the legend of the apostle's mission to Aethiopia, his confrontation with the magicians Zaroes and Arphaxat, his raising of the king's daughter, his martyrdom under the king's brother Hirtacus who wanted the consecrated virgin Iphigenia for himself — and conclude what the early church already knew: most of the _acta_ is fabulous, descended from a sixth-century pseudo-Abdias the historians have learned to discount. What survives, however, is older. Eusebius and Origen and Clement of Alexandria all knew that Matthew had preached east of Palestine after the dispersion of the apostles in 42, that he had written his gospel for Hebrew Christians and probably first in Aramaic before the Greek redaction, and that he had ended in martyrdom somewhere on the upper Nile or beyond it — _Aethiopia_ in the broad ancient sense, perhaps Axum, perhaps further south, perhaps Persia. He is venerated under the high altar at Salerno, where his relics were translated in 954 from Brittany, where they had been hidden during the Saracen raids, having come west across the Mediterranean a long way from where the apostle laid them down.

What he himself wrote of the call he received outranks anything written about him afterwards. _Follow me,_ the Lord said, and he arose. The Greek of his own gospel uses the same verb — _anastas_ — that the gospels use, after the resurrection, for the Lord. Matthew got up.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"And he arose, and followed him."_ — Matthew on his own call, _Matthew_ 9:9]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "22", name: "Maurice and the Theban Legion"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 22]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Maurice and the Theban Legion]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Officer and his soldiers, martyred at Agaunum in the Alps, c. 287]

#v(0.5em)

The legion was Egyptian — recruited from the Christian population of the upper Thebaid where Pachomius would soon be founding monasteries — and it had been moved across the empire in the 280s to reinforce Maximian's campaigns against the Bagaudae of Gaul. By the late summer of 287 the legion was crossing the Pennine Alps toward the Rhone valley, billeted at Agaunum in the narrow gorge of the Valais where the river squeezes between cliffs. The emperor had ordered every unit of the marching army to sacrifice to the gods before the next engagement and to put any Christians among them to death. Maurice, the _primicerius_ — first centurion of the legion — refused on his own behalf and on behalf of his men.

The standard hagiography preserved by Eucherius of Lyon a century and a half later, which the Bollandists print after weighing it and finding it substantially defensible, gives the speech he sent back to the emperor. _We are your soldiers,_ he wrote, _but we are also Christ's. We owe you our military service; we owe him our innocence. From you we accept our pay; from him we have life. We cannot follow our emperor into a denial of our God. If we are required to do further wickedness — to shed innocent blood, to persecute the faithful — we will rather suffer the sword. We are armed and we will not resist. We will die rather than live by killing the innocent._

The emperor decimated the legion: every tenth man pulled out of line and beheaded. The survivors held. He decimated them again. And again. The Bollandists are careful with the round number — six thousand six hundred Christians is the medieval count, and probably an exaggeration — but the core of the story they regard as ancient and authentic. A whole unit of imperial soldiers, in the hour they had been trained for, refused.

The bodies were left where they fell at the river-bend below the cliffs of Agaunum. A Christian community grew up around the burial. Theodore, bishop of Octodurum, raised a small church over them around 380; Sigismund of Burgundy expanded it in 515; the abbey of Saint-Maurice d'Agaune that grew from those buildings is the oldest continuously functioning monastery north of the Alps. The men who refused to draw their swords against their fellow Christians have not been disturbed in fifteen hundred years.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"We are your soldiers, but we are also Christ's."_ — Maurice, in the message to Maximian]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "23", name: "Adamnan of Iona"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 23]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Adamnan of Iona]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Abbot, biographer, peacemaker, c. 624–704]

#v(0.5em)

He was born in Donegal a generation after Columba died, a kinsman of the Cenél Conaill — Columba's own people — and entered Iona as a young monk under the abbot Seghine, who had himself been trained by men who remembered the founder. He was elected ninth abbot of Iona in 679, in his middle fifties, and held the office for twenty-five years. The community he inherited was the head of a great federation of Columban houses spread from western Ireland to the Hebrides to Lindisfarne — the largest monastic network in the British Isles at the time — and the most pressing piece of unfinished business the founder had left him was the question of when to keep Easter. Iona kept the old Celtic computation; Rome and most of the rest of the church kept the newer Alexandrian calendar; the difference could put the date as much as a month apart. Adamnan, after a visit to Wearmouth-Jarrow in 686 where he met Bede's master Ceolfrid, became persuaded that the Roman date was correct. He spent the rest of his life trying, and largely failing, to bring his own community along. Iona did not adopt the Roman Easter until eleven years after his death.

He wrote two books that have outlasted the controversy. _De Locis Sanctis_ — a careful guidebook to the holy places of the East, written down from the eyewitness account of a Frankish bishop named Arculf who had been shipwrecked on Iona on his way back from Jerusalem — became the standard pilgrim's reference of the early medieval church and was abridged by Bede a generation later. _Vita Sancti Columbae_ — three books on the founder, drawn from the community's living memory and the records kept since Columba's death in 597 — is one of the most beautiful early-medieval saints' lives.

His other great work was political. In 697, at the synod of Birr in the Irish midlands, he gathered the bishops and kings of Ireland and persuaded them to enact what is still called _Cáin Adomnáin_ — the Law of the Innocents — the first written legislation in Western Europe to forbid the killing of women, children, and clergy in war. He died on Iona in his eightieth year. The biography of Columba he had written, the law he had given Ireland, the Easter he could not quite carry: three works of patience, two of which lasted.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"In every conflict, spare the women, the children, and those who pray."_ — _Cáin Adomnáin,_ 697]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "24", name: "Gerard of Csanád"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 24]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Gerard of Csanád]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Venetian monk, missionary bishop, martyr of the Magyars, c. 980–1046]

#v(0.5em)

He was born Gerardo Sagredo to a noble Venetian family, entered the Benedictine abbey of San Giorgio Maggiore as a boy, was elected abbot in his early thirties, and resigned the office to set out for the Holy Land. He never got there. Stopped in Hungary on the way through, he was recognized by King Stephen — who had been gathering missionary monks for the new Christian kingdom for twenty years — and persuaded to stay. Stephen made him tutor to the prince Imre, then sent him into the southern marches of the kingdom, the wild country between the Danube and the Tisza where the Magyar tribes were still half-pagan and the priests were few. Gerard fixed his see at Csanád on the Maros river, the first bishopric of southeastern Hungary, and stayed there for fifteen years.

He was a Benedictine of the strict observance: rose at midnight to chant the office, kept perpetual silence except for preaching and confession, ate one meal a day. His preaching was famous — he learned enough Magyar to preach in it — and he wrote, while bishop, a biblical commentary called the _Deliberatio supra hymnum trium puerorum,_ one of the earliest surviving works of theology composed in central Europe, in which he meditated on Daniel 3 and the witness of those who would not bow to the king's image. The Bollandists print the older eleventh-century _Vita,_ careful to distinguish it from a fifteenth-century interpolation that mixes legend into the simpler narrative.

When King Stephen died in 1038 the Christian settlement of Hungary nearly came apart. The pagan party rose under a chieftain named Vata; the next two kings were assassinated within four years; the missionary bishops were marked. In September 1046 Gerard was traveling toward Buda to meet the new king when his party was attacked at the ford of the Danube below Pest. He was sixty-six. The pagan warriors stoned him, broke his cross over his head, and threw him over the cliff into the river, on what is still called Gellért Hill in Budapest — the _montis Gerardi._ The body was recovered that night and taken back to Csanád, where Stephen's grandnephew Ladislaus elevated his relics in 1083 alongside Stephen and Imre. The Magyar nation he had helped baptize keeps his memory still: _Szent Gellért,_ the bishop of the bridge.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Suscipiam coronam, quam mihi Dominus paravit."_ — _Gerard, when warned of the ambush at the Danube ford_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "25", name: "Cleophas"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 25]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Cleophas]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[The Emmaus disciple, kinsman of the Lord, fl. 30, d. by tradition c. 36]

#v(0.5em)

He is named once in Luke and once in John, and the tradition has spent two thousand years trying to fit the two mentions into one man. Luke 24 calls him _Kleopas,_ one of the two disciples walking out from Jerusalem on the afternoon of the first Easter — leaving the city, the gospel says, in defeat — when a stranger fell into step with them on the road. They told him about the women's report from the empty tomb and about their own crushed hopes; _we had hoped,_ they said, _that it had been he which should have redeemed Israel._ The stranger walked seven miles with them opening the scriptures, sat down at their table at Emmaus, took the bread, broke it, and at that moment they recognized him. He vanished. They got up — _eadem hora,_ the Vulgate has, _the same hour_ — and walked the seven miles back to Jerusalem in the dark.

John's gospel mentions a _Klopas_ once, the husband of one of the Marys at the foot of the cross. The early church almost universally took the two Cleophases to be the same man, and Hegesippus (preserved in Eusebius) makes him further the brother of Joseph the husband of the Virgin — which would make him the Lord's uncle by marriage, and his son Simeon, who became the second bishop of Jerusalem, the Lord's cousin. The Bollandists, working through Bede and Ado and the Roman _Parvum_ and the Greek menologies, treat the genealogy as plausible if unprovable. What they treat as more secure is the old Latin tradition the Roman martyrology preserves: that Cleophas was killed at his own house in Emmaus, perhaps in the dispersion of the Jerusalem disciples after the death of Stephen around 36, and was buried in the room where he had once given Christ supper.

He was not an apostle, not an evangelist, not a writer; the church does not even know what day he died. What it remembers is the conversation on the road. He had walked seven miles in defeat. He walked seven miles back in joy. Most discipleship happens in the time between.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Did not our hearts burn within us, while he talked with us by the way, and while he opened to us the scriptures?"_ — Cleophas to his companion, _Luke_ 24:32]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "26", name: "Cyprian and Justina"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 26]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Cyprian and Justina]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[A magician converted, a virgin who held, martyrs of Nicomedia, c. 304]

#v(0.5em)

He was not the Cyprian of Carthage who fell to the sword twelve days earlier in the calendar — the Bollandists are firm about the distinction — but a different man, a pagan magician of Antioch in Syria, son of priestly families on both sides who had been initiated into the mysteries at Athens, Argos, Sparta, Eleusis, Phrygia, and finally at Memphis under the priests of Egypt. He had become, by the time he settled at Antioch, the most accomplished sorcerer in the East. He had a paying clientele. One of them, a young Greek named Aglaides, came to him in the spring of 304 because he was in love with a Christian girl in the city named Justina, daughter of a converted priest of Apollo, and Justina would not have him.

Cyprian undertook the commission. He sent the demon of fornication — first one, then a second more powerful, then the prince of all of them, the demon his arts were strongest at compelling. None of them could touch her. She prayed; she fasted; she wore the cross openly at her throat; the demons returned to Cyprian humiliated and were sent back; they returned again; they would not go a fourth time. Cyprian, in the dialogue Gregory of Nazianzen preserves in his eighteenth oration, asked the strongest of his servants what kind of being it was that they could not move. _One,_ the demon answered, _who has the sign of the crucified._ Cyprian understood, in that moment, that the powers he had served were neither what he had thought them to be nor as strong as what stood against them. He went home, burned his books in the public square in front of his neighbors, and presented himself for baptism at the Antiochene church.

Justina forgave him. He was eventually ordained, then made bishop of his own city; she became deaconess and abbess of a community of consecrated women under his pastoral care. The persecution caught up with them both. They were arrested by the prefect at Damascus, transferred to Nicomedia, and beheaded together with a young man named Theoctistus who had stepped forward at the trial to confess himself a Christian on the spot. Their bodies were thrown to the dogs and recovered by Christian sailors who carried them to Rome, where they have been venerated ever since at the Lateran. The magician who had tried to deliver an innocent woman to a young man's lust was delivered, by her, to Christ.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"One who has the sign of the crucified."_ — _the demon's answer to Cyprian, on Justina_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "27", name: "Cosmas and Damian"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 27]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Cosmas and Damian]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Arabian twin physicians, the silverless ones, martyrs at Aegae, c. 287]

#v(0.5em)

They were Arabian Christians, twin brothers — _gemini,_ the _acta_ call them — born to a noblewoman named Theodota who had brought them up alone after their father died, raising them in the faith and in the medical art that ran in their mother's family. They studied at Syria and Cilicia, settled at Aegae on the Cilician coast across the gulf from Cyprus, opened a small dispensary near the harbor, and began treating the poor of the town. Their distinguishing practice — the one that gave them their Greek name, _anargyroi,_ the silverless — was that they would not accept payment. Not from the poor, who could not have paid. Not from the rich, who would have. The Bollandists print the long _acta_ preserved in Bede and Ado: a steady traffic of the lame and the lepers and the demon-tormented, healings credited not to their skill but to their prayers, three brothers — Anthimus, Leontius, Euprepius — who joined the practice and worked alongside them.

The persecution found them in 287, perhaps 297; the dating is uncertain, but the prefect Lysias was real and the trial is preserved in the protocol shape of authentic court records. The twins were arrested with their three brothers and brought before him. Asked their names and their country, Cosmas answered for all five: _We are from Arabia. Our names are Cosmas and Damian. We are Christians. We have brothers also, Anthimus, Leontius, and Euprepius._ The order was for sacrifice. They refused. Lysias tried, in turn — the _acta_ are stylized but consistent — bound and thrown into the sea, from which an angel released them; bound and cast into a fire, which would not burn them; lapidated, the stones turning back on the stone-throwers; shot at with arrows, the arrows returning likewise; finally, when the spectacle had run out and the prefect was tired, beheaded.

The twin brothers who had refused to accept silver became the patrons of the medical guilds. Felix IV built them a basilica in the Roman Forum in 526 — the _santi Cosma e Damiano_ still stands, the apse mosaic among the great sixth-century works of Christian art — and the Medici family of Florence took them as patrons because of the punning likeness of _medici_ to physicians. Their feast remains, in the East and the West, a day for praying over those who treat the sick.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"We are Christians. We have brothers also: Anthimus, Leontius, and Euprepius."_ — Cosmas, before Lysias]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "28", name: "Wenceslaus of Bohemia"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 28]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Wenceslaus of Bohemia]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Duke and martyr, the good king of the carol, c. 907–935]

#v(0.5em)

His grandmother Ludmila, who had been baptized along with her husband Bořivoj by Methodius the Apostle of the Slavs, raised him in the new religion against the strong opposition of his pagan mother Drahomíra. When his father died young in battle around 921, Drahomíra became regent and had Ludmila strangled in her oratory at Tetín to break the Christian party at court. Wenceslaus, fourteen, was sent into hiding. He came back at eighteen, took the throne with the support of the bishops and the German emperor Henry the Fowler, sent his mother into internal exile, and began to govern.

His biographer Christian — a monk of Břevnov writing within fifty years of the events, himself a great-grandnephew of the duke — gives him the warmest possible portrait. He kept perpetual chastity in his palace, rose before dawn for the office, walked barefoot to Mass through the snow even in winter, brought corn to widows and firewood to the cottages above the Vltava, paid the tribute to the emperor without resentment, founded the church of Saint Vitus on the hill where the cathedral now stands, gathered priests to teach his people, and — the hardest part — pardoned his enemies repeatedly, including his younger brother Boleslav, who hated him from boyhood and resented being kept from the throne.

In September 935 Boleslav invited him to the dedication of a new church at his estate at Stará Boleslav, a day's ride down the Elbe. Wenceslaus came. The two brothers ate together, drank together, parted for the night embracing, and the next morning, as Wenceslaus walked alone to the early Mass, Boleslav and three of his retainers met him at the chapel door. Wenceslaus saw the swords and said, very quietly — Christian preserves the line — _Frater, Dominus tecum sit._ _Brother, may the Lord be with you._ Boleslav struck him down on the threshold. He was twenty-eight.

The body was carried back to Prague and buried in the church of Saint Vitus he had built; the cult was immediate and immense; within a generation Boleslav himself, in old age and remorse, was begging for his brother's intercession. The English know him from the carol — _Good King Wenceslas looked out, on the Feast of Stephen_ — but he was not a king and the Stephen of the carol is the day after Christmas, not his day. What the carol does have right is the open hand: bread and pine-logs to a poor man's house in deep snow, and a master who would carry the load himself when the page was failing.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Frater, Dominus tecum sit."_ — Wenceslaus, at the chapel door at Stará Boleslav]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "29", name: "Michael, and All the Holy Angels"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 29]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Michael, and All the Holy Angels]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Archangel and the heavenly host, feast known as Michaelmas]

#v(0.5em)

The day was originally a dedication. Around the year 490 a herdsman named Gargano, looking for a stray bull on the rocky promontory that bears his name in southern Italy, found the animal kneeling at the mouth of a cave high on Monte Sant'Angelo. He shot an arrow at it; the arrow turned in the air and struck the herdsman. The local bishop, hearing the story, fasted and prayed; an angel appeared and identified himself as Michael, _guardian of this place,_ and asked for a church on the cave. The shrine raised over the cave's entrance — the basilica of San Michele Arcangelo on Monte Gargano — became, with Saint James at Compostela and the tomb of the apostles in Rome, one of the three great pilgrimage sites of medieval Western Christianity. Its dedication day, the twenty-ninth of September, the Latin church kept thereafter as the feast of Michael and all the angels.

The Bollandists trace the spread of the cult: the second basilica that Pope Boniface raised at Rome on the Circus Maximus in the seventh century; the third on the Via Salaria seven miles from the city; the great Norman foundation of Mont-Saint-Michel rising out of the tide on the border of Normandy and Brittany after a vision in 708; the further chain of Michael shrines that ran the whole length of the _via Sacra Langobardorum_ down the Italian peninsula and across the Channel to Cornwall and Skellig Michael off the Irish coast. The angel of the Apocalypse who fights the dragon, of Daniel who guards the people of Israel, of Jude's letter who would not bring railing accusation against the devil but said only _the Lord rebuke thee_ — Michael is the angel the church set as the patron of soldiers, of the dying, of high places, of the moment when one age yields to another.

The day in the West has always belonged to the angels generally. The Eastern church keeps the _Synaxis of the Bodiless Powers_ on November 8 — Michael, Gabriel, Raphael, Uriel, the cherubim, the seraphim, the thrones, the dominions — but the Latin tradition has kept the autumn feast as a feast of the whole heavenly host. The crops are in by the twenty-ninth in northern Europe; the rents are due; the equinox has just turned. The angels who watch the changing of the seasons are remembered when the season changes.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Quis ut Deus?"_ — _Michael's name in Hebrew: Who is like God?_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "September", day: "30", name: "Jerome"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[September 30]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Jerome]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Translator, polemicist, hermit of Bethlehem, c. 347–c. 420]

#v(0.5em)

He was born at Stridon on the border between Dalmatia and Pannonia, a small frontier town the barbarians erased from the map within his lifetime. He was sent to Rome as a teenager to study grammar and rhetoric under the great Donatus, baptized there in his late teens by Pope Liberius, took up the Christian life slowly — he confesses in the letters that he loved Cicero too well, that the famous dream in which the Lord rebuked him as _Ciceronianus, non Christianus, more Ciceronian than Christian,_ was the turning. He went east in his thirties, into the Syrian desert at Chalcis, where he lived for four years as a hermit, learning Hebrew from a converted rabbi to give his mind something to grip while it broke its more delicate addictions. The languages stayed with him. The mood, mostly, did not.

He returned to Rome in 382 as secretary to Pope Damasus, who set him the task that would consume the rest of his life: a single Latin Bible to replace the chaos of competing Old Latin versions then circulating. Damasus died in 384; Jerome left Rome under a cloud — the Roman clergy who had hoped to succeed Damasus had not appreciated his blunt assessment of their morals — and went back to the East with Paula and her household. They settled at Bethlehem in 386. Paula founded a monastery for women on the slope below the basilica of the Nativity; Jerome founded one for men on the opposite slope. He stayed for thirty-four years.

The Vulgate took him most of those years. Working from the Hebrew (against the conventional preference for the Greek Septuagint), revising the gospels against the Greek, polishing one book and then another, he produced — with Paula's literary criticism and her household's transcription — the Latin Bible that would be the only Bible the Western church knew for a thousand years, and the foundation of every translation that came after. The Bollandists take three full opening sections to defend him against his detractors. _Even the prophets and the apostles,_ they note dryly, _spoke sharply when the case required._ Jerome's case very often required.

He died at his desk in his cell beside the Cave of the Nativity, an old man, perhaps in his early seventies, on a September evening around 420. The translation was finished. The temper, by then, had quieted. He was buried near Paula and Eustochium, who had gone before him.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Ignoratio Scripturarum, ignoratio Christi est."_ — Jerome, _Commentary on Isaiah,_ prologue]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "1", name: "Remigius of Reims"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 1]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Remigius of Reims]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, apostle to the Franks, c. 437–c. 533]

#v(0.5em)

He was the third son of a Gallo-Roman senatorial family on the Marne, born at Laon around 437, the youngest of three children whose lives all left marks: his sister Latiana, mother of Bishop Lupus of Soissons; his elder brother Principius, bishop of Soissons before Lupus; and Remigius himself, made bishop of Reims at twenty-two over his loud objections, in a city whose Roman walls were already half-empty as the imperial administration of northern Gaul collapsed around him. He held the see for seventy-two years, the longest episcopate any of the Gallic chronicles record. He outlived the Empire he had been ordained in, the kingdoms of the Visigoths and Burgundians that filled the vacuum, and finally most of the Frankish chieftains who had divided the country once Childeric was gone.

What he is remembered for is one Christmas Eve. Clovis, the young Salian Frank who had killed his way to leadership of the western Franks at fifteen, married the Burgundian Christian princess Clotilde in 493; she had been working on him for four years; her two sons by him she had baptized over his shouted protests, the elder dying as an infant. In 496 Clovis was nearly defeated at Tolbiac by the Alemanni and, the chronicler Hincmar tells the story Gregory of Tours had told before him, called on his wife's God: _if you give me victory I will be baptized._ The Alemanni broke. He marched home to Reims. Remigius, who had been waiting for this conversation for ten years, took the king into the cathedral on the eve of the Nativity of 496 — three thousand of his warriors with him by the Frankish chronicles, perhaps fewer in fact — and baptized him with the famous formula that has been preserved without serious doubt: _Worship what you have burned and burn what you have worshipped._

The Bollandists devote a long opening section to the difficulty of dating Remigius's other miracles and translations — three separate elevations of his body in three centuries — and to the manuscript tradition of his Vita. What survives the editorial care is the man behind the formula: a bishop who held one chair through every collapse and through the conversion of the people who would build Europe out of the rubble.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Mitis depone colla, Sicamber: adora quod incendisti, et incende quod adorasti."_ — Remigius to Clovis, at the font, 496]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "2", name: "Leodegar of Autun"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 2]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Leodegar of Autun]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop blinded in the politics of the Merovingian collapse, c. 616–679]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Frankish nobleman of the Burgundian aristocracy, raised at the court of the dowager queen Bathild who had been his guardian after his father's early death, educated at Poitiers under his uncle the bishop, ordained deacon, then archdeacon, then in 663 sent up the Saône to Autun as bishop of one of the most important sees in Burgundy. He stayed there for nine years and rebuilt the city. Autun in the 660s had been gutted by the dynastic fighting around the Merovingian throne; the cathedral was unroofed, the canonical residence was a ruin, the diocese had not had a settled bishop for three years. Leodegar repaired the buildings, restored the schools, fed the poor through three bad harvests, and held the western synod of Autun in 670 that issued the canons against the Monothelite heresy and the Trinitarian formulas the Western church would adopt for a thousand years.

Then Frankish politics caught up with him. Ebroin, the mayor of the palace under the boy-king Theuderic III, had been deposed in 673 in favor of a more moderate party; in 675 he came back, and he came back hunting the men who had deposed him. Leodegar had been one of them. The bishop was arrested at Autun in 676, brought to Ebroin's villa at Fécamp, blinded with a hot iron, his lips and tongue cut out, and held for two years in a Norman monastery to make sure he died slowly. He did not die. In 678 Ebroin had him pulled from the cell, given a hasty trial in absentia, deposed from his see, and beheaded in the forest of Sarcing in Artois.

His brother Gerin, who had stood by him through the whole captivity and refused to renounce him, was stoned to death in 676 at the same king's villa, in the gory parallel the Bollandists devote a long opening section to documenting. Their joint _acta_ — the older Vita by an anonymous Autun cleric who knew Leodegar personally, then the longer recension by a monk of Saint-Symphorien — survive together. The translation of Leodegar's body to the abbey of Saint-Maixent in Poitou in 681 set off a cult that ran across half of France: more than two hundred medieval parishes were dedicated to him, and the village of Saint-Léger turns up in nearly every department.

He held his see for nine years and was tortured for three, and the tortured years are the ones the church remembered.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Etiam si me totum lacerare voluerit, quod meum est non perdam."_ — Leodegar, to Ebroin's interrogator]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "3", name: "The Two Ewalds"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 3]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[The Two Ewalds]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[English missionaries among the continental Saxons, d. c. 695]

#v(0.5em)

They had the same name. Bede distinguishes them only by the color of their hair — _Ewald the Black_ and _Ewald the White,_ dark and fair — and by the fact that the dark Ewald was the more learned of the two, a Scripture scholar of some accomplishment, while the fair Ewald was the more prayerful, a man of long silences. They had both been trained in Ireland, both ordained priest, and both heard in 692 that Willibrord was leaving for Frisia to do for the continent what Augustine had done in England a hundred years earlier. The Ewalds did not go to Frisia. They walked east instead.

They crossed into the country of the Bructeri, between the Lippe and the Ruhr, and stayed at the house of the local headman, who agreed to take them to the chieftain of his tribe. The headman delayed. The Ewalds sang the office every morning and the local people noticed; the news reached the warriors of the village before it reached the chieftain; the warriors decided that strangers who set up an altar in a borrowed cottage and sang in a language none of them knew were planning to convert their lord, and that conversions of lords meant the end of the old gods. They came for the Ewalds in the morning. The fair Ewald they killed first, by the sword. The dark Ewald, because of his learning, they tortured first and then dismembered. The bodies were thrown into the Rhine.

The chieftain, when he heard, ordered the village burned and a hundred and fifty of the inhabitants killed in penalty. The bodies of the Ewalds floated forty miles up the Rhine to the Christian settlement at Cologne, where they were recovered and buried by Pippin of Heristal, who had heard the report and sent men down the bank looking for them.

Two priests with the same name had walked into a country that was not asking for them, sung the office in a borrowed cottage, and died on a Sunday morning before they had baptized anyone. The mission they had begun without seeing the end of it was carried on by Suidbert, by Boniface, by the long generations after them. _No man knows the day,_ the dark Ewald used to say of his own death, _but a man may know what he ought to do today._

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"A man may know what he ought to do today."_ — Ewald the Black, in the older _Passio_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "4", name: "Francis of Assisi"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 4]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Francis of Assisi]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Founder of the Friars Minor, the Poor One, 1181–1226]

#v(0.5em)

He was the son of a cloth merchant in Assisi, a vain and charming youth who spent his teens in extravagant suppers and his early twenties at war — first against Perugia, where he was captured and held for a year, then in arms again, then a long illness, then the sudden mid-riding return from a campaign he could no longer make himself care about. He was twenty-three when he kissed the leper outside the gates of Assisi and felt, he would say afterwards, _what had been bitter become sweet._ He was twenty-four when he heard the painted crucifix at San Damiano speak: _Francis, repair my house, which as you see is falling into ruins._ He took the words at their plainest, sold his father's bolts of cloth, and started to rebuild the chapel with his own hands. His father took him to court; he stripped naked in the bishop's presence and gave back the clothes; the bishop covered him with his own cloak.

The first companions came in 1208. By 1209 there were eleven of them, and Innocent III approved their first rule. The order grew with a speed neither Francis nor anyone else had foreseen. By 1217 there were five thousand brothers; by 1219 Francis was in Egypt with the Fifth Crusade, walking unarmed across no man's land to preach to the Sultan al-Kamil, who heard him for several days, refused to convert, but gave him safe passage out. He returned to find an order he could no longer govern, resigned the leadership, withdrew into the Umbrian hills.

In September 1224 on Mount La Verna, after forty days of fasting, he received in his own hands and feet and side the wounds of the Christ he had been preaching for sixteen years — the first stigmatic in church history, attested by the brothers who washed and dressed him in his last two years. He died at the Portiuncula on the evening of the third of October, 1226, lying naked on the bare ground at his own request, singing the _Canticle of the Sun,_ in his forty-fifth year.

Three biographies were written within a generation. Most of what is in them, Thomas of Celano says, _I heard from his own mouth, or from faithful and tested witnesses._ The witness is what the church has loved. A man who took a verse at its plainest, who kissed what he had recoiled from, who heard a painted Christ ask for his house and gave it.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Lord, make me an instrument of your peace."_ — _the prayer attributed to him, fitting if not strictly from his pen_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "5", name: "Galla of Rome"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 5]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Galla of Rome]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Senatorial widow, hidden mystic, c. 489–c. 546]

#v(0.5em)

She was the daughter of Symmachus the consul, the great Roman statesman whom Theodoric the Goth executed in 525 along with her cousin Boethius — Symmachus had stood by his son-in-law to the end and paid for it. Galla was perhaps thirty when her father was killed; she had been recently married, recently widowed, and was suddenly the head of a household stripped of its protector by an Arian king with a long memory. She did not remarry. She gave away the family lands, kept enough to live on, walked across the Tiber to the cluster of small monastic houses that had grown up against the walls of Saint Peter's basilica, and asked to be received as one of them.

Gregory the Great, who had grown up in Rome hearing the Vatican community talk about her, devotes a chapter of his _Dialogues_ to her. The story is small and quiet. She had developed, in middle age, a painful condition the physicians of Rome warned her was fatal — they had told her that she would have to remarry to remain healthy, that the celibate life would kill her. She thanked them and stayed. She lived another sixteen years. She slept in a small cell against the wall of the basilica, took the office every night with the brothers across the way, ran a hospice in her front room for the women among the Roman poor whose husbands had died in the Gothic wars and who had nowhere to go. _She was a woman of grave habits,_ Gregory writes, _who so much preferred the love of God to the love of the world that she gave her thoughts to nothing else._

In her last illness she saw, the night before she died, the apostle Peter coming to her cell. She asked him whether her sins were forgiven. _Veni,_ he answered — _come._ She asked him to wait three days for her dearest friend, the abbess Benedicta, to be ready to come with her. He nodded; he came back three days later for both of them. Galla died first; Benedicta was buried beside her thirty days later, at the same Mass.

She built no order, wrote no book, founded no monastery, and held a hospice in her front room for sixteen years. The widow of a murdered consul kept a small cell against a basilica wall and lived there until Peter came for her. _She gave her thoughts to nothing else._

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Veni."_ — Peter to Galla, in the vision of her last days]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "6", name: "Bruno of Cologne"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 6]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Bruno of Cologne]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Founder of the Carthusians, the silent reformer, c. 1030–1101]

#v(0.5em)

He was born into a noble Cologne family, sent south for his schooling at Reims, and stayed for thirty years — first as student of the cathedral school, then as its master, then as its director. He taught grammar, dialectic, theology; the men he trained included the future Pope Urban II, who would never forget him. In 1075 he was elected chancellor of the see of Reims under Archbishop Manasses, and in that office he discovered, slowly and with horror, that his archbishop was openly simoniacal — selling ordinations, appointments, dispensations — and that the diocesan administration had become a market. He spent five years pressing for reform from inside, was driven out of the city when Manasses had him exiled, was vindicated when the council of Autun deposed the archbishop in 1080, was offered the see itself, and refused it.

He was past fifty by then. He had decided, while in exile, that his life would not run any longer in the cathedral world. With six companions he walked south through the autumn of 1084 to ask the bishop of Grenoble — Hugh, his old student — for a wild place in the mountains where they could live as hermits under a common rule. Hugh took them up to a high alpine valley above the Guiers Mort river, four thousand feet in the air, snowbound seven months a year, with a rim of cliffs all around it. _Cartusia,_ the place was called: the Charterhouse. They built seven small cells around a central church, kept silence except in choir, ate one meal a day, took their work alone in their cells, met only for the night office and the chapter of faults. The form they invented — neither cenobitic nor purely eremitic but a middle thing — survived them. The Carthusians have kept the same rule for nine hundred years, with so few changes that the saying _Cartusia numquam reformata, quia numquam deformata_ — _never reformed, because never deformed_ — has been taken into proverbs.

Bruno would not have liked the proverb. He was modest under praise, humble in office. In 1090 his old student Urban II called him to Rome as papal advisor; he came reluctantly, served briefly, refused the archbishopric of Reggio Calabria, withdrew further south to a wild Calabrian valley where he founded a second Charterhouse at La Torre. He died there on the sixth of October, 1101.

He left no rule in writing, no formal life. What he left was the silence the order has kept since. The cathedral master who would not be archbishop went up the mountain and built seven cells, and nine hundred years later they are still being lived in.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"O bonitas!"_ — _the only saying his disciples preserved of him, his frequent ejaculation in choir: O goodness!_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "7", name: "Justina of Padua"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 7]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Justina of Padua]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Virgin-martyr, patron of the Republic of Venice, d. probably c. 304]

#v(0.5em)

Her _acta,_ the Bollandists frankly concede, are late and unreliable — a tenth-century _passio,_ a parallel account attributed to her teacher Prosdocimus, both clearly post-Lombard fabrications written when the original sources had been destroyed in the sack of Padua. What survives the editorial knife is a daughter of the Veneto Christian senatorial class, baptized in childhood by Prosdocimus — the legendary first bishop of Padua — and put to death by the sword on the seventh of October during the persecution of Maximian. The _acta_ set the death-scene at the southern gate of Padua, by the marshes of the Bacchiglione, where the basilica that bears her name now rises.

Whatever the historical core, the cult is ancient and unbroken. The Lombard king Opilio raised the first basilica over her grave in the sixth century; the Benedictines took the adjacent monastery in the ninth; the great abbey of Santa Giustina, rebuilt and rebuilt, became one of the most important houses in northern Italy and the seed of the Cassinese reform that swept the Italian Benedictine world in the fifteenth century. The Republic of Venice took her as one of its patron saints — second only to Mark — and the Venetian fleet under Don Juan of Austria sailed to its great victory over the Ottoman navy at Lepanto on the morning of the seventh of October, 1571. The Christian galleys carried her image at their mastheads. The whole maritime league had been fasting on her vigil. Pius V, watching from a window in Rome, knew the battle had been won at the moment it was won. The day became, by his bull the next year, the feast of Our Lady of Victory — later renamed Our Lady of the Rosary — and the autumn calendar gained a Marian feast on the day Justina had held for thirteen hundred years.

The basilica at Padua still keeps her bones, beneath the immense Venetian-Renaissance dome at the head of the Prato della Valle. She is older than the cathedral that holds her, older than the city walls she lies inside, almost older than the church she belonged to: a girl of perhaps fifteen who would not pour wine on a pagan altar in 304, and whose silence has shaped one corner of Italian Christian memory ever since.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Justina virgo, in cuius natalitio fugavit Christianus de mari Turcam."_ — Pius V's own note, 1571]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "8", name: "Demetrius of Thessalonica"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 8]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Demetrius of Thessalonica]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Soldier-martyr, the Megalomartyr of the East, c. 270–c. 306]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Roman officer in the army of Maximian Galerius, born in Thessalonica to a senatorial Christian family that had so far escaped the imperial notice. The persecution that opened in 303 caught up with him in the autumn of 306, when his father, the consularis of Thessaly and Macedonia, died and the emperor appointed Demetrius to succeed him in the office. Demetrius accepted the appointment and used it to begin openly catechizing the Thessalonican Christian community in the porticoes of the city's bath-house, where his official duties gave him an excuse to gather crowds. Galerius, passing through the city for one of his winter campaigns, heard of it. He summoned Demetrius. The young officer admitted everything. He was confined to a cell beneath the public baths to await trial.

In the cell he was visited by a young Christian named Nestor who was about to fight in the amphitheater the next day against Lyaeus, the emperor's champion gladiator. Nestor came to Demetrius for a blessing. _In the name of the God of Demetrius,_ he was to say at the start of the bout, and he did, and he killed the champion. Galerius, furious, had Nestor executed at once and ordered a squad of soldiers down to the cellar to dispatch Demetrius with their spears. They came at dawn and pierced him through the side. He bled out into the floor of the cellar.

The cellar where he died, eventually paved over and built into the foundation of a fifth-century basilica, became the most visited shrine in Greek Christianity outside Constantinople. The basilica that grew over it — the great five-aisled Hagios Demetrios in the upper city of Thessalonica, twice destroyed by fire and twice rebuilt — has held its place at the head of Greek hagiography for fifteen hundred years. Demetrius is the _megalomartyr,_ paired in the East with Saint George; the patron of Thessalonica, whose icon was credited with defending the city against Avar, Slav, Bulgar, Norman, and Ottoman in turn; the _myrrh-streaming_ one whose tomb, in the medieval centuries, was reported to weep an oil that healed sicknesses.

A young officer who used the privileges of his rank to teach the catechism in a public place, who blessed a friend going into a fight, who waited in his cell for the spears to come, has been remembered for a thousand seven hundred years.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"In the name of the God of Demetrius."_ — Nestor, in the amphitheater at Thessalonica, 306]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "9", name: "Denis of Paris and Companions"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 9]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Denis of Paris and Companions]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[First bishop of Paris, with Rusticus and Eleutherius, c. 250–c. 286]

#v(0.5em)

He had been sent into Gaul, as Gregory of Tours preserves it, with six other bishops in the time of Pope Fabian — around 250 — to plant the church in the chief cities of the Roman north: Saturninus to Toulouse, Trophimus to Arles, Martial to Limoges, Gatian to Tours, Austremoine to Clermont, and Dionysius to Lutetia, the small island-city on the Seine that the Romans had begun to call Paris. He took with him a presbyter named Rusticus and a deacon named Eleutherius. They built the first chapel of the city on the right bank and began to baptize.

The persecution that caught them came perhaps thirty years later, under Valerian or under Maximian — the Bollandists conclude that the early 280s is most likely. The three were arrested together, brought before the prefect, scourged, racked, and finally beheaded on the high northern hill above the city — _Mons Mercurii,_ the Latin called it, _Montmartre,_ the hill of the martyrs, after them. The cephalophore legend that grew up around the death-scene — Denis bending down to pick up his own severed head and walking with it three Roman miles north to the place where he wanted to be buried, preaching as he went — first turns up in the ninth-century life by Hilduin of Saint-Denis, and the Bollandists handle it with patient skepticism. What they accept as older and more secure is the burial site itself: a little Christian cemetery in the Frankish villa of _Catulliacum,_ north of Paris, where the bodies of the three were laid quietly in a wooden coffin, and where the chapel that grew over them eventually became, four hundred years later under Dagobert and again under Suger, the great basilica of Saint-Denis, the mausoleum of the kings of France.

The older _Passio_ treats the three as the historical figures they were: a missionary bishop, his presbyter, and his deacon, who walked into a Roman city of strangers, established a chapel, baptized, were arrested, and died together on the same morning on the same hill. Their fellow-feeling was preserved in the burial: the three bodies in one trench, _unico tumulo_ — _in a single grave._

The kings of France would crown themselves in their basilica for a thousand years. The chapel on the right bank of the Seine became Notre-Dame. The mission three men had begun — bishop, presbyter, deacon, walking into a city that was not asking for them — outlived them by every measure they would have known how to count.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Unico tumulo."_ — _the older_ Passio, _on the burial of Denis, Rusticus, and Eleutherius_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "10", name: "Paulinus of York"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 10]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Paulinus of York]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[First archbishop of York, missionary to Northumbria, c. 565–644]

#v(0.5em)

He had been one of the second band of Roman missionaries sent into England in 601 — Augustine had asked Gregory the Great for reinforcements after the early successes of the Kentish mission, and Gregory had sent Mellitus, Justus, Paulinus, and Rufinianus along with the books and vestments and pallium that arrived at Canterbury that year. Paulinus stayed in Kent for twenty-four years as a working priest, learning the country and the politics, until in 625 the king of Northumbria — pagan Edwin, who had asked for the hand of the Christian princess Æthelburh of Kent — agreed as a condition of the marriage that his queen could bring her own bishop with her. Justus consecrated Paulinus on the twenty-first of July, 625, and put him on the road north with the wedding party.

Bede gives him the warmest portrait of any of the Italians. Paulinus was tall and slightly stooped — _gracilis statura, paululum incurvus,_ working from the recollections of an old priest named Deda who had seen him — with a dark hawk-like face, very black hair, an ascetic thinness, and a voice quiet enough that those farther back in the hall had to lean forward to hear him. He preached for two years through the cold spring courts of Northumbria without converting the king. The vote came at the great council in 627 in the king's hall at Goodmanham, after the long argument the king's chief priest Coifi famously closed with the speech about the sparrow flying through the lighted hall in winter — _brief is the warmth, soon is the wind on the other side._ The court accepted the new religion. Edwin was baptized at York that Easter.

Paulinus baptized for the next six years up and down the Northumbrian rivers — in the Glen at Yeavering, in the Swale at Catterick, in the Trent at Littleborough — and built the first stone cathedral at York around the wooden chapel where Edwin had received the sacrament. The Northumbrian conversion held until the autumn of 633, when Cadwallon of Gwynedd and Penda of Mercia together overthrew Edwin at Hatfield Chase. The king was killed; the queen and Paulinus and the children fled south by sea to Kent. He never returned. He served as bishop of Rochester for the rest of his life — eleven more years, quietly, in the Kentish coastal town — and died there on the tenth of October, 644, perhaps eighty years old.

The cathedral he had built at York burned, was rebuilt, burned, and rebuilt again. The men he had baptized in the rivers became the grandfathers of the Northumbrian renaissance Bede grew up inside.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Brief is the warmth; soon is the wind on the other side."_ — _the unnamed counselor's sparrow-speech in Bede,_ on the day Edwin's court accepted the gospel]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "11", name: "Bruno the Great"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 11]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Bruno the Great]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Archbishop of Cologne, brother of an emperor, scholar-prince, 925–965]

#v(0.5em)

He was the youngest son of Henry the Fowler, the Saxon duke who had been elected king of the East Franks in 919 and whose sons would build the empire of the Ottos. Otto I, who was to be crowned emperor at Rome in 962, was thirteen years older; Henry of Bavaria was nine years older; Bruno was the fourth child, the dynastic spare, and his parents — perhaps his mother Mathilda, who had her own Saxon piety — set him aside for the church when he was four years old. He went to the cathedral school at Utrecht at six. He stayed in school for the next twenty-five years.

What he became was the first great scholar-prince of the Ottonian renaissance. He read Greek — not just letters but the philosophers and the Cappadocians; he assembled the largest private library north of the Alps in his own day; he kept up a correspondence with the literary luminaries of the time. When Otto, having beaten the Hungarians at Lechfeld in 955, decided he needed a brother in the see of Cologne to anchor the Rhineland, Bruno was appointed archbishop in 953, at twenty-eight, and was simultaneously named duke of Lotharingia, the long border province from the North Sea down to the Vosges. He held both offices for twelve years and held them well.

He kept his cathedral school open seven days a week to anyone who could read; he reformed the Rhenish abbeys with the new Cluniac discipline coming up from Burgundy; he patronized the manuscript ateliers that produced the great Ottonian gospel-books of Reichenau and Echternach; he settled three civil wars in his own duchy without resort to arms. He wrote, in the long winter evenings, lives of his predecessors in the Cologne see, and at his death his secretary Ruotger — who had served him for eleven years — wrote his life back. The _Vita Brunonis_ by Ruotger is one of the great documents of the tenth century, gracious and unsparing.

He died at Reims on the way back from putting down a Lotharingian rebellion, on the eleventh of October, 965, at forty years old. The fourth son the family had given to the church returned to the family the order it had needed: a Latin prince who had been first a scholar, who held both crozier and sword for twelve years and never let either eclipse the other, and whose library was the quiet thing the empire had not known it required.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Sapientia ædificavit sibi domum."_ — _the verse Ruotger took for the epitaph: Wisdom has built herself a house_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "12", name: "Edwin of Northumbria"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 12]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Edwin of Northumbria]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[King and martyr, the convert Paulinus baptized, c. 586–633]

#v(0.5em)

He was the son of Ælle, king of Deira, the southern half of Northumbria, and grew up in exile after his father's defeat by Æthelfrith of Bernicia in the late 590s — first in the kingdom of Gwynedd among the British, then at the court of Rædwald of East Anglia, where the young exile spent his late twenties waiting for the chance to claim his inheritance. The chance came in 616 when Æthelfrith, alarmed by Rædwald's protection of his rival, marched south to demand Edwin be handed over for execution. Rædwald refused, met Æthelfrith at the river Idle in Nottinghamshire, killed him in battle, and put Edwin on the throne of a reunited Northumbria — Bernicia and Deira together, the largest English kingdom, stretching from the Humber to the Forth.

He governed for seventeen years and brought the kingdom to a peace it had not known in a generation. Bede preserves the famous detail: Edwin set up brass drinking-cups at the wayside springs along the Roman roads of Northumbria, _and no one,_ the historian writes, _would dare touch them but for the use intended,_ such was the order of the kingdom. In 625 he asked for the hand of Æthelburh, the Christian princess of Kent. Her brother sent her north on condition that she could keep her religion and bring her own bishop, and the bishop they sent with her was Paulinus.

It took Paulinus two years. The famous council of 627 — the great hall at Goodmanham, the speech of the unnamed counselor about the sparrow flying through the lighted hall in winter, the chief priest Coifi's own dramatic ride out to throw a spear into the pagan temple — closes the conversation. Edwin was baptized at York the next Easter, with his daughter Eanflæd and most of his court. The decade that followed was the high point of early-medieval English Christianity: a baptized king, a missionary bishop, six years of travelling the Northumbrian rivers baptizing crowds.

It ended at Hatfield Chase in October 633. Cadwallon of Gwynedd, the British king, allied with the pagan Penda of Mercia and marched against Northumbria. They met Edwin and his sons at the marsh-edge of Hatfield south of the Don. Edwin was killed in the line; his elder son Osfrid was killed beside him; the younger son was captured and murdered later by Penda. The body was decapitated, the head taken back as a trophy. A monk of Whitby — perhaps a man Paulinus had baptized — recovered the head some time later and brought it to York, where it was buried in the porch of the cathedral Edwin had begun. The relics survive nowhere now; the cult of the king who had been baptized and martyred for his baptism survived him by a thousand years.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"And no one dared touch them but for the use intended, such was the order of his kingdom."_ — Bede, on the brass drinking-cups Edwin set at the wayside springs]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "13", name: "Theophilus of Antioch"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 13]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Theophilus of Antioch]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Sixth bishop of Antioch, apologist of the second century, c. 115–c. 183]

#v(0.5em)

He was born in the country east of the Euphrates, in the small Syriac-speaking villages around Edessa, and came to faith as an adult — _I too,_ he writes near the end of the third book _Ad Autolycum,_ _was once an unbeliever, until I read the prophets and the gospels and was persuaded by the Holy Spirit._ He was elected sixth bishop of Antioch around 169, succeeding Eros — sixth, that is, in the line that began with Peter — and held the see for fourteen years through the long persecution of Marcus Aurelius and the brief reprieve under Commodus. The Latin tradition remembers him on October 13.

He wrote three books _Ad Autolycum_ — _To Autolycus,_ a pagan friend who had pressed him for a defense of Christianity against the standard charges of atheism, immorality, and historical novelty — and the books survive intact, perhaps the only complete second-century apology that does. They are slow, patient, often surprising. The first book defends the Christian doctrine of God by the via negativa: _if you say to me, show me your God, I will say to you, show me your man — show me the man behind your eyes, behind the speech that is spoken, behind the desires you cannot see — show him to me, and I will show you my God._ The second runs through the Old Testament chronology against the Greek myths and concludes with the line that gave Christian theology one of its most permanent words. _In a similar way the three days before the lights of heaven were created are figures of the Trinity — God, his Word, and his Wisdom._ It is the first time the word _triad_ — _trinitas_ — appears in surviving Christian writing.

He wrote also a treatise against Marcion, a refutation of Hermogenes, several catechetical works, and the earliest commentary on the gospels in Greek of which we have notice. None of those survive. What survives is the three books to a friend who had asked an honest question and got, slowly and with great courtesy, an honest answer.

He died at his post around 183, perhaps in his late sixties, and was buried at Antioch in the cathedral he had administered. He was the bishop who first set down what every catechumen since has been taught to confess: that the God who made the world is one God in three. The word came to him not from a council but from a friend's question, and from the patience to answer it three books long.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Show me the man behind your eyes, and I will show you my God."_ — Theophilus, _Ad Autolycum_ 1.2]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "14", name: "Callistus I"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 14]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Callistus I]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Pope, freed slave, contested architect of Roman discipline, c. 160–222]

#v(0.5em)

He had been born a slave in the household of a wealthy Christian named Carpophorus, sometime around 160, and entrusted in his early manhood with the management of a small Roman bank his master had set up to receive the deposits of poor Christians. The bank failed. The deposits were lost. Callistus, blamed and probably innocent, was caught fleeing on a ship to Portus, beaten, sentenced to the treadmill, and eventually shipped to the lead mines of Sardinia for what was meant to be the rest of his short life. The empress Marcia, the Christian concubine of Commodus, secured a general pardon for the Christian prisoners around 190; Callistus came home a marked man, settled on the small church-stipend Pope Victor offered him, and rose through the deaconate under Pope Zephyrinus, who eventually trusted him enough to make him the administrator of the great Christian cemetery on the Appian Way that has borne his name ever since. He was elected pope on Zephyrinus's death in 217.

His five-year pontificate became the most controversial of any third-century Roman bishop. Hippolytus, the most learned theologian of the Roman church, refused to recognize Callistus's election and set himself up as anti-pope — the first in the history of the Roman see — and his _Refutation of All Heresies_ attacks Callistus by name as a man who was readmitting penitent adulterers to communion, ordaining men who had been married more than once, and tolerating clandestine marriages between Roman matrons and Christian slaves whom they could not lawfully marry under Roman law. Callistus replied that the church was not a club of the perfect but a hospital, and that the keys had been given to bind and to loose. He stood by the policy. The third-century Latin church, slowly and against Hippolytus's bitter opposition, ratified him.

He died, the _acta_ preserve, in a popular riot rather than an imperial persecution — beaten in his own house in Trastevere by a mob who had been told the Christians were responsible for a recent flood of the Tiber, thrown out a high window with a stone tied around his neck, dragged into a well, and covered over with rubble. The presbyter Asterius recovered the body that night and buried him in the cemetery on the Aurelian Way, on the fourteenth of October, 222. Santa Maria in Trastevere — built where his house had stood — keeps his name on the dedication still.

The slave who had administered the bank for poor Christians governed the Roman church for five years and was killed by a Roman mob, and the policy he insisted on — that mercy is not weakness — has not been revoked.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Misericordia exaltat se super judicium."_ — Callistus, in Hippolytus's hostile paraphrase: _mercy exalts itself over judgment_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "15", name: "Teresa of Ávila"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 15]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Teresa of Ávila]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Discalced Carmelite reformer, Doctor of the Church, 1515–1582]

#v(0.5em)

She was born at Ávila to a family of _converso_ descent — her grandfather had been reconciled to the Inquisition in 1485 — and grew up the most charming of nine children in a strict Castilian merchant household. Her mother died when she was twelve. She read romances of chivalry through her teens — _I have come,_ she would later tell her sisters, _from many vanities_ — and ran away to a Carmelite convent at twenty, partly out of vocation and partly to escape her father's plans for her marriage. The convent at the Encarnación held a hundred and eighty nuns under a relaxed observance; she stayed there for twenty-six years; she was bored for most of them. Her conversion came at thirty-nine, in front of an _Ecce Homo_ in a chapel corner — _the very God of my heart,_ she writes in the _Vida._ The mystical experiences began in her early forties.

She was forty-seven when she founded the first reformed Carmelite convent — San José of Ávila, four nuns in absolute enclosure under the original Carmelite rule — against the resistance of the diocese, the city council, and her own provincial. She would found sixteen more in the next twenty years. The reform was hard and meant to be hard: discalced, strict poverty, perpetual fasting, no large dowries, no relaxations. She walked or rode mule-back across Castile, La Mancha, Andalusia, Catalonia, and Aragon to plant houses in town after town, in summer heat and winter snow, sometimes sleeping in stables, often arriving at midnight to a building her advance party had not yet been able to make habitable. _Lord,_ she said once on the road in the rain, _if this is how you treat your friends, no wonder you have so few._ She wrote, in the same years, the _Vida,_ the _Way of Perfection,_ the _Foundations,_ and the _Interior Castle_ — four of the great works of Spanish prose.

The _Interior Castle_ — a vision of the soul as a great crystal globe with seven mansions inside it, the king dwelling in the innermost — is the work the church has loved most. She died on the road at Alba de Tormes on the fourteenth of October, 1582 (the calendar reform took effect at midnight; her death-day is dated the fifteenth in the new calendar), at sixty-seven, from exhaustion. _I am a daughter of the church,_ she said when she was dying. The seventeen houses she had founded were five hundred within a century. Paul VI made her, in 1970, the first woman Doctor of the Church in fifteen hundred years.

A nun bored for twenty-six years, converted at thirty-nine in front of a painting, founded an order, wrote four masterpieces, and died on a road. The patience that came before was the soil the rest grew from.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"I am a daughter of the church."_ — Teresa, repeated several times in her last hours]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "16", name: "Gall of St. Gallen"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 16]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Gall of St. Gallen]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Irish hermit-monk, apostle of the Allemanni, c. 550–c. 645]

#v(0.5em)

He was the youngest of the twelve Irish monks who left Bangor in Ulster around the year 590 with their abbot Columbanus to evangelize the continent. They went first to Brittany, then up the Rhone to Burgundy, then over the Alps to Lake Constance — a journey of thirteen years, through three kingdoms and four languages, planting monasteries as they went. By 612 they had reached Bregenz on the eastern shore of the lake, where Columbanus's abrasive insistence on the Irish style of penance had finally put him beyond the patience of the Frankish-Burgundian queen Brunhilde. The little community broke up. Columbanus crossed the Alps south into Lombardy and founded Bobbio. Gall, who had fallen ill with a fever and could not travel, was left behind with two Allemanic disciples and his abbot's parting rebuke — _as long as I live, you shall not say Mass_ — for his refusal to follow.

He recovered slowly through the winter of 612 in a small wooden cell near Arbon. In the spring he walked east into the heavily-forested upper Steinach valley with the deacon Hiltibod, found a clear stream and a high spring above a cliff, set the foundation-stones of a chapel, and stayed for the rest of his life. The legend preserves the small details: the bear who tried to take his fire and whom he rebuked into bringing him firewood instead and who was venerated for centuries afterward in the abbey arms; the local Allemanic chieftain who gave him the surrounding woodlands when he saw the cell; the Frankish bishop of Constance who tried to make him take the see and whom he refused with a smile. He kept his abbatial silence after Columbanus's death in 615, when a Bobbio messenger arrived with the abbot's pastoral staff and the news that the rebuke had been lifted. He said his first Mass at sixty-five.

He died perhaps in 645, an old man among his Allemanic disciples, and was buried in the wooden chapel he had built. A century later the abbey of Saint Gall would rise on the spot — one of the great monastic foundations of Carolingian Europe, the model house of the Benedictine reform north of the Alps, the producer of the _Plan of St. Gall,_ of the _Antiphonary,_ of historians and hymn-writers, of a school whose graduates staffed the cathedrals of Cologne and Mainz and Liège for three hundred years. The valley that had been an empty forest when Gall set down his stones has been a center of Christian Europe for a thousand four hundred years.

The disobedient disciple who would not cross the Alps, who stayed where he was sick and built a hut, planted the most fruitful Irish foundation on the continent.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Maneas in Christo, frater, ego in his terris peregrinabor."_ — Gall to Columbanus, on parting at Bregenz: _Stay you in Christ, brother; I will pilgrim in these lands_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "17", name: "John Colobus"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 17]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[John Colobus]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[John the Dwarf, abba of Scetis, c. 339–c. 409]

#v(0.5em)

He was a peasant boy from the Egyptian Thebaid, four feet tall by the most generous estimate — _colobos_ in the Greek, _parvus_ in the Latin, the _dwarf,_ the _little one,_ a name that stuck for the rest of his life and a thousand years afterwards. He went out into the desert of Scetis as a teenager, around 357, and put himself under the discipline of the great old Pambo, who took him as a novice in the same little circle that included Macarius the Egyptian and Moses the Ethiopian — the founders of the form of monasticism that would shape the desert tradition for the next sixteen centuries.

The story is the famous one — repeated in the _Apophthegmata,_ repeated in Cassian, repeated by every spiritual master from Benedict to Dorotheus of Gaza. Pambo took the new novice out into the dry valley below the cell, planted a dead walnut staff in the sand, and said _water this every day until it fruits._ The nearest well was three miles away. John walked with the bucket every morning before the office and every evening before sleep. He kept walking for three years. The branch budded, leafed, and fruited. The old men of Scetis, when they heard, came out and ate the walnuts and called the tree _the tree of obedience._

He stayed in Scetis for fifty years. He was famous in the desert for the smallness of his body and for the precision of his answers in the _Apophthegmata patrum._ _Abba,_ a young brother once asked him, _what should I do with this temptation?_ John told him: _sit in your cell, and your cell will teach you everything._ Another brother: _what is the most necessary virtue?_ He answered: _humility, because where humility is, there is no temptation that can stand against it._ Another: _I have many evil thoughts._ John told him: _the wind blows where it will; you cannot stop it; but you can keep your sails reefed._ When the desert-raiding Mazikes broke up the Scetis communities in 408, John fled to Suez with the surviving brothers and died there, perhaps in his seventy-first year, longing to return to the cell he had not been allowed to keep.

He had founded no monastery, written no book, governed no community larger than the brothers in his immediate cell. The dead branch he had watered for three years outlived him by twelve hundred years.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Sit in your cell, and your cell will teach you everything."_ — John Colobus, in the _Apophthegmata patrum_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "18", name: "Luke the Evangelist"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 18]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Luke the Evangelist]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Physician, gospel-writer, companion of Paul, fl. c. 50–80]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Greek of Antioch — born at the great cosmopolitan city on the Orontes that had become, by his lifetime, the third city of the Roman Empire and the cradle of Gentile Christianity. He was a physician, trained at the medical schools that had grown up around the temple of Asclepius in his hometown. He was probably a freed slave, perhaps the household physician of one of the Antiochene Christian patrons whose names Acts preserves — perhaps even of the household of Theophilus to whom he eventually addressed both his books. The Christian community in Antioch met him as an adult convert sometime in the early fifties of the first century, and the apostle Paul met him there shortly afterward.

He travelled with Paul. The famous "we-passages" of Acts — _we sailed away from Philippi after the days of unleavened bread, and came unto them to Troas in five days_ — are the moments where Luke, who has been writing in the third person about the apostle's journeys, slips back into the first plural and signals that he had been there himself. He was on the second missionary journey from Troas to Philippi; he stayed at Philippi for the next seven years; he rejoined Paul on the third journey and travelled with him to Jerusalem; he went with him on the long sea-voyage to Rome that fills the last chapters of Acts; he stayed at Rome through the apostle's first imprisonment and was apparently with him for the second one too. _Luke alone is with me,_ Paul writes from his death-cell in 2 Timothy 4:11. The two had been together, by then, for seventeen or eighteen years.

He wrote his gospel and the Acts of the Apostles in those years — both of them dedicated to a Theophilus who may be patron, fellow-physician, or simply an honored Greek reader. The gospel is the most literary of the four and the most attentive to women, to the poor, to the Samaritans, to the _anawim_ of late-Second-Temple piety; it preserves the _Magnificat,_ the _Benedictus,_ the _Nunc Dimittis,_ the prodigal son, the good Samaritan, the Emmaus road. The Acts that follows is the only narrative source we have for the first thirty years of the church, and the apostolic missions outside Judea would be largely opaque to us without him.

After Paul's death he travelled, the older traditions preserve, in Greece, in Achaia, in Bithynia, where he died at seventy-three years old, _plenus Spiritu Sancto,_ full of the Holy Spirit. His body was eventually translated to Padua, where it has lain in the basilica of Santa Giustina (next to the city's other patron, eleven days back in this calendar) for fifteen hundred years. The physician who stayed when others left wrote the two books on which the church's memory of its own beginning depends.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Luke alone is with me."_ — Paul to Timothy, 2 _Timothy_ 4:11]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "19", name: "Peter of Alcántara"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 19]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Peter of Alcántara]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Discalced Franciscan reformer, mentor of Teresa of Ávila, 1499–1562]

#v(0.5em)

He was born at Alcántara on the Tajo, the small Extremaduran town whose Roman bridge still spans the river, the son of a noble family in straitened circumstances — the father was a lawyer, the mother had brought a small dowry, the boy was educated by the Augustinians at Salamanca and entered the Observant Franciscans at sixteen. He was already known in the convent for the fact that he ate one meal every three days, slept ninety minutes a night, and never lifted his eyes when he walked — _I never knew the color of the wall of my cell,_ he would say in old age, _for I never raised my head._ He was ordained priest at twenty-five. He preached for twenty-five years through the Extremadura and Andalusia, gathering a small group of followers around him who wanted to live the original Franciscan rule before the relaxations had set in.

In 1554 he founded the first house of his reform at Pedroso, in the Sierra Morena, with seven friars: barefoot, eating only what was begged door to door, sleeping on bare boards in cells four feet by six, keeping nightly vigil. The houses spread; by 1561 there were sixteen of them; the _Alcantarine_ reform of the Franciscans became the most rigorous branch of the order in the Spanish-speaking world for the next four hundred years.

His most consequential single work, however, was a friendship. In 1559 the Carmelite nun Teresa of Cepeda y Ahumada, then in her early forties at the convent of the Encarnación at Ávila, was being investigated by the Inquisition for the unsettling visions she had begun to have and for the equally unsettling proposal she was making to break away from her own community and found a stricter house of Carmel. She was alone, terrified, and on the edge of being silenced. Peter of Alcántara, then sixty and the most respected ascetic in Spain, came to Ávila at the request of Bishop Mendoza to interview her. He spent three days with her in the convent parlor. He came out persuaded that the visions were of God, the proposal was of the Holy Spirit, and the Inquisition needed to leave her alone. _He understood me by experience,_ Teresa would write in the _Vida,_ _which is the only thing here below that altogether suffices._ He stayed her champion for the next three years until his death.

He died at the friary of Arenas de San Pedro on the eighteenth of October, 1562, kneeling in his cell, standing and praying — though he was sixty-three he looked, his brothers said, like a man of ninety. Teresa had a vision of him at the moment of his death three hundred miles away in Ávila and recorded it. He had told her once, near the end, _daughter, all of this and the world besides will pass; only love endures._ The reform he had begun outlasted him by four hundred years; the friend he had defended outlasted him in another way altogether.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Daughter, all of this and the world besides will pass; only love endures."_ — Peter of Alcántara, to Teresa, in the parlor at Ávila]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "20", name: "Acca of Hexham"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 20]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Acca of Hexham]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Northumbrian bishop, scholar of Bede's circle, c. 660–740]

#v(0.5em)

He had been raised in the household of Bishop Bosa of York, taken into the cathedral school as a child, brought along when Wilfrid — the great, contentious bishop of York whose long career filled the second half of the seventh century in Northumbria — moved his episcopal household first to Ripon, then back to York, then to Hexham on the south Tyne. Acca was Wilfrid's secretary, then his deacon, then his chief presbyter, then in 709 his successor as bishop of Hexham. He held the see for twenty-four years.

What he gave the Northumbrian church in those years, by Bede's grateful accounting, was a school and a library. The cathedral library at Hexham under Acca was the best in northern England — the great Roman manuscripts Wilfrid had brought back from his pilgrimage journeys, the Greek codices Theodore of Tarsus had passed to him through Canterbury, the patristic and historical works Acca had himself copied or imported from the continental abbeys. Bede, who lived twelve miles down the road at Jarrow, regarded Acca as a personal friend and dedicated five of his works to him: the commentaries on Mark, on Luke, on the _Hexaemeron,_ on the _Song of Songs,_ on the _Acts of the Apostles._ The dedicatory letters survive. They are warm, technical, full of the precise gratitude one scholar owes another who has lent him books. _Te animarum medicum nominari mihi licet,_ Bede writes in the prologue to _In Marcum_ — _I may be permitted to call you the physician of souls._

Acca was also the last bishop of Hexham to govern it as a free see. In 732 the Northumbrian succession-fight that had brought King Ceolwulf to the throne caught up with him; he was deposed, perhaps for political reasons. He spent the last eight years of his life in some kind of internal exile — Bede says only that he left Hexham. He died on the twentieth of October, 740, and was brought back home for burial. The cathedral he had loved kept his bones in two carved stone crosses set up at the head and foot of his grave outside the south wall — the _crosses of Acca,_ the antiquarians called them; one survives in the Hexham museum, half its eight feet of carved interlace and acanthus still legible after thirteen centuries.

The library he assembled was scattered when the Vikings burned Hexham in 875, and his books were lost; the cathedral he had governed survived the burning and was rebuilt. The man who lent Bede the books has not had to lend the church any more — what Bede made of them is what survives.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Te animarum medicum nominari mihi licet."_ — Bede to Acca, prologue _In Marcum:_ _I may call you the physician of souls_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "21", name: "Hilarion of Gaza"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 21]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Hilarion of Gaza]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Father of Palestinian monasticism, the Western pupil of Anthony, c. 291–371]

#v(0.5em)

He was born to pagan parents in the village of Thabatha, five miles south of Gaza in the Roman province of Palestine, and was sent as a teenager to the Christian schools of Alexandria. He was fifteen when he made his way out into the desert beyond Memphis to find Anthony — already, by 306, the most famous monk in the world — and stayed with him for two months. He came home to find his parents dead. He sold the small inheritance, distributed it to the poor and to his married brother, and walked seven miles down to the wilderness behind Maiuma, the port of Gaza, on the Mediterranean shore. He was sixteen. He stayed in that wilderness, in increasing isolation, for the next sixty-five years.

The _Vita Hilarionis_ by Jerome — written around 390, less than twenty years after Hilarion's death, drawn directly from the recollections of his companion Hesychius — is one of the most loved of all the early monastic biographies. It is also one of the funniest. Jerome paints a young man who lived for thirty years on twelve dried figs a day, who slept on a reed mat in a hut made of mud and straw four feet high, who broke a vase of incense over a band of bandits who had come to steal his hut and persuaded them to repent and become monks themselves, who refused every visitor who came for healing and healed them anyway, who once raced a chariot in the games at Gaza on the prayer of a Christian charioteer and beat the pagan champion with the _signum crucis_ drawn over the wheels.

He was driven out of Palestine in his sixties by the crowds — he could not get any solitude — and spent the last fifteen years of his life on the run from his own fame. He went to Egypt; he was found out. He went to Sicily; he was found out. He went to Dalmatia; the demoniacs queued at the harbor with their relatives. He went finally to Cyprus. The brothers at Paphos hid him in a small cave above a stream three days' ride into the mountains. He stayed for five years, surrounded only by his old companion Hesychius, and died there at eighty years old, on the twenty-first of October, 371.

The boy who had walked seven miles down the Mediterranean coast at sixteen had founded, by living a particular sort of small life there, the entire Palestinian and Syrian monastic tradition. Sabas, Euthymius, Theodosius — the great fathers of the next century — all looked back to Hilarion as their _abba._ The fame he ran from for fifteen years has been, for sixteen hundred years, the only thing about him most readers know.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"O Anima mea! Egredere; quid times? Egredere, quid dubitas?"_ — Hilarion's death-prayer in Jerome: _Out, my soul; why do you fear? Out, why do you hesitate?_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "22", name: "Abercius of Hierapolis"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 22]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Abercius of Hierapolis]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, traveller, author of his own epitaph, c. 120–c. 200]

#v(0.5em)

He was bishop of Hierapolis in Phrygia in the second half of the second century — successor of Papias, who had been a disciple of John the Apostle, and writing before the persecution of Septimius Severus had begun. The acts the Bollandists print are full of the standard hagiographic apparatus — the demoniacs cured, the well that sprang up at his prayer, the trip to Rome to exorcise the daughter of Marcus Aurelius — and the editors handle the legendary material with patience. What survives the editorial knife, what no one disputes, is the epitaph he wrote for himself.

The _Inscription of Abercius_ — first quoted in the late-fourth-century Greek life, then partly recovered in stone in 1883 by the Scottish archaeologist William Ramsay in the wall of a public bath at Hieropolis — is the oldest substantial Christian inscription that survives. It is twenty-two hexameter lines, in the _koine_ Greek of the eastern Roman world. It was carved on the marble sarcophagus Abercius prepared for himself in his late seventies, on the eve of one of his pastoral journeys, and the slab now hangs in the Vatican Museum.

_A citizen of an elected city,_ the epitaph begins, _I made this in my lifetime that there might be in plain view a place to receive my body. My name is Abercius, the disciple of a holy shepherd who feeds his flocks on the mountains and on the plains, who has great eyes that look down everywhere, who taught me the trustworthy writings._ The shepherd is Christ. The writings are the gospels. _He sent me to royal Rome to behold a queen with golden robe and golden sandals; and a people I saw there having a shining seal. I saw the plain of Syria and all the cities — Nisibis after I crossed the Euphrates. Everywhere I had brothers. Paul I had with me; faith led me everywhere; everywhere food was placed before me — the fish from the spring, mighty and pure, which the chaste virgin caught and gave to her friends to eat continually, having sweet wine and giving the cup with the bread._

The fish from the spring is Christ. The chaste virgin is the Church. The cup with the bread is the Eucharist. The epitaph is, in a single block of second-century marble, a complete profession of Christian faith — Trinitarian, sacramental, ecclesial, eucharistic — set into the gravestone of a Phrygian bishop a hundred years before the persecutions ended and three hundred years before any of the great creeds were written. He died around the year 200, at perhaps seventy-five. The marble slab he had cut survived. What he had wanted plainly visible — _in plain view,_ the epitaph says — has been visible ever since.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"My name is Abercius, the disciple of a holy shepherd who feeds his flocks on the mountains and on the plains."_ — _Inscription of Abercius,_ lines 3–4]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "23", name: "Severinus of Cologne"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 23]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Severinus of Cologne]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Gallo-Roman bishop on the Rhenish frontier, c. 340–c. 403]

#v(0.5em)

He was born in Aquitaine and sent north as a young presbyter to the Rhine frontier in the 360s, when Valentinian I was rebuilding the limes and the Roman administration of the Rhineland was still standing, just barely. He succeeded Euphrates as bishop of Cologne sometime around 370, perhaps in his early thirties, and held the see for the next thirty-three years.

What he inherited was a frontier church under siege. The Cologne diocese ran from the small Roman colony at _Colonia Agrippina_ north along the lower Rhine to the Frisian marshes, and east into the German forests where the Franks and the Allemanni were beginning the slow westward pressure that would, within his lifetime, push across the Rhine for good. The Christian community Severinus inherited had been founded perhaps two generations earlier, was perhaps two thousand strong in a city of forty thousand, and was hemmed in by an Arian Gothic minority on one side and a still-pagan Frankish military presence on the other. He governed it for thirty years with a steady, unspectacular, practical capacity. He built the basilica of SS. Cornelius and Cyprian on the south side of the city. He resettled the small church on the north side that the Frankish raids of 388 had burned. He buried, when no other bishop would come, the Christian dead of the great Frankish raid of 392. He kept the cathedral school open through years when the supply of papyrus from the south was unreliable.

The _Vita_ preserves the small detail Gregory of Tours preserves first: that Severinus was at the office in his cathedral one Sunday morning in November of 397 when, at the moment of the _agnus Dei,_ he stopped, looked up, and said audibly to his archdeacon, _Listen — do you hear the angels singing? My brother Martin of Tours is dead._ The archdeacon, who wrote the report down for the cathedral chronicle, marked the day. The news that Martin had died at Candes that morning reached Cologne by post-courier two weeks later. The dating matched.

He died perhaps in 403 and was buried in the basilica he had built. The basilica that grew out of the cult of his tomb, _Sankt Severin_ in the south of the old city, is still there, a fourteenth-century Gothic church on Roman foundations on Carolingian foundations on the original tomb-shrine. The Cologne diocese he had governed survived the Roman collapse, survived Charlemagne, survived the Reformation, and is one of the oldest continuously functioning dioceses in northern Europe. The Aquitanian who had been sent up to a frontier see for thirty years had, simply, kept it.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Audi: angeli canunt. Frater meus Martinus migravit ad Dominum."_ — Severinus, in the cathedral at Cologne, on the morning of Martin's death]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "24", name: "Felix of Thibiuca"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 24]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Felix of Thibiuca]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[North African bishop, refused to surrender the books, d. 303]

#v(0.5em)

The first edict of Diocletian, posted at Nicomedia in February of 303, reached Roman North Africa in the early summer. It commanded the Christian clergy to surrender their _libri sacri_ — the codices of the gospels and the epistles, the working bishopric's most valuable physical possession — for public burning. Those who complied became known, in the bitter Latin that followed, as the _traditores,_ the _handers-over._ Those who refused became the proto-martyrs of the persecution. Felix, bishop of the small _municipium_ of Thibiuca in Africa Proconsularis — about thirty-five Roman miles southwest of Carthage — was the first North African bishop publicly to refuse.

The trial survives in detail. Felix was sixty-six years old, bishop for some years; the local _curator,_ Magnelianus, was an old neighbor, and the conversation between them in the city forum was constrained by familiarity as much as by procedure. _I have the books,_ Felix said. _Bring them out and burn them,_ Magnelianus answered. _I will not._ _Then surrender them to me._ _I will not._ _Why?_ _Because it is better that I should be burned than they._

He was sent in chains to Carthage, then held a fortnight while the proconsul Anulinus tried persuasion, then put on a ship to Italy when the proconsul declined to execute him in his own province. The execution was carried out at Venusia in southern Apulia — the small inland town where Horace had been born three centuries earlier — by the local magistrate on instructions from Rome. Felix was beheaded on the thirtieth of August, 303. The day his community at Thibiuca received the news from Italy is what the African calendar afterwards observed.

Five of his deacons and lectors died with him: Audactus, Januarius, Fortunatianus, Septiminus, and a sixth whose name varies in the manuscripts. They too had refused. _Better that I should be burned than they_ became one of the small permanent sayings of the African church. Within a generation the _traditores_ who had handed over their codices for the fire would become the central case of the Donatist controversy that tore the African church apart — and the men of Thibiuca who had not handed them over would be the heroes both sides could agree on.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Better that I should be burned than they."_ — Felix, in the forum at Thibiuca]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "25", name: "Gaudentius of Brescia"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 25]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Gaudentius of Brescia]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop, advocate of John Chrysostom, c. 360–c. 410]

#v(0.5em)

He was a presbyter at Brescia in northern Italy under Bishop Philastrius, and grew up in his teacher's household, learning the patristic tradition and the spoken Latin of fourth-century Cisalpine Gaul. In the late 380s he set out on a long pilgrimage to the East. He went to Cappadocia, where the biographer of Basil of Caesarea gave him a portion of the relics of the Forty Martyrs of Sebaste; he went down to the Holy Land; he spent time at the new Latin monastic foundations beginning to grow in Bethlehem and Jerusalem. While he was on the road Philastrius died, and the Brescian church — without consulting him, against his repeated protests by letter — elected him bishop in absence. He returned, the eastern bishops having refused him communion until he agreed to come home, and was ordained by Ambrose of Milan in 388.

He governed the see for twenty years. The twenty-one sermons that survive — preached at Brescia, recorded by his hearers — are among the great pieces of late-fourth-century Italian preaching. He preached on the eucharist, on the paschal vigil, on the difficult sayings of the Lord, on the saints whose relics he had brought back from the East. He built a basilica on the south side of Brescia to house those relics, the _Concilium Sanctorum,_ the first major reliquary church of northern Italy.

His most consequential act was a journey he could not refuse. In the spring of 405 the western bishops, scandalized by the deposition of John Chrysostom from Constantinople, sent a high-level legation to the emperor Arcadius to demand his restoration. Innocent I of Rome chose Gaudentius to lead it. He went, with two Roman senators, on a winter voyage from Brindisi to Athens to Constantinople. The embassy was a disaster. Arcadius would not receive them. They were detained three months, refused audiences, threatened, finally put on a leaky boat and sent home through a storm that nearly drowned them. Chrysostom, exiled in the Caucasus, was never restored. He died there two years later. Four letters survive in Greek that he wrote from the road of his exile, thanking the Brescian bishop for what he had attempted and could not complete.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Quos non potui de manu hostium liberare, debui certe attentasse."_ — Gaudentius, on the failed legation: _those I could not free from the hand of the enemy, I was at least bound to have attempted to_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "26", name: "Cedd of Lastingham"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 26]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Cedd of Lastingham]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Northumbrian missionary bishop, brother of Chad, d. 664]

#v(0.5em)

He was the eldest of four brothers raised at the monastic school on Lindisfarne under Aidan in the 640s — Cedd, Cynebill, Caelin, Chad — all four of them eventually ordained, all four of them missionary priests, all four in the front line of the Bernician evangelization of the next generation. Bede, who had grown up in the monastery Cedd's brother Chad would later govern at Lastingham, gives the family the warm portrait of book three of the _Historia Ecclesiastica._

He was sent south in 653 at the request of King Peada of the Middle Angles, who had married a Northumbrian Christian princess and wanted missionaries for his country. He worked the South Yorkshire and Derbyshire moorlands for two years; Penda's death at Winwaed in 655 ended the period of pagan Mercian dominance and opened the south for the gospel; Cedd was reassigned to the East Saxons in 656 at the request of King Sigeberht the Good. He spent the next eight years founding the church of Essex from scratch.

He worked from a small wooden chapel built into the ruined Roman fortress of _Othona_ on the Blackwater estuary — the _Saxon shore_ fort the Romans had built in the third century to guard against North Sea raiders, abandoned for two hundred years, the long sea-walls still standing. The chapel he raised on the foundations of the fortress chapel — the chapel of _Saint-Peter-on-the-Wall,_ perhaps the oldest still-standing church in England — is still there, the Roman tile and stone of its walls visible in the seventh-century masonry. From Bradwell-on-Sea he ordained priests, built churches up the rivers, organized a small Essex diocese, and went home each summer to Lindisfarne for the long northern monastic chapter.

In 664, returning from the Synod of Whitby — at which he had been one of the conciliators between the Roman and the Celtic Easter parties — he caught the plague that was sweeping the country and died at his own foundation at Lastingham on the North York Moors. His brother Chad came down to bury him and then stayed on as the new abbot. Bede preserves the small detail that Cedd had founded Lastingham on what had been a pagan burial ground; the brother had cleansed it with forty days of prayer and fasting before laying the foundation. The cleansing held. The abbey lasted four centuries. The chapel at Bradwell-on-Sea, looking out over the cold North Sea where it has stood for thirteen hundred years, has not had to be rebuilt.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Locus iste sanctus est, in nomine Domini factus."_ — _the Lastingham foundation prayer Bede preserves of Cedd_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "27", name: "Frumentius of Ethiopia"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 27]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Frumentius of Ethiopia]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Apostle of the Aksumites, first bishop of Ethiopia, c. 305–c. 380]

#v(0.5em)

He was a young Christian Tyrian boy travelling on a Red Sea trading-ship with his uncle Meropius, an Alexandrian philosopher hired to map the unsurveyed coastlines of the African and South Arabian shores for the imperial geographers. The boy was perhaps twelve years old; his older brother Edesius, perhaps fourteen, was also on board. Sometime around 316 the ship put in for water at a small port on the African coast of the Red Sea. The local tribe, hearing that the Romans had broken their treaty with the Aksumite kingdom, attacked the ship. The crew was killed. The two boys, found sitting under a tree on the shore reading their books, were spared and brought up to the king at Aksum.

The king of Aksum was Ella Amida. He took to the boys. He kept Edesius as his cup-bearer and Frumentius as his secretary and treasurer. They lived in the palace for the next twenty years, learning Ge'ez, the court administration, the trade routes south to the Nile and east to Yemen. When Ella Amida died around 330, leaving an infant son under the regency of the queen mother Sophia, the queen begged the two foreigners to stay on until the boy Ezana came of age. They agreed. For the next decade Frumentius effectively ran the kingdom.

He used the office to plant the gospel. The Roman traders coming through Aksum had been Christian for two generations; Frumentius gave them land for chapels, exempted them from taxes for liturgical purposes, encouraged them to gather Sunday assemblies in the towns where they did business. By the time Ezana came of age around 340 there was a substantial Christian community in Aksum and along the coast at Adulis. The two brothers asked permission to go home. Edesius went back to Tyre; Frumentius went down to Alexandria.

He went to see the new patriarch — Athanasius, just elected at thirty-three — and laid the situation in front of him. He did not ask for himself; he asked for a bishop. Athanasius listened and answered, in the line Rufinus preserves: _And what other man shall we find as fit, in whom is the Spirit of God as in you, who can finish what you have begun?_ He consecrated Frumentius bishop of Aksum on the spot. Frumentius spent the next forty years building the Ethiopian church, baptizing King Ezana in the late 340s, training the first generation of Ethiopian-born clergy, translating the gospels into Ge'ez. Ezana set the cross on his coinage. The Ethiopian Orthodox Church, the second-oldest national Christian community after Armenia, traces its episcopate to him. They call him _Abuna Salama,_ the Father of Peace.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Et quem alium inveniemus virum talem, in quo sit Spiritus Dei sicut in te?"_ — Athanasius to Frumentius, in council, c. 340]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "28", name: "Simon and Jude"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 28]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Simon and Jude]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Apostles, the Zealot and the brother of James, fl. c. 30–c. 70]

#v(0.5em)

The synoptic gospels list them together at the end of the Twelve, the last two before Judas Iscariot. The Simon of October 28 is _Simon the Cananaean,_ called _the Zealot_ in Luke and Acts — the surname probably reflecting an earlier political affiliation with the militant Galilean nationalist movement that would, three decades after the Lord's death, lead the great Jewish revolt against Rome. The Jude is _Judas of James_ in Luke's list, _Thaddaeus_ in Mark and Matthew — a younger brother of the apostle James the Less, perhaps a cousin of the Lord through the family of Mary of Clopas, perhaps the author of the short canonical letter that bears his name.

The gospels say almost nothing of either of them apart from the name in the list. John preserves one line of Jude in the long discourse at the Last Supper: _Lord, how is it that thou wilt manifest thyself unto us, and not unto the world?_ — a question that draws from the Lord one of the most searching answers of the upper room. _If a man love me, he will keep my words: and my Father will love him, and we will come unto him, and make our abode with him._ Of Simon the Cananaean John records nothing. They are, in the gospel narrative, members of a quiet majority — apostles who walked with the Lord for three years, ate with him at the supper, ran from the garden, gathered in the upper room, received the Spirit at Pentecost, scattered into the world to preach.

The early traditions have them work first separately and then together — Simon in Egypt and the Maghreb, Jude in Edessa and Mesopotamia, both of them eventually in Persia, where they joined forces and converted the eastern Persian _magi_ before being killed together at the city of Suanir, perhaps in the early 70s. Jude was clubbed; Simon was sawn in two. The legend gives them their iconographic attributes — the saw and the club, on every Western altarpiece since the Middle Ages — but their joint feast on the twenty-eighth of October goes back to the seventh century in Rome and the fourth in the East, and rests on the older and more durable tradition that they died together for the gospel they had together believed.

The two apostles whose names are barely on the page held to that gospel through a generation longer than most. The Lord's quiet promise to Jude in the upper room — _we will come unto him and make our abode with him_ — was the promise he is now remembered to have lived inside.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Lord, how is it that thou wilt manifest thyself unto us, and not unto the world?"_ — Jude, in the upper room, _John_ 14:22]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "29", name: "Narcissus of Jerusalem"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 29]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Narcissus of Jerusalem]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop of the Holy City, accused and vindicated, c. 99–c. 215]

#v(0.5em)

He was elected the thirtieth bishop of Jerusalem around the year 180 — twenty-sixth in the line, by Eusebius's count, after the twelve original Hebrew Christian bishops who had governed the church before the Bar Kokhba revolt of 135 destroyed the original community. The see he inherited had been quietly rebuilt in the long second century by the small Greek-speaking community that had moved back into Aelia Capitolina, the new Roman city Hadrian had built on the ruins of Jerusalem. Narcissus governed it through the Quartodeciman controversy and signed the decisive Palestinian council under Theophilus of Caesarea around 195 that aligned the eastern churches on the Sunday-after-Passover reckoning Rome had insisted on.

Eusebius preserves the most famous story about him. One Easter eve the deacons discovered, late on Holy Saturday, that there was no oil for the lamps for the great paschal vigil — the supply had run out, the markets were closed, the congregation was already gathering. They came to Narcissus in distress. He told them to fill the lamps with water from the cathedral well; he prayed over it, blessed it, and instructed them to light the lamps. The water burned as oil. The vigil proceeded. Eusebius adds that several of the brothers preserved samples of the water-turned-oil for many years afterwards.

The other famous story is darker. Three local enemies of Narcissus concocted a charge against him so grave and obscene that the _Historia Ecclesiastica_ declines to specify it. They each swore to it under the oldest form of imprecatory oath: the first that he might be burned alive if he were lying, the second that he might be eaten with the wasting disease, the third that he might lose his eyes. The Jerusalem church, knowing Narcissus's life, did not believe them. Narcissus, however, weary of the controversy and longing for the contemplative life he had wanted as a young man, slipped away into the desert and was not seen for some years. The community, eventually concluding he had died, elected three successive bishops in his absence. Then Narcissus reappeared. The three accusers had, in the meantime, all suffered the curses they had sworn. The first had been burned to death in a house-fire; the second had wasted away; the third, seeing the fates of the first two, had publicly confessed the perjury and wept himself blind.

Narcissus resumed the see, governed it some years more, and finally — by then, Eusebius says, in his middle hundreds — accepted Alexander of Cappadocian Caesarea as his coadjutor. He died perhaps in his hundred-and-seventeenth year, certainly past one hundred. The successor he had asked for would die in Decius's persecution two generations later, the chair of James still rooted where Narcissus had set it.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"That sinners may not say in their hearts: there is no judgment."_ — _Eusebius's gloss on the fates of Narcissus's accusers_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "30", name: "Marcellus the Centurion"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 30]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Marcellus the Centurion]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman officer, refused the imperial sacrifice, c. 260–298]

#v(0.5em)

He was a centurion of the Legio VII Gemina, the Trajanic legion that had been stationed at León in Roman Spain for two centuries and was, by the late third century, the principal garrison of the Iberian peninsula. He had been a Christian since at least his early manhood — perhaps from childhood; the family was Christian — and had served in the legion for at least fifteen years without conflict between his religion and his rank, since the Roman army of the period required no specifically pagan act of its soldiers as a condition of service. Maximian's persecution edicts of 297 changed that. The standing orders required every soldier to participate in the public sacrifice on the emperor's birthday — to eat the meat from the altar, to pour the libation, to acknowledge the imperial divinity. The birthday came around on the twenty-first of July, 298.

Marcellus's legion, then on garrison duty at Tingis (Tangier) in Roman Mauritania, was assembled in the parade-ground for the imperial sacrifice. The wine was poured; the altar was lit; the troops formed up. Marcellus stepped out of the line, unbuckled his _cingulum militare_ — the broad bronze-fitted leather belt that was the soldier's badge of rank — and laid it on the ground in front of the altar with his sword and his vine-staff on top of it. _I am a Christian,_ he said. _I cannot serve the emperors except by serving Christ. From this day on I cease to follow your gods of wood and stone, who are deaf and dumb idols._ The standard-bearers stared. The other centurions shouted at him to pick up the belt. He left it where it lay.

He was arrested on the spot, held a week, and brought before the deputy prefect of the African provinces, Astasius Fortunatus, who tried for four hours to talk him into recanting. The court protocol is unusually full and unusually patient — Astasius was a humane administrator and clearly did not want to execute a fifteen-year veteran with a clean record. He kept offering alternatives: leave of absence, transfer, a quiet retirement. Marcellus answered each with the same line. _It is not lawful for a Christian to bear arms for the things of this world._ Astasius, finally exhausted, transferred the case to the higher court and let Marcellus be charged formally with desertion. He was beheaded outside the city walls of Tingis on the thirtieth of October, 298, by the local centurion's _vitis_ — the same vine-staff he had himself once carried.

A court-recorder named Cassian, present for the sentencing, threw down his wax tablets and refused to write the death-warrant. He was killed with Marcellus the next month. Their two _acta_ travel together in every Carthaginian recension. The fifteen-year centurion who would not pour the wine has been remembered in the Roman martyrology for seventeen hundred years.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Non licet Christiano homini molestiis sæcularibus militare, qui Christo Domino militat."_ — Marcellus, in the courtyard at Tingis, the line his judge could not get him to retract]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "October", day: "31", name: "Quintinus of Vermand"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[October 31]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Quintinus of Vermand]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Roman senator, missionary to the Belgae, martyr of Picardy, c. 260–c. 287]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Roman of the senatorial class, son of one of the consular families of the second century, well-educated in the household, baptized as a young man under Pope Caius around 282. He went up into Gaul with the great Roman missionary band that the Gallic chronicles call _the seven bishops_ — Denis to Paris, Saturninus to Toulouse, Trophimus to Arles, Paul to Narbonne, Martial to Limoges, Gatian to Tours, Lucian to Beauvais — and Quintinus, the youngest of them, walked north with Lucian into the _Augusta Veromanduorum,_ the Roman frontier-town that the medieval French would call Saint-Quentin after him. He was perhaps twenty-five.

He preached for two or three years up and down the Somme valley with what success the figures put at six hundred conversions in a single afternoon at the central forum — the standard hagiographic round number, but the Roman senator's Latin and manners would have given him a hearing among the half-Romanized Belgic gentry of the region, and the Christian community of Vermand was real and substantial by the time the persecution caught up with it. The persecution was the small private hunt of a single Roman officer named Rictiovarus, _præfectus prætorio Galliarum_ under Maximian, who carried his charge with a personal viciousness none of the historians of the period could quite explain. He hunted down the missionary clergy of northern Gaul one at a time. He took Quintinus in 287.

The trial-acta are circumstantial in a way that suggests they descend from a real court protocol, much elaborated. Quintinus was beaten with leaded scourges, then racked, then stretched between two stakes; boiling oil was poured down his back; nails were driven through his fingers; quicklime, mustard, and vinegar were forced down his throat; eventually two iron bars were driven through his body from neck to thigh. Through all of it, the interrogator could get only the same answer: _Christianus sum._ When the prefect finally ordered the beheading, on the thirty-first of October, the _acta_ preserve the small detail the Bollandists love most: a white dove rose from the body and disappeared into the morning sky. Rictiovarus, perhaps superstitiously, perhaps simply tired, ordered the body weighted with lead and dropped into the Somme just downstream of the bridge.

Fifty-five years later the body was found by a blind Roman noblewoman named Eusebia who had been instructed in a vision to dredge the river at the bend below the bridge. She found it uncorrupt, lifted it out, was healed of her blindness when she touched it, and gave it to the local Christian community for burial. The basilica that grew over the burial became the cathedral of the diocese the Carolingians would rebuild around it in the ninth century, and the medieval town that grew around it took the saint's name. The Roman senator who had walked north to evangelize a foreign country has been the patron of that country for seventeen hundred years.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Christianus sum."_ — Quintinus, in the long interrogation at Augusta Veromanduorum]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "November", day: "1", name: "Austremonius of Clermont"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[November 1]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Austremonius of Clermont]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Apostle of the Auvergne, first bishop of Clermont, traditionally fl. c. 250]

#v(0.5em)

He was one of the seven bishops the Roman church sent into Gaul in the middle of the third century — Saturninus to Toulouse, Trophimus to Arles, Paul to Narbonne, Martial to Limoges, Gatian to Tours, Denis to Paris, and Austremonius (in the Auvergnat speech that grew up around his shrine, _Stremoine_) to the great volcanic plateau in the heart of Roman Gaul where the Arverni had once held against Caesar. He arrived at _Augustonemetum_ — the Roman name for what would become Clermont — perhaps around 250, and walked into a city of perhaps thirty thousand inhabitants where the Christian community he found was, at most, a handful.

He built a small chapel inside the city walls, the _acta_ preserve, _non palam sed occulte_ — not openly, but in secret. He preached. He baptized. He ordained, from the small group of converts who collected around him, the first generation of Auvergnat presbyters and deacons. The community grew. Within a few years he was running the catechumenate openly; within a generation the diocese he had founded had spread up the Allier and out across the high pasturelands of the central massif. He was perhaps the first martyr of the see — the older _acta_ have him beheaded by the local pagans, having converted the children of a leading family and aroused the father to fury — though whether his life ended in martyrdom or in peaceful old age the Bollandists declined to settle.

The cult was immediate and unbroken. Gregory of Tours — who grew up in the Auvergne in the sixth century and who knew the Clermont cathedral inside and out — preserves the eucharistic miracle the _acta_ report at length: a peasant who had been given consecrated bread by Austremonius after Mass, having stowed it in his satchel for the road home, was caught by a sudden flood at the crossing of the Allier and would have drowned but for the satchel, which floated him to shore as if it were a boat. Gregory adds that this is why, in the Auvergne in his day, the country people kept the practice of eating the _eulogia_ — the blessed bread distributed at the end of Sunday Mass — before any other food on the Lord's day.

He was buried at first inside the city walls and later translated to the great abbey of Issoire, twenty miles south of Clermont, where his bones still lie. The Clermont he founded crowned its kings of France there and held its first crusade-council there in 1095 and rebuilt its black-stone cathedral over his crypt in the thirteenth century. The first bishop of the chair has been buried for seventeen hundred years. The chair has not been empty since.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Eulogias ante alium cibum:"_ — _the Auvergnat custom Gregory of Tours preserves: the blessed bread, before any other food_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "November", day: "2", name: "Ambrose of Agaune"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[November 2]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Ambrose of Agaune]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Burgundian abbot, second father of Saint-Maurice, c. 470–c. 521]

#v(0.5em)

He had been abbot of the small monastery of Île-Barbe — _the Barbarian Island,_ the wooded outcrop in the middle of the Saône just upriver from Lyon — when the Burgundian king Sigismund, recently converted from Arianism, decided in 515 to refound the great Frankish monastery at Agaunum in the alpine Valais. Agaunum was the site of the martyrdom of Maurice and the Theban Legion — the Egyptian legion who had refused to draw their swords against fellow Christians, executed in the gorge of the Rhone perhaps in 287 — and the small chapel that had grown up over their grave had become, by the early sixth century, run-down, half-secularized, and inhabited largely by laymen who had drifted into it for the patronage. Sigismund wanted a real monastic community. The Burgundian bishops, gathered for the dedication on September 22, 515, had recommended Hymnemodus of Grigny for the office. He took it; he died within five years; the brothers asked for Ambrose to succeed him.

Ambrose came up from the Saône with a few of his Île-Barbe brothers in the autumn of 520. The reform he installed at Agaune would shape the great Frankish monastic foundations of the next two centuries: the _laus perennis,_ the perpetual praise, by which the brothers were divided into nine choirs that succeeded each other through the twenty-four hours of the day so that the office of the Mass and the singing of the psalms continued unbroken at the tomb of the martyrs from year's end to year's end. The custom had been imported from the East — from the _acoemetae_ of Constantinople, the _sleepless_ monks — and the Burgundian foundation became its first Western installation. The form spread. By the eighth century the _laus perennis_ would be observed at Saint-Denis, at Saint-Riquier, at Reichenau, at Lorsch, at every great Carolingian house of the Frankish heartland.

Ambrose served as abbot for perhaps a year. He died on the second of November, 521. The _Vita sanctorum abbatum Agaunensium_ — written within a generation of his death by an Agaune monk who had known him personally — gives him a quiet portrait: a small, careful, deeply learned man who had spent his short rule holding the brothers to the rule and the silence, who had asked to be buried not under his own name but at the foot of the martyrs' altar, _quia non est dignus servus super dominum suum,_ because the servant is not worthy to be over his lord.

The abbey he reformed has not stopped chanting. _Saint-Maurice d'Agaune_ is the oldest continuously functioning monastery in the West — fifteen hundred years on the same foundation, fifty years older than Monte Cassino — and the office Ambrose installed has never been entirely silenced. The Augustinian canons who govern the abbey today still keep the vigil at the martyrs' tomb. The servant who would not be over his lord has been at his lord's feet for fifteen hundred years.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Quia non est dignus servus super dominum suum."_ — Ambrose, on his refusal to be buried under his own name]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "November", day: "3", name: "Hubert of Liège"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[November 3]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Hubert of Liège]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Bishop of the Ardennes, the stag in the forest, c. 656–727]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Frankish nobleman of the Aquitanian royal house — perhaps the eldest son of Bertrand of Toulouse, the last duke of Aquitaine before the Carolingian absorption — born around 656 to the world of court intrigue and forest hunting that the Frankish _grandes_ lived in the long century of decline before Pepin and Charles Martel. He was raised at the court of King Theuderic III. He married a Bavarian princess named Floribana, by whom he had a son. He served, in his early thirties, as a count of the palace under Pepin of Herstal. He was a famous hunter — Bishop Jonas of Orléans's ninth-century _Vita_ preserves the family memory of him in this period: red-haired, light-eyed, expert with hawk and hound, fond of his own horses, occasionally given to anger.

The conversion came on a Good Friday in his late thirties. The court had assembled for the great spring stag-hunt in the forest of the Ardennes; Hubert had ridden out alone before dawn, against his confessor's advice, to course a great white stag he had been tracking for weeks. The chase carried him deep into the forest. The stag, when at last he closed on it in a clearing high above the Lesse river, turned and faced him; between its antlers, the _Vita_ says, was a luminous cross. The stag spoke: _Hubert, why are you hunting me, when you should be hunting your own salvation?_ Hubert dismounted. The cross was still there. He went home that evening, gave away his hounds and his horses, told his wife and his son, and walked east into the wilderness with no possessions and no plan.

He took shelter at the small monastic foundation of Lambert at Maastricht. Lambert was the bishop of the Tongres-Liège diocese — a missionary see that ran from the upper Meuse north into Frisia and east into the German forest country — and Lambert took the new convert in, taught him the office, ordained him priest, and over the course of a dozen years made him the indispensable second-in-command of the Maastricht church. When Lambert was assassinated in 705, Hubert was elected his successor against his strenuous protest. He governed the see for twenty-two years.

What he did with the office was missionary work. The Ardennes forest he had once hunted in was still half-pagan in the early eighth century — small Frankish villages with sacred groves and standing stones, a thin overlay of Christian baptism on a mostly older religion — and Hubert spent twenty years on horseback going from clearing to clearing, baptizing, building chapels, ordaining local priests, settling disputes, healing the dying. The cathedral he built at Liège on the banks of the Meuse, where he had translated Lambert's body and where the city of Liège grew around the basilica he had raised, became the seat of a diocese that has not moved since. He died of a hunting-injury infection at Tervueren on the thirtieth of May, 727; the cult-day was moved to the third of November, the day his body was translated to Liège.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Why are you hunting me, when you should be hunting your own salvation?"_ — _the white stag in the Ardennes forest, on the Good Friday Hubert was converted_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "November", day: "4", name: "Emeric (Imre) of Hungary"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[November 4]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Emeric (Imre) of Hungary]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Crown prince of the Magyars, son of Stephen, d. 1031]

#v(0.5em)

He was the only surviving son of Stephen of Hungary — the king who had taken the Christian crown from Pope Sylvester II at the dawn of the new millennium — and his Bavarian wife Gisela, sister of the emperor Henry II. He was born around 1007, named for his uncle the emperor (the Hungarian _Imre_ is the Magyar form of the German _Heinrich_), and raised at the new Christian court at Esztergom in the strict piety his father had imported from the Bavarian and Lotharingian missionary houses. The tutor Stephen chose for him was Gerard of Csanád, the Venetian Benedictine bishop eventually killed by the pagan revolt of 1046. The _Vita Maior Sancti Stephani_ preserves the program of the boy's education: the seven liberal arts in the morning, the _Rule of Saint Benedict_ in the afternoon, the _Imitatio_ of the Eastern desert fathers in the evening. The prince was being raised to be the second Christian king of Hungary.

He was being raised with the assumption that the throne was his and would come to him in middle life. Stephen, by 1030, was sixty years old; the kingdom was secure; the succession was clear; the long work of converting Hungary from a pagan tribal confederation into a Christian European kingdom was at the point where it could finally be turned over to the next generation. Imre had been knighted, betrothed, and was preparing in the autumn of 1031 to assume the regency of the southern Croatian-Serbian frontier when, on the second of September, he was killed by a wild boar in a hunting accident in the forests of the Bihar mountains. He was twenty-four years old.

The shock to his father was the central wound of the rest of Stephen's life. The _Vita_ preserves the king's prayer: _Lord, you have taken from me what you gave me; what shall I give you back?_ The answer, as Stephen formed it over the next seven years of his life — he died in 1038 — was the dedication of the kingdom itself. With no heir, with the next of his line a cousin who could not be trusted with the crown, with the Christian settlement of Hungary still fragile, Stephen lifted the crown of his kingdom on his own deathbed and gave it to the Virgin Mary as her perpetual fief: _Regnum Marianum,_ the Marian kingdom, Hungary as Mary's land. The dedication held. The Hungarian nation has called itself by that name ever since.

What survives of Imre is small. The _Vita_ preserves his vow of perpetual chastity, taken on his wedding night with his bride's consent. It preserves his nightly reading of the psalter, his almsgiving to the poor of Esztergom, his unfailing presence at his father's morning Mass, the gentleness with which he treated the small Christian community that was just beginning to take root in his future kingdom. He was canonized in 1083 by Pope Gregory VII along with his father and his tutor Gerard — three saints in one family in one generation, the only such triple canonization in medieval church history. The young man who had been preparing to govern the kingdom became, in dying before he could, the cause of its dedication to Mary. The father had given the kingdom away because the son had gone before him.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Lord, you have taken from me what you gave me; what shall I give you back?"_ — Stephen, in the _Vita,_ at the news of Imre's death]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "November", day: "5", name: "Zechariah and Elizabeth"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[November 5]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Zechariah and Elizabeth]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Parents of John the Baptist, fl. c. 5 B.C.]

#v(0.5em)

The first chapter of Luke is what we have. He was a priest of the temple, of the _course of Abijah_ — one of twenty-four rotating divisions of the Aaronic priesthood, each of which served at Jerusalem for one week twice a year — married to a woman who was herself of the priestly line and who was, in some way the gospel does not specify, kin to the mother of the Lord. They lived in the hill-country of Judah, perhaps at Ein Karem six miles west of Jerusalem where the medieval tradition has placed them. They were old. They had no children. They had given up praying for one.

Zechariah's week of service came around in the late summer or early autumn of perhaps 6 B.C. He drew the lot for the morning incense — the most prized of the daily offerings, distributed by sortition among the priests of the day's course who had not yet performed it — and went into the _hekal,_ the inner sanctuary, alone. The angel was on the right side of the altar of incense. The terror was immediate. The promise — _thy prayer is heard; thy wife Elizabeth shall bear thee a son, and thou shalt call his name John_ — was older than Zechariah expected, going back beyond his prayer to the long Israelite line of which he and his wife had been the apparent late dead end. The doubt was honest. _Whereby shall I know this? for I am an old man, and my wife well stricken in years._ The sign was the silence — _thou shalt be dumb, and not able to speak, until the day that these things shall be performed._ He came out of the temple and made signs to the people, and they understood that he had seen a vision. He went home dumb.

Elizabeth conceived. She kept herself hidden five months — _thus hath the Lord dealt with me in the days wherein he looked on me, to take away my reproach among men._ In the sixth month her young cousin Mary came up from Nazareth. The greeting at the door — _blessed art thou among women, and blessed is the fruit of thy womb_ — is the foundation of the _Hail Mary;_ the _Magnificat_ that follows is one of the four canticles of the Christian liturgy.

The child was born. They circumcised him on the eighth day. They tried to call him Zacharias for his father; the mother insisted on John, the name the angel had given; the father wrote it on a tablet — _his name is John_ — and his tongue was loosed and he began to prophesy. _Benedictus Dominus Deus Israel,_ the third of the gospel canticles, is what came out. He blessed his son, kissed his wife, and walked back into a quiet old age. Of his death and of Elizabeth's the gospel is silent. The medieval tradition has him martyred in the temple by Herod's troops at the time of the Massacre of the Innocents, but the scripture has nothing of it. What it has is the silence of nine months and the song that broke it.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"His name is John."_ — Zechariah, on the wax tablet, _Luke_ 1:63]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "November", day: "6", name: "Winnoc of Wormhoudt"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[November 6]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Winnoc of Wormhoudt]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Anglo-Breton monk, abbot of Wormhoudt, c. 640–c. 717]

#v(0.5em)

He was Welsh-Breton by family — a member of the Cornish-Breton royal house of Domnonia, perhaps a son of King Judicael of Brittany — and was sent across the Channel as a young man to study under Bertinus at the new Benedictine foundation at Sithiu in Frankish Flanders. The monastery he came to had been started by Audomarus and Mommolinus only thirty years earlier, and Winnoc became one of the four Britons whom Bertinus accepted as novices in the 670s, perhaps the most accomplished of them. He took the habit, made profession, and stayed at Sithiu for the next twenty years.

In about 695 Bertinus, now an old man, sent Winnoc north into the partly-pagan country between the Aa and the North Sea — the Frankish _pagus Mempiscus,_ a flat marshy region of small fishing-villages and isolated farms where the gospel had not yet taken hold — with three British companions to plant a daughter house. They built a small cell at Wormhoudt, a hamlet on a low rise above the marsh, and over the next two decades raised it into a working monastery: refectory, dormitory, scriptorium, an enclosed garden for the brothers, an open one for the poor. Winnoc was its first prior and eventually its abbot. The community was small — perhaps a dozen brothers — and Winnoc, though now in his sixties and the abbot, refused to be excused from any of the manual work. He took his turn at the kitchen, the laundry, the pigsty, the scriptorium. When he had grown too old to work in the fields, he asked to be assigned to the mill, where he would grind the brothers' grain.

The mill at Wormhoudt was one of the small hand-mills the monasteries used to grind a day's flour at a time. A man Winnoc's age — he was in his late seventies by then — ought not to have been able to keep up with the strength of the millstones. The brothers noticed, after a few months, that the meal-bin behind the mill was always full when they came to draw flour from it, and that the stones were turning at speeds the old man could not possibly have produced by his own arm. One of the brothers, curious, peered through the gap in the mill-shed wall one afternoon to see how he managed. The mill stopped immediately. The brother went blind on the spot. He stumbled into the chapel weeping; the brothers brought him to Winnoc's feet; Winnoc, gentle and amused, blessed his eyes in the Lord's name and the man saw again.

He died at Wormhoudt on the sixth of November, perhaps 717, eighty years old at least. The body was translated to Bergues-Saint-Winoc when the abbey there was founded under his name in the late ninth century, and it has lain there since. The Welsh-Breton prince who had been sent across the Channel as a young man and never went home founded one of the great small monasteries of Frankish Flanders by being willing, at the end of a long life, to grind the brothers' grain.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Tene quod habes, ne alius accipiat coronam tuam."_ — _the verse hung above Winnoc's mill at Wormhoudt: Hold fast that which you have, that no man take your crown_]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "November", day: "7", name: "Willibrord of Utrecht"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[November 7]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Willibrord of Utrecht]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Apostle of the Frisians, first archbishop of Utrecht, c. 658–739]

#v(0.5em)

He was a Northumbrian, born around 658 in the kingdom of Deira, the southern half of the Northumbrian heptarchy where Edwin had ruled the previous generation. His father Wilgis was a married presbyter of the Yorkshire countryside who, after the boy's birth, set himself up as a hermit on the cliffs above the Humber and lived another forty years there as a solitary. Willibrord was sent at seven to the great Northumbrian abbey of Ripon under Wilfrid and was raised there as one of Wilfrid's own children. At twenty he went to the Irish monastery of Rathmelsigi to learn Greek and patristic Latin and continental computus; he stayed for twelve years.

The proposal to plant a mission in Frisia came from his old Irish abbot Egbert in 690. The Frankish _maior domus_ Pippin of Herstal, having just defeated the Frisian king Radbod in a frontier campaign that opened the Christianizable parts of the lower Rhine country to the gospel, was looking for missionary clergy. Egbert sent Willibrord with eleven companions — _erant autem numero XII,_ the twelve apostles of Frisia. They landed at the mouth of the Rhine, walked east along the coast to Utrecht, and settled in the small Roman chapel that the great Frankish missionary Amandus had built sixty years earlier and that the pagan Frisians had reduced to ruins.

Willibrord went to Rome in 695 to be consecrated bishop. Pope Sergius received him in the Lateran, gave him the new name _Clemens,_ put the pallium on his shoulders, and sent him back as the first archbishop of the Frisians. He took his see at Utrecht, the small Roman _castellum_ on the lower Rhine that the Frankish administration had been using as a frontier customs-post. He governed the Frisian church for forty-four more years.

The work was patient and slow. He ordained a generation of Frisian-speaking presbyters; he founded the great daughter monastery of Echternach in 698 on a piece of land Pepin's mother-in-law Irmina gave him; he made repeated missionary journeys north into Denmark (the Danish king Ongendus refused him hospitality but gave him thirty Danish boys to be raised as missionaries — they were the first Christian-Scandinavian generation), into the Frankish-Saxon borderlands, into the Frisian island of Heligoland, where he baptized in the spring at the foot of the pagan idol of Fositesland, a defiant act that drew the murderous attention of King Radbod. Bede, who knew him personally, gives him the warmest portrait of any of the second-generation continental missionaries. He was, Bede writes, _moderatus, gravis, prudens, in conversatione humilis et iocundus_ — measured, serious, prudent, in his conversation humble and pleasant. He died at Echternach on the seventh of November, 739, eighty-one years old, having walked into a swampy frontier country at thirty-two and walked out of it having given the country a church.

#v(0.4em)
#block(inset: (left: 0.8em), stroke: (left: 0.5pt + gray))[#text(size: 9.5pt)[_"Moderatus, gravis, prudens, in conversatione humilis et iocundus."_ — Bede, _Historia Ecclesiastica_ 5.11]]

#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "November", day: "8", name: "Godfrey of Amiens"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[November 8]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Godfrey of Amiens]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Reform abbot, contested bishop, c. 1066–1115]

#v(0.5em)

He was given to the Benedictines of Mont-Saint-Quentin near Péronne as an _oblatus_ at five years old, the customary medieval offering of a younger son to the religious life. He stayed there for thirty-five years. He was a quiet, intense, careful man — _reverend, sober, pacific,_ the source Nicolas of Soissons writes — and he rose through the offices of the community in the steady way the Cluniac reform had begun to make standard for the well-governed houses of the late eleventh century: novice-master at twenty, prior at twenty-eight, in 1096 elected abbot of Nogent-sous-Coucy, the half-ruined small house in the Aisne forest that had been waiting for someone who could repair it. He went, with a half-dozen brothers from Mont-Saint-Quentin. He stayed for eight years. He raised it from twelve brothers to forty, refounded the dormitory and the chapter-house, restored the conventual office to its full Benedictine observance, and made the abbey one of the model Cluniac reformed houses of Picardy. The chronicler Guibert of Nogent — who succeeded him as abbot — calls him _speculum totius religionis,_ the mirror of the whole religion.

The election to the see of Amiens in 1104 came against his strongest protest. The previous bishop had been a noted simoniac whom Pope Urban II had deposed; the chapter wanted a reformer of unimpeachable life; the king and the count of Amiens both backed Godfrey. He resisted for months — he believed an abbot should not be made a bishop, and he believed himself unequal to the office — and was finally consecrated under what amounted to an ecclesiastical compulsion in November 1104.

The eleven years that followed were the most contested of any episcopate in northern France in the early twelfth century. Godfrey was a reformer of the strict observance: he disciplined the cathedral chapter, broke the long-standing simoniacal traffic in benefices, refused to consecrate any candidate who could not show the canonical age and education, and (most explosively) supported the Amiens _commune_ — the elected civic government the burghers had set up against the count and the bishop's predecessors — when the count tried to have him repudiate it. _I will rather suffer exile than withdraw from the just,_ he is reported to have said when the count's representatives threatened him. He was twice driven out of the city by political enemies and twice returned at the head of a peaceful procession of his own clergy. He died in 1115 at the abbey of Saint-Crispin in Soissons, returning from a long preaching tour, sixty years old. His enemies in Amiens refused to have the body brought home; the Cistercians at Soissons buried him themselves, and his bones have been there since.

What survives, between Guibert's hostile-but-respectful chapters and Nicholas of Soissons's adoring _Vita,_ is a man who would not be moved: _bonum quod amplexus erat tenebat firmiter et constantissime defendebat,_ Guibert writes, even when he disliked him — _the good he had embraced he held firmly and defended with the greatest constancy._

