#pagebreak(weak: true)
#metadata((kind: "toc", month: "November", day: "10", name: "Tryphon of Lampsacus"))

#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray)[#smallcaps[November 10]]

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 20pt, weight: "semibold")[Tryphon of Lampsacus]

#text(size: 11pt, style: "italic", fill: rgb("#444"))[Boy goose-herd, healer, martyr, c. 232–c. 250]

#v(0.5em)

He was a peasant child of the small Phrygian village of Kampsada, near Apamea on the high inland plateau of Asia Minor, born to a Christian family of goose-herders perhaps in the early 230s. The Greek _acta_ preserve the memory of him as a small boy in a long woollen tunic, herding the family's flock of geese on the high pastures above the village, praying as he walked. He had a gift, the _acta_ preserve, for the healing of small things: the lambs that had been struck by snakes, the calves that would not nurse, the vines that the locusts had begun to take. The local farmers brought him their failing crops and their sick livestock; he laid his small hands on them; the affliction lifted. By the time he was thirteen the gift had grown to humans, and the regional pagan authorities had begun to take notice.

The fame brought him, the _acta_ preserve, to the imperial court itself. The emperor Gordian III's young daughter fell into a sustained possession that none of the court physicians or the priests of Apollo could relieve. The emperor's chamberlain, hearing reports of the Phrygian boy, sent for him. Tryphon walked the eight hundred miles from Phrygia to Rome and, brought before the possessed girl, prayed over her in the language of the Galilean fishermen. The girl was freed. Gordian sent him home with a small purse and a court protection. He went back to his geese.

The protection lapsed when Gordian was murdered in 244 and Decius eventually came to the throne in 249. The Decian persecution opened in early 250. Tryphon was perhaps eighteen or nineteen by then. The local _praefectus,_ a man named Aquilinus, had him arrested at his village and brought to Nicaea — the imperial city on the Sea of Marmara that would later host the great council — for the standard interrogation. The _acta_ preserve the trial in close detail: Aquilinus was charming, then threatening, then enraged. The boy answered with the patience of someone who had been answering this question for years. _Christianus sum. I am a Christian. The names you would have me invoke are the names of demons. The God I serve is the God who made the geese._ Aquilinus put him through the standard tortures — the rack, the sharpened iron combs, hot lead poured down the chest, finally horse-shoes nailed to his feet so they could drag him through the streets behind a chariot. The boy held. Eventually, on the tenth of November, 250, Aquilinus tired of it and ordered the head struck off.

The body was recovered by the Christian community of Nicaea and buried at his village. The relics traveled west during the Saracen disruptions of the seventh century and ended up at Rome under the high altar of the church of _Santo Spirito in Sassia_ — the small Saxon church the Anglo-Saxon kings had built for English pilgrims in the eighth century — where they have stayed since. The boy who had healed his sick goslings is the patron, in the Greek tradition, of farmers and gardeners and small children sick with fever. Eight hundred years after his death the field-priests of the Aegean were still blessing the fields of new-sown wheat in his name on his feast day.

