#metadata((kind: "back", title: "Afterword"))
#heading(level: 1, outlined: false, numbering: none)[#text(size: 22pt, weight: "semibold")[Afterword]]

#v(1em)

This book ends where the _Acta Sanctorum_ ends. The Bollandists worked through the calendar in order, a volume at a time, and they were still working when the world overtook them. The last of their great folios to treat the saints day by day reach only into the early part of November; the volume for the tenth, where this book closes, was among the final ones to appear. December was never finished in the same fashion. In 1940, as a second war broke over Europe, the society issued a single supplementary volume for December — a martyrology rather than a gathering of lives — and the three-hundred-year labor of the day-by-day _Acta_ came, in effect, to rest a few weeks short of the year's end.

It was not for want of will. The original Society of Bollandists — named for Jean Bolland, who took up at Antwerp the plan Heribert Rosweyde had drawn in 1607 — was suppressed in 1788, in the upheavals that scattered the old Catholic learning, its library broken up and its presses stopped. Half a century later, in 1837, the Bollandists were revived at Brussels, and they have worked without interruption since. But they did not take the folios up again and drive them to December. The age of the great calendar had passed; what the work now required was tools — to sort the genuine from the spurious, to catalogue every manuscript life of every saint. And so the modern Bollandists turned to that: to the journal _Analecta Bollandiana_, begun in 1882 and still appearing; to the _Bibliotheca Hagiographica_ that indexes the lives in Latin, Greek, and the languages of the East; to the patient critical method of scholars like Hippolyte Delehaye, who taught the Church to read its own legends with reverence and honesty at once. They are at the work still, in Brussels.

So the tenth of November is not an arbitrary stopping place. It is the edge of an unfinished thing — the point where one of the longest labors in the history of Christian scholarship set down its pen, and where its heirs took up a different one. This book has followed the saints only as far as the Bollandists carried them. Beyond it lie the rest of November, and December, and all the saints the calendar still keeps.
